COPY public.questions (id, subject_id, year, text, options, correct_option, explanation, topic, image_url) FROM stdin;
15dbc5f4-9113-4e86-855f-3824d8c705a9	pathology_micro	BDS-I	A patient with a history of rheumatic fever requires a dental extraction. To prevent Subacute Bacterial Endocarditis (SABE), the clinician must target which group of organisms most commonly associated with this condition?	{"Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pyogenes","Viridans group streptococci","Enterococcus faecalis"}	2	St. Viridans (including S. sanguis and S. mutans) are noted as the primary organisms leading to SABE and late-onset prosthetic valve endocarditis .	Cardiac Infections	\N
bc84086d-dd7c-427b-99a6-2d61bce75939	pathology_micro	BDS-I	A child presents with multiple dental caries. According to the sources, which organism is the primary etiological agent, and how does it compare to its closest relative in this process?	{"S. sanguis > S. mutans","S. mutans > S. sanguis","S. mitis > S. mutans","S. salivarius > S. mutans"}	1	Dental caries is caused primarily by Streptococcus mutans, followed by Streptococcus sanguis .	Oral Microbiology	\N
fd972a04-9c21-4608-a45b-12cbd034b51c	pathology_micro	BDS-I	An HIV-positive patient presents with non-scrapable white patches on the lateral borders of the tongue. The source identifies the most likely etiologic agent of this "Oral Hairy Leukoplakia" as:	{"Candida albicans","Human Papillomavirus","Epstein-Barr Virus","Herpes Simplex Virus-1"}	2	Oral Hairy Leukoplakia is a non-scrapable whitish patch on the tongue typically caused by the Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) .	Virology / HIV	\N
86e862a7-1060-44da-8e78-5bdbd50f109b	pathology_micro	BDS-I	While performing a routine check, a microbiologist identifies a gram-positive bacillus that exhibits "Tumbling motility" at 25°C but becomes non-motile at 37°C. This organism is:	{"Bacillus anthracis","Listeria monocytogenes","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Clostridium tetani"}	1	Listeria monocytogenes shows differential motility based on temperature: it is motile (tumbling motility) at 20-25°C but non-motile at 37°C .	Bacteriology	\N
76966659-7330-4e97-97c9-8bdceb3e1818	pathology_micro	BDS-I	A clinician suspects a patient has a "Primary Chancre" on the lip. Which microscopy method is the gold standard for visualizing the spiral-shaped organisms that cause this condition?	{"Phase contrast","Dark field microscopy","Electron microscopy","Fluorescent microscopy"}	1	Spirochetes (like Treponema pallidum which causes Syphilis) are observed using Dark Field Microscopy, where the background is dark and reflected light is used .	Diagnostic Tools	\N
d4efb18d-1b9c-4923-b281-93183c3bf6a7	pathology_micro	BDS-I	A 12-year-old boy presents with "lockjaw" and spastic muscle contractions. If the causative agent shows "drumstick appearance" spores, what is the specific biochemical mechanism of the toxin involved?	{"Blockage of Acetylcholine release","Inhibition of GABA/Glycine release","Activation of Adenylate cyclase","Inhibition of Protein Synthesis"}	1	Tetanospasmin (from C. tetani) acts pre-synaptically to inhibit the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine, leading to spastic paralysis .	Bacterial Toxins	\N
82bb9589-b303-4fb0-af79-c824e80ea7e8	pathology_micro	BDS-I	During the sterilization of heat-sensitive dental instruments like plastic tips or pre-packed syringes, which method is considered both microbicidal and sporicidal due to its high penetrating power?	{"Autoclave (121°C)","Glutaraldehyde (2%)","Ethylene Oxide (ETO)",Pasteurization}	2	Ethylene Oxide (ETO) is a gaseous chemical sterilant used for pre-packed syringes and dental equipment because it has high penetrating power and is both microbicidal and sporicidal .	Sterilization	\N
5d46eccd-8190-4a06-93a0-6a2ddaf28151	pathology_micro	BDS-I	Which immunoglobulin is found in the lowest concentration in the body but is responsible for the "Homocytotropism" phenomenon (affinity for mast cells) in Type I Hypersensitivity?	{IgG,IgM,IgE,IgD}	2	IgE has the lowest concentration in the body and shows Homocytotropism (movement towards mast cells), making it the mediator of Type I Hypersensitivity .	Immunology	\N
16869f54-5d8c-4eaa-93ee-4710c6c9b10e	pathology_micro	BDS-I	In a suspected case of Mycobacterium leprae, the clinician uses a modified Ziehl-Neelsen stain. What is the specific percentage of acid-fastness for this organism compared to M. tuberculosis?	{"1% H2SO4","5% H2SO4","20% H2SO4","0.25% H2SO4"}	1	M. leprae is 5% acid-fast, whereas M. tuberculosis is 20% acid-fast. Bacterial spores are 0.25% acid-fast, and Nocardia is 1% acid-fast .	Staining Techniques	\N
0128dd27-c6bc-43be-9c27-6126e21461ab	pathology_micro	BDS-I	A microbiologist is trying to differentiate Corynebacterium diphtheriae from Corynebacterium pseudotuberculosis. Which biochemical test is the most definitive for this purpose?	{"Catalase test","Oxidase test","Urease test","Tellurite reduction"}	2	C. pseudotuberculosis is urease positive, which differentiates it from C. diphtheriae .	Bacteriology	\N
c46e33b2-a97e-4707-b968-a337a11d7a75	pathology_micro	BDS-I	A patient with a prosthetic hip joint develops a fever. The clinician suspects a "late-onset" infection. According to the sources, the most likely organism found in normal skin flora responsible for prosthetic valve endocarditis is:	{"S. aureus","S. epidermidis","S. mutans","S. pyogenes"}	1	Staphylococcus epidermidis is a normal commensal of skin but is the most common organism involved in early prosthetic valve endocarditis and infections related to IV cannulas .	Nosocomial Infections	\N
b464eda0-feda-447b-b695-d2b56a618004	pathology_micro	BDS-I	In the clinical diagnosis of Infective Endocarditis, which of the following is considered a "Major Criterion" under the Modified Duke Criteria?	{"Fever > 38.0°C","Osler's nodes","Typical IE organism from two separate blood cultures",Splenomegaly}	2	A Major Criterion for IE includes a positive blood culture with a typical IE organism (like Viridans Streptococci or HACEK group) from two separate sets .	Cardiac Infections	\N
9433c923-ec15-4cd4-ba12-62ccc2822625	pathology_micro	BDS-I	Gram staining differentiates bacteria based on the thickness of which layer, and what is the specific color transition for a gram-negative organism?	{"LPS; Purple to Pink","Peptidoglycan; Purple to Pink","Peptidoglycan; Pink to Purple","Teichoic acid; No change"}	1	Gram staining differentiates based on the Peptidoglycan layer (thick in +ve, thin in -ve). Gram-negative bacteria appear pink after the safranin counterstain .	Basics of Microbiology	\N
be8ecfbc-bdfe-40be-b722-94c03256032c	pathology_micro	BDS-I	Mycobacterium tuberculosis is 20% acid-fast. According to the sources, what is the primary biochemical component responsible for this ability to resist decolorization by acid?	{"Teichoic acid",LPS,"Mycolic acid","Dipicolinic acid"}	2	Acid fastness is due to the presence of mycolic acid. The higher the mycolic acid content, the greater the resistance to decolorization .	Staining Techniques	\N
64354350-035e-429f-b2b5-68a0f70c599a	pathology_micro	BDS-I	In Biomedical Waste Management, human anatomical waste (e.g., teeth/tissue) should be placed in:	{"Red bag","Yellow bag","Blue bag","White container"}	1	Human anatomical waste is disposed of in Yellow bags for incineration .	Waste Management	\N
