e6fe22e3-eb4f-4fd9-be4c-9fc083442d14	materials	BDS-I	A new diagnostic test for dental caries has a sensitivity of 0.80 and a specificity of 0.50. If used on 100 teeth (90 healthy, 10 carious), what is the most significant clinical disadvantage?	{"Failing to detect 8 carious teeth","Correctly diagnosing 45 healthy teeth","Diagnosing 45 healthy teeth as carious","Failing to detect 5 carious teeth"}	2	High sensitivity (0.80) correctly detects 8/10 carious teeth. However, low specificity (0.50) leads to 45/90 healthy teeth being wrongly diagnosed as carious (False Positives), potentially leading to unnecessary treatment.	Patient Assessment & Diagnosis	\N
eceb2934-7b3e-4a27-a928-4619247b6ad6	materials	BDS-I	When preparing a tooth for a polycrystalline restorative material like dental amalgam, which factor is the primary reason for requiring a minimum material thickness (e.g., 1.5 mm)?	{"To enhance chemical bonding","High flexural strength of the material","Limited ability to flex without fracturing","To prevent secondary caries"}	2	Polycrystalline materials (amalgam, glass-ceramics) have limited ability to flex without fracturing. Therefore, they require sufficient bulk (depth) to resist fracture under occlusal loading, unlike polymeric materials.	Fundamentals of Tooth Preparation	\N
a9bd7fba-fef2-4543-a626-e8bab1a351cb	materials	BDS-I	In the context of polymerization shrinkage in composite resins, which cavity configuration (C-Factor) presents the highest risk of marginal debonding?	{"Class IV (two bonded, four unbonded surfaces)","Class II (three bonded, three unbonded surfaces)","Class I (five bonded, one unbonded surface)","Class V (one bonded, five unbonded surfaces)"}	2	The C-Factor is the ratio of bonded to unbonded surfaces. A Class I occlusal cavity has 5 bonded surfaces and only 1 unbonded surface, resulting in the highest C-factor and maximum stress on the adhesive interface.	Adhesion & Composite Resins	\N
d5f2af73-78bf-484e-b1f7-cd200df7b0bf	materials	BDS-I	A patient complains of sharp pain when drinking cold water immediately after a Class II amalgam restoration. The most likely physical property of the material responsible is:	{"High Coefficient of Thermal Expansion","Low Modulus of Elasticity","High Thermal Conductivity","High Solubility"}	2	Amalgam is an excellent thermal conductor. In deep preparations with low remaining dentin thickness (RDT), temperature changes are rapidly transmitted to the pulp unless an insulating base/liner is used.	Physical Properties of Materials	\N
6e7fd924-8e2b-4fc3-8b8b-79d3f764319f	materials	BDS-I	Zirconia (Zirconium Oxide) is considered "law-tolerant" due to a stress-induced phase transformation. This process involves:	{"Monoclinic to Tetragonal transformation","Tetragonal to Monoclinic transformation","Amorphous to Crystalline transformation","Cubic to Rhombohedral transformation"}	1	Stress-induced transformation from the metastable tetragonal form to the stable monoclinic form (t→m) occurs near a propagating crack. This causes a volume increase that closes the crack tip, preventing further propagation.	Dental Ceramics	\N
