952da54a-19a9-4437-a9bd-c43cc039ae03	materials	BDS-I	During the excavation of deep dentin caries, "firm dentin" is encountered. According to clinical protocols, this tissue should:	{"Be removed to ensure a bacteria-free cavity","Be removed only if it is at the DEJ","Be left in place as it can remineralize","Be treated with hydroluoric acid before restoration"}	2	Firm dentin has lost mineral but not to the point of collagen exposure. If isolated from the oral environment by a restoration, it will remineralize and therefore should not be removed.	Caries Management	\N
adad0ba8-c39f-416d-b158-0bdc69db1190	materials	BDS-I	While restoring a root canal with resin-based materials, a clinician faces an extremely unfavorable C-factor. What is the estimated magnitude of this factor in a root canal?	{"1 to 5","Approximately 50","Exceeds 200","Exactly 10"}	2	While direct coronal restorations typically have C-factors from 1 to 5, the restricted space of a root canal results in an estimated C-factor exceeding 200. This makes luting procedures involving radical polymerization resins very challenging.	Adhesion & Composite Resins	\N
