33c064d1-d38c-4656-90a0-4fa65df5a71e	materials	BDS-I	A clinician is "bonding" a zirconia restoration. According to modern protocols, why is phosphoric acid cleaning after try-in ineffective?	{"It etches the zirconia surface too deeply","Zirconia has a high affinity for proteins that acid cannot remove","It causes a monoclinic transformation","Phosphoric acid only works on glass-matrix ceramics"}	1	Zirconia has a strong affinity for proteins in saliva and blood. These proteins cannot be removed with phosphoric acid; instead, a sodium hydroxide solution (e.g., Ivoclean) is required for 20 seconds.	Dental Ceramics	\N
b14976c5-cf89-42e1-86b2-1e9783fe23c8	materials	BDS-I	In the context of "Wet-Field" bonding, what is the specific consequence of over-drying dentin before applying GIC?	{"Excessive fluoride release","The cement withdraws water from dentinal tubules, causing pain","Improved chemical bonding to collagen","Accelerated setting reaction"}	1	If dentin is over-dried, GIC will withdraw water needed for its setting reaction from the dentinal tubules. This hydraulic movement results in postoperative pain.	Glass Ionomer Cements	\N
ab0f8239-226e-4cad-b478-a99eda191035	materials	BDS-I	Which component is added to GIC powder specifically to increase its final strength and radiopacity through a higher atomic number?	{Silica,Alumina,Strontium,"Calcium fluoride"}	2	Some GIC products substitute calcium (Atomic No. 20) with Strontium (Atomic No. 38). This substitution provides better radiopacity because the higher atomic number allows for more X-ray absorption without altering the chemical reaction.	Glass Ionomer Cements	\N
902b788f-b6a5-4b27-a28f-5b9c4dc12859	materials	BDS-I	During the maturation of GIC, why is the formation of aluminum polysalts delayed compared to calcium polysalts?	{"Aluminum is less reactive","Calcium is a trivalent ion","Aluminum ions prevent premature gelation by forming complexes","Aluminum is only found in the liquid"}	2	During setting, fluoride ions form strong, soluble aluminofluoride complexes (AlF2+). These complexes prevent the premature gelation of polyions by aluminum ions, allowing for a workable setting time.	GIC Chemistry	\N
1ea8b615-5293-4661-ae0c-df82fa7b8045	materials	BDS-I	Which "twisted" physical property of dental amalgam causes it to lose 50% of its strength when a patient consumes a very hot beverage (60°C)?	{"High Creep","High Thermal Conductivity","Temperature-dependent strength loss","Mercury evaporation"}	2	Amalgam is sensitive to temperature; it loses 15% of its strength at mouth temperature and 50% of its strength if elevated to 60°C, such as when drinking hot coffee.	Dental Amalgam	\N
