c64ffeff-4a00-45f1-a495-97455624a43e	materials	BDS-I	What is the primary clinical advantage of using "Spherical" zinc-containing amalgam alloys?	{"Higher fluoride release","Better interproximal contacts","Ease of condensation in small retentive areas","Lower mercury content"}	2	Spherical alloys are desirable when strong condensation force is difficult to apply, such as in small slots, potholes, or Class V lesions.	Amalgam Alloys	\N
5f1beed9-4ea8-49ad-963b-f5327d3718ea	materials	BDS-I	In "Dual-Cured" resin cements, what happens if the clinician fails to use a curing light?	{"The cement will not set","The cement may not polymerize efficiently","The cement becomes more radiopaque","The fluoride release increases"}	1	Some materials marketed as "dual cure" may not polymerize efficiently in the absence of a curing light. Their physical properties improve significantly when light-activated.	Resin Cements	\N
d3f0c9dc-30d6-4d73-92a6-30315c52e1f2	materials	BDS-I	Which physical property of "Microfilled" composites makes them the material of choice for NCCLs?	{"High compressive strength","High filler loading","Low Modulus of Elasticity","High thermal expansion"}	2	Microfilled composites have a low modulus of elasticity, allowing them to flex with the tooth during loading. This resilience is ideal for cervical areas where tooth flexure occurs.	Composite Resins	\N
6716bdfa-73e8-485d-814a-d6da9d0c541e	materials	BDS-I	Why is "Polyacrylic Acid" less irritating to the pulp than "Phosphoric Acid" used in zinc phosphate cements?	{"It has a neutral pH","Its molecules are larger, limiting diffusion into tubules","It is a stronger acid","It does not release heat"}	1	Polyacrylic acid molecules have a high molecular weight and large diameter. This limits their ability to diffuse through dentinal tubules toward the pulp.	Biocompatibility	\N
