ea107277-cca7-41c5-ae73-82b06a651aa7	materials	BDS-I	What is the "Standard" radiant exposure (J/cm²) suggested to potentially cause pulpal injury due to a 5.5°C temperature increase?	{"10 J/cm²","50 J/cm²","The threshold is based on applied exposure [Arrais graph]","5 J/cm²"}	2	Research indicates that intrapulpal temperature rise is linear with applied radiant exposure; exposures resulting in a 5.5°C increase are considered potentially injurious.	Light Curing	\N
d51ad5b9-3e9b-49dc-bc4b-ebf2a1f2b2d5	materials	BDS-I	Why is "Polycarboxylate" cement an unfavorable luting agent for "Long-span Bridges"?	{"It is too acidic","It exhibits significant plastic deformation on loading","It has no chemical bond","It releases too much heat"}	1	Polycarboxylate has a low elastic modulus (4-6 GPa), leading to plastic deformation under the heavy loads associated with long-span prostheses.	Dental Cements	\N
64eb3e3e-b5df-439d-b06e-c6aec790c01f	materials	BDS-I	What is the clinical indicator that "Self-Polymerizing" GIC has abundant carboxyl groups available for chemical adhesion?	{"It looks chalky","It has a glossy, wet appearance","It has reached its setting time","It becomes opaque"}	1	Abundant carboxyl groups are present when the mix is still glossy and wet; the cement should not be used once it becomes dull or forms a "skin".	Clinical Technique	\N
