2e1fa272-af44-49f2-a64c-8264efa18e5a	materials	BDS-I	In "Indirect Composite" processing, why is a "Nitrogen Atmosphere" used during curing?	{"To increase humidity","To eliminate the Oxygen Inhibited Layer (OIL) and improve DC","To reduce thermal conductivity","To prevent fluoride leaching"}	1	Nitrogen eliminates internal oxygen before curing, preventing polymerization inhibition and voids, which leads to a higher degree of conversion and better wear resistance.	Indirect Composites	\N
5cb1d030-03c1-4856-97a6-8057eaaac16d	materials	BDS-I	"Griffith Laws" (or flaws) in dental ceramics are essentially:	{"Chemical impurities","Microscopic defects from which crack propagation begins","Crystalline leucite particles","Surface stains"}	1	Ceramics fail because stress concentrates at microscopic defects called Griffith laws, which then propagate into catastrophic fractures.	Dental Ceramics	\N
03f1cd1a-c936-44b3-9084-dc533ea28eb7	materials	BDS-I	Which "Rheological" property allows a cement mix to be thick initially but flow readily upon seating with pressure?	{Thixotropy,"Pseudoplasticity (Shear Thinning)",Rheopexy,Viscoelasticity}	1	Pseudoplastic materials, like zinc polycarboxylate, flow readily under pressure because their viscosity decreases as the shear rate increases.	Rheology	\N
f5c654a3-de46-40d9-83cd-83901d6d1a94	materials	BDS-I	"Hoop Stress" generated by GIC luting cements is primarily a result of:	{"High compressive force","Water absorption (expansion)","Polymerization shrinkage","Fluoride release"}	1	GIC absorbs water and swells within the rigid crown, creating tensile stresses known as "Hoop stress".	Dental Ceramics	\N
8fd41517-720d-4b74-9856-42a8b3871f69	materials	BDS-I	Which specific form of "Direct filling Gold" is characterized as "E-Z Gold"?	{"Gold Foil","Crystalline Gold","Wax-coated powdered gold pellets","Non-cohesive foil"}	2	E-Z Gold refers to pellets of powdered gold supplied with a wax coating that must be burned off before use.	Direct Filling Gold	\N
ba32e158-02a3-4b1a-9de6-d96ebb23cacf	materials	BDS-I	Why should "Zirconia" restorations be treated with Ivoclean (sodium hydroxide) for 20 seconds after try-in?	{"To etch the surface","To remove contaminating proteins that phosphoric acid cannot","To increase translucency","To prevent low-temperature degradation"}	1	Salivary proteins contaminate zirconia during try-in and cannot be removed by standard acid; sodium hydroxide is the routine procedure for effective removal before cementation.	Dental Ceramics	\N
