032d1d09-25d1-4a43-9d3b-52c6bb304a03	orthodontics	BDS-I	In modern orthodontics, the shift from Angle's paradigm to the "Soft Tissue Paradigm" completely altered treatment planning. Which of the following accurately describes the primary diagnostic and treatment goal difference in the soft tissue paradigm?	{"The primary goal is achieving an ideal Class I molar relationship, trusting the soft tissues to adapt perfectly.","The primary goal focuses on temporomandibular joint stability over dental occlusion.","The primary goal is establishing normal soft tissue proportions and adaptations, placing the teeth and jaws as needed to achieve this facial esthetic.","The primary goal is maintaining an intact dentition by entirely avoiding extractions."}	2	The soft tissue paradigm places the primary treatment goal on normal soft tissue proportions and adaptations, meaning the clinician plans the ideal soft tissue relationships first, and then places teeth and jaws as needed to achieve this, rather than just pursuing Angle's ideal occlusion.	Malocclusion & Contemporary Society	\N
8bfeb463-1201-430b-a804-8ceeab64618a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In a medical emergency, what is the "ABC" sequence?	{"Assessment, Breathing, Circulation","Airway, Breathing, Circulation","Airway, Bleeding, Circulation","Assessment, Bleeding, Compressions"}	1	Airway, Breathing, and Circulation is the standard BLS sequence. [Box 2-3]	Medical Emergencies	\N
87822785-9858-4327-bb51-38c56ae12f40	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the "cephalocaudal gradient of growth," how do the relative proportions of the human face and jaws change from fetal life through adulthood?	{"The cranium grows faster and longer than the facial structures.","The maxilla undergoes more prolonged growth than the mandible.","Structures further from the brain (mandible) tend to grow more and later than structures closer to the brain (maxilla).","The upper limbs exhibit more post-natal growth than the lower limbs."}	2	The cephalocaudal gradient implies that there is an axis of increased growth extending from the head toward the feet. Consequently, the mandible (being further from the brain) grows more and later than the maxilla, resulting in facial proportions becoming less convex over time.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
065b06f6-e9b6-4af2-b6b7-5aa105a661e6	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating a child's growth using Scammon's curves, a clinician notes a specific tissue system that proliferates to roughly 200% of its adult size by age 10, before undergoing massive involution during puberty. Which tissue system exhibits this unique pattern?	{"Neural tissue","Maxillary intramembranous bone","Genital tissue","Lymphoid tissue"}	3	Scammon's curves demonstrate that lymphoid tissue (tonsils, adenoids, etc.) proliferates far beyond the adult amount (up to 200%) in late childhood and then undergoes rapid involution at the same time the genital tissues accelerate during puberty.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
7d90f057-21fb-4054-9e1c-ebfa74a2bf4d	orthodontics	BDS-I	True or False: Statement 1: The maxilla grows downward and forward because of bone apposition on its anterior surface. Statement 2: The anterior surface of the maxilla is primarily an area of resorption.	{"Both statements are true.","Both statements are false.","Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false, Statement 2 is true."}	3	The maxilla grows downward and forward, its front surfaces are remodeled, and bone is removed from most of the anterior surface. Therefore, the anterior surface of the maxilla is primarily an area of resorption, not apposition, making the first statement false and the second statement true.	Maxilla Growth	\N
be962051-2fc0-4259-b294-d9797e467a6a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Björk’s classic implant studies revolutionized our understanding of jaw growth by utilizing tantalum pins. What major growth phenomenon in the mandible did this implant radiography method definitively reveal that standard cephalometrics previously missed?	{"The mandible grows exclusively by interstitial cartilage expansion.","True internal rotation of the mandible during growth is highly prevalent but is masked by opposing surface remodeling (apposition/resorption) at the lower border.","The mandibular condyle translates downward and backward.","The gonial angle becomes more acute solely due to muscular hyper-contraction."}	1	Björk’s implants provided stable internal landmarks, revealing that the mandible undergoes significant internal rotation during growth. However, surface remodeling (e.g., resorption at the lower border of the gonial angle) masks this rotation, leading conventional cephalometrics to underestimate the true rotational pattern.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
246273ec-5a71-43bc-8917-a3fa090468f7	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Melvin Moss’s "Functional Matrix Theory," how is the growth of the cranial vault and jaws primarily controlled?	{"By intrinsic genetic programming within the sutural tissues.","By the innate growth potential of the condylar and septal cartilages acting as pacemakers.","By epigenetic, functional demands of the surrounding soft tissues and spaces (e.g., brain expansion, airway size) which passively displace the bones.","By the direct apposition of endochondral bone driven by the pituitary gland."}	2	The Functional Matrix Theory posits that neither bone nor cartilage determines craniofacial growth. Instead, growth of the face and skull occurs as a response to functional needs and is mediated by the soft tissues in which the jaws are embedded (the functional matrix).	Theories of Growth Control	\N
8405a4ca-763f-4c90-abdc-ca734b70d890	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 4-year-old child exhibits high anxiety during a dental visit. The dentist decides to use a strategy where a painful stimulus (a mild scolding tone) is immediately withdrawn the moment the child exhibits cooperative behavior. According to behavioral psychology, what specific conditioning technique is this?	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Punishment,"Classical extinction"}	1	Negative reinforcement involves the withdrawal of an unpleasant stimulus after a desired response. Like positive reinforcement, it increases the likelihood of a response in the future. This is distinct from punishment, which presents an unpleasant stimulus to decrease a behavior.	Psychological Development	\N
70b3860c-fe45-4823-b9eb-e89755b343a9	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 5-year-old orthodontic patient believes their removable appliance is a "living monster" that bites them if they don't clean it. According to Jean Piaget’s theory of cognitive development, this child is displaying "animism." Which specific developmental period does this characterize?	{"Sensorimotor period","Preoperational period","Concrete operations period","Formal operations period"}	1	Animism (investing inanimate objects with life) and egocentrism are classic characteristics of the Preoperational Period (ages 2 to 7). Children at this stage cannot rely on logical reasoning and are dominated by immediate sensory impressions.	Cognitive Development	\N
29ddf3cc-420a-4d41-b036-f1008795f33a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Primary failure of eruption (PFE) is a severe condition where non-ankylosed permanent teeth fail to erupt despite a cleared eruption path. Recent genetic studies have linked this condition to a mutation in which specific gene?	{Msx1,"Sonic Hedgehog (SHH)","PTHR1 (Parathyroid hormone receptor 1)",TCOF1}	2	A mutation in the parathyroid hormone receptor gene (PTHR1) leads to primary failure of eruption. In affected individuals, the involved teeth do not respond to orthodontic force and cannot be moved into position.	Early Stages of Development	\N
9d5dae52-75a0-4f5a-a042-8876435dbca2	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the "Pre-emergent" phase of tooth eruption, two distinct biological processes are required. First, the overlying bone and primary tooth roots must resorb. What is the second necessary process?	{"The formation of an apical cyst to generate hydrostatic pressure.","The establishment of a mature periodontal ligament to pull the tooth.","A propulsive mechanism that moves the tooth into the cleared space, which relies on the dental follicle.","The complete mineralization of the root apex."}	2	Pre-emergent eruption requires (1) resorption of bone/primary roots overlying the crown, and (2) a propulsive mechanism moving the tooth into the cleared path. The dental follicle provides the signals for both the resorption (clearing the path) and the propulsion.	Early Stages of Development	\N
89c96756-0486-477c-811e-f4c60d9d6579	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Moribund" patient not expected to survive without the operation is ASA:	{IV,V,VI,III}	1	ASA V is a moribund patient. [Box 1-8]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
59eee924-f21e-4c1a-88f4-848cfe8d9e97	orthodontics	BDS-I	Researchers observing the circadian rhythm of tooth eruption via continuous video microscopy noted a fascinating pattern regarding when a tooth actively erupts. During what time period does the majority of post-emergent tooth eruption occur?	{"Continuously and evenly over 24 hours.","Early morning (6 AM to 10 AM) during high cortisol release.","Midday during maximum masticatory function.","During the late evening and night (8 PM to 1 AM)."}	3	Eruption occurs primarily during a critical period between 8 PM and midnight or 1 AM. During the day, biting forces and soft tissue pressures interrupt or depress the eruptive mechanism, meaning eruption is not a continuous process.	Early Stages of Development	\N
47384682-e501-4af9-ba56-980914594dd4	orthodontics	BDS-I	"Primate spaces" are localized diastemas naturally present in a normal primary dentition to allow for future alignment. Where are these primate spaces specifically located in the maxillary and mandibular arches?	{"Maxilla: Between central and lateral incisors. Mandible: Between lateral incisor and canine.","Maxilla: Between lateral incisor and canine. Mandible: Between canine and first primary molar.","Maxilla: Between canine and first primary molar. Mandible: Between first and second primary molars.","Maxilla: Between central incisors. Mandible: Between central incisors."}	1	In the maxilla, the primate space is located between the lateral incisors and canines. In the mandible, the primate space is located between the canines and first primary molars.	Early Stages of Development	\N
8432e9bf-1f73-4ad1-8641-d7a59b87299a	orthodontics	BDS-I	"Incisor liability" refers to the obligate space discrepancy created because permanent incisors are significantly larger than the primary incisors they replace. Which of the following is NOT a physiological mechanism by which the developing child naturally accommodates this incisor liability?	{"Spontaneous expansion of the inter-canine arch width during permanent incisor eruption.","Labial positioning of the permanent incisors relative to the primary incisors.","Utilization of interdental spacing (including primate spaces) present in the primary dentition.","Significant distal movement of the permanent first molars into the tuberosity."}	3	The incisor liability is overcome by (1) a slight increase in arch width across the canines, (2) labial positioning of the permanent incisors (arranging them on a wider arc), and (3) repositioning of the canines backward into primate spaces. First molars do not move distally to relieve incisor crowding.	Early Stages of Development	\N
776c48f4-0846-4ae5-b186-9ea6ef65baa7	orthodontics	BDS-I	In a primary dentition with a "flush terminal plane" relationship of the second primary molars, how does the transition to a normal Class I permanent molar relationship typically occur?	{"By the massive forward growth of the maxilla relative to the mandible.","Through the differential forward growth of the mandible combined with the mesial shift of the mandibular permanent molar into the \\"leeway space.\\"","By the natural distal drifting of the maxillary permanent first molar.","By the early loss of the primary maxillary canines."}	1	A flush terminal plane transitions to a Class I molar relationship via a combination of differential forward growth of the mandible relative to the maxilla and the "early/late mesial shift" of the lower permanent molar taking advantage of the larger mandibular leeway space.	Early Stages of Development	\N
0262fa58-16ce-4687-85d2-1898802effc9	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the classic numerical value of the "Leeway Space" in the mandibular arch, and why is it larger than the maxillary leeway space?	{"1.5 mm per side; because the maxillary primary molars are larger.","2.5 mm per side; because the size difference between the primary molars and permanent premolars is greater in the mandible.","5.0 mm per side; because the mandibular permanent canines are exceptionally small.","0 mm; leeway space only exists in the maxilla."}	1	The leeway space (the size difference between the primary canine/molars and the permanent canine/premolars) is approximately 2.5 mm per quadrant in the mandible and only about 1.5 mm per quadrant in the maxilla. This difference allows the lower molar to drift mesially more than the upper.	Early Stages of Development	\N
b531a3f0-3a24-4de8-8763-b70ac09c834d	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the stages of sexual and physical maturation during adolescence, how does the timing of "menarche" (onset of menstruation) in females correlate with the peak of the pubertal height growth spurt?	{"Menarche occurs exactly 1 year before the peak height velocity.","Menarche reliably signals the absolute onset (beginning) of the growth spurt.","Menarche invariably occurs after the peak velocity of the growth spurt has passed, indicating declining growth.","Menarche coincides exactly with the absolute peak of the height growth spurt."}	2	Menarche is an excellent indicator of sexual maturity, but it always comes after the peak of height velocity has passed. When a girl reaches menarche, the peak of her adolescent growth spurt is already over, and growth is decelerating.	Later Stages of Development	\N
c98e5b9e-a110-4dac-9aa8-b26e5ccb1976	orthodontics	BDS-I	The facial skeleton completes its growth in three distinct planes of space at different chronological times. What is the normal sequence of growth completion in the jaws?	{"Length completes first, then Width, then Height.","Height completes first, then Length, then Width.","Width completes first, then Length, then Height.","They all complete simultaneously at age 18."}	2	Growth in width is completed first (often before the adolescent growth spurt ends), followed by growth in length, and finally growth in height (which continues into the late teens or early twenties).	Later Stages of Development	\N
46790b81-e672-46f2-b2b3-cf77434ee989	orthodontics	BDS-I	An orthodontist examines a patient and notes a "short face" pattern with a deep anterior overbite and square jaw angles. According to the mechanics of jaw rotation during growth, what type of rotation has the mandible predominantly undergone?	{"Extreme backward rotation of the core with a highly obtuse gonial angle.","Forward (anterior) internal rotation where the core rotates upward anteriorly, uprighting the incisors and creating a deep bite.","Purely translation with zero internal rotation.","External rotation swinging the chin completely downward and backward."}	1	A short-face, deep-bite pattern (often with crowding and upright incisors) is typically the result of excessive forward internal rotation of the mandible during growth. This rotation flattens the mandibular plane and brings the anterior mandible upward, closing the bite.	Later Stages of Development	\N
3d6aff7d-b62f-4041-a76d-3fb20972a24c	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the growth of the cranial base, bands of cartilage called synchondroses act as important growth centers. Which synchondrosis remains active the longest, typically fusing in the early twenties?	{"Spheno-ethmoidal synchondrosis","Intersphenoid synchondrosis","Spheno-occipital synchondrosis","Midpalatal suture"}	2	The spheno-occipital synchondrosis remains active well into early adulthood, whereas the intersphenoid fuses at birth and the spheno-ethmoidal fuses around age 6-7.	Cranial Base Growth	\N
8fbce57c-e59f-4694-bf52-552fbc1fbf8c	orthodontics	BDS-I	Long-term follow-up studies of adults (such as those by Behrents) have shattered the myth that craniofacial growth stops at age 20. Which of the following statements accurately reflects aging changes in the facial soft tissues and dentition in adulthood?	{"The lips tend to sag downward with aging, leading to decreased display of the maxillary incisors and increased display of the mandibular incisors at rest.","The face shrinks significantly in overall vertical dimension regardless of tooth loss.","The pulp chambers of the teeth aggressively expand due to a reversal of dentinogenesis.","Adult facial growth involves a massive backward rotation of both jaws, severely increasing overjet."}	0	With aging, the lips and facial soft tissues sag downward. The result is a decrease in the exposure of the upper incisors at rest and on smile, and an increase in the exposure of the lower incisors. Craniofacial skeletal dimensions also show a slow but continuous small increase in adulthood.	Later Stages of Development	\N
8867824d-04f2-4106-9aa7-2f267459bbe4	orthodontics	BDS-I	Embryologic craniofacial development occurs in five distinct stages. During which specific stage does the failure of neural crest cell migration lead to severe midface deficiencies such as Treacher Collins syndrome (Mandibulofacial dysostosis)?	{"Stage 1 (Germ layer formation, Day 17)","Stage 2 (Neural tube formation, Days 18-23)","Stage 3 (Origin, migration, and interaction of neural crest cells, Days 19-28)","Stage 5 (Final differentiation of tissues, Day 50-birth)"}	2	Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis) and hemifacial microsomia are classic examples of defects arising during Stage 3 (Days 19-28), which involves the origin, migration, and interaction of cranial neural crest cells.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
819a5a9a-67ba-4f7f-9b66-3856325f9eeb	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child born with a cleft of the secondary palate (but an intact primary palate and lip) experienced a teratogenic disruption during a highly specific window of embryologic development. When does the secondary palate normally elevate and fuse?	{"2 to 3 weeks in utero","4 to 5 weeks in utero","6 to 8 weeks in utero","12 to 14 weeks in utero"}	2	Clefts of the secondary palate occur due to an interference with the elevation and fusion of the palatal shelves, which normally takes place around the 6th to 8th week of embryonic development (later than the lip/primary palate, which fuse around weeks 4-6).	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
bdee420b-e4e8-49d8-9682-701a37f00633	orthodontics	BDS-I	Fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS) is characterized by severe deficiencies of midline facial tissue. In embryologic terms, what is the fundamental teratogenic mechanism by which high levels of ethanol cause this defect?	{"It competitively inhibits calcium deposition in the secondary cartilages.","It causes a defect in the initial formation of the neural plate and germ layers (Stage 1) due to extreme embryonic intoxication.","It prevents the fusion of the palatal shelves specifically at week 8.","It selectively kills the osteoblasts of the cranial vault."}	1	FAS defects are due to deficiencies of midline tissue of the neural plate very early in embryonic development (Stage 1, ~Day 17) caused by exposure to very high levels of ethanol, leading to holoprosencephaly-like features and midface deficiency.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
6a6a0e42-2ab0-4a93-a794-ad414de64526	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 9-year-old child presents with a massive anterior open bite. The parents report a severe, ongoing thumb-sucking habit. According to the Equilibrium Theory of tooth position, what specific factor of the habit is the most critical in causing this severe dental displacement?	{"The intense magnitude of the force applied when the thumb pops out of the mouth.","The duration of the force (hours per day) resting against the teeth.","The temperature of the thumb affecting collagen maturation.","The specific finger used (thumb vs index finger)."}	1	The Equilibrium Theory states that the duration of a force is far more important than its magnitude. Light, continuous pressures (resting lips, tongue, or prolonged thumb sucking over 6 hours/day) cause teeth to move, while short, heavy forces (mastication, swallowing) do not.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
60e1d0c4-06de-4d93-95b0-80fb0c23aa93	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient demonstrates a "tongue thrust" swallow, placing the tongue tip between the anterior teeth. Modern orthodontic perspective views this specific swallowing pattern primarily as:	{"The absolute primary etiologic cause of their anterior open bite.","A dangerous neuro-muscular disease requiring immediate surgical intervention.","A secondary physiological adaptation to a pre-existing anterior open bite, necessary to create an anterior seal for swallowing.","A habit strictly caused by premature loss of primary molars."}	2	A tongue thrust swallow is rarely the primary cause of an open bite because the duration of a swallow is too short to move teeth (Equilibrium theory). Instead, it is usually a physiologic adaptation to seal off the front of the mouth when an open bite already exists.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
5101898b-c3c5-4bcb-a09f-853103981c99	orthodontics	BDS-I	Chronic mouth breathing has long been associated with a specific pattern of facial growth often termed "adenoid facies." Which of the following skeletal adaptations is highly characteristic of a severe, obligate mouth breather?	{"Upward and forward rotation of the mandible leading to a deep bite.","Downward and backward rotation of the mandible, excessive anterior face height, and a narrow, constricted maxillary arch.","Massive lateral expansion of the mandibular angles.","Severe Class III skeletal prognathism with edge-to-end incisors."}	1	To establish an oral airway, the mandible drops down and back, and the head tips back. If maintained during growth, this leads to over-eruption of posterior teeth, downward/backward rotation of the mandible, increased face height, and a narrow maxilla (due to lack of resting tongue pressure).	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
7f2acbc3-6816-411d-a0ef-8821832040fe	orthodontics	BDS-I	An orthodontic diagnosis relies heavily on clinical evaluation of facial proportions. When evaluating a patient's facial profile, "convexity" is a critical parameter. A highly convex facial profile (a sharp angle formed by the glabella, subnasale, and soft tissue pogonion) is most indicative of which skeletal relationship?	{"Skeletal Class I","Skeletal Class II","Skeletal Class III","Bimaxillary retrusion"}	1	A highly convex profile (where the jaw is positioned well behind the maxilla) strongly indicates a skeletal Class II jaw relationship. Conversely, a straight profile indicates Class I, and a concave profile indicates a Class III relationship.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
dc300842-d38f-4008-9d02-5a6034022329	orthodontics	BDS-I	The overall downward and forward translation of the maxilla during growth is accompanied by surface remodeling. What is the primary remodeling activity on the anterior surface of the maxilla?	{"Heavy apposition",Resorption,"Cartilage proliferation","Interstitial expansion"}	1	As the maxilla translates downward and forward, its anterior surface actually undergoes resorption (bone is removed), which is a seemingly paradoxical remodeling process.	Maxillary Growth	\N
b28a696c-e08e-447f-8098-75f0595dc275	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the evaluation of smile esthetics (mini-esthetics), the "smile arc" is a crucial factor for an attractive appearance. How is a "consonant" (ideal) smile arc defined?	{"The incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth form a flat, straight line parallel to the interpupillary line.","The contour of the maxillary anterior teeth's incisal edges matches the curvature of the lower lip upon smiling.","The upper lip completely covers the gingival margins, exposing exactly 50% of the central incisors.","The buccal corridors are completely completely eliminated, showing only teeth from premolar to premolar."}	1	A consonant smile arc is present when the contour of the incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth follows the natural curvature of the lower lip during a smile. A flat smile arc is generally considered less attractive and older-looking.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
eaceaa76-b872-4443-bad8-284cbd9c791f	orthodontics	BDS-I	A practitioner is utilizing the "Bolton Analysis" on a set of dental casts to assess tooth size discrepancies. What specifically does the anterior Bolton ratio measure, and what is its ideal percentage?	{"The summed mesiodistal widths of the mandibular 6 anterior teeth divided by the maxillary 6 anterior teeth; ideal is roughly 77.2%.","The sum of all 12 mandibular teeth divided by the 12 maxillary teeth; ideal is 91.3%.","The width of the maxillary central incisor divided by its height; ideal is 80%.","The width of the mandibular canine divided by the maxillary canine; ideal is 62%."}	0	The anterior Bolton analysis compares the sum of the mesiodistal widths of the mandibular 6 anterior teeth to the maxillary 6 anterior teeth. The ideal normal anterior ratio is approximately 77.2%.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
af0a9f79-af37-47ed-b6df-26140687f06d	orthodontics	BDS-I	When performing space analysis in the mixed dentition, the clinician must accurately estimate the size of the unerupted permanent canines and premolars. Which method utilizes a statistical correlation where the width of the fully erupted mandibular incisors is used to predict the width of these unerupted buccal teeth?	{"Tanaka-Johnston analysis","Bolton analysis","Howe's analysis","Pont's analysis"}	0	Methods like the Moyers or Tanaka-Johnston prediction use the combined width of the erupted mandibular incisors to predict the size of the unerupted permanent canines and premolars via a statistical regression chart or formula.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
7cd37455-0531-4022-bbd4-490e7c0b740a	orthodontics	BDS-I	In cephalometric analysis, the clinician traces various cranial landmarks to relate the jaws to the cranial base. Which line, formed by connecting the Porion to the Orbitale, is commonly used to approximate the patient's natural head position?	{"Sella-Nasion (SN) plane","Frankfort Horizontal plane","Mandibular plane","Occlusal plane"}	1	The Frankfort Horizontal plane, connecting Porion (upper edge of external auditory meatus) to Orbitale (lowest point on the inferior margin of the orbit), is traditionally used as a reference line that closely approximates the true horizontal line when the patient is in natural head position.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
acb1ed0e-d995-4ba4-a51b-3a0b7bc58c51	orthodontics	BDS-I	The development of the cranial base involves endochondral ossification at specific bands of cartilage called synchondroses. Which of the following synchondroses is the last to fuse, remaining active through puberty and contributing to the downward and forward displacement of the maxilla?	{"Intersphenoid synchondrosis","Spheno-ethmoidal synchondrosis","Spheno-occipital synchondrosis","Frontonasal suture"}	2	The spheno-occipital synchondrosis remains an active major site of growth until late puberty, whereas the intersphenoid and spheno-ethmoidal synchondroses fuse much earlier in childhood (by around age 7).	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
bebee8ab-0844-4a18-8ca7-187d126d89a0	medicine	BDS-I	"Bence Jones proteins" are monoclonal:	{"Gamma globulins","Light chains","Heavy chains","Albumin dimers"}	1	Bence Jones proteins are light chains found in Multiple Myeloma.	Blood Disease	\N
f2b5f583-80ed-4ec8-90e7-317e74a5e843	orthodontics	BDS-I	Enlow's "V principle" of growth states that bone remodeling can cause a bone to move in a specific direction while expanding in size. In the growing maxilla, the anterior surface (the front of the maxilla) is actually a predominantly resorptive surface. How, then, does the maxilla translate downward and forward?	{"Due to massive interstitial expansion of the premaxillary suture.","Due to active push from the developing nasal septum and heavy bone apposition posteriorly at the tuberosity and superiorly at the sutures.","Due to the heavy muscular pull of the orbicularis oris dragging the bone forward.","Due to the functional matrix expanding the sinus, causing the anterior wall to bow outward."}	1	While the anterior surface of the maxilla is remodeling by resorbing (moving backward anatomically), the entire maxillary complex is being physically translated downward and forward by push from the cranial base/nasal septum and massive apposition of bone at the posterior tuberosity and superior sutural attachments.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
cb779715-90dd-480b-8a07-12f544d90176	orthodontics	BDS-I	Mandibular condylar cartilage behaves differently than an epiphyseal plate of a long bone. What unique characteristic makes the condylar cartilage highly adaptable to environmental and orthodontic forces?	{"It is highly vascularized inside the cartilage matrix.","The cartilage cells are not arranged in organized columns, and proliferation is independent of intrinsic genetic drive, acting instead as a reactive secondary cartilage.","It calcifies completely by age 3 via intramembranous ossification.","It lacks a fibrous periosteum covering."}	1	The condylar cartilage is a "secondary cartilage." Unlike epiphyseal plates, its cells are not rigidly ordered in columns, and its growth is largely reactive/compensatory to the downward/forward translation of the mandible, making it highly susceptible to modification by functional appliances.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
0541dcf6-d2b2-499b-a779-654e61eef2c1	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 14-year-old boy presents for orthodontic treatment. The clinician needs to determine if he has passed his pubertal growth spurt. Radiographs of the hand and wrist are highly reliable for assessing skeletal age. Which specific bone's ossification strongly correlates with the peak of the pubertal growth spurt?	{"Fusion of the radius to the ulna.","The appearance of the adductor sesamoid of the thumb.","The calcification of the pisiform.","The complete fusion of the distal phalanx of the middle finger."}	1	In hand-wrist radiographs, the initial appearance of the adductor sesamoid of the thumb roughly coincides with the beginning or acceleration phase of the pubertal growth spurt, serving as an excellent clinical marker.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
10f9e104-6f9e-4fee-bb49-8eb496dcd175	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient on Heparin should have elective oral surgery delayed for at least how many hours after the last IV dose?	{"1 hour","6 hours","24 hours","1 week"}	1	IV Heparin effects wear off in approximately 6 hours. [Box 1-23]	Medical Management	\N
0b5ce15c-f4be-426b-859c-4d8db88c21a5	orthodontics	BDS-I	During embryonic facial development, the upper lip is formed by the merging of specific prominences. Which distinct processes must fuse correctly to prevent a cleft lip?	{"The two lateral nasal processes with each other.","The medial nasal processes with the maxillary processes.","The maxillary processes with the mandibular processes.","The medial nasal processes with the frontonasal prominence."}	1	A cleft lip occurs due to a failure of fusion between the medial nasal processes and the maxillary processes on one or both sides.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
4445f39a-2477-47c8-a06c-7c2d473f4ea2	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the hierarchical "Index of Treatment Need" (IOTN), certain severe occlusal traits mandate orthodontic treatment on a purely health/functional basis. Which of the following conditions constitutes a Grade 5 (Extreme Need) category?	{"An overjet of 4 mm with competent lips.","A midline diastema of 1 mm.","Impeded eruption of teeth (e.g., impacted canines) excluding third molars.","A completely normal occlusion with minor crowding of 1 mm."}	2	In the IOTN grading system, Grade 5 represents extreme need. This includes impeded eruption of teeth (like impacted maxillary canines), extensive hypodontia, extreme overjet (>9 mm), and cleft lip/palate anomalies.	Demand for Treatment	\N
4d05a975-156e-4d46-ad6a-6504a687161a	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the fundamental difference between 'classical conditioning' (Pavlovian) and 'operant conditioning' (Skinner) regarding patient behavior in the dental chair?	{"Classical conditioning involves voluntary actions resulting from rewards; Operant conditioning involves involuntary reflexes based on past trauma.","Classical conditioning relies exclusively on negative reinforcement; Operant conditioning relies on punishment.","Classical conditioning associates a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus to elicit an involuntary reflexive response (e.g., white coat causing fear); Operant conditioning shapes voluntary complex behavior based on the consequences (rewards/punishments) that follow the behavior.","Classical conditioning is only effective in adults; Operant conditioning is only effective in children."}	2	Classical conditioning (Pavlov) explains involuntary associations (white coat = pain = crying). Operant conditioning (Skinner) explains voluntary behavior choices based on consequences (cooperating = getting a toy; throwing a tantrum = losing privileges).	Psychological Development	\N
7a2c5d2f-494b-434b-a6be-a2451864fa1f	orthodontics	BDS-I	An orthodontic patient is classified into Piaget's "Formal Operations" period of cognitive development. What is the defining characteristic of a child in this stage?	{"The child is completely egocentric and views the dental drill as a living monster.","The child learns purely by associating sensory stimuli with physical actions.","The child can think abstractly, understand complex logical concepts, and project into the future to understand the long-term benefits of wearing braces.","The child can only understand instructions if they are physically demonstrated with tangible objects right in front of them."}	2	The Formal Operations period (ages 11+) is characterized by the ability to think abstractly and grasp complex logical and hypothetical concepts, allowing the adolescent to understand the long-term goals of orthodontic treatment.	Cognitive Development	\N
bd831c5a-1b4d-4b94-8522-d014973bb237	orthodontics	BDS-I	A common developmental anomaly is an extra tooth found directly in the maxillary midline. This specific type of supernumerary tooth, which frequently causes a midline diastema or blocks the eruption of permanent central incisors, is termed a:	{Mesiodens,Paramolar,Distodens,Odontoma}	0	A mesiodens is a supernumerary tooth located in the maxillary midline. It is the most common supernumerary tooth and often interferes with the normal eruption and spacing of the central incisors.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
db573089-1326-41c1-b52c-354a5cc24116	orthodontics	BDS-I	In cephalometric analysis, if a patient exhibits excessive forward rotation of the mandible during growth, which phenotypic facial characteristic is most likely to develop?	{"Long lower anterior face height and anterior open bite.","Short anterior lower face height and a deep bite malocclusion.","Excessive flaring of the maxillary incisors.","A severe Class III malocclusion with posterior open bite."}	1	Individuals characterized by short anterior lower face height have excessive forward rotation of the mandible during growth. This rotation results in a nearly horizontal palatal plane, a low mandibular plane angle, and a deep bite malocclusion with crowded incisors.	Jaw Rotation (Phenotype)	\N
12d94d34-b29e-4fd1-aea8-0b416a4f17ee	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 7-year-old child suffered a severe fall resulting in a unilateral fracture of the left mandibular condyle. Although the condyle usually regenerates, the child unfortunately developed ankylosis of the TM joint on that side. How will this unilaterally restricted growth manifest facially by age 14?	{"The chin will deviate strongly to the unaffected (right) side.","The chin will deviate strongly to the affected (left) side, with fullness on the unaffected side.","The mandible will undergo a massive symmetrical Class III prognathic shift.","The maxilla will instantly fuse to the sphenoid bone."}	1	If one condyle is ankylosed and its growth/translation is restricted, the normal side continues to grow downward and forward. This causes the mandible to swing around the restricted joint, deviating the chin strongly toward the affected side.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
a3ee48e7-92fb-4577-bb91-34ef37c180b2	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating a patient's macro-esthetics in the transverse plane, the "Rule of Fifths" is utilized. According to this rule, the width of the central fifth of the face (the base of the nose) should ideally be perfectly equal to:	{"The inter-canthal distance (the distance between the inner corners of the eyes).","The width of the mouth from commissure to commissure.","The bi-zygomatic width.","The width of the chin at the pogonion."}	0	According to the Rule of Fifths, the face should be vertically divisible into five equal parts. The central fifth (alar base of the nose) should equal the inter-canthal distance (the width of one eye).	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
01ce0580-9c58-4e89-bfdd-f3c2fbe2c806	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "Golden Proportion" is an aesthetic concept heavily applied to the apparent widths of the maxillary anterior teeth when viewed from the straight frontal perspective. According to this proportion, if the apparent width of the lateral incisor is 1.0, what should the apparent width of the central incisor be?	{0.618,1,1.618,2.5}	2	The Golden Proportion dictates that the apparent width of the central incisor should be roughly 1.618 times that of the lateral incisor, and the lateral incisor 1.618 times the apparent width of the mesial aspect of the canine.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
23eb8692-3eff-44b1-afd2-e78812f40693	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which drug is used to reverse the effects of Heparin in an emergency?	{"Vitamin K","Protamine Sulfate",Naloxone,Glucagon}	1	Protamine sulfate is the specific reversal agent for Heparin. [Box 1-23]	Medical Management	\N
e6d3c49f-8f7a-4d0b-bc3a-0d86d24b6b61	orthodontics	BDS-I	When using cephalometric superimpositions to evaluate the growth of a patient over a 3-year period, where is the accepted standard reference area to superimpose the tracings to demonstrate overall changes in the maxilla and mandible relative to the cranial base?	{"The anterior nasal spine (ANS)","The palatal vault","The symphysis of the mandible","The anterior cranial base (superimposing on the SN line, registering at Sella)"}	3	To evaluate overall facial growth changes, serial cephalograms are routinely superimposed on the stable anterior cranial base structures (specifically the Sella-Nasion line, registered at Sella), as this area ceases significant growth very early.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
48640811-680f-447d-94f3-b2df88beb997	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 12-year-old patient presents with a Class II, division 1 malocclusion. Cephalometric analysis reveals an SNA angle of 82 degrees and an SNB angle of 74 degrees. What does the resulting ANB angle indicate?	{"A severe mandibular prognathism relative to the maxilla.","A skeletal Class II jaw discrepancy due to a retrognathic mandible.","A perfectly ideal skeletal Class I relationship.","A severe skeletal open bite."}	1	An SNA of 82 is normal, but an SNB of 74 is low (mandibular retrognathia). The ANB angle (SNA - SNB) is 8 degrees. An ANB angle > 4 degrees indicates a skeletal Class II discrepancy, in this case driven by a retruded mandible.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
657cb9c0-a1eb-41a5-abf1-6743eb836dd4	orthodontics	BDS-I	Proffit describes the phenomenon of "juvenile occlusal equilibrium." What does this concept explain regarding tooth eruption?	{"The rapid burst of eruption that occurs the moment a tooth emerges through the gingiva.","The process by which teeth in occlusion continue to erupt at a rate that perfectly parallels the vertical growth of the mandibular ramus to maintain contact.","The physiological drift of teeth medially to close primate spaces.","The immediate cessation of all eruption once roots are completely calcified."}	1	Juvenile occlusal equilibrium explains that once teeth hit occlusion, they don't stop erupting. They continue to erupt imperceptibly at exactly the same rate that the jaws are growing apart vertically, maintaining occlusal contact during childhood and adolescence.	Early Stages of Development	\N
87cce97e-fc58-4939-80e2-34d5878089ad	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the classification of malocclusion, Edward Angle opposed extraction therapy because he firmly believed that establishing normal occlusion would force the jaws and face to adapt optimally. Which part of his definition was fundamentally flawed in light of modern cephalometrics?	{"He assumed that the maxillary first molar was an invariable, stable anatomical landmark that never changed position relative to the cranial base.","He believed the mandibular incisors were the sole key to occlusion.","He failed to recognize that teeth could be rotated.","He stated that crossbites were purely genetic."}	0	Angle based his entire classification system on the premise that the maxillary first molar was in a stable, immutable position in the cranium (the "key to occlusion"), and that all discrepancies were due to the lower jaw. Cephalometrics later proved that the maxilla and its teeth can also be positioned incorrectly.	Malocclusion & Contemporary Society	\N
ca79176b-bc69-4126-9ffd-b6c1ceacc17b	orthodontics	BDS-I	When comparing cephalometric radiographs, the Frankfort Horizontal plane and the True Horizontal plane are used to evaluate facial profile. The True Horizontal is usually determined by:	{"Drawing a line from Sella to Nasion.","Having the patient look straight ahead into their own eyes in a mirror (Natural Head Position).","Drawing a line parallel to the occlusal plane.","Drawing a line from the external auditory meatus to the anterior nasal spine."}	1	True horizontal is determined by recording the patient in Natural Head Position (NHP), usually achieved by having the patient look straight ahead at a mirror or distant point. This relies on physiologic posture rather than intracranial landmarks, correcting for anatomical variations in the ear or orbit.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
b203ee1c-2a30-44ac-a626-c5058ee4664c	orthodontics	BDS-I	The administration of vital stains like Alizarin has been historically used in animal studies to track bone growth. What is the specific biochemical mechanism by which Alizarin marks growing bone?	{"It selectively binds to rapidly dividing osteoclast nuclei.","It replaces phosphorus in the hydroxyapatite crystal.","It reacts strongly with calcium at sites where bone is actively mineralizing at the exact time of injection.","It stains the cartilaginous matrix of the synchondroses exclusively."}	2	Alizarin and other vital stains are injected into a living animal and bind to calcium precisely at the sites of active bone mineralization at that specific moment, leaving a permanent colored line embedded in the bone that can be viewed later.	Concepts of Growth and Development	\N
f0b57f07-40d0-4c80-ac4b-7a40b11fa6a1	public_health	BDS-I	A Debris Index-Simplified (DI-S) score of 2 indicates:	{"Debris covering < 1/3","Debris covering 1/3 to 2/3","Debris covering > 2/3","No debris"}	1	Scores are 0 (none), 1 (<1/3), 2 (1/3-2/3), 3 (>2/3).	Oral Hygiene Indices	\N
36f343ff-ad79-4e7b-b0ed-fedf91c70407	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "Profile Analysis" is a crucial part of the clinical examination. If a vertical reference line is dropped from the soft tissue glabella downward, where should the upper and lower lips ideally lie in relation to this line in a well-balanced face?	{"Both lips should be positioned exactly 5 mm behind the line.","The upper lip should be slightly ahead of the line, and the lower lip should touch or lie slightly behind the line.","Both lips should protrude at least 10 mm beyond the line.","The lips are completely irrelevant to profile analysis."}	1	In a well-balanced Caucasian profile, the upper lip typically rests slightly ahead of a true vertical line dropped from the glabella or bridge of the nose, and the lower lip rests exactly on or slightly behind it. Variations exist heavily based on ethnicity and race.	Orthodontic Diagnosis	\N
f2eb17dd-c5dd-45f8-a10c-9b4ef2c7d7be	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the diagnostic interview, discovering a history of childhood jaw fracture is critical. According to Proffit, if a condylar fracture occurred at age 5 but the mandible shows absolutely no asymmetry or growth deficit by age 12, what is the most likely physiological explanation?	{"The child simply developed a crossbite to hide the asymmetry.","The condylar cartilage is the primary pacemaker of growth, so it completely halted bilateral growth to match the fractured side.","The condyle possesses an excellent capacity to completely regenerate from the periosteum following injury, provided ankylosis did not occur.","The maxilla stopped growing downward to compensate for the mandible."}	2	In most children, a fractured condyle will actually regenerate perfectly from the periosteum at the fracture site, returning to its original size and shape with no long-term growth deficit, as long as scarring/ankylosis does not physically tether the jaw.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
98153efb-202c-4f0d-9251-8cdbc2a0667b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a blood pressure of 155/95 mm Hg and no other symptoms is categorized as ASA:	{I,II,III,IV}	1	Mild to moderate hypertension is ASA II. [Box 1-17, Box 1-8]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
5f1409c9-c618-4951-bb54-ced88d667e44	orthodontics	BDS-I	At what stage of root development does a permanent tooth normally begin its active pre-emergent eruptive movement toward the oral cavity?	{"Before the crown is even fully calcified.","Only after the root is 100% completely formed and the apex is closed.","When the root formation begins and is roughly 1/4 to 1/2 complete.","When the primary tooth falls out, regardless of root length."}	2	Eruptive movement begins soon after the root begins to form. As a general rule, a tooth emerges through the gingiva when its root is approximately 3/4 complete, and takes another 2-3 years after emergence for the apex to completely close.	Early Stages of Development	\N
daa01df3-a0bb-4553-8c1b-0f423c0175cb	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Moss's Functional Matrix Theory, which of the following is considered the major determinant of the growth of the maxilla and mandible?	{"Interstitial growth of the cartilaginous nasal septum","Proliferation of the epiphyseal plates and synchondroses","Enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities in response to functional needs","Direct genetic control acting on the periosteum and sutures"}	2	Moss theorized that the major determinant of growth of the maxilla and mandible is the enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities, which grow in response to functional needs. He argued that cartilages of the nasal septum and mandibular condyles are not important determinants of growth, and that their loss would have little effect if proper function could be obtained.	Theories of Growth Control	\N
304c4b9e-28c8-4d8d-8574-42642a8b36f6	orthodontics	BDS-I	In Björk's implant studies regarding the rotation of jaws during growth, the term "internal rotation" specifically refers to:	{"The rotation of the mandibular plane relative to the cranial base.","The rotation of the mandibular core relative to the cranial base.","The angular remodeling of the lower border of the mandible.","The rotation of the mandibular plane relative to the core of the mandible."}	1	Björk defined "internal rotation" (also known as total rotation) as the rotation of the mandibular core relative to the cranial base. Conversely, the rotation of the mandibular plane relative to the core of the mandible is termed "intramatrix rotation" (external rotation), and the rotation of the mandibular plane relative to the cranial base is "matrix rotation" (apparent rotation).	Rotation of Jaws During Growth	\N
2aae8be8-d76c-4019-a0ad-293b1f114dfe	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on NHANES III survey data regarding the epidemiology of malocclusion in the U.S. population, which of the following statements is true?	{"Severe deep bite (≥ 5 mm) is nearly twice as prevalent in blacks and Hispanics as it is in whites.","Midline diastema (> 2 mm) is more than twice as likely to be found in white populations compared to black populations.","Severe crowding in the lower arch is most prevalent in the Hispanic population compared to whites and blacks.","Severe deep bite (≥ 5 mm) is nearly twice as prevalent in whites as it is in blacks or Hispanics."}	3	According to the NHANES III data, severe deep bite (negative overbite ≥2 mm) is nearly twice as prevalent in whites as blacks or Hispanics. Conversely, deep bite >2 mm is five times more prevalent in blacks than in whites or Hispanics, and blacks are more than twice as likely to have a midline diastema than whites or Hispanics.	Epidemiology of Malocclusion	\N
d641ad65-307d-4596-b976-318302e59228	orthodontics	BDS-I	A mother was prescribed 13-cis Retinoic acid (Accutane) during her first trimester of pregnancy. Based on known teratogenic effects on dentofacial development, which of the following anomalies is her infant most at risk for?	{"Premature suture closure",Anencephaly,"Malformations similar to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome","Central midface deficiency"}	2	13-cis Retinoic acid (Accutane) is a known teratogen that causes malformations similar to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome. Aminopterin is associated with anencephaly, ethyl alcohol with central midface deficiency, and vitamin D excess with premature suture closure.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
a1d389c4-c671-4332-a60c-80c3222c4a75	orthodontics	BDS-I	An orthodontist tells a pediatric patient that if she maintains excellent oral hygiene and doesn't break any brackets for the next three visits, she will no longer have to wear her extraoral headgear. According to behavioral theories, this is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","Omission (time-out)","Classical conditioning"}	1	Negative reinforcement involves the withdrawal of an unpleasant stimulus (in this case, wearing the headgear) after a response (excellent oral hygiene/no broken brackets), which increases the probability of that response occurring in the future. This is fundamentally different from punishment, which diminishes a response.	Social and Behavioral Development	\N
3600ad25-7cd6-424b-aa83-33f3470162d0	orthodontics	BDS-I	When analyzing facial proportions in the frontal view, the face is typically divided into bilateral fifths. In a well-proportioned face, the central fifth is determined by which of the following measurements?	{"Interpupillary distance","Width of the mouth","Intercanthal distance","Width of the nasal alar base"}	2	In the frontal view, proportional facial width is evaluated using the rule of fifths. The central fifth of the face is defined by the intercanthal distance (the distance between the inner corners of the eyes), which should ideally equal the width of the nose.	Orthodontic Diagnosis (Facial Proportions)	\N
131c164f-b818-4c50-95e9-735df2bfc0d2	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following best describes the normal dimensional characteristics of the "leeway space" during the transition from the mixed to the permanent dentition?	{"It averages about 2.5 mm per side in the maxilla and 1.5 mm per side in the mandible.","It averages about 1.5 mm per side in the maxilla and 2.5 mm per side in the mandible.","It is generated by the eruption of permanent molars pushing the deciduous teeth anteriorly.","It refers to the space left by the loss of the primary incisors."}	1	The size difference between the primary molars and their permanent successors (premolars) is known as the leeway space. Because primary molars are larger than the premolars that replace them, this space averages about 1.5 mm per side in the maxillary arch and 2.5 mm per side in the mandibular arch.	Early Stages of Development	\N
d6823564-ad33-42e9-b4f6-7e2bc7f704a2	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which diagnostic radiographic method utilizes the morphological characteristics of specific structures visible on a standard lateral cephalogram to accurately assess a patient's current stage of skeletal development and proximity to the adolescent growth spurt?	{"Hand-wrist radiography","Evaluation of the spheno-occipital synchondrosis","Cervical vertebral maturation (CVM) method","Bolton analysis"}	2	The characteristics of the cervical vertebrae, which are visible on a standard lateral cephalometric radiograph, can be used to assess skeletal age and evaluate where an individual is in their adolescent growth spurt. This negates the need to take a separate hand-wrist radiograph.	Assessment of Skeletal Age	\N
f5721ee0-5c49-4ce5-a0a4-63336660fef7	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the soft tissue paradigm of modern orthodontic treatment, which of the following best describes the primary goal and its relation to Angle’s ideal occlusion?	{"The primary goal is Angle's ideal occlusion, with soft tissue proportions being a secondary consideration.","The primary goal is soft tissue relationships, which is often incompatible with Angle's ideal occlusion.","The primary goal is soft tissue relationships and adaptations, acknowledging that ideal occlusion cannot always be the major focus.","The primary goal is functional occlusion, with soft tissue parameters dictating the exact condylar position."}	2	The primary goal of treatment becomes soft tissue relationships and adaptations, not Angle's ideal occlusion. This broader goal is not incompatible with Angle's ideal occlusion, but it acknowledges that the ideal occlusion cannot always be the major focus of a treatment plan.	Modern Treatment Goals	\N
d1a2f51e-994f-4e31-9ccf-6ed3735936b4	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following conditions is classified under Grade 5 (Extreme/Need Treatment) in the Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN)?	{"Less extensive hypodontia requiring prerestorative orthodontics.","Reverse overjet greater than 3.5 mm with reported masticatory and speech difficulties.","Increased overjet greater than 6 mm but less than or equal to 9 mm.","Severe contact point displacements greater than 4 mm."}	1	Reverse overjet greater than 3.5 mm with reported masticatory and speech difficulties is classified as Grade 5m (Extreme/Need Treatment) in the IOTN. Increased overjet > 6 mm to 9 mm is Grade 4a, and severe contact point displacements > 4 mm is Grade 4d.	Index of Treatment Needs	\N
5032576b-ceeb-4444-a586-e209f5740a91	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Scammon's curves of systemic tissue growth, which tissue type proliferates far beyond the adult amount in late childhood and then undergoes involution at the same time the genital tissues accelerate rapidly?	{"Neural tissue","Maxillary skeletal tissue","Lymphoid tissue","General body tissues"}	2	Lymphoid tissues proliferate far beyond the adult amount in late childhood, reaching nearly 200% of the adult size, and then undergo involution at the same time that growth of the genital tissues accelerates rapidly.	Growth Patterns	\N
056b11c0-c1af-4a5e-baa6-cc087f55a151	orthodontics	BDS-I	At birth, what percentage of the total body length is constituted by the head and face, and how does this reflect the cephalocaudal gradient of growth?	{"50%, reflecting a gradient where growth is concentrated in the head early on and shifts towards the lower limbs postnatally.","30%, reflecting an axis of increased growth extending from the head toward the feet.","12%, reflecting the fact that the cranial structures grow faster than the limbs during fetal life.","30%, reflecting the slower postnatal growth of the lower limbs compared to the upper limbs."}	1	At birth, the head and face represent about 30% of the total body length. The overall pattern of growth reflects a "cephalocaudal gradient of growth," meaning there is an axis of increased growth extending from the head toward the feet, resulting in more growth of the lower limbs than the upper limbs during postnatal life.	Cephalocaudal Gradient	\N
bdcb7f29-7c17-44c6-8406-3d01c59fcd52	orthodontics	BDS-I	In evaluating the stages of facial development in utero, an infant is born with a cleft of the secondary palate. During which timeframe of embryonic development did the failure of fusion most likely occur?	{"Day 17","Days 18-23","Days 28-38","Days 42-55"}	3	The formation of the primary palate (associated with cleft lip and/or palate) occurs between days 28-38, whereas the formation of the secondary palate (associated with cleft palate only) occurs between days 42-55 of embryonic development.	Embryologic Development	\N
be998ecb-ad88-4789-9309-b7734082a9dc	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following teratogens is correctly paired with its associated craniofacial defect?	{"Ethyl alcohol - Premature suture closure","Thalidomide - Malformations similar to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome","Aminopterin - Central midface deficiency","Vitamin D excess - Anencephaly"}	1	Thalidomide is a teratogen associated with malformations similar to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome. Ethyl alcohol is associated with central midface deficiency, Aminopterin with anencephaly, and Vitamin D excess with premature suture closure.	Etiology of Orthodontic Problems	\N
31f8d842-dcc2-499f-8093-032ee49483d9	orthodontics	BDS-I	During pre-emergent tooth eruption, the overlying bone is resorbed to clear a path. If an unerupted permanent tooth is wired to the adjacent bone when a jaw fracture is repaired, what is the expected outcome regarding its eruption?	{"The tooth will continue to erupt, stretching the wire.","The tooth will stop erupting, but bone resorption will continue to clear an eruption path.","The tooth will stop erupting, and bone resorption will also cease immediately.","The tooth will undergo dilaceration to bypass the wire."}	1	When a tooth is wired to the adjacent bone, eruption of the tooth stops, but bone resorption to clear an eruption path continues. This indicates that the signal for resorption of bone over the crown of the tooth is activated by the completion of the crown, independently of the propulsive movement of the tooth.	Eruption of Permanent Teeth	\N
b8543d86-dd30-4244-ab55-ca9db1b9f27f	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient is diagnosed with Primary Failure of Eruption (PFE). Genetic testing is most likely to reveal a mutation in which gene?	{Runx2/Cbfa1,MSX1,PTHR1,"SHH (Sonic Hedgehog)"}	2	Primary failure of eruption, where posterior teeth fail to erupt despite a cleared path, is linked to a mutation in the parathyroid hormone receptor gene (PTHR1).	Eruption Abnormalities	\N
326d948e-111b-4fcc-aab6-7145a1eb3bc3	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the principles of endochondral vs intramembranous ossification, which statement accurately describes the growth of the maxilla and mandible?	{"The maxilla grows entirely via endochondral ossification postnatally, while the mandible uses both methods.","The mandible is formed entirely by endochondral ossification, with the condylar cartilage as the primary center.","The maxilla develops postnatally entirely by intramembranous ossification, while the mandible utilizes both endochondral and periosteal activity.","Both jaws form entirely by intramembranous ossification from Meckel's cartilage."}	2	The maxilla develops postnatally entirely by intramembranous ossification, as there is no cartilage replacement. In contrast to the maxilla, both endochondral and periosteal activity are important in the growth of the mandible.	Skeletal Growth	\N
8b2dcf27-751a-4762-825f-8528e12bbfef	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the mandible, how does the ramus physically move posteriorly to accommodate the eruption of the permanent molars?	{"Interstitial growth within the ramus body.","Apposition on the anterior surface and resorption on the posterior surface.","Resorption on the anterior surface and apposition on the posterior surface.","Expansion of the mental symphysis."}	2	The mandible grows longer by the removal (resorption) of bone from the anterior surface of the ramus and the addition (apposition) of bone to the posterior surface.	Mandibular Growth	\N
16171252-59af-4eda-9971-114a532d0c4b	orthodontics	BDS-I	In a 7-year-old child, what mechanism most significantly contributes to providing the necessary space to overcome "incisor liability" in the anterior maxilla?	{"The distal movement of the permanent first molars into the leeway space.","A large increase in the anteroposterior dimension of the dental arch.","A slight increase in arch width across the canines and labial positioning of the permanent incisors relative to the primary incisors.","The early loss of the primary second molars."}	2	The extra space to overcome incisor liability comes from three sources: a slight increase in arch width across the canines (about 2 mm), labial positioning of the permanent incisors relative to the primary incisors, and repositioning of mandibular canines backward into the primate space. There is no mechanism by which the mandible can easily become longer in its anterior region.	Space Relationships (Incisors)	\N
83c5da1e-414d-41d5-86ea-bb06c395b293	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child presents with an anterior open bite, severely flared maxillary incisors, and a constricted, narrow upper arch. Which specific environmental etiology is the most logical cause?	{"Mouth breathing due to adenoid hypertrophy.","A prolonged non-nutritive sucking habit.","Hypertrophy of the mandibular elevator muscles.","A forward resting posture of the tongue without a sucking habit."}	1	A prolonged sucking habit (thumb or pacifier) is characterized by flared and spaced maxillary incisors, lingually positioned lower incisors, an anterior open bite, and a narrow upper arch. The narrow arch results from a combination of direct pressure on the teeth and an alteration in the pattern of resting cheek and lip pressures (increased cheek pressure and lowered tongue).	Environmental Influences	\N
0dffa1b8-ca49-4a1c-b20c-67adb29a7666	orthodontics	BDS-I	A pediatric patient throws tantrums to force the dentist to shorten the procedure. The dental team inadvertently reinforces this behavior by allowing the child to leave early. According to behavioral theories, this is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Punishment,"Omission (time-out)"}	1	The behavior (tantrum) succeeds in allowing the child to escape the visit to the clinic; the behavior has been negatively reinforced (withdrawal of the unpleasant stimulus of the dental procedure) and is more likely to occur the next time.	Learning and Behavior	\N
0c1499bb-09fb-44a2-8bb8-5a6163b1ee37	orthodontics	BDS-I	Piaget described cognitive development as occurring in four major stages. A child who can solve a liquid conservation problem (understanding that water poured into a taller, narrower glass is still the same amount) has most likely reached which stage?	{"Sensorimotor period","Preoperational period","Period of concrete operations","Period of formal operations"}	2	During the preoperational period, a child's capabilities for logical reasoning are limited, and they would usually say the tall glass has more water because their impressions are dominated by the greater height. A child who understands the conservation of volume has reached the concrete operational period (ages 7-11).	Cognitive Development	\N
dc6aa05e-a52c-436d-9ea1-9726a7a69a64	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on the "rule of fifths" for evaluating facial proportions in the frontal view, what is the ideal width of the nasal alar base?	{"It should equal the interpupillary distance.","It should equal the distance between the inner canthi (intercanthal distance).","It should be slightly wider than the central fifth of the face.","It should equal the width of the mouth."}	1	The separation of the eyes and the width of the eyes should be equal, determining the central and medial fifths of the face. The width of the nose at the base should be the same as or slightly wider than the central fifth, which is equal to the intercanthal distance.	Macro-Esthetics	\N
fb3757e1-2c89-44fa-bbc1-e2c84e762969	orthodontics	BDS-I	When comparing the Bolton analysis between a patient's upper and lower arches, a significant discrepancy is detected. What is the primary purpose of this analysis in orthodontic diagnosis?	{"To determine the exact skeletal pattern of the patient.","To identify discrepancies between the mesiodistal widths of the maxillary and mandibular teeth, which might prevent an ideal occlusion.","To assess the amount of crowding in the dental arches independent of the opposing arch.","To predict the growth rotation of the mandible."}	1	Tooth size analysis, often called Bolton analysis, is carried out by measuring the mesiodistal width of each permanent tooth and is used to compare the summed widths of the maxillary to the mandibular teeth . An anomaly in the size of the upper lateral incisors or variations in premolar sizes can lead to a mismatch, meaning the upper teeth will be too large or too small to fit properly with the lower teeth .	Diagnostic Records (Tooth Size)	\N
dc873f31-c3e5-446c-9e8d-fe8d9cd16ded	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the mixed dentition, a "flush terminal plane" relationship of the primary second molars most frequently transitions into which Angle molar relationship in the permanent dentition, assuming differential jaw growth occurs?	{"Class II, Division 1","Class III","Class I","End-to-end relationship indefinitely"}	2	The primary dentition equivalent of Angle's Class I is the mesial step. A flush terminal plane relationship is the normal relationship in the primary dentition, and the amount of differential mandibular growth and molar shift into the leeway space determines the molar relationship; most typically, it transitions to a Class I relationship in the permanent dentition.	Space Relationships (Molars)	\N
1d0dac34-4155-47a5-a150-81443460734b	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the terminology proposed by Proffit based on Björk's implant studies, the "total rotation" of the mandible is mathematically represented as:	{"Matrix rotation plus apparent rotation.","Internal rotation minus external rotation.","Intramatrix rotation plus angular remodeling.","Core rotation minus matrix rotation."}	1	According to the terminology for rotational changes of the jaws by Proffit, Total rotation = internal rotation - external rotation. Internal rotation refers to the rotation of the mandibular core relative to the cranial base, while external rotation refers to the angular remodeling of the lower border.	Jaw Rotation	\N
505dd2e7-7474-490d-a94b-2febc204441a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Nitroglycerin used for an anginal attack should be replaced:	{"Every 5 years","If it is not fresh/expired","Only if it is liquid","Every month"}	1	Fresh nitroglycerin must be available; expired tablets lose potency rapidly.	Ischemic Heart Disease	\N
35c96317-461f-4890-b46f-db24d01ad7e3	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following is an accurate description of "primate spaces" in the human primary dentition?	{"They are located between the maxillary central and lateral incisors, and between the mandibular canine and first primary molar.","They are located between the maxillary lateral incisor and canine, and between the mandibular canine and first primary molar.","They are spaces left by the early exfoliation of primary central incisors.","They refer to the size difference between the primary molars and permanent premolars."}	1	In the maxillary arch, the primate space is located between the lateral incisors and canines, whereas in the mandibular arch, the space is between the canines and first molars.	Eruption of Primary Teeth	\N
1d247edf-98dd-436c-bfee-fa76c6f2e4dc	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Erikson's "Eight Ages of Man," a 4-year-old child visiting the dentist is in a stage where the major task is to channel physical activity and curiosity into manageable tasks. The primary psychosocial crisis at this stage is:	{"Basic Trust vs. Basic Mistrust","Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt","Initiative vs. Guilt","Industry vs. Inferiority"}	2	At the stage of 3 to 6 years, the child is in the stage of "Initiative vs. Guilt". A child in this stage exhibits initiative shown by physical activity and active, extreme curiosity. Failure to achieve goals in this stage leads to guilt.	Emotional Development	\N
7770cd0b-83e6-4836-be01-19805cd59f53	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 9-year-old patient presents with a pronounced lisp and distortion when pronouncing sibilant sounds (/s/ and /z/). Based on typical speech-malocclusion relationships, which clinical finding is most likely to be observed?	{"Skeletal Class III relationship","Anterior open bite","Lingual position of maxillary incisors","Severe anterior deep bite"}	1	The distortion of sibilant sounds (/s/ and /z/), commonly presenting as a lisp, is most directly related to an anterior open bite, where there is a large gap between the incisors. Lingual positioning of incisors typically affects /t/ and /d/ sounds, while a Skeletal Class III affects /f/ and /v/ sounds.	Diagnosis: Speech Function	\N
2fc72ace-84eb-4983-a2b7-ce5cea2d043a	orthodontics	BDS-I	During an orthodontic screening, a patient exhibits difficulty in producing labiodental fricatives (/f/ and /v/). Which underlying skeletal or dental discrepancy is the most probable cause?	{"Anterior open bite","Maxillary midline diastema","Skeletal Class III","Severe Class II division 1"}	2	Difficulty or distortion in pronouncing labiodental fricative sounds (/f/ and /v/) is classically associated with a Skeletal Class III malocclusion, where the inverted relationship of the jaws makes lower lip-to-upper incisor contact difficult.	Diagnosis: Speech Function	\N
c16eb922-83bc-472e-827b-39678b7b98b7	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN), a patient presenting with a reverse overjet of 4.0 mm who also reports significant masticatory and speech difficulties would be categorized under which grade?	{"Grade 3 (Moderate Need)","Grade 4 (Severe Need)","Grade 5 (Extreme Need)","Grade 2 (Little Need)"}	2	A reverse overjet greater than 3.5 mm combined with reported masticatory and speech difficulties is classified as Grade 5m, representing an Extreme/Need Treatment category in the IOTN.	Demand for Treatment	\N
76201172-1c7f-47b6-83b6-6aa12e8b5737	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following conditions represents a Grade 4 (Severe/Need Treatment) anomaly according to the IOTN?	{"Increased overjet greater than 9 mm.","Defects of cleft lip and palate.","Less extensive hypodontia requiring prerestorative orthodontics.","Reverse overjet greater than 3.5 mm with masticatory difficulties."}	2	Less extensive hypodontia requiring prerestorative orthodontics (1 tooth per quadrant) is a Grade 4h anomaly. Overjet >9mm, cleft defects, and severe reverse overjet with functional issues are all classified as Grade 5 (Extreme Need).	Demand for Treatment	\N
3c4e09e0-31aa-487e-8a4c-a7caddf4ae18	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating the cephalocaudal gradient of growth in humans, which of the following statements is geometrically and developmentally accurate?	{"The head represents about 50% of total body length at birth.","Postnatally, the upper limbs grow at a significantly faster rate than the lower limbs.","The mandible, being further from the brain, tends to grow more and later than the maxilla.","Lymphoid tissues follow the exact same cephalocaudal gradient as general body tissues."}	2	The cephalocaudal gradient dictates that there is an axis of increased growth extending from the head toward the feet. Because of this gradient, the mandible, which is further away from the brain than the maxilla, grows more and later than the maxilla.	Growth Patterns	\N
ec09201f-f2a5-431a-bcaa-1fe5268c180a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on Scammon's curves of systemic tissue growth, which statement correctly compares the growth trajectories of the maxilla and the mandible?	{"The mandible follows the neural growth curve more closely than the maxilla.","The maxilla follows the neural growth curve more closely than the mandible.","Both follow the lymphoid curve, peaking before adolescence.","Both exactly parallel the general body growth curve."}	1	The growth of the jaws is intermediate between the neural and general body curves, with the mandible following the general body curve more closely, and the maxilla following the neural growth curve more closely than the mandible.	Growth Curves	\N
7d2be4c8-bd6a-43a0-b167-c076141d8092	orthodontics	BDS-I	While evaluating the ossification centers of a developing fetus, a researcher notes that the mandible undergoes a complex dual-ossification process. Which portion of the mandible develops primarily via endochondral ossification?	{"The anterior body and symphysis","The alveolar processes","The condylar process","The coronoid process"}	2	The condylar cartilage develops initially as an independent secondary cartilage and undergoes endochondral ossification. The rest of the mandible, including the body and alveolar processes, forms via intramembranous ossification.	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
5a8b9397-1643-4dcc-af9c-9bf9323b3f78	orthodontics	BDS-I	The cranial vault increases in size to accommodate the rapidly growing brain during infancy. This growth is achieved exclusively through which of the following mechanisms?	{"Endochondral ossification at the synchondroses","Interstitial growth within the mineralized bone","Intramembranous bone formation at the fontanelles and sutures","Proliferation of secondary cartilage"}	2	The cranial vault is made up of flat bones formed directly by intramembranous bone formation, without cartilaginous precursors. Growth occurs primarily via apposition of new bone at the sutures.	Cranial Vault Growth	\N
1aac6c41-697d-49e4-983f-3549bea13be7	orthodontics	BDS-I	The overall growth of the nasomaxillary complex is often described as a paradox because it translates downward and forward, yet:	{"Its anterior surface undergoes significant bone resorption.","It is driven entirely by the spheno-occipital synchondrosis.","It relies exclusively on endochondral ossification.","The sutural attachments migrate superiorly and posteriorly."}	0	As the maxilla grows downward and forward, its front surfaces are actually remodeled, and bone is removed (resorbed) from most of the anterior surface. The forward movement is due to translation from growth of structures behind it, not anterior apposition.	Maxillary Growth	\N
fd2e465b-1d23-47ca-908a-2eed22da1f6d	orthodontics	BDS-I	During mandibular growth, the ramus moves posteriorly to accommodate the erupting molars. This posterior movement is primarily accomplished by:	{"Endochondral replacement at the symphysis.","Apposition of bone on the anterior surface and resorption on the posterior surface.","Apposition of bone on the posterior surface and resorption on the anterior surface of the ramus.","Expansion of the spheno-occipital synchondrosis."}	2	The mandible grows longer as the ramus moves away from the chin; this occurs by removal (resorption) of bone from the anterior surface of the ramus and deposition (apposition) of bone on the posterior surface.	Mandibular Growth	\N
d9f485f1-b71c-41f7-aac5-6e5c481e9f8a	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the context of modern orthodontic treatment planning, what does the "Soft Tissue Paradigm" dictate regarding the relationship between the teeth and facial esthetics?	{"Dental occlusion must be perfected first, and soft tissues will automatically adapt.","Soft tissue proportions and adaptations are the primary goal, determining where the hard tissues should be positioned.","Functional occlusion of the TMJ completely dictates soft tissue drape.","Angle's ideal occlusion is incompatible with ideal soft tissue esthetics and should be abandoned."}	1	The soft tissue paradigm dictates that the primary goal of treatment becomes soft tissue relationships and adaptations. The clinician plans ideal soft tissue relationships and then places the teeth and jaws as needed to achieve this.	Modern Treatment Goals	\N
19e18879-7b45-4f80-b48b-652d79423a55	orthodontics	BDS-I	In Björk's implant studies, the "internal rotation" of the mandible is often masked clinically because of simultaneous surface remodeling. What is the definition of internal rotation?	{"The rotation of the mandibular plane relative to the cranial base.","The angular remodeling of the lower border of the mandible.","The rotation of the mandibular core (body) relative to the cranial base.","The rotation of the maxilla relative to the mandibular core."}	2	Internal rotation is defined as the rotation of the mandibular core relative to the cranial base. External rotation (angular remodeling of the lower border) often compensates for internal rotation, meaning the net visible change (total rotation) may be small.	Jaw Rotation	\N
0fb81061-6ebe-408a-9d78-7809fd539a8e	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient presenting with a "short anterior lower face height" typically exhibits which underlying pattern of mandibular growth?	{"Excessive backward rotation","Excessive forward rotation","Lack of internal rotation","Extreme angular remodeling of the gonial angle"}	1	Individuals characterized by short anterior lower face height have excessive forward rotation of the mandible during growth, which results in a nearly horizontal palatal plane, a low mandibular plane angle, and a deep bite malocclusion.	Jaw Rotation	\N
ebb8a7ee-a6c4-4daa-b720-3b09ce7c54ed	orthodontics	BDS-I	A mother ingested Valium during the first trimester of pregnancy. According to known teratogenic effects, the fetus is at the highest risk for developing which craniofacial anomaly?	{Anencephaly,"Central midface deficiency","Malformations similar to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome","Premature suture closure"}	2	Valium (along with Thalidomide and 13-cis Retinoic acid) is a teratogen known to cause malformations similar to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome.	Etiology of Malocclusion	\N
12eb5671-2c82-4c09-ae9c-4dc74e9e36e3	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the equilibrium theory of tooth position, which of the following forces plays the most critical role in determining the final position of the teeth?	{"The heavy, intermittent forces of mastication","The heavy, intermittent forces of swallowing","The light, long-lasting resting pressures from the lips, cheeks, and tongue","The transient forces from playing a wind instrument"}	2	Equilibrium is dictated by duration, not just magnitude. Light pressures of long duration from resting posture (lips, cheeks, tongue) are far more important in determining tooth position than the heavy but short-acting forces of chewing or swallowing.	Equilibrium Theory	\N
ef60e487-2659-4cee-aa2e-9d369e711fbc	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 6-year-old child has a chronic thumb-sucking habit. Clinical examination reveals a constricted, narrow maxillary arch. What is the physiological mechanism behind this specific narrowing?	{"The direct upward pressure of the thumb against the palate expands the mid-palatal suture.","The tongue is lowered while cheek pressure is increased during sucking, altering the muscular equilibrium.","The habit stimulates excessive growth of the mandibular condyles.","Thumb-sucking causes premature fusion of the mid-palatal suture."}	1	A narrow upper arch in thumb-suckers results from a combination of direct pressure and an alteration in resting cheek and lip pressures; specifically, the tongue is lowered away from the palate, and cheek pressure increases, causing maxillary constriction.	Environmental Influences	\N
02398b99-e0cf-4f6a-8352-7ce629ed9c75	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child with chronic total nasal obstruction presents with "adenoid facies." Which cephalometric change is most characteristic of this respiratory pattern's effect on jaw growth?	{"Forward rotation of the mandible and a deep bite.","The mandible rotates downward and backward, opening the bite anteriorly and increasing overjet.","The maxilla grows excessively in the transverse dimension.","Premature eruption of all permanent teeth."}	1	To breathe through the mouth, the posture of the head, jaw, and tongue must change. The mandible rotates downward and backward, which opens the bite anteriorly, increases overjet, and increases the lower face height (characteristic of adenoid facies).	Respiratory Pattern	\N
68d0c676-8ba5-4d9b-a282-533242b8085a	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the congenital syndrome Cleidocranial Dysplasia, eruption of succedaneous teeth is severely delayed or absent. What is the primary mechanical or biological failure preventing eruption in this condition?	{"Lack of a propulsive mechanism within the dental follicle.","Defective bone resorption leading to an uncleared eruption path.","Premature ankylosis of the primary teeth.","Absence of the periodontal ligament."}	1	In cleidocranial dysplasia, there is a defect in bone resorption. The succedaneous teeth have normal eruptive potential, but the overlying bone is not resorbed to clear a path, halting pre-emergent eruption.	Eruption Mechanisms	\N
2aee5da8-787a-4495-957c-85ae026e58d0	orthodontics	BDS-I	Continuous monitoring of human premolar eruption using a video microscope reveals that post-emergent tooth eruption is not a continuous, steady process. When does the maximum rate of tooth eruption typically occur?	{"During heavy mastication","Consistently throughout the daylight hours","Between 8 PM and midnight","Immediately upon waking in the morning"}	2	Experiments have shown a circadian rhythm in eruption; the short-term movements track eruption occurring only during a critical period between 8 PM and midnight or 1 AM, while eruption stops or teeth intrude slightly during the day.	Post-emergent Eruption	\N
c86772a5-ca41-45ad-bd10-1aaa5cece9c4	orthodontics	BDS-I	At what approximate age do the primary mandibular central incisors typically erupt?	{Birth,"6 months","12 months","24 months"}	1	The primary mandibular central incisors are usually the first teeth to erupt, typically around 6 months of age.	Eruption of the Primary Teeth	\N
b4ac0413-aec1-499a-b5b5-41b6d9dbf3ab	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the normal chronological sequence of tooth development, a permanent tooth usually begins its clinical emergence into the oral cavity when its root is approximately:	{"One-quarter completed","One-half completed","Three-fourths completed","Fully completed with a closed apex"}	2	A tooth usually emerges when about three-fourths of its root has been completed. The apex is typically not completed until 2 to 3 years after the tooth has erupted.	Tooth Development	\N
9700d09f-6ade-4991-ac29-0d12a90770cc	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the mandibular arch, the leeway space represents the size difference between the primary molars/canines and their permanent successors. What is the average total leeway space available in the mandibular arch?	{"1.5 mm","2.5 mm","3.0 mm","5.0 mm"}	3	The leeway space averages about 2.5 mm per side in the mandibular arch (totaling 5.0 mm) and 1.5 mm per side in the maxillary arch (totaling 3.0 mm).	Leeway Space	\N
60dc35c3-6733-4919-99fa-f80f11f5392c	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 5-year-old child presents with a "flush terminal plane" relationship of the primary second molars. Provided normal differential jaw growth occurs, what is the most likely occlusal outcome in the permanent dentition?	{"Class II division 1","Class I","Class III","Edge-to-edge incisor relationship"}	1	A flush terminal plane is normal in the primary dentition. With normal differential forward growth of the mandible relative to the maxilla and mesial movement of the lower molars into the leeway space, it typically transitions into a Class I permanent molar relationship.	Molar Relationships	\N
47a3be60-add7-4bd4-b302-0ec6edbc449e	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the transition from the primary to the permanent dentition, the "incisor liability" refers to the fact that permanent incisors are larger than primary incisors. Which of the following is NOT a mechanism that helps provide space to overcome this liability?	{"Slight increase in arch width across the canines.","Labial positioning of the permanent incisors.","Repositioning of mandibular canines backward into the primate space.","Substantial bone apposition at the anterior symphysis of the mandible."}	3	The mechanisms to overcome incisor liability include a slight increase in arch width, labial positioning of permanent incisors, and repositioning of canines into primate spaces. There is no mechanism by which the mandible can easily become longer in its anterior region via apposition at the symphysis postnatally.	Space Relationships	\N
1c20f4f2-eabd-434c-9ffd-3bbfc64324f0	orthodontics	BDS-I	Where are the "primate spaces" typically located in a normal human primary dentition?	{"Maxilla: Between central and lateral incisors / Mandible: Between lateral incisors and canines","Maxilla: Mesial to the canine / Mandible: Distal to the canine","Maxilla: Distal to the canine / Mandible: Mesial to the canine","Maxilla: Between primary first and second molars / Mandible: Between primary first and second molars"}	1	In the maxillary arch, the primate space is located between the lateral incisors and canines (mesial to the canine), whereas in the mandibular arch, the space is between the canines and first molars (distal to the canine).	Primary Dentition	\N
68ab65f5-d565-44f5-b439-570166cf2803	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following statements regarding arch length (the distance from the midline to the mesial of the first permanent molars) during the transition from the mixed to the permanent dentition is correct?	{"Arch length increases significantly due to anterior bone growth.","Arch length remains perfectly stable.","Arch length decreases.","Arch length increases only in the maxillary arch."}	2	Arch length decreases in the transition from the mixed to the permanent dentition, primarily due to the mesial drift of the permanent first molars into the leeway space as the primary molars exfoliate.	Arch Dimensions	\N
f942e8a5-15b3-4971-af32-4cd8e10f96ff	orthodontics	BDS-I	An orthodontist performs a Bolton analysis on a patient's study models. What is the primary diagnostic purpose of this specific analysis?	{"To calculate the exact angulation of the maxillary incisors relative to the cranial base.","To measure the transverse discrepancy between the maxilla and the mandible.","To identify a mesiodistal tooth size discrepancy between the maxillary and mandibular teeth.","To predict the final height of the patient based on tooth eruption."}	2	Tooth size analysis, or Bolton analysis, is carried out by measuring the mesiodistal width of each permanent tooth to compare the summed widths of the maxillary teeth to the mandibular teeth, identifying any size discrepancies that would prevent an ideal occlusion.	Diagnostic Records	\N
d77fa2c9-55f8-4094-bc3b-54c23583c91a	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating facial proportions in the frontal view using the "rule of fifths," the width of the nasal base should ideally be equal to the:	{"Interpupillary distance","Distance between the medial and lateral canthus of one eye","Intercanthal distance","Width of the mouth"}	2	According to the rule of fifths for frontal facial proportions, the central fifth is defined by the intercanthal distance (distance between the inner corners of the eyes), and ideally, the width of the nasal base should equal this distance.	Facial Proportions	\N
94e09fb5-31e0-48c4-895d-72c84398014a	orthodontics	BDS-I	In clinical facial profile analysis, a patient is described as having "bimaxillary dentoalveolar protrusion." What clinical feature defines this condition?	{"Severe retrusion of the chin point relative to the maxilla.","Both the upper and lower lips are prominent and everted.","A prominent nose accompanied by a deficient mandible.","Upright upper incisors with a deep mentolabial sulcus."}	1	Bimaxillary dentoalveolar protrusion simply means that in both jaws, the teeth protrude. Clinically, this is expressed as both the upper and lower lips being prominent and everted relative to the facial profile.	Profile Analysis	\N
7d0e540f-c280-410b-a76b-9c91414a46f7	orthodontics	BDS-I	When analyzing micro-esthetics and gingival contour, the "gingival zenith" (the most apical point of the gingival tissue) of the maxillary lateral incisor should ideally be positioned:	{"Exactly symmetric and level with the central incisors and canines.","More apically than the central incisors.","Distal to the long axis of the tooth.","Coincident with the vertical long axis of the tooth."}	3	The gingival zenith for the maxillary central incisors and canines should be located distal to the longitudinal axis of the tooth. However, for the maxillary lateral incisors, the zenith should coincide directly with their longitudinal axis.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
67c7d15c-a4f5-4372-9830-8d839d6a8572	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "golden proportion" is a mathematical ratio sometimes used to guide the esthetic restoration or positioning of anterior teeth. When viewed from the front, what is the ideal apparent width of the maxillary lateral incisor relative to the central incisor?	{0.5,0.62,0.8,1}	1	According to the golden proportion, when viewed from the front, the apparent width of the lateral incisor should be 62% of the width of the central incisor.	Dental Appearance	\N
7b2b9e65-0a2c-45ec-806a-80114d1b3d8a	medicine	BDS-I	"Bence Jones proteins" are monoclonal light chains associated with:	{CLL,AML,"Multiple Myeloma",Hodgkin’s}	2	Bence Jones proteins in urine are a diagnostic marker for Multiple Myeloma.	Blood Disease	\N
8976077a-e4c6-4e0d-a141-57d106430505	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Angioedema" is distinguished from "Urticaria" because it:	{"Is always redder","Involves larger areas of deep tissue with less erythema","Only occurs on the palms","Is caused by bacteria"}	1	Angioedema is deep tissue swelling with less redness than urticaria. [Table 2-3]	Medical Emergencies	\N
16136448-ab44-4577-9610-e236522c36ad	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 4-year-old child begins to cry immediately upon seeing a dental assistant wearing a white coat, even before any procedure has started. According to behavioral psychology, the white coat has become a(n):	{"Unconditioned stimulus","Conditioned stimulus","Positive reinforcer","Unconditioned response"}	1	In classical conditioning (Pavlovian), an originally neutral stimulus (the white coat) becomes associated with an unconditioned stimulus (pain/fear). The white coat then becomes a conditioned stimulus that elicits a conditioned response (crying).	Behavioral Development	\N
787863c4-54d5-4d0d-af51-4d32e532cc92	orthodontics	BDS-I	A dentist promises an uncooperative teenager that if they wear their rubber bands consistently for a month, they will not have to wear headgear at night. This technique aims to increase a desired behavior by removing an undesirable stimulus. This is known as:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Punishment,"Omission (Time-out)"}	1	Negative reinforcement involves the withdrawal of an unpleasant stimulus (headgear) after a response (wearing rubber bands), which increases the probability of that response occurring in the future.	Learning and Behavior	\N
a76f9c47-c3c2-43e7-be7d-ebe81d403cf9	orthodontics	BDS-I	If a previously terrified child has several pleasant, pain-free visits to the dentist and subsequently stops exhibiting fear and anxiety upon entering the clinic, this phenomenon is referred to in classical conditioning as:	{Assimilation,Accommodation,Extinction,Generalization}	2	Once a conditioned response (fear) has been established, it is necessary to reinforce it occasionally to maintain it. If the conditioned association is consistently presented without the unconditioned stimulus (pain), the response will no longer occur. This is referred to as extinction.	Behavioral Development	\N
81a8d4f5-0bb9-4740-93d7-7194690164e3	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Piaget's stages of cognitive development, a 4-year-old child who believes that the dental drill "is angry" or "wants to bite" is demonstrating a characteristic thought process known as:	{Egocentrism,Conservation,Animism,"Formal operations"}	2	During the preoperational period (ages 2-7), a characteristic of thought processes is animism, which involves investing inanimate objects with life and lifelike qualities.	Cognitive Development	\N
be22b728-9c28-41fd-b9d8-7827669add42	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Erikson's "Eight Ages of Man," a child between the ages of 3 to 6 years is navigating the psychosocial crisis of:	{"Basic Trust vs. Mistrust","Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt","Initiative vs. Guilt","Industry vs. Inferiority"}	2	At the stage of 3 to 6 years, the child is in the stage of Initiative vs. Guilt. The ultimate ability to initiate new ideas and activities depends on how well they are able to express new thoughts without being made to feel guilty.	Emotional Development	\N
a5168792-ce78-4d46-a8f3-d6758dd43ca2	orthodontics	BDS-I	During embryologic development, a cleft of the primary palate (cleft lip) occurs due to the failure of fusion between which two structures?	{"The two lateral palatal shelves","The median nasal process and the maxillary processes","The mandibular processes","The lateral nasal processes and the maxillary processes"}	1	Clefting of the lip (primary palate) occurs because of a failure of fusion between the median and lateral nasal processes and the maxillary prominence, which normally occurs in humans during the sixth week of development (days 28-38).	Embryologic Development	\N
b509cd70-476e-4d0b-83a9-b9717edbdb76	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "neonatal line" is a distinct microscopic feature found in certain teeth that represents a disruption in enamel formation. This line is typically found in which of the following?	{"All permanent incisors","Only the primary incisors","All primary teeth and the first permanent molars","Only teeth that erupt during infancy"}	2	The interruption in growth produced by the physiologic shock of birth results in a noticeable "neonatal line" across the enamel. This line is found in all primary teeth and the permanent first molars, as their calcification begins before birth.	Early Development	\N
997bd6e5-fd46-4a56-b608-8e1e65108813	orthodontics	BDS-I	Regarding the timing of the adolescent growth spurt, which of the following statements is statistically accurate?	{"Boys generally experience their peak growth velocity 2 years earlier than girls.","Girls generally experience their peak growth velocity 2 years earlier than boys.","Both sexes generally experience peak growth velocity at exactly the same age.","Jaw growth in boys is completed before girls."}	1	There is a great deal of individual variation, but puberty and the adolescent growth spurt occur on the average nearly 2 years earlier in girls than in boys.	Later Stages of Development	\N
fb1b34a7-ade3-45d7-97a3-c2884f788e7c	orthodontics	BDS-I	As individuals transition from young adulthood into middle and older age, what is a typical aging change observed in the facial soft tissues?	{"The lips shorten, exposing more of the maxillary incisors.","The lips sag downward, decreasing maxillary incisor exposure and increasing mandibular incisor exposure.","The vermilion border of the lips becomes significantly thicker.","The intercanthal distance rapidly decreases."}	1	With aging, there is a decrease in exposure of the upper incisors, both at rest and on smile, and an increase in exposure of the lower incisors. This occurs because the lips sag downward with aging.	Maturation and Aging	\N
88c2d972-6135-4beb-92bb-3fb7b46ed240	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 45-year-old patient comes in for a routine checkup. Radiographs reveal that their pulp chambers are significantly smaller than they were in a radiograph taken at age 18. This reduction in pulp chamber size is primarily due to:	{"Pathologic root resorption","Rapid laying down of primary dentin","The slow, continuous addition of secondary dentin","Increased size of the apical foramen"}	2	At the time a permanent tooth erupts, the pulp chamber is relatively large. As time passes, additional dentin (secondary dentin) slowly deposits on the inside of the tooth, so that the pulp chamber gradually becomes smaller with increasing age.	Aging Changes in Teeth	\N
8fb0b21c-befd-4a77-9841-28b2e314cafb	orthodontics	BDS-I	Anthropologic studies comparing modern humans to primitive populations suggest that the increased prevalence of malocclusion in modern times is most directly correlated with:	{"A genetic mutation specific to modern humans that increases tooth size.","An evolutionary decrease in jaw size linked to a softer, modern diet, outstripping the decrease in tooth size.","An increase in the prevalence of dental caries and periodontal disease.","The introduction of refined sugars into the diet."}	1	The progressive reduction in jaw size, likely related to disuse atrophy from a softer, modern diet requiring less masticatory effort, has paralleled the development of modern civilization. The jaws have become smaller, but tooth size and number have not decreased as rapidly, leading to crowding.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
40d06125-55bd-4ade-99a3-929fda7cb4fb	public_health	BDS-I	"Calibration" of examiners in a survey is done to reduce:	{"Sample size","Random error","Inter-examiner variation","Time taken"}	2	Calibration ensures that all examiners apply the diagnostic criteria in a standardized, uniform manner.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
59d1e57e-2a0e-4ea2-a9bd-eba67deab916	orthodontics	BDS-I	During a clinical examination of jaw function, what is the expected normal range of maximum opening for an adult patient without TMJ dysfunction?	{"20 mm","35 mm","50 mm","75 mm"}	2	A normal maximum opening for an adult is around 50 mm. A maximum opening significantly less than this may indicate TMJ dysfunction or muscle restriction.	Clinical Evaluation	\N
61efcaab-2cd3-4903-8ef0-1a60440cb265	orthodontics	BDS-I	When comparing the radiation doses of modern diagnostic equipment, which of the following records typically exposes the patient to the highest effective dose in microsieverts (µSv)?	{"Digital panoramic radiograph","Digital cephalometric radiograph","Full mouth series (FMX) with D speed film and round cone","Cone-Beam Computed Tomography (CBCT) small field of view"}	2	According to the Doses and Risk table, an FMX with D Speed film and round cone (older methodology) delivers 388 µSv, which is significantly higher than a digital panoramic (16 µSv), digital cephalometric (5.5 µSv), or standard CBCT.	Diagnostic Records	\N
5be552f9-ccd8-482d-a65d-2e1826cd7097	orthodontics	BDS-I	For accurate diagnosis, orthodontic dental casts are traditionally trimmed symmetrically. The bases of the maxillary cast are specifically trimmed perpendicular to which anatomic landmark?	{"The incisive papilla","The midsagittal line (midpalatal raphe)","The buccal frenum","The terminal molar"}	1	Orthodontic casts have traditionally been trimmed with symmetric bases. The backs of the casts are trimmed perpendicular to the midsagittal line, most easily visualized as the midpalatal raphe for most patients.	Diagnostic Records	\N
91a2d8fe-4a3c-45cf-a7b1-c6f63f6e63c0	orthodontics	BDS-I	When superimposing serial cephalometric radiographs to evaluate overall craniofacial growth over time, which plane or structure is generally considered the most stable reference base?	{"The mandibular plane","The occlusal plane","The anterior cranial base (e.g., Sella-Nasion line)","The palatal plane"}	2	The cranial base, particularly the anterior portion (often represented by the Sella-Nasion line), is relatively stable after early childhood and is widely used as a reference area for superimposing cephalometric tracings to evaluate overall changes in the maxilla and mandible.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
1ec3334e-75a1-4302-b489-1bf7399bd8f5	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient presenting with an extremely deep bite and a "short face" profile usually exhibits a mandibular growth pattern characterized by:	{"Minimal ramus growth and forward translation.","Upward and forward eruption of posterior teeth preventing anterior rotation.","Forward internal rotation of the mandible that is not entirely compensated for by external remodeling.","Backward internal rotation of the mandible."}	2	Short face individuals are characterized by forward rotation of the mandible during growth. The internal rotation of the core is directed forward, and this rotation is not entirely masked by external remodeling, leading to a flatter mandibular plane and deep bite.	Jaw Rotation	\N
97e0923f-18a4-46f4-ab84-457a0143a4d0	orthodontics	BDS-I	Crowding of the mandibular incisors that develops in the late teens and early twenties (late incisor crowding) is a common phenomenon. According to current evidence, this is primarily caused by:	{"Pressure from erupting third molars pushing the anterior teeth forward.","Continued late forward growth and rotation of the mandible when the maxilla has stopped growing.","Increased width of the mandibular arch due to muscular function.","Gingival recession and loss of periodontal support."}	1	Late incisor crowding is primarily related to the pattern of late mandibular growth. As the mandible continues to grow forward late in the teens (often after the maxilla has stopped), the lower incisors are displaced lingually by the lips, leading to crowding. Pressure from third molars is generally not considered the primary cause.	Maturational Changes	\N
2f1e6881-299f-46d9-8c00-7936d07396c1	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which theory of craniofacial growth posits that bone and cartilage lack intrinsic growth potential and that the true determinants of skeletal growth are the surrounding soft tissues and spaces?	{"The Genetic Theory","The Cartilage-Directed Theory","The Functional Matrix Theory","The Sutural Dominance Theory"}	2	Moss's Functional Matrix Theory theorized that the major determinant of growth of the maxilla and mandible is the enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities (the functional matrices), which grow in response to functional needs, while the bones react passively.	Theories of Growth	\N
e965e714-9a7d-4ed9-835b-add154085e5d	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the most common normal sequence of eruption for the permanent teeth in the mandibular arch?	{6-1-2-4-5-3-7,6-1-2-3-4-5-7,6-1-2-5-4-3-7,1-2-6-3-4-5-7}	1	The most common eruption sequence for the mandibular permanent teeth is first molar (6), central incisor (1), lateral incisor (2), canine (3), first premolar (4), second premolar (5), and second molar (7).	Eruption Sequence	\N
c4c85425-a5ca-43a3-a8ca-6b81a3aaffa4	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN), what is the aesthetic component based on?	{"A cephalometric tracing analysis","A scale of 10 standard photographs showing varying degrees of dental attractiveness","The exact millimeter measurement of overjet","A psychological questionnaire"}	1	The aesthetic component of the IOTN is derived from the patient's or clinician's answer to a set of 10 standard photographs representing a range of dental attractiveness.	Demand for Treatment	\N
21f85904-2920-4de2-ae1b-e916565d2a30	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on NHANES III data, what percentage of the U.S. adult population is considered to have "ideal alignment" with no incisor irregularity?	{~10%,~35%,~50%,~75%}	0	Only about 34% of adults have well-aligned lower incisors, and the percentage with "ideal alignment" (0 mm irregularity) is very low, hovering around 10-15% across different ethnic groups in adults.	Epidemiology of Malocclusion	\N
843d39c8-bcb3-4fcc-bb63-54ed0a5f7e99	orthodontics	BDS-I	What evolutionary trend in the human dentition is most directly correlated with the modern prevalence of malocclusion?	{"Rapid increase in tooth size.","Loss of the primary dentition.","A progressive reduction in jaw size linked to a softer diet, outstripping the decrease in tooth size.","The evolutionary disappearance of maxillary central incisors."}	2	The fossil record shows a decrease in jaw size (disuse atrophy from modern diets) that has occurred much faster than the evolutionary decrease in tooth size and number, leading to widespread crowding.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
16702a92-1a5c-4d04-9d63-828d62b37aa5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For a patient with a history of seizures, which of the following is NOT a preventive measure?	{"Anxiety reduction","Avoiding fatigue","Prophylactic Nitroglycerin","Checking medication compliance"}	2	Nitroglycerin is for angina, not seizures. [Box 1-24]	Medical Management	\N
4547c358-7dfd-43fa-b5a9-7ee9a1bf355e	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which theory of craniofacial growth posits that the sutures of the face are reactive areas, not primary determinants of growth?	{"Genetic Theory","Sutural Dominance Theory","Cartilage-Directed Theory","Functional Matrix Theory"}	3	The Functional Matrix theory (and modern understanding) views sutures as reactive areas that respond to the expansion of surrounding soft tissues or the translation of bones, rather than being primary growth pacemakers.	Theories of Growth Control	\N
99878d98-80e9-43b4-9b79-a09f7cc5eef2	surgery	BDS-I	"Jefferson fracture" is a burst fracture of:	{"C1 Atlas","C2 Axis",C7,L1}	0	A burst fracture of C1 caused by axial loading.	Trauma	\N
e41d151b-5973-4330-8e11-e9186d080a7f	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the development of the cranial vault, how is new bone primarily formed?	{"Endochondral ossification at the synchondroses","Intramembranous ossification at the sutures","Interstitial growth within the parietal bones","Replacement of Meckel's cartilage"}	1	The cranial vault is made up of flat bones formed directly by intramembranous bone formation. Postnatal growth occurs primarily via apposition of new bone at the sutures.	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
463584e6-7415-4315-8258-23a94243ef90	orthodontics	BDS-I	The spheno-occipital synchondrosis is a major growth center in the cranial base. When does it typically fuse?	{"At birth","By 6 years of age","Around 10-12 years of age","In the late teens or early twenties"}	3	Unlike the intersphenoid (fuses at birth) and spheno-ethmoidal (fuses at 6-7 years), the spheno-occipital synchondrosis remains active much longer, typically fusing in the early twenties.	Cranial Base Growth	\N
531d5e9c-56d6-4dcc-b55e-f8c538f4511c	orthodontics	BDS-I	As the maxilla translates downward and forward during growth, how does the hard palate remodel?	{"Resorption on the palatal surface and apposition on the nasal floor","Resorption on the nasal floor and apposition on the palatal roof","Apposition on both surfaces","Resorption on both surfaces"}	1	The palatal vault moves downward not just by translation, but also by bone being removed (resorbed) from the floor of the nose and added (apposition) to the roof of the mouth.	Maxillary Growth	\N
181ccc82-dbc3-49dc-8c05-46f9eed281ab	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the mandible, growth in length occurs primarily by which mechanism at the ramus?	{"Resorption on the posterior surface and apposition on the anterior surface.","Apposition on both the anterior and posterior surfaces.","Resorption on the anterior surface and apposition on the posterior surface.","Expansion of the mental symphysis."}	2	The mandible grows longer as the ramus moves away from the chin; this occurs by removal (resorption) of bone from the anterior surface of the ramus and deposition (apposition) of bone on the posterior surface.	Mandibular Growth	\N
452f68af-5973-4dc3-9686-8280c5328319	orthodontics	BDS-I	Operant conditioning describes how consequences affect behavior. Using "voice control" (speaking sharply to gain a child's attention) is classified as which type of conditioning?	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Punishment,Omission}	2	Voice control is considered a mild form of punishment, where an unpleasant stimulus (a sharp, firm voice) is presented to decrease the probability of an undesired behavior.	Learning and Behavior	\N
8659eb98-c6b1-4ea9-8242-e42921f34e0b	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Piaget, a child who believes their teddy bear is scared of the dentist is demonstrating:	{Egocentrism,Conservation,Animism,"Formal operations"}	2	Animism is a characteristic of the preoperational stage where a child invests inanimate objects with life and lifelike qualities.	Cognitive Development	\N
562118c4-a73a-40f8-b490-781d57ae51ef	orthodontics	BDS-I	During which stage of embryonic development does Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS) primarily originate?	{"Germ layer formation (Day 17)","Neural tube formation (Days 18-23)","Formation of the primary palate (Days 28-38)","Final differentiation of tissues (Day 50+)"}	0	Deficiencies of midline tissue of the neural plate (like FAS) occur in the very early stages of embryonic development, specifically during germ layer formation around day 17.	Embryologic Development	\N
8f76e110-bcfb-49b2-beb6-bc6c5d29c93a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Hemifacial microsomia is associated with an issue during which stage of embryonic development?	{"Germ layer formation","Origin, migration, and interaction of neural crest cells","Formation of the secondary palate","Final differentiation of tissues"}	1	Craniofacial microsomia (and Treacher Collins syndrome) are related to problems in the origin, migration, and interaction of neural crest cells, typically around days 19-28.	Embryologic Development	\N
587358e3-c4b3-4024-b30a-007e91ee44cd	orthodontics	BDS-I	Clefting of the lip occurs due to a failure of fusion between which specific embryonic processes?	{"Lateral palatal shelves","Median nasal, lateral nasal, and maxillary processes","Mandibular and maxillary processes","Frontonasal and medial nasal processes"}	1	Cleft lip occurs when there is a failure of fusion between the median and lateral nasal processes and the maxillary prominence, which normally occurs between days 28-38.	Embryologic Development	\N
5eb1b7a4-cf75-4457-8f2a-e29f71abbf8d	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the most common supernumerary tooth found in humans?	{"Maxillary lateral incisor","Mandibular second premolar",Mesiodens,"Maxillary fourth molar"}	2	The most common supernumerary tooth appears in the maxillary midline and is called a mesiodens, which can physically disrupt the eruption of the central incisors.	Specific Causes of Malocclusion	\N
f1753d39-0218-41f5-aa45-146642d34fb1	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which genetic condition is characterized by missing clavicles, supernumerary teeth, and a failure of succedaneous teeth to erupt due to a defect in bone resorption?	{"Crouzon's Syndrome","Cleidocranial Dysplasia","Treacher Collins Syndrome","Hemifacial Microsomia"}	1	Cleidocranial dysplasia is characterized by missing clavicles and supernumerary teeth. The primary teeth do not shed normally because of a defect in bone resorption, halting the eruption of permanent teeth.	Disturbances of Dental Development	\N
b0eb7d12-568d-4903-84b9-8851cec5e09c	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the equilibrium theory of tooth position, why do the massive forces of mastication not cause teeth to move permanently?	{"The forces are balanced by the tongue.","The forces are too light.","The forces are of extremely short duration.","The periodontal ligament paralyzes bone remodeling during chewing."}	2	Equilibrium is dictated by duration, not just magnitude. Mastication forces are very heavy but last for a very short duration (only minutes per day total), which is insufficient to trigger the biologic response needed for tooth movement.	Equilibrium Considerations	\N
0de4e60e-4b54-4ebb-b861-2e757288871e	medicine	BDS-I	"Osler’s nodes" in endocarditis are:	{Painless,Painful,Retinal,Macular}	1	Osler’s nodes are painful fingertip swellings.	Cardiovascular	\N
c7caf0d4-0703-4b62-a8e4-0101bf2f780a	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child presents with an anterior open bite and a confirmed tongue-thrust swallow. What is the contemporary view on the relationship between the tongue thrust and the open bite?	{"The tongue thrust is the primary cause of the open bite.","The tongue thrust is a physiologic adaptation to the open bite to create an anterior seal.","The tongue thrust causes maxillary constriction, which secondarily causes the open bite.","Tongue thrusting only occurs in children with skeletal Class III patterns."}	1	Modern understanding views tongue thrust during swallowing primarily as an adaptation to an existing open bite (to create a seal to swallow), rather than the primary cause of the open bite itself.	Sucking and Other Habits	\N
5b082cbe-954b-4350-8ac9-7d1019ad7af1	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which permanent tooth is most commonly impacted (excluding third molars)?	{"Maxillary central incisor","Mandibular first premolar","Maxillary canine","Mandibular lateral incisor"}	2	Due to its long path of eruption and late emergence relative to adjacent teeth, the maxillary canine is the most frequently impacted tooth (after third molars).	Traumatic Displacement / Eruption Problems	\N
79db4ae0-5864-4c18-a92d-8b1ab400d2a1	orthodontics	BDS-I	Chronic mouth breathing can lead to "adenoid facies." Which of the following skeletal features is characteristic of this condition?	{"Downward and backward rotation of the mandible","Forward rotation of the mandible","Maxillary prognathism","Deep anterior overbite"}	0	To breathe through the mouth, the mandible drops and rotates downward and backward. If maintained, this alters the equilibrium, leading to increased lower face height, anterior open bite, and a narrow maxillary arch.	Respiratory Pattern	\N
7814b16e-7e63-44e3-96b2-4cf9c86944ed	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "neonatal line" is a prominent line of developmental arrest found in the enamel of certain teeth. Which of the following teeth would typically not exhibit a neonatal line?	{"Primary maxillary central incisor","Primary mandibular second molar","Permanent maxillary first molar","Permanent maxillary central incisor"}	3	The neonatal line forms due to the physiologic shock of birth. It is found in all primary teeth and permanent first molars (which begin calcifying before birth). Permanent central incisors begin calcifying after birth.	Late Fetal Development and Birth	\N
a4ee281e-541d-4f0f-a795-8d9e9a5e43a0	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the primary dentition, the "primate space" in the maxillary arch is typically located where?	{"Between the central and lateral incisors","Between the lateral incisor and canine","Between the canine and first primary molar","Between the first and second primary molars"}	1	In the maxillary arch, the primate space is located between the lateral incisors and canines. In the mandibular arch, it is located between the canines and first primary molars.	Eruption of the Primary Teeth	\N
cac3e9ef-a6dc-4f73-9e2a-ff86062c5cc2	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which measurement describes the "leeway space" in the transition to the permanent dentition?	{"The size difference between the primary incisors and permanent incisors.","The space created by the expansion of the midpalatal suture.","The size difference between the primary molars/canines and their permanent successors (premolars/canines).","The space between the primary canines and first molars."}	2	The leeway space is the size difference between the primary molars and canines and the permanent premolars and canines that replace them.	Space Relationships	\N
da811403-609d-417b-8326-69ff9b4d40d0	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the average amount of leeway space available in the mandibular arch per side?	{"0.5 mm","1.5 mm","2.5 mm","4.0 mm"}	2	The leeway space averages about 2.5 mm per side in the mandibular arch and 1.5 mm per side in the maxillary arch.	Space Relationships	\N
525b796f-9c29-467d-a0d2-d97762770885	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child presents with a "flush terminal plane" primary molar relationship. What permanent molar relationship is most likely to develop if normal differential mandibular growth occurs?	{"Class II","Class I","Class III","End-to-end permanently"}	1	A flush terminal plane usually transitions into a Class I permanent molar relationship due to the differential forward growth of the mandible and the mesial shift of the lower molars into the leeway space.	Space Relationships	\N
d80c73b6-1203-4c28-8b1c-3c0c9514dbe8	orthodontics	BDS-I	During post-emergent tooth eruption, the rate of eruption matches the vertical growth of the mandibular ramus. This continuous slow eruption throughout adolescence is called:	{"Pre-emergent drift","The juvenile occlusal equilibrium","The adult occlusal equilibrium",Translocation}	1	After a tooth reaches the occlusal level, it continues to erupt at a rate that parallels the vertical growth of the mandibular ramus. This phase of slow eruption is termed the juvenile occlusal equilibrium.	Eruption Sequence and Timing	\N
4d6ed13b-9cfd-40c4-bbe9-f2dc68ba8511	orthodontics	BDS-I	In cephalometric analysis, the phenomenon where a tooth moves through space because the jaw bone it is embedded in is moving is called:	{"Active eruption",Translation,Translocation,"Interstitial growth"}	1	Movement of the teeth relative to the cranial base obviously could be produced by a combination of translation (as the tooth moves along with the jaw) and true eruption (movement of the tooth within its jaw).	Interaction Between Jaw Rotation and Tooth Eruption	\N
b9d95fc6-f9fa-40b2-8a4e-bd14c52434f2	orthodontics	BDS-I	The onset of the adolescent growth spurt is triggered by hormones. Which endocrine gland is the initial source of releasing factors that start this cascade?	{"Pituitary gland","Adrenal cortex",Hypothalamus,"Thyroid gland"}	2	The cascade begins when neurons in the hypothalamus secrete releasing factors, which then stimulate the anterior pituitary to release pituitary gonadotropins.	Initiation of Adolescence	\N
1b772bd7-caed-4503-ab27-f56b985f4799	orthodontics	BDS-I	When comparing the timing of the adolescent growth spurt between sexes, which statement is true?	{"Boys reach peak growth velocity earlier than girls.","Girls reach peak growth velocity nearly 2 years earlier than boys.","Both sexes reach peak growth velocity at exactly the same age.","Jaw growth ceases earlier in boys than in girls."}	1	There is a great deal of individual variation, but on average, puberty and the adolescent growth spurt occur nearly 2 years earlier in girls than in boys.	Timing of Puberty	\N
24db5a28-7a60-404c-ad75-2fdab6692779	orthodontics	BDS-I	In female patients, the onset of menstruation (menarche) indicates what regarding the adolescent growth spurt?	{"The growth spurt is just beginning.","The patient is exactly at peak height velocity.","Peak height velocity has already occurred.","Growth has completely finished."}	2	Menarche always follows the peak velocity of the adolescent growth spurt. Therefore, its occurrence indicates that the peak of growth has passed.	Timing of Puberty	\N
eba1fc52-b682-4f08-920a-b6bb3bfb8ee9	orthodontics	BDS-I	Growth in the craniofacial complex normally ceases in a specific order across the three planes of space. What is the correct sequence of growth completion?	{"Transverse (width), Anteroposterior (length), Vertical (height)","Vertical, Anteroposterior, Transverse","Anteroposterior, Transverse, Vertical","Transverse, Vertical, Anteroposterior"}	0	Growth in width is completed first, followed by growth in length (anteroposterior), and finally growth in height (vertical), which continues longer than the other two dimensions.	Dimensional Changes	\N
a6e08548-1db9-43b3-9313-6d46ed891d4a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Björk defined "internal rotation" of the mandible as:	{"Rotation of the mandibular plane relative to the cranial base.","Angular remodeling of the lower border.","Rotation of the mandibular core (body) relative to the cranial base.","Total rotation minus apparent rotation."}	2	Internal rotation is the rotation of the core of the mandible relative to the cranial base. Total rotation equals internal rotation minus external rotation (angular remodeling).	Rotation of Jaws During Growth	\N
3f520996-82be-428e-bf5d-b9cf2205f1b7	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to NHANES III data, which occlusal characteristic is heavily influenced by racial/ethnic background, being twice as prevalent in blacks as in whites?	{"Severe anterior open bite","Midline diastema > 2 mm","Ideal alignment","Class II division 2"}	1	Midline diastemas > 2 mm are more than twice as likely to have a midline diastema than whites or Hispanics.	Epidemiology	\N
13c6550b-19b5-40bc-883f-6134bffedac2	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient with a "long face" and an anterior open bite typically exhibits which pattern of jaw rotation during growth?	{"Excessive forward internal rotation","Backward internal rotation","No internal rotation","Extreme angular remodeling of the gonial angle hiding forward rotation"}	1	Long face individuals typically exhibit backward rotation of the mandible during growth. This rotation is associated with an increase in anterior lower face height and a tendency toward anterior open bite.	Rotation of Jaws During Growth	\N
cffe4d3a-85c5-4054-977f-8fb996ea97d3	orthodontics	BDS-I	Late crowding of the mandibular incisors in the early twenties is most strongly associated with:	{"Pressure from erupting third molars.","Continued late forward growth and rotation of the mandible relative to the maxilla.","Eruption of the maxillary canines.","A sudden decrease in arch width due to muscle pressure."}	1	Late incisor crowding often develops as the mandible continues to grow forward slightly late in the teens (often after the maxilla has stopped). The lower incisors are displaced lingually by the lips, leading to crowding.	Alignment and Occlusion	\N
7b66ef98-8333-4d76-bbe0-de1882220f5a	orthodontics	BDS-I	As adults age from their 20s to their 50s, a predictable change in the facial soft tissues occurs, resulting in:	{"Increased exposure of maxillary incisors and decreased exposure of mandibular incisors.","Decreased exposure of maxillary incisors and increased exposure of mandibular incisors at rest.","Shortening of the upper lip.","Widening of the intercommissure distance."}	1	With aging, the soft tissues sag downward. This leads to a decrease in the exposure of the upper incisors and an increase in the exposure of the lower incisors at rest and on smile.	Changes in Facial Soft Tissues	\N
09c6d66b-d181-491e-bee3-cc451cfa9858	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following aging changes occurs within the tooth itself over decades?	{"The pulp chamber expands.","The root undergoes physiologic resorption.","Secondary dentin is deposited, shrinking the pulp chamber.","The enamel thickens due to remineralization."}	2	As time passes, additional dentin (secondary dentin) slowly deposits on the inside of the tooth, causing the pulp chamber to gradually become smaller with increasing age.	Aging Changes in Teeth and Supporting Structures	\N
51316f47-0ef7-4b40-a4dc-0be72b23aa45	orthodontics	BDS-I	During an orthodontic clinical exam, checking for an asymmetric shift from centric relation (CR) to maximum intercuspation (MI) is crucial for identifying:	{"True skeletal asymmetry vs a functional shift","The presence of a tongue thrust","The severity of an anterior open bite","The exact millimeter size of the leeway space"}	0	A unilateral crossbite might be a true skeletal asymmetry, or it could be a pseudo-asymmetry caused by a functional shift of the mandible upon closure due to a tooth interference. Evaluating CR to MI is vital to distinguish these.	Jaw and Occlusal Function	\N
fce869e5-dfff-4956-9aea-faeaaccbf5a8	orthodontics	BDS-I	When analyzing facial proportions in the frontal view, the width of the nasal base should ideally be equal to:	{"The width of the mouth","The interpupillary distance","The intercanthal distance","The width of the chin"}	2	According to the rule of fifths, the central fifth of the face is determined by the intercanthal distance (distance between the inner corners of the eyes), which should equal the width of the nasal base.	Facial Proportions: Macro-Esthetics	\N
b049c4e3-3c39-4ed3-afc1-5ed2b0b53fc6	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating the facial profile, "bimaxillary dentoalveolar protrusion" refers to:	{"Protrusion of the upper and lower jaws (bones)","Prominent, everted upper and lower lips caused by protruding incisors","A prominent chin and a prominent nose","A convex skeletal profile"}	1	Bimaxillary dentoalveolar protrusion means the teeth in both jaws protrude. Clinically, this is expressed as both the upper and lower lips being prominent and everted relative to the facial profile.	Facial Proportions: Macro-Esthetics	\N
f7e56f07-a45c-4ac5-bd56-5786bb2049cf	orthodontics	BDS-I	In smile analysis (mini-esthetics), the "smile arc" is considered ideal when:	{"The incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth are completely flat.","The incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth follow the curvature of the lower lip.","The maxillary incisors are hidden entirely by the upper lip.","The mandibular incisors are fully visible above the lower lip."}	1	An ideal smile arc occurs when the contour of the incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth matches the curvature of the lower lip upon smiling.	Facial and Dental Appearance	\N
2123ab11-acc7-4021-8a2e-f13d285ab84e	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "golden proportion" states that when viewed from the front, the apparent width of the lateral incisor should be what percentage of the apparent width of the central incisor?	{0.5,0.62,0.8,1}	1	The golden proportion suggests a ratio of 1.0 : 0.62 : 0.38 for the apparent widths of the central incisor, lateral incisor, and canine when viewed directly from the front.	Dental Appearance: Micro-Esthetics	\N
76d475b4-0dcb-46cb-a3b1-21553fa28c43	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which theory of craniofacial growth argues that the cartilages of the nasal septum and mandibular condyles are NOT primary determinants of growth, but rather respond to the expansion of surrounding spaces?	{"Cartilage-Directed Theory","Genetic Theory","Sutural Dominance Theory","Functional Matrix Theory"}	3	Moss's Functional Matrix theory states that the enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities (functional needs) drives growth, while bone and cartilage react passively.	Theories of Growth	\N
c20f2086-712f-4b06-864e-12a9f7bee5a8	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient develops "black triangles" between their maxillary incisors after orthodontic alignment. This is typically due to:	{"Hypertrophy of the gingival papilla","An allergic reaction to nickel-titanium wires","Loss of crestal bone and reshaping of crowded/rotated teeth","Premature eruption of the canines"}	2	Short interdental papillae leave an open gingival embrasure (black triangle). This often becomes apparent when severely crowded or rotated incisors are aligned in adults who have experienced some loss of crestal bone height.	Dental Appearance: Micro-Esthetics	\N
628d06c8-53ba-4eae-8f74-eedc88f4f346	orthodontics	BDS-I	For routine orthodontic diagnostic records, which radiograph is considered the standard for evaluating dental age, pathology, and overall dental health?	{"Lateral cephalogram","Submental vertex","Panoramic radiograph","Cone Beam CT (small field)"}	2	A panoramic radiograph is valuable for orthodontic evaluation as it yields a broader view of the teeth and jaws to detect pathology, supernumerary or impacted teeth, and radiation exposure is much lower than a full mouth series.	Which Diagnostic Records Are Needed?	\N
c6265c5d-be84-454c-aa07-a5fac899fc64	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which radiographic technique exposes the patient to the lowest effective dose of radiation?	{"Digital panoramic radiograph","Digital lateral cephalometric radiograph","Cone Beam Computed Tomography (large field)","Full mouth series with D speed film"}	1	According to Table 6-6, a digital cephalometric radiograph exposes the patient to about 5.5 µSv, which is lower than a digital panoramic (16 µSv), CBCT, or FMX.	Doses and Risk	\N
b686ecbd-c544-4150-af46-35dfdbf07b99	orthodontics	BDS-I	When trimming orthodontic casts for a diagnostic setup, why are the bases traditionally trimmed symmetrically?	{"To fit into commercial storage boxes","To ensure the casts balance without articulating paper","To help the observer easily detect asymmetries within the dental arches","To perfectly align the curve of Spee"}	2	Digital or physical casts are displayed with symmetric bases—partly to emphasize that they are equivalent to physical casts and partly because the symmetric base helps the observer detect asymmetries within the dental arches.	Cast Analysis	\N
587efbab-5333-4433-9820-4ce5bbc7d8d5	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the primary purpose of conducting a Bolton Analysis on orthodontic casts?	{"To calculate the exact angulation of the maxillary incisors","To measure the amount of crowding in millimeters","To detect a tooth size discrepancy between the maxillary and mandibular teeth","To predict the final height of the patient"}	2	Tooth size analysis (Bolton analysis) is carried out by measuring the mesiodistal width of each permanent tooth to compare the summed widths of the maxillary teeth to the mandibular teeth, identifying size discrepancies that would prevent an ideal occlusion.	Cast Analysis: Tooth Size	\N
4d6effab-8808-469b-8032-f811bb3e2d0c	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the Bolton analysis, a normal ratio for the anterior teeth (mandibular sum / maxillary sum) is approximately:	{0.62,0.77,0.91,1}	1	The anterior Bolton ratio (mandibular anterior sum divided by maxillary anterior sum) is normally around 77.2%.	Cast Analysis: Tooth Size	\N
aba07d5a-755a-48f3-a908-0779a5ca81ee	orthodontics	BDS-I	On a standard lateral cephalometric radiograph, the anterior cranial base is typically represented by a line drawn between which two landmarks?	{"Porion and Orbitale","Sella and Nasion","Gonion and Menton","Anterior Nasal Spine and Posterior Nasal Spine"}	1	The anterior cranial base is classically represented by the line connecting Sella (the center of the sella turcica) and Nasion (the frontonasal suture).	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
c311cbf0-0c6f-4915-8499-f48f8199b21b	orthodontics	BDS-I	When superimposing serial cephalometric radiographs to evaluate a patient's overall facial growth, which reference structure is considered the most stable?	{"The mandibular plane","The occlusal plane","The anterior cranial base","The palatal plane"}	2	The anterior cranial base, formed primarily by endochondral ossification and finishing growth relatively early, is used as the stable reference area for evaluating overall facial growth changes over time.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
51e44599-cd80-43ce-bd7d-ac8e7098bc7d	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient has a condition where the posterior teeth fail to erupt despite an open, cleared eruption path. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the PTHR1 gene. What is this condition called?	{"Cleidocranial dysplasia","Primary failure of eruption (PFE)",Ankylosis,"Ectopic eruption"}	1	Primary failure of eruption is a rare but well-documented syndrome where posterior teeth fail to erupt, apparently due to a defect in the propulsive mechanism. It has been linked to a mutation in the parathyroid hormone receptor gene (PTHR1).	Disturbances in Dental Development	\N
2ed294fd-e776-4344-8fa8-9c0d52b6e4ef	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the most likely consequence if a developing permanent incisor tooth bud is physically displaced by a traumatic blow to the overlying primary tooth?	{"The permanent tooth will immediately ankylose.","The permanent root will form at an angle to the crown, a condition called dilaceration.","The permanent tooth will resorb completely.","The primary tooth will erupt into the nasal cavity."}	1	Trauma can displace the developing crown of a permanent tooth. If root formation continues, the root will form at an angle to the displaced crown, creating a distortion known as dilaceration.	Traumatic Displacement of Teeth	\N
81dd693a-42d6-401b-b615-6666155f35a8	orthodontics	BDS-I	Studies evaluating the heritability of malocclusion (using twins) indicate that:	{"Skeletal characteristics have high heritability, but dental (occlusal) characteristics have lower heritability.","Dental characteristics are 100% determined by genetics.","Environmental factors play almost no role in jaw size.","Skeletal characteristics have low heritability compared to soft tissue."}	0	Heritability of craniofacial (skeletal) characteristics is relatively high and increases with age. However, heritability of dental (occlusal) characteristics like crowding and overjet is lower, indicating a stronger environmental influence.	Genetic Influences	\N
845b9c3a-3be5-4f5d-b106-ed1e5f5312b6	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which clinical sign is an early indicator that a patient might have an impaired nasal airway leading to an altered respiratory pattern (mouth breathing)?	{"Bimaxillary retrusion","High, V-shaped palatal vault and anterior open bite","Spacing in the mandibular anterior segment","Delayed eruption of all permanent teeth"}	1	Mouth breathing causes the mandible to drop and the tongue to lower, altering the muscular equilibrium. This typically leads to a narrow, high V-shaped palatal vault, posterior crossbite, and an anterior open bite.	Respiratory Pattern	\N
488e3d43-6d60-4cb1-9756-8bcbacc3732e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	According to Hupp, "3D Virtual computerized surgical planning" is discussed specifically for:	{"Root canal treatment","Correction of dentofacial deformities",Blepharoplasty,"Simple extractions"}	1	3D virtual planning is a new focus for orthognathic surgery. [Preface]	Orthognathic	\N
5e1d4e77-78e9-4cdc-b240-8e1b3f968f2e	orthodontics	BDS-I	A mother expresses concern that her 8-year-old child's erupting maxillary lateral incisors have gaps between them and the central incisors. What is the most appropriate response?	{"This is abnormal and requires immediate braces to close the space.","This is the normal \\"ugly duckling\\" stage; the spaces will likely close when the permanent canines erupt.","The child definitely has congenitally missing canines.","The child needs a frenectomy immediately to prevent a permanent diastema."}	1	Spacing in the maxillary anterior region during the mixed dentition (the "ugly duckling" stage) is normal. The spaces tend to close spontaneously as the permanent canines erupt and push the incisors together mesially.	Space Relationships	\N
a6c5e638-ab72-4117-a312-2d56e50c1f3e	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Scammon's curves, which tissue type grows rapidly in early childhood, peaks at nearly 200% of adult size before puberty, and then shrinks?	{Neural,Maxillary,Lymphoid,Genital}	2	Lymphoid tissues proliferate far beyond the adult amount in late childhood, reaching a peak just before adolescence, and then undergo involution (shrinkage) as genital tissue growth accelerates.	Growth Patterns	\N
b1e90c3d-fd58-4b34-a6b0-61a0d0b8c291	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating skeletal growth, "hyperplasia" refers to:	{"An increase in the size of individual cells.","An increase in the number of cells.","The secretion of extracellular material.","The calcification of cartilage."}	1	Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of cells. Hypertrophy is an increase in the size of individual cells.	The Nature of Skeletal Growth	\N
3753efd3-bf95-4a40-950f-0602defce219	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following bones does NOT grow by intramembranous ossification?	{"Parietal bone",Maxilla,"Frontal bone","Ethmoid bone"}	3	The ethmoid, sphenoid, and basioccipital bones form the cranial base and undergo endochondral ossification (replacing a cartilaginous model). The cranial vault (parietal/frontal) and maxilla grow via intramembranous ossification.	Cranial Base Growth	\N
7346437f-6bc9-4bc9-ada5-28dda2fd8808	orthodontics	BDS-I	During embryonic development, what structure gives rise to the conductive ossicles of the middle ear (malleus and incus)?	{"The frontonasal process","Meckel's cartilage","The primary palate","The palatal shelves"}	1	Remnants of Meckel's cartilage (the cartilage of the first pharyngeal arch) are transformed into a portion of two of the small bones that form the conductive ossicles of the middle ear.	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
89a6d8d1-65e4-4ba2-990d-acdf902df662	oral_path	BDS-I	"Anchovy sauce"colour logic:	{Yellow,Chocolate/Brown,White,Red}	1	Blood mixed with necrotic liver tissue gives this chocolate colour.	Tropical Surgery	\N
74659148-174a-46b6-b987-4b12653cfc0e	orthodontics	BDS-I	In orthodontic behavioral management, a child observing an older sibling happily receiving dental treatment and subsequently behaving well themselves is an example of:	{"Classical conditioning","Operant conditioning","Observational learning (modeling)",Extinction}	2	Observational learning is an important tool; a young child observing an older sibling being rewarded for good behavior is likely to imitate that behavior, acting as a model.	Learning and the Development of Behavior	\N
37a16c83-4511-4968-a888-b5af095f73bc	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Erikson, the primary psychosocial crisis of adolescence (ages 12-17) is:	{"Basic Trust vs Mistrust","Industry vs Inferiority","Identity vs Role Confusion","Intimacy vs Isolation"}	2	Adolescence is the period of "Development of Personal Identity." Establishing one's own identity requires a partial withdrawal from the family and increasing reliance on the peer group, heavily influencing a patient's motivation for orthodontic treatment.	Stages of Emotional Development	\N
b7f4efbd-f243-4330-af6e-7e6afbf050ec	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient is classified as having a Class II malocclusion based on Angle's classification. What defines a Class II molar relationship?	{"The lower molar is positioned mesially relative to the upper molar.","The lower molar is positioned distally relative to the upper molar.","The mesiobuccal cusp of the upper molar occludes in the buccal groove of the lower molar.","The upper incisors are significantly flared."}	1	In an Angle Class II malocclusion, the lower molar is distally positioned relative to the upper molar (the mesiobuccal cusp of the upper first molar occludes anterior to the buccal groove of the lower first molar).	Orthodontic Classification	\N
9f93b88a-d4e5-4bea-8d21-6bd54900aa9c	orthodontics	BDS-I	What does the term "reverse overjet" refer to?	{"Excessive horizontal overlap of the maxillary incisors over the mandibular incisors","Anterior crossbite where the mandibular incisors are ahead of the maxillary incisors","Excessive vertical overlap of the incisors","An open bite measuring greater than 5 mm"}	1	Reverse overjet (anterior crossbite) occurs when the lower incisors are positioned horizontally ahead of the upper incisors.	Demand for Treatment	\N
c29e949c-9a56-44a3-81c3-0ee9b9d7a62a	orthodontics	BDS-I	An 8-year-old patient presents with a 2.5 mm midline diastema. What is the most statistically appropriate intervention at this exact time?	{"Immediate surgical frenectomy","Observation, as it will likely close when the permanent canines erupt.","Placement of a heavy elastic band around the two central incisors.","Extraction of the primary canines."}	1	A maxillary midline diastema is relatively common during the mixed dentition and often disappears or decreases in width as the permanent canines erupt. Intervention is usually not recommended unless it exceeds 2 mm after canine eruption.	Demand for Treatment	\N
fcff31ba-20b3-40dc-b4ff-c3e573b35ab3	orthodontics	BDS-I	In an adult, what is the normal range of maximum jaw opening measured between the incisal edges?	{"15-20 mm","30-35 mm","50-60 mm","80-90 mm"}	2	A normal maximum opening for an adult is approximately 50 mm. Significant restriction may indicate TMJ or muscle dysfunction.	Clinical Evaluation	\N
28c7e181-b948-4c3c-b90f-e6c0c3aa36ef	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient presents with a pronounced "gummy smile" (excessive gingival display). Which underlying skeletal pattern is most frequently associated with this finding?	{"Short anterior lower face height","Long anterior lower face height (vertical maxillary excess)","Severe mandibular prognathism","Skeletal Class III pattern"}	1	Excessive display of the maxillary gingiva is typically associated with a long face pattern, often due to vertical maxillary excess (excessive downward growth of the maxilla).	Facial Proportions: Vertical	\N
6aa0797c-f483-456c-a863-f897066efe4c	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating the dental arches, the term "curve of Spee" refers to:	{"The transverse curvature of the maxillary arch","The anteroposterior curvature of the occlusal plane","The angle of the maxillary incisors relative to the cranial base","The lateral shift of the mandible on closure"}	1	The curve of Spee is the anteroposterior curvature of the occlusal plane, naturally found as a slight upward curve from the premolars to the molars.	Alignment and Occlusion	\N
922856b5-cf80-4364-9a7b-748c88d16018	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which diagnostic record is absolutely essential to precisely determine the angulation of the incisors relative to the supporting jaw bones?	{"Panoramic radiograph","Study casts","Lateral cephalometric radiograph","Frontal photograph"}	2	A lateral cephalometric radiograph allows direct measurement of the bony skeletal dimensions and the angulation of the incisors relative to the jaw bones and cranial base.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
7b4f0984-6233-4e6a-8e22-806d32eb4118	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "cephalocaudal gradient of growth" explains why:	{"The maxilla grows more than the mandible postnatally.","The mandible, being further from the brain, grows more and later than the maxilla.","The arms grow faster than the legs in a teenager.","The brain continues to grow rapidly until age 20."}	1	The cephalocaudal gradient means growth is concentrated towards the head early on, shifting towards the lower limbs later. Therefore, structures further from the brain (like the mandible) tend to grow more and later than those closer (like the maxilla).	Growth: Pattern, Variability, and Timing	\N
72352516-a87d-46b1-888b-5b7ef43073ca	orthodontics	BDS-I	In cephalometrics, the "mandibular plane" can be constructed by drawing a line through which two landmarks?	{"Gonion and Menton","Sella and Nasion","Porion and Orbitale","Anterior Nasal Spine and Posterior Nasal Spine"}	0	The mandibular plane is classically drawn connecting Gonion (the lowest, most posterior point on the angle) and Menton (the lowest point on the symphysis) or constructed tangent to the lower border.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
dd816813-06c8-4af6-8afa-4400051778f8	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient has a "Class II division 2" malocclusion. What classic dental feature distinguishes this from a Class II division 1?	{"A severe anterior open bite.","Retroclined (upright) maxillary central incisors with a deep overbite.","Proclined (flared) maxillary central incisors with a large overjet.","A complete posterior crossbite."}	1	Class II division 2 is characterized by a Class II molar relationship combined with retroclined (upright or backward-tipping) maxillary central incisors and a deep overbite, unlike the flared incisors of division 1.	Orthodontic Classification	\N
055a59ee-8164-46f0-b738-e01b82ea34a4	orthodontics	BDS-I	The eruption of permanent teeth is a complex process. What happens to the rate of eruption of a permanent premolar immediately after it penetrates the gingiva and emerges into the mouth?	{"It slows down significantly.","It stops entirely until the opposing tooth erupts.","It experiences a rapid spurt of eruption until it reaches the occlusal level.","It erupts apically."}	2	Once a tooth emerges into the mouth, it erupts rapidly until it approaches the occlusal level and is subjected to the forces of mastication (the post-emergent spurt).	Eruption of the Permanent Teeth	\N
3f541684-bb15-4759-b5aa-28c8b6a07242	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which soft tissue matrix is considered the primary determinant for the downward and forward growth of the maxilla according to the Functional Matrix Theory?	{"The functioning of the TMJ","The enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities","The action of the masseter muscles","The tension from the periodontal ligament"}	1	Moss theorized that the major determinant of the growth of the maxilla and mandible is the enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities, which grow in response to functional needs.	Theories of Growth Control	\N
2fef09c6-1d03-45bd-8b72-22b335ee8811	orthodontics	BDS-I	An 11-year-old girl is in stage 2 of the adolescent growth spurt, experiencing peak height velocity. What visible dental development stage typically coincides with this?	{"Eruption of the permanent first molars.","Eruption of the permanent second molars.","Completion of the roots of the primary dentition.","Shedding of the primary central incisors."}	1	The eruption of the second molars often coincides with the peak of the adolescent growth spurt, typically around age 11-12 in girls.	Eruption Sequence and Timing	\N
b7e829c1-5018-4cfd-8533-7dd2d785829d	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient is missing their maxillary lateral incisors congenitally. This is an example of a disturbance during which stage of dental development?	{"Initiation and proliferation",Histodifferentiation,Apposition,Calcification}	0	Congenitally missing teeth (hypodontia) represent a failure during the initial stages of dental development (initiation and proliferation of the dental lamina).	Disturbances of Dental Development	\N
b551f7b1-f328-4fd0-b87a-690946b2b232	orthodontics	BDS-I	What cephalometric line is used to evaluate the "cant of the occlusal plane"?	{"The line connecting Sella and Nasion","The line through the overlapping cusps of the first premolars and first molars","The line connecting Porion and Orbitale","The line tangent to the lower border of the mandible"}	1	The occlusal plane is typically drawn through the overlapping cusps of the first premolars and first molars on a lateral cephalogram.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
02144774-fbfe-45ba-88c2-0a7a8af43481	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 4-year-old sucks their thumb forcefully. Which intrinsic muscle force most directly contributes to the narrowing of the maxillary arch in this habit?	{"Increased pressure from the tongue against the palate","Increased pressure from the buccinator muscles (cheeks)","Decreased pressure from the orbicularis oris","Hyperactivity of the masseter muscles"}	1	Thumb sucking lowers the tongue, removing its resting pressure from the palate, while the suction increases the pressure from the cheek muscles (buccinators), which physically narrows the maxillary arch.	Sucking and Other Habits	\N
cef3ef68-9095-4056-9b41-ed42118027f3	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the "Rule of Fifths" for facial proportions, the distance between the medial and lateral canthus (width of the eye) should be equal to:	{"The width of the mouth","The intercanthal distance","The width of the face at the gonial angles","Half the interpupillary distance"}	1	The face can be divided into bilateral fifths. The width of one eye (from medial to lateral canthus) should equal the distance between the eyes (intercanthal distance), defining the medial and central fifths.	Facial Proportions: Macro-Esthetics	\N
d15c0aaa-2325-4458-9f07-624119d711b1	orthodontics	BDS-I	In a patient with a severe skeletal Class III malocclusion (prognathic mandible), what dental compensation is most frequently observed naturally?	{"Proclined maxillary incisors and retroclined mandibular incisors","Retroclined maxillary incisors and proclined mandibular incisors","Severe spacing in both arches","Posterior crossbite on one side only"}	0	Nature attempts to compensate for a skeletal discrepancy by tipping the teeth. In a skeletal Class III, the lower incisors tip backward (retrocline) and the upper incisors tip forward (procline) to try to maintain contact.	Interaction Between Jaw Rotation and Tooth Eruption	\N
7b2f3185-78a6-46a2-bba6-b1f33ebfbffe	oral_surgery	BDS-I	The "Chief Complaint" should be recorded in the patient record:	{"Using ICD-10 codes","Verbatim (in the patient's own words)","Only if it involves pain","Using legal terminology"}	1	Recording the complaint verbatim establishes priorities and reliability. [Box 1-1]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
943b7471-3135-41a6-b20d-2313d6ce2c48	orthodontics	BDS-I	During embryonic development, what triggers the differentiation of the neural crest cells that migrate to form the facial structures?	{"Signals from the developing heart","Signals from the underlying mesoderm and neural tube","Mechanical pressure from the amniotic fluid","Maternal hormones"}	1	Neural crest cells originate near the neural tube and their differentiation and migration are complex processes guided by genetic signals (like Hox genes) and interactions with surrounding tissues.	Disturbances in Embryologic Development	\N
0f1d5c97-4c62-4bbc-a378-54a7b36433e8	orthodontics	BDS-I	A pediatric dentist places a child in a "time-out" by stopping a cartoon they are watching because they kicked the assistant. This is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Omission,"Classical conditioning"}	2	Omission (time-out) involves the removal of a pleasant stimulus (the cartoon) after a particular response (kicking) to decrease the likelihood of that behavior occurring again.	Learning and Behavior	\N
4a0156ee-d2f9-4bbe-86e1-5b6d5451b3f4	orthodontics	BDS-I	In evaluating a patient's profile, the "facial convexity" is determined by looking at the angle formed by which three points?	{"Nasion, Subnasale, Pogonion","Sella, Nasion, Point A","Porion, Orbitale, Pogonion","Gonion, Gnathion, Menton"}	0	The angle of facial convexity is typically assessed by looking at the profile curve from the bridge of the nose (Nasion) to the base of the upper lip (Subnasale) to the chin (Pogonion).	Facial Proportions: Profile	\N
6dbba80e-c561-4381-9360-10006e5f9afd	orthodontics	BDS-I	When performing a tooth size analysis, what condition might artificially inflate the maxillary sum, making it seem like the patient has a maxillary tooth size excess?	{"Congenitally missing lateral incisors","Microdontia (peg-shaped lateral incisors)","A retained primary second molar with no permanent successor","Severe interproximal attrition"}	2	Primary molars are larger mesiodistally than the premolars that replace them. Including a retained primary molar in the sum will falsely inflate the arch's tooth size sum.	Cast Analysis: Tooth Size	\N
283efaf6-dce1-4d66-80d0-2f3b16613116	orthodontics	BDS-I	A cephalogram reveals a very steep mandibular plane angle. What vertical facial characteristic is this strongly correlated with?	{"Short anterior lower face height and deep bite","Long anterior lower face height and tendency toward open bite","A perfectly horizontal smile arc","Severe maxillary retrognathia"}	1	A steep mandibular plane angle usually means the mandible has grown primarily in a downward direction (backward rotation), correlating with a long lower face and a tendency toward an anterior open bite.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
6d0de0bd-2d22-4348-ac83-889b2a54061e	orthodontics	BDS-I	What happens to the rate of facial growth during the period known as the "juvenile pause" (just before the adolescent growth spurt)?	{"It accelerates rapidly.","It becomes completely static.","It decelerates, reaching a low plateau.","It reverses direction."}	2	The rate of overall physical and facial growth declines from the rapid pace of infancy, stabilizing at a lower, steady level (the juvenile pause) before accelerating again during puberty.	Physical Development in Late Childhood	\N
27e65c5a-ed7e-4992-bb09-62e7f2259426	orthodontics	BDS-I	An adult patient reports their lower incisors have become noticeably more crowded over the last 10 years. According to long-term studies like those by Behrents, what is the most likely primary cause?	{"Eruption of the third molars at age 25.","Continued slow, subtle skeletal growth and rotation of the jaws throughout adulthood.","Loss of bone due to gingivitis.","Lack of chewing tough foods."}	1	Long-term cephalometric studies revealed that craniofacial growth continues at a slow rate throughout adult life, often involving a subtle forward rotation of the mandible that can crowd the lower incisors against the upper arch.	Facial Growth in Adults	\N
8d1517e2-353c-47c0-a3bf-d1d311fc99ad	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the first stage of primary tooth eruption (pre-emergent), the tooth follicle moves through bone. This movement is absolutely dependent on:	{"The physical pushing force of the elongating root.","Resorption of bone overlying the crown by osteoclasts.","Pressure from the tongue.","The calcification of the enamel."}	1	Eruptive movement requires the resorption of bone and primary tooth roots overlying the crown. If this resorption fails (as in cleidocranial dysplasia), the tooth will not erupt, regardless of root growth.	Pre-emergent Eruption	\N
6b96beff-8342-4f03-972a-5d318a9e0748	orthodontics	BDS-I	When diagnosing an anterior crossbite, it is essential to gently guide the patient's mandible into centric relation (CR). If the incisors touch edge-to-edge in CR, but the patient slides their jaw forward into a full crossbite in MI, this is termed a:	{"True skeletal Class III","Pseudo-Class III (Functional shift)","Skeletal Class II division 1","Posterior crossbite"}	1	A functional shift forward to avoid an incisor interference is called a pseudo-Class III. The true skeletal discrepancy is much less severe than it appears in maximum intercuspation.	Jaw and Occlusal Function	\N
f0c1df5a-c73a-4ad5-a5a2-ee016b37586d	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which radiographic landmark is defined as the lowest, most anterior point on the contour of the mandibular symphysis?	{Pogonion,Menton,Gnathion,Gonion}	1	Menton is the lowest point on the symphysis. Pogonion is the most anterior point. Gnathion is midway between Pogonion and Menton.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
1025fced-4704-4ba3-ba4e-7ff4f26de6b5	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient has a "deep bite." This specifically means:	{"The upper incisors are flared horizontally.","The posterior teeth do not touch.","There is excessive vertical overlap of the upper incisors over the lower incisors.","The patient breathes primarily through their mouth."}	2	Overbite is the vertical overlap of the incisors. A "deep bite" means this vertical overlap is excessive.	The Usual Orthodontic Problems	\N
9f447a51-1b39-404c-adb3-7df915943f14	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the IOTN, an overjet of 5 mm with competent lips is classified as:	{"Grade 1 (No Need)","Grade 2 (Mild/Little Need)","Grade 3 (Moderate/Borderline Need)","Grade 4 (Severe Need)"}	1	Grade 2.a is increased overjet greater than 3.5 mm but less than or equal to 6 mm with competent lips. (Incompetent lips would make it Grade 3.a).	Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN)	\N
1db4d336-32ae-4409-9761-018cbc1cbe62	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "lip catch" is a clinical sign where the lower lip rests behind the maxillary incisors. This habit commonly exacerbates which malocclusion?	{"Class III","Class I crowded","Class II division 1 (excessive overjet)","Anterior open bite"}	2	In severe Class II division 1 (large overjet), the lower lip often rests behind the upper incisors, pushing them further forward and the lower incisors backward, exacerbating the overjet.	Sucking and Other Habits	\N
3c0fcbfc-1bc5-4b9a-99fb-1ac9adae351c	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following is true regarding the growth of the nasal septum?	{"It ceases entirely at birth.","It acts as a major pacemaker pulling the maxilla forward, according to the cartilage theory.","It grows exclusively via intramembranous ossification.","It has no influence whatsoever on facial growth."}	1	The cartilage-directed theory posited that the cartilaginous nasal septum serves as a pacemaker, pushing or pulling the maxilla downward and forward as it grows. (Though the functional matrix theory challenges this as the sole cause).	Cartilage as a Determinant	\N
bd6dd6b2-bd08-4bb3-bad3-51af19aea25f	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the Soft Tissue Paradigm, when planning an orthodontic case, what should the clinician evaluate first?	{"The molar classification","The cephalometric numbers","The facial soft tissue proportions and display","The exact dimensions of the leeway space"}	2	The soft tissue paradigm dictates that the clinician first establishes the ideal soft tissue relationships and adaptations (the primary goal), and then determines how to move the teeth and jaws to achieve this.	Modern Treatment Goals	\N
75dd06e3-3fe1-4a92-bc7e-6e9e45fc603b	orthodontics	BDS-I	The primary mechanism for the postnatal growth in size of the cranial base is:	{"Intramembranous ossification at the fontanelles","Endochondral ossification at the synchondroses","Surface apposition on the superior surface","Expansion of the sphenoid sinus"}	1	Postnatal growth of the cranial base occurs primarily by endochondral ossification at the bands of cartilage called synchondroses (e.g., spheno-occipital synchondrosis).	Cranial Base Growth	\N
9b920b2b-e084-4c45-a100-9a660e2403f9	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient has missing maxillary lateral incisors. To create an ideal esthetic result using the golden proportion, the restorative dentist must ensure the canine substituting for the lateral appears to be what fraction of the central incisor's width?	{0.38,0.5,0.62,1}	2	To look aesthetically pleasing according to the golden proportion, the tooth occupying the lateral incisor space (even if it's a reshaped canine) should have an apparent frontal width equal to 62% of the central incisor.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
20afb5bc-a7f6-4ae4-824f-3c5476f7f616	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the main clinical advantage of taking a panoramic radiograph over a full mouth series (FMX) of periapicals for an initial orthodontic evaluation?	{"Higher radiation dose for better contrast","Superior resolution for detecting tiny carious lesions","A broader view of the jaws, TMJ, and impacted teeth at a much lower radiation dose","The ability to measure the curve of Spee accurately"}	2	The panoramic image yields a broader view of the entire maxillofacial region (including TMJs and areas where teeth might be impacted) and the radiation exposure is much lower than an FMX.	Diagnostic Records	\N
1ab7baa5-e8d2-4e8b-a673-659b87ad57d4	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the transition to the permanent dentition, arch length (measured from the midline to the mesial of the first molar) typically:	{"Increases significantly","Remains completely static",Decreases,"Doubles in size"}	2	Arch length decreases during the transition from the mixed to the permanent dentition, primarily due to the mesial drift of the permanent first molars into the leeway space.	Arch Dimensions	\N
5d1c0e53-ff92-4ad2-a05a-a19b4e43720a	public_health	BDS-I	The "PMA Index" (Schour and Massler) is primarily used to assess:	{Malocclusion,"Gingivitis in children","Oral cancer",Fluorosis}	1	PMA stands for Papillary, Marginal, and Attached gingiva, often used in epidemiological studies of gingivitis.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
4fa8b06a-8fe4-4452-b9bb-1b4d6647bd1d	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which cephalometric measurement specifically evaluates the vertical facial proportion (the relative height of the lower face)?	{"The SNA angle","The SNB angle","The Mandibular Plane Angle (SN-MP)","The Interincisal angle"}	2	The mandibular plane angle evaluates the vertical dimension. A high angle indicates a long lower face, while a low angle indicates a short lower face.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
bcfc8484-335e-437e-ac42-23195103cd3a	orthodontics	BDS-I	A "mesial step" in the primary dentition is most likely to transition into which permanent molar relationship?	{"Class I","Class II","Class III","It will not change"}	0	A mesial step in the primary dentition (the primary equivalent of an Angle Class I) normally transitions directly into a Class I permanent molar relationship.	Space Relationships	\N
cb6a44c9-b226-408c-83df-338130c551c4	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the hierarchy of clinical evidence, which of the following study designs provides the highest quality of evidence for orthodontic treatment outcomes?	{"Retrospective study based on treatment response","Randomized clinical trials","Case reports","Meta-analysis of multiple randomized trials"}	3	In the evidence hierarchy, a meta-analysis of multiple randomized clinical trials represents the absolute peak of clinical effectiveness data, minimizing bias.	Evidence-Based Treatment	\N
7a175ef3-15a2-4dfa-8120-0f6dcbb96d07	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on the NHANES III survey, which ethnic group exhibits the highest prevalence of severe Class II and severe Class III problems in the United States?	{"Non-Hispanic Whites","Non-Hispanic Blacks",Hispanics,"Asian Americans"}	2	Severe Class II and severe Class III problems are noted to be more prevalent in the Hispanic population than in the white or black groups according to NHANES III.	Epidemiology	\N
332d2438-8272-41a1-ac0d-3842691d029f	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following best explains the evolutionary trend contributing to the high prevalence of malocclusion in modern humans?	{"Rapid evolutionary increase in tooth size.","Decrease in jaw size due to a softer diet outstripping the evolutionary decrease in tooth size/number.","A genetic mutation specific to agricultural societies.","Premature loss of primary dentition due to modern refined sugars."}	1	The transition to softer diets led to disuse atrophy and a reduction in jaw size that happened faster than the evolutionary reduction in tooth size, causing crowding.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
65b5f92b-cbe0-4869-8461-5a1a99464293	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Scammon's curves of growth, which tissue type undergoes significant involution (shrinkage) just as the genital tissues begin their rapid acceleration at puberty?	{Neural,Maxillary,Lymphoid,"General body"}	2	Lymphoid tissues proliferate to nearly 200% of adult size by late childhood and then undergo involution (shrinkage) at the same time genital tissue growth accelerates.	Growth Patterns	\N
68d5d78f-4875-423d-9ded-fda5c4cfd223	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following structures develops postnatally entirely via intramembranous ossification without any cartilaginous precursor?	{"Mandibular condyle",Maxilla,"Spheno-occipital synchondrosis","Ethmoid bone"}	1	The maxilla develops postnatally entirely by intramembranous ossification. The cranial base and mandibular condyle involve endochondral ossification.	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
d33497d7-defb-4fd8-ab6e-b4120568b02a	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Björk's implant studies, "internal rotation" of the mandible is defined as the rotation of the:	{"Mandibular plane relative to the cranial base.","Mandibular core relative to the cranial base.","Mandibular plane relative to the mandibular core.","Palatal plane relative to the cranial base."}	1	Internal rotation is defined as the rotation of the mandibular core relative to the cranial base, often masked by external angular remodeling of the lower border.	Jaw Rotation	\N
6613dccd-83c4-493d-9336-7a5b56ae8d40	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child who becomes extremely anxious and cries at the mere sight of a dentist in a white coat, due to a previous painful injection, is demonstrating:	{"Operant conditioning","Negative reinforcement","Classical conditioning","Observational learning"}	2	Classical (Pavlovian) conditioning occurs when a neutral stimulus (white coat) becomes associated with an unconditioned stimulus (pain), eliciting a conditioned response (fear).	Behavioral Development	\N
31069908-6994-466b-bcd6-ac75ddef765f	orthodontics	BDS-I	In operant conditioning, allowing a cooperative child to choose a toy from a prize bin at the end of a successful appointment is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Omission,Extinction}	0	Positive reinforcement involves presenting a pleasant stimulus following a desired behavior, increasing the likelihood that the behavior will occur again.	Learning and Behavior	\N
10948717-0328-4766-b54c-451446f8bb56	oral_path	BDS-I	"Jefferson" burst logic:	{"C2 Axis","C1 Atlas",C7,L1}	1	Caused by axial loading on the C1 ring.	Trauma	\N
7ac7bf91-d071-44b6-8acc-4520b021f412	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Piaget, a 4-year-old child who believes the dental chair "is tired and needs to rest" is displaying a cognitive characteristic known as:	{Conservation,Egocentrism,Animism,"Formal operations"}	2	Animism, common in the preoperational stage, involves investing inanimate objects with life and lifelike qualities.	Cognitive Development	\N
a010b509-6e34-4b7a-9587-7df4ed4ffd42	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Erikson's stages of psychosocial development, the primary crisis faced by adolescents (ages 12-17) seeking orthodontic treatment to improve their peer acceptance is:	{"Basic Trust vs. Mistrust","Initiative vs. Guilt","Industry vs. Inferiority","Identity vs. Role Confusion"}	3	Adolescents are in the stage of developing personal identity, withdrawing from family, and relying on peer groups, which heavily influences their motivation for treatment.	Emotional Development	\N
12a23269-4f0d-4ddc-94f2-2fc65d7bc3eb	orthodontics	BDS-I	A cleft of the primary palate (cleft lip) occurs due to the failure of fusion between the median/lateral nasal processes and the maxillary prominence. During which embryonic timeframe does this normally occur?	{"Days 17-20","Days 28-38","Days 42-55","Days 60-75"}	1	Formation and fusion of the primary palate (associated with cleft lip) occurs during the sixth week of embryonic development, specifically between days 28-38.	Embryologic Development	\N
ba98122a-9f04-45ca-b61c-0c18f5e71e1d	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which teratogen is classically associated with central midface deficiency?	{Thalidomide,"13-cis Retinoic acid (Accutane)","Ethyl alcohol","Vitamin D excess"}	2	Ethyl alcohol exposure in utero can lead to Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, which is characterized by central midface deficiency and other facial anomalies.	Etiology: Specific Causes	\N
daf4c24e-3dc3-43fd-ba3b-43f625f24a8b	orthodontics	BDS-I	In Cleidocranial Dysplasia, the permanent teeth fail to erupt because:	{"The roots do not form.","The periodontal ligament is absent.","There is a genetic defect preventing bone resorption in the eruption path.","The primary teeth undergo ankylosis."}	2	In cleidocranial dysplasia, succedaneous teeth have eruptive potential, but the overlying bone is not resorbed due to a systemic defect, blocking eruption.	Disturbances in Dental Development	\N
8845483d-6235-4760-8695-3f28200f57f1	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child suffers a traumatic blow to a primary incisor, driving it into the developing permanent tooth bud. The root of the permanent tooth continues to form at a sharp angle to the crown. This condition is called:	{Ankylosis,Dilaceration,Hypercementosis,Concrescence}	1	Trauma can displace the developing crown. If root formation continues, it forms at an angle to the displaced crown, causing a severe distortion known as dilaceration.	Traumatic Displacement	\N
552936fe-c8ac-4f31-ace2-4785892b4539	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the equilibrium theory of tooth position, why do heavy masticatory forces NOT cause permanent tooth movement?	{"The tongue provides an equal and opposite heavy force.","The forces are of extremely short duration.","The periodontal ligament paralyzes osteoclasts.","Teeth are ankylosed during function."}	1	Equilibrium is dictated by duration, not magnitude. Mastication forces are heavy but last only minutes per day, which is insufficient to trigger bone remodeling for tooth movement.	Equilibrium Considerations	\N
7257eb2d-e3cf-455e-ad6a-0997d64b06e4	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following is the most accurate contemporary view regarding "tongue thrust" swallowing and anterior open bite?	{"Tongue thrust is the primary etiologic factor causing the open bite.","Tongue thrust is a physiologic adaptation to seal the oral cavity due to a pre-existing open bite.","Tongue thrust causes the mandible to rotate forward.","Tongue thrust primarily causes posterior crossbites."}	1	Modern understanding views the tongue thrust swallow primarily as an adaptation to an existing open bite (to create a seal), rather than the root cause of the open bite.	Sucking and Habits	\N
da6826c3-ca0c-47ea-93a7-94fac9522492	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child with chronic nasal obstruction (mouth breathing) typically exhibits an altered posture that leads to "adenoid facies." Which skeletal change is characteristic?	{"Forward rotation of the mandible","Downward and backward rotation of the mandible","Expansion of the maxillary arch","Severe deep bite"}	1	To breathe through the mouth, the mandible drops and rotates downward and backward, leading to increased lower face height and an anterior open bite tendency.	Respiratory Pattern	\N
25606fbb-fe6b-4c17-9cae-3e242568a23d	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "neonatal line" is a line of developmental arrest found in enamel. Which permanent tooth typically exhibits a neonatal line?	{"Maxillary central incisor","Mandibular lateral incisor","Maxillary first molar","Mandibular second premolar"}	2	The neonatal line is found in all primary teeth and the permanent first molars, because these teeth begin calcifying before the physiologic shock of birth.	Early Development	\N
5aa51aec-0708-4a73-a86b-28cee1151544	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the primary dentition, where is the "primate space" located in the mandibular arch?	{"Between the central and lateral incisors","Between the lateral incisor and canine","Between the canine and first primary molar","Between the first and second primary molars"}	2	In the mandibular arch, the primate space is located between the canine and the first primary molar. In the maxilla, it is between the lateral incisor and canine.	Primary Dentition	\N
5a991cf7-fd24-4410-87b0-12d10463cca9	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "leeway space" averages approximately how many millimeters per side in the mandibular arch?	{"0.5 mm","1.5 mm","2.5 mm","4.0 mm"}	2	Because primary molars are larger than the premolars that replace them, the leeway space averages about 2.5 mm per side in the mandible and 1.5 mm per side in the maxilla.	Mixed Dentition	\N
eb0c637c-3c67-43cb-a258-cad82ef63d15	orthodontics	BDS-I	A "flush terminal plane" in the primary dentition most commonly transitions into which permanent molar relationship, assuming normal differential mandibular growth?	{"Class II","Class I","Class III","End-to-end indefinitely"}	1	With normal differential forward growth of the mandible and mesial shift of lower molars into the leeway space, a flush terminal plane usually becomes a Class I relationship.	Space Relationships	\N
0d290c4d-6bb7-4d61-99d3-e6a04a6e1428	orthodontics	BDS-I	Regarding the timing of the adolescent growth spurt, which of the following is generally true?	{"Boys experience peak height velocity 2 years earlier than girls.","Girls experience peak height velocity ~2 years earlier than boys.","Both sexes peak at exactly the same age.","Menarche occurs precisely at peak height velocity."}	1	On average, the adolescent growth spurt and puberty occur nearly 2 years earlier in girls than in boys. Menarche occurs after the peak velocity has passed.	Later Stages of Development	\N
2c34a97d-9ad5-4a72-a18c-ce2548110b8a	orthodontics	BDS-I	At birth, the head and face constitute what percentage of the total body length?	{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.75}	1	At birth, the head and face represent about 30% of total body length, demonstrating the cephalocaudal gradient where early growth is concentrated in the head.	Cephalocaudal Gradient	\N
7ef0e319-8197-469c-9d83-5447439542ac	orthodontics	BDS-I	In what sequence is growth in the craniofacial complex typically completed?	{"Transverse, Anteroposterior, Vertical","Vertical, Anteroposterior, Transverse","Anteroposterior, Transverse, Vertical","Transverse, Vertical, Anteroposterior"}	0	Growth in width (transverse) completes first, followed by length (anteroposterior), and finally height (vertical), which continues longest.	Dimensional Changes	\N
fc22cb30-0e3c-40c1-ab2e-ca32cc5eaf1e	orthodontics	BDS-I	Late crowding of the mandibular incisors (in the late teens/early 20s) is most strongly correlated with:	{"Pressure from erupting third molars.","Continued late forward growth and rotation of the mandible.","Loss of arch width due to early extraction of primary canines.","Hypertrophy of the mentalis muscle."}	1	Late incisor crowding primarily results from the mandible continuing to grow forward slightly late in the teens, displacing the lower incisors lingually against the lips.	Maturational Changes	\N
6b9b2080-c364-44f3-beae-8d8e7798db70	orthodontics	BDS-I	As adults age, what happens to the size of the dental pulp chamber?	{"It expands due to internal resorption.","It remains exactly the same size.","It gradually becomes smaller due to the deposition of secondary dentin.","It calcifies completely by age 40."}	2	As time passes, additional secondary dentin is slowly deposited on the inside of the tooth, causing the pulp chamber to gradually shrink with age.	Aging Changes in Teeth	\N
6aaee220-629e-4005-8ede-39dede8bafa1	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the rule of fifths for facial proportions, the intercanthal distance (distance between the eyes) should ideally equal:	{"The width of the mouth","The width of the nasal alar base","The interpupillary distance","The width of the chin"}	1	The central fifth of the face is defined by the intercanthal distance, which should ideally equal the width of the nose at its base.	Macro-Esthetics	\N
e3dd1f46-343a-4734-96e1-e984c5c4cce9	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the primary purpose of conducting a Bolton Analysis on orthodontic casts?	{"To measure the exact overjet in millimeters.","To identify a mesiodistal tooth size discrepancy between the upper and lower arches.","To calculate the cant of the occlusal plane.","To assess the skeletal discrepancy between the maxilla and mandible."}	1	Bolton analysis compares the summed mesiodistal widths of the maxillary teeth to the mandibular teeth to identify size discrepancies that prevent ideal occlusion.	Cast Analysis	\N
9cd83ff3-b432-43db-9eae-adc3b5a8391e	orthodontics	BDS-I	On a lateral cephalogram, the anterior cranial base is typically represented by a line connecting which two landmarks?	{"Porion and Orbitale","Gonion and Menton","Sella and Nasion","ANS and PNS"}	2	The line connecting Sella (center of sella turcica) and Nasion (frontonasal suture) classically represents the anterior cranial base, used as a stable reference line.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
a7169610-0fa2-482b-9a21-95c2d7f376a8	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN), a reverse overjet greater than 3.5 mm with reported masticatory and speech difficulties falls into which grade?	{"Grade 2 (Mild Need)","Grade 3 (Moderate Need)","Grade 4 (Severe Need)","Grade 5 (Extreme Need)"}	3	This severe functional impairment combined with a large reverse overjet is classified as Grade 5m (Extreme/Need Treatment) in the IOTN.	Demand for Treatment	\N
78100a34-89be-499f-accb-ea395a45d576	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the IOTN, less extensive hypodontia requiring prerestorative orthodontics (e.g., one missing tooth per quadrant) is classified as:	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	2	Less extensive hypodontia requiring space management is classified as Grade 4h (Severe/Need Treatment).	Demand for Treatment	\N
ab0a31fa-3e5e-4e63-b774-e6f1baee273b	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the transition from the mixed to the permanent dentition, the arch length (midline to the mesial of the first molar) typically:	{"Increases significantly","Remains static",Decreases,Doubles}	2	Arch length decreases primarily due to the mesial drift of the permanent first molars into the leeway space as primary molars exfoliate.	Arch Dimensions	\N
a5538856-1f4b-41e1-92d5-4d3857861cfc	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 9-year-old presents with a 1.5 mm maxillary midline diastema. The permanent canines are unerupted. What is the most appropriate management?	{"Immediate surgical frenectomy","Braces to close the space","Observation, as it often closes when permanent canines erupt","Extraction of primary canines"}	2	A midline diastema ≤ 2 mm during the "ugly duckling" stage is normal and usually closes spontaneously as the permanent canines erupt and push the incisors mesially.	Mixed Dentition	\N
b6c2aa20-441a-4373-933f-d2a23edaa11d	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating jaw function, what is the normal range of maximum opening for an adult?	{"20-30 mm","35-40 mm","50-60 mm","75-85 mm"}	2	A normal maximum jaw opening for an adult is around 50 mm. Significant restriction may indicate TMJ or muscular dysfunction.	Clinical Evaluation	\N
ee3fa876-34df-467a-baea-8b60fbc4e6b3	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Bruit" heard during neck auscultation indicates:	{"Normal thyroid function","Carotid artery narrowing or turbulence","Laryngeal fracture","High oxygen levels"}	1	Bruits are sounds of turbulent blood flow in narrowed arteries. [Box 1-6]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
72550b93-e70a-4f90-98fe-484b4fe4c4a8	orthodontics	BDS-I	The anteroposterior curvature of the occlusal plane, which naturally curves upward from the premolars to the molars, is called the:	{"Curve of Wilson","Curve of Spee","Catenary curve","Mandibular plane"}	1	The curve of Spee is the natural anteroposterior curvature of the occlusal plane. A flat or slightly accentuated curve is normal.	Alignment and Occlusion	\N
d50870fa-a667-4c04-8090-6561ce1867fd	orthodontics	BDS-I	Primary Failure of Eruption (PFE), where posterior teeth fail to erupt despite a clear path, has been genetically linked to a mutation in which gene?	{MSX1,RUNX2,PTHR1,SHH}	2	PFE is linked to a mutation in the parathyroid hormone receptor gene (PTHR1), affecting the propulsive mechanism of eruption.	Eruption Disturbances	\N
7f6cd2d6-01d5-4c53-aacd-ff2ab9184ef3	orthodontics	BDS-I	In an Angle Class II malocclusion, the mandibular first molar is positioned:	{"Mesially relative to the maxillary first molar.","Distally relative to the maxillary first molar.","Directly end-to-end with the maxillary first molar.","In a complete buccal crossbite."}	1	Class II is defined by the lower molar being positioned distally relative to the upper molar (the MB cusp of the upper molar occludes anterior to the buccal groove of the lower).	Orthodontic Classification	\N
11e0b6a4-eb91-4db9-94c7-3eacad4f1708	orthodontics	BDS-I	A Class II, Division 2 malocclusion is clinically distinguished from Division 1 primarily by the presence of:	{"Flared (proclined) maxillary incisors with large overjet.","Retroclined (upright) maxillary central incisors and a deep overbite.","A unilateral posterior crossbite.","A severe anterior open bite."}	1	Division 2 is characterized by retroclined central incisors and a deep bite, whereas Division 1 features proclined, protruding incisors.	Orthodontic Classification	\N
70d8b205-671f-41c6-8e9f-8be934c9f09f	orthodontics	BDS-I	In skeletal growth, an increase in the number of cells is referred to as:	{Hypertrophy,Hyperplasia,"Interstitial expansion",Apposition}	1	Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of cells, whereas hypertrophy is an increase in the size of individual cells.	Cellular Growth	\N
317857ca-537e-4e14-9636-0a3c0804bb92	orthodontics	BDS-I	The clinical term "bimaxillary dentoalveolar protrusion" describes a condition where:	{"The maxilla is prognathic and the mandible is retrognathic.","Both upper and lower incisors protrude, causing prominent, everted lips.","The chin is excessively prominent.","There is a severe anterior open bite due to thumb sucking."}	1	Bimaxillary protrusion means the teeth in both jaws protrude, resulting in prominent and everted upper and lower lips.	Macro-Esthetics	\N
c61bbe9d-d085-457b-9b3a-1fd40cf651e9	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the "golden proportion" in micro-esthetics, the apparent frontal width of the lateral incisor should be what percentage of the central incisor?	{0.38,0.5,0.62,0.8}	2	The golden proportion suggests the apparent width ratio of Central : Lateral : Canine should be 1.0 : 0.62 : 0.38 when viewed from the front.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
f939ff0f-ed79-4455-b71c-1878013d3c66	orthodontics	BDS-I	For ideal gingival aesthetics, the gingival zenith of the maxillary lateral incisor should be located:	{"Distal to the long axis of the tooth.","Mesial to the long axis of the tooth.","Coincident with the vertical long axis of the tooth.","2 mm apical to the central incisor zenith."}	2	The zenith for central incisors and canines is slightly distal to the long axis, but for the lateral incisors, it should coincide with the vertical long axis.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
4354b968-38ef-48b8-b6bd-eb768e4ec659	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following radiographic techniques exposes the patient to the lowest effective dose of radiation?	{"FMX with D speed film and round cone","Cone Beam CT (large field)","Digital panoramic radiograph","Digital lateral cephalometric radiograph"}	3	A digital cephalometric radiograph exposes the patient to about 5.5 µSv, which is lower than a digital pan (16 µSv), CBCT, or FMX (388 µSv).	Diagnostic Records	\N
b73ba257-f3c2-416f-8892-6840045b202c	orthodontics	BDS-I	The mandibular plane angle, which helps evaluate vertical facial proportions, is classically constructed using which two landmarks?	{"Sella and Nasion","Porion and Orbitale","Gonion and Menton","ANS and PNS"}	2	The mandibular plane is drawn connecting Gonion (at the angle of the mandible) and Menton (at the symphysis).	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
f06f9b9c-37f8-4fbf-8ec8-8d6cf4ca2f27	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which theory posits that the primary determinant of jaw growth is the enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities?	{"Genetic Theory","Functional Matrix Theory","Cartilage-Directed Theory","Sutural Theory"}	1	Moss's Functional Matrix theory asserts that soft tissue spaces (nasal/oral cavities) grow in response to function, driving the passive growth of the bones.	Theories of Growth	\N
c24b0578-a3aa-4a2d-89a5-203a6304f6ec	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the Cartilage-Directed Theory of growth, which structure was incorrectly thought to act as the primary pacemaker pulling the maxilla forward?	{"Spheno-occipital synchondrosis","Mandibular condyle","Cartilaginous nasal septum","Midpalatal suture"}	2	The theory hypothesized that the nasal septum acted as a pacemaker pushing/pulling the maxilla forward, though experiments have shown it is not the sole determinant.	Theories of Growth	\N
cd88d6e3-1134-4639-9039-315b66afa1aa	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the significance of the spheno-occipital synchondrosis in craniofacial growth?	{"It fuses at birth.","It undergoes intramembranous ossification.","It is a major contributor to postnatal cranial base growth, fusing in the early 20s.","It exclusively controls the transverse width of the maxilla."}	2	It is a principal growth center in the cranial base via endochondral ossification and remains active until early adulthood.	Cranial Base Growth	\N
fdc90f02-7d34-471c-a1fb-3b2312ce19d9	orthodontics	BDS-I	During adolescence, what is the typical growth pattern of lip thickness?	{"It reaches its maximum during adolescence and then decreases.","It continuously increases throughout adulthood.","It decreases rapidly at puberty.","It remains perfectly static after age 6."}	0	Lip thickness reaches its maximum during adolescence and then decreases, leading to thinner lips and less incisor display in adulthood.	Soft Tissue Changes	\N
20c7fc2d-6365-4aa1-82c3-04cb722205b3	orthodontics	BDS-I	Alizarin is used in animal growth studies primarily because it:	{"Selectively destroys osteoclasts.","Is a vital stain that binds to calcium at sites of active bone mineralization.","Is a radioactive isotope that tracks collagen formation.","Stains cartilage blue to differentiate it from bone."}	1	Alizarin is a vital stain that binds to calcium, leaving a colored line in the bone being mineralized at the time of injection, allowing researchers to track appositional growth.	Methods for Studying Growth	\N
21031386-2455-42de-b79c-45d888103f84	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Tinnitus" is a symptom related to which organ system?	{Eyes,Ears,Nose,Throat}	1	Tinnitus is ringing in the ears. [Box 1-4]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
df877c7d-9fd3-4eca-afb2-3d928694d78d	orthodontics	BDS-I	The use of inert metallic implants to study facial growth longitudinally was pioneered by which researcher?	{"Edward Angle","B.F. Skinner","Melvin Moss","Arne Björk"}	3	Arne Björk utilized metallic implants in the jaws to provide stable landmarks, revolutionizing the understanding of jaw rotation during growth.	Implant Radiography	\N
e309b73e-1a18-4546-a34c-2828fd0a1a6f	orthodontics	BDS-I	Treacher Collins syndrome and Hemifacial Microsomia are primarily caused by disturbances in:	{"Germ layer formation (Day 17).","Origin, migration, and interaction of neural crest cells (Days 19-28).","Fusion of the palatal shelves (Days 42-55).","Fetal alcohol exposure in the third trimester."}	1	These syndromes result from issues with neural crest cell migration and interaction during the early embryonic period.	Embryologic Disturbances	\N
a078b402-3746-4f4a-a404-e52d334e86c7	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the most common supernumerary tooth, and where does it appear?	{"Maxillary fourth molar","Mandibular second premolar","Mesiodens, appearing in the maxillary midline","Maxillary lateral incisor"}	2	A mesiodens in the maxillary midline is the most common supernumerary tooth and frequently interferes with the eruption of central incisors.	Specific Causes of Malocclusion	\N
86129c1d-6efd-47e9-b087-6f318a1b58d4	orthodontics	BDS-I	After a permanent tooth emerges into the oral cavity, it experiences a "post-emergent spurt." When does this rapid eruption phase stop?	{"When the root apex completely closes.","When it reaches the occlusal level and meets the opposing tooth.","Exactly 6 months after emergence.","When the adolescent growth spurt ends."}	1	The tooth erupts rapidly until it approaches the occlusal level and is subjected to the forces of mastication, which halts the spurt.	Post-emergent Eruption	\N
f54fc482-6809-45e7-8462-13e7a4065f8b	orthodontics	BDS-I	The continuous, extremely slow eruption of teeth throughout adulthood to compensate for normal occlusal wear is termed:	{"Pre-emergent drift","Juvenile occlusal equilibrium","Adult occlusal equilibrium",Translocation}	2	During adult life, teeth continue to erupt at an extremely slow rate to compensate for wear, maintaining vertical dimension; this is the adult occlusal equilibrium.	Eruption Sequence	\N
dd01ce91-7162-455a-9199-deaf686babf3	medicine	BDS-I	"Atypical lymphocytes" on a blood film are suggestive of:	{TB,"Iron deficiency","Infectious Mononucleosis",Malaria}	2	Atypical lymphocytes are a laboratory feature of Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV).	Infectious Disease	\N
b49be357-76ea-4801-97da-589e910deed8	orthodontics	BDS-I	In female patients, the onset of menstruation (menarche) is an excellent clinical indicator that:	{"The adolescent growth spurt is just beginning.","The patient is exactly at peak height velocity.","The peak of the adolescent growth spurt has passed.","Facial growth will continue for another 5 years at maximum velocity."}	2	Menarche always follows the peak velocity of the adolescent growth spurt, indicating that growth is decelerating.	Timing of Puberty	\N
e4bc0f0c-629b-4bfa-b0e5-13dc4001a579	orthodontics	BDS-I	Patients with a "short anterior lower face height" typically exhibit which pattern of mandibular rotation?	{"Excessive backward internal rotation","Excessive forward internal rotation","No rotation","Severe external angular remodeling increasing the mandibular plane angle"}	1	Short face individuals have excessive forward rotation of the mandible, leading to a flatter mandibular plane, deep bite, and crowded incisors.	Jaw Rotation	\N
0be710d7-0ca1-4d59-9e0c-b25163fbb404	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following is a normal aging change in facial soft tissues from the 20s to the 50s?	{"Upper lip shortens, increasing maxillary incisor display.","Soft tissues sag downward, decreasing maxillary incisor display and increasing mandibular incisor display.","Lips become significantly thicker and more everted.","The philtrum length decreases."}	1	With aging, the soft tissues sag, causing the upper lip to cover more of the upper incisors and expose more of the lower incisors at rest and smiling.	Aging Changes	\N
6ad44625-7498-4d6b-bf96-a903e1ba6280	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient presents with an anterior crossbite. When guided into Centric Relation (CR), the incisors touch edge-to-edge, but the patient slides forward to close into Maximum Intercuspation (MI). This is termed a:	{"True Skeletal Class III","Pseudo-Class III (Functional Shift)","Class II Division 2","Bilateral posterior crossbite"}	1	A forward functional shift to avoid an incisor interference creates a pseudo-Class III, meaning the true skeletal discrepancy is less severe than the MI posture suggests.	Clinical Evaluation	\N
23acada2-de15-4679-a786-c5f65df5eb5d	orthodontics	BDS-I	Why is it critical to evaluate the path of closure from rest position to maximum intercuspation?	{"To measure the exact leeway space.","To differentiate between a true skeletal asymmetry and a functional shift.","To determine the stage of cervical vertebral maturation.","To calculate the Bolton ratio."}	1	Detecting a lateral or anterior shift upon closure is vital to distinguish a simple occlusal interference (functional shift) from a true underlying skeletal asymmetry.	Jaw and Occlusal Function	\N
25ac3f0b-4fac-4edf-9822-0bdac255c329	orthodontics	BDS-I	Why are orthodontic diagnostic casts traditionally trimmed with symmetric bases (e.g., perpendicular to the midpalatal raphe)?	{"To ensure they fit in standard storage boxes.","To emphasize they are equal to physical casts.","To help the clinician easily detect asymmetries within the dental arches.","To calculate the exact curve of Spee."}	2	Symmetrical trimming relative to the midline makes it much easier for the observer to visually detect transverse asymmetries and malalignments in the arches.	Cast Analysis	\N
1004b93a-f9c8-4101-9183-63cab0a1d869	orthodontics	BDS-I	During pre-emergent eruption, what is the absolute prerequisite for the tooth to move through the bone?	{"The physical pushing force of the elongating root.","Resorption of the overlying bone and primary tooth roots.","Pulpal pressure pushing the crown.","Deposition of secondary dentin."}	1	Eruptive movement requires the resorption of overlying bone. If resorption fails (as in cleidocranial dysplasia), the tooth will not erupt regardless of root growth.	Eruption Mechanisms	\N
74aca314-c886-45a5-9a55-6c17fe4a018e	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the most common normal sequence of eruption for permanent teeth in the mandibular arch?	{6-1-2-4-5-3-7,6-1-2-3-4-5-7,1-2-6-3-4-5-7,6-1-2-5-4-3-7}	1	The typical mandibular eruption sequence is first molar, central incisor, lateral incisor, canine, first premolar, second premolar, second molar (6-1-2-3-4-5-7).	Eruption Sequence	\N
69d772e2-917b-4d76-850a-afcdba42104c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Patients with Liver Cirrhosis often have a deficiency in which factors?	{"Vitamin C factors","Vitamin K-dependent factors (II, VII, IX, X)","Factor VIII only","Insulin factors"}	1	Liver disease depresses the synthesis of Vitamin K-dependent clotting factors. [Box 1-18]	Medical Management	\N
9efa2a5c-972a-4dd6-a0c7-efbaf4c0e14a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Studies comparing twins reveal that regarding malocclusion:	{"Dental characteristics have higher heritability than skeletal characteristics.","Skeletal characteristics have higher heritability than dental characteristics.","Both are 100% determined by environment.","Both are 100% determined by genetics."}	1	Skeletal craniofacial characteristics have relatively high heritability, whereas dental/occlusal characteristics (like crowding) have lower heritability and are more environmentally influenced.	Genetic Influences	\N
84d7ff32-8d7e-46c4-805a-93852844c505	orthodontics	BDS-I	Excluding third molars, which permanent tooth is the most frequently impacted?	{"Maxillary central incisor","Mandibular second premolar","Maxillary canine","Mandibular lateral incisor"}	2	Due to its long path of eruption and late emergence, the maxillary canine is the most frequently impacted tooth after third molars.	Eruption Problems	\N
4e5e27ff-c898-43b2-8b83-a1add694f909	orthodontics	BDS-I	In a Bolton analysis, the normal ratio of the sum of the mandibular anterior teeth to the maxillary anterior teeth is approximately:	{0.62,0.77,0.91,1}	1	The anterior Bolton ratio is normally around 77.2%.	Cast Analysis	\N
914f4b7e-0da8-4098-b240-9e0505030347	orthodontics	BDS-I	When evaluating a patient's face, the transverse plane is best assessed using which diagnostic record?	{"Lateral cephalogram","Frontal photograph","Submental vertex radiograph","Periapical radiographs"}	1	Frontal photographs (and clinical frontal examination) are used to evaluate bilateral symmetry and transverse facial proportions (Rule of Fifths).	Macro-Esthetics	\N
1b17615f-33e0-46a3-b2e2-c63629be2d4a	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient with a severe Class II division 1 malocclusion often exhibits a "lip catch." What effect does this habit have?	{"It retroclines the maxillary incisors.","It exacerbates the condition by pushing the maxillary incisors forward and mandibular incisors backward.","It causes a posterior crossbite.","It completely closes an anterior open bite."}	1	Resting the lower lip behind the upper incisors increases the overjet by tipping the upper teeth forward and the lower teeth backward.	Habits and Malocclusion	\N
9fffff49-850f-47fd-9ad8-544fea4e3259	orthodontics	BDS-I	Under the "Soft Tissue Paradigm," what is the primary goal of orthodontic treatment planning?	{"Perfecting Angle's ideal occlusion first, assuming soft tissues will follow.","Establishing ideal soft tissue proportions and placing the teeth to achieve this.","Ensuring the TMJ is in perfect centric relation, regardless of facial profile.","Expanding the arches maximally to avoid any extractions."}	1	The paradigm shifts focus to soft tissue adaptations as the primary goal, determining hard tissue positioning based on ideal soft tissue outcomes.	Modern Treatment Goals	\N
018fbdb4-fb3b-4b70-849a-bce7df95079a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which type of Diabetes involves an absolute insulin deficiency and typically begins in childhood?	{"Type I","Type II",Gestational,Insipidus}	0	Type I (Insulin-dependent) is characterized by the inability to produce insulin.	Diabetes Mellitus	\N
9c4fb7ce-e812-4ea8-90e7-14017522abb9	orthodontics	BDS-I	Remnants of Meckel's cartilage (from the first pharyngeal arch) are transformed into which structures?	{"The hard palate","The nasal septum","The malleus and incus of the middle ear","The styloid process"}	2	Meckel's cartilage is largely replaced by the bony mandible, but a portion transforms into the malleus and incus (conductive ossicles).	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
315df12f-3b8a-4d51-b9ca-332c41957e9c	orthodontics	BDS-I	A young child observing an older sibling happily receiving a dental reward and subsequently behaving well themselves is an example of:	{"Classical conditioning",Omission,"Observational learning (modeling)","Negative reinforcement"}	2	Observational learning (modeling) is a powerful tool where a child imitates the reinforced behavior of a role model.	Learning and Behavior	\N
a500ed14-0444-4ac5-ac47-76b5b59ec051	orthodontics	BDS-I	A "reverse overjet" is clinically defined as:	{"The upper incisors vertically overlapping the lower incisors by >5 mm.","The lower incisors being positioned horizontally ahead of the upper incisors (anterior crossbite).","A posterior crossbite involving all molars.","The maxillary incisors being excessively flared."}	1	Reverse overjet (anterior crossbite) occurs when the lower incisors are labial to the upper incisors.	Demand for Treatment	\N
9ebf66ba-0fe9-4775-8e88-d6f649a19917	orthodontics	BDS-I	Vertical maxillary excess (excessive downward growth of the maxilla) typically results in which clinical appearance?	{"A \\"short face\\" with a deep bite.","A \\"gummy smile\\" and a long anterior lower face.","Severe mandibular prognathism.","An ideal smile arc with no gingival display."}	1	Vertical maxillary excess causes a long lower face and excessive display of maxillary gingiva upon smiling (gummy smile).	Facial Proportions	\N
d0500c8e-3884-43e6-aa73-76e52a1d22d0	orthodontics	BDS-I	During pre-emergent tooth eruption, the extremely slow labial or buccal drift of the tooth follicle within the bone is referred to as:	{"Active eruption",Translation,"Pre-emergent drift",Translocation}	2	Pre-emergent drift is the slow movement of the follicle within the bone before the tooth erupts into the oral cavity.	Eruption Mechanisms	\N
fc3390b0-f0e8-4e4d-b7d4-d2f4d2bcc9d9	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which part of the skull relies heavily on endochondral ossification for its growth?	{"Cranial vault (Parietal/Frontal)",Maxilla,"Cranial base (Ethmoid, Sphenoid, Basioccipital)","Nasal bones"}	2	The cranial base bones are preformed in cartilage and grow via endochondral ossification at synchondroses.	Cranial Base Growth	\N
fccc3c0d-2fbf-4f37-9bf8-62c120b8195b	orthodontics	BDS-I	How does the cartilage of the mandibular condyle differ from an epiphyseal plate of a long bone?	{"It grows via interstitial growth only.","It acts as a primary pacemaker driving jaw growth.","It is an independent secondary cartilage that grows appositionally and lacks inherent growth potential.","It completely calcifies before birth."}	2	Condylar cartilage is secondary cartilage, grows by apposition on its surface, and transplantation studies show it lacks the innate growth potential of an epiphyseal plate.	Mandibular Growth	\N
9d84abdc-25f9-43f2-a732-7412396eb738	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to modern growth theories, the facial sutures (like those connecting the maxilla) act primarily as:	{"Primary genetic pacemakers driving bone apart.","Reactive areas that respond to tension by forming new bone.","Endochondral growth centers.","Rigid joints that fuse by age 2."}	1	Sutures are reactive areas; when pulled apart by the growth of surrounding soft tissues or the cranial base, they respond by forming new bone (apposition).	Theories of Growth	\N
f6164327-e234-4132-9dd1-81c172145c73	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Polyuria" and "Polydipsia" are classic signs of:	{Hyperthyroidism,"Diabetes Mellitus","Renal failure",Hypertension}	1	These are caused by glucose-induced osmotic diuresis. [Chapter 1, Diabetes]	Medical Management	\N
41d4c0ad-9126-4f4b-ac8c-634a2d6f24f9	orthodontics	BDS-I	The surgical technique of slowly separating bone segments to allow new bone formation in the gap, heavily used for severe mandibular deficiency, is called:	{"Orthognathic camouflage","Distraction osteogenesis","Ankylosis breaking","Synchondrosis expansion"}	1	Distraction osteogenesis involves surgically separating bone and slowly distracting it, stimulating new bone formation in the gap, modeled after natural sutural growth.	Treatment Possibilities	\N
dbc16da1-a9d7-4cb7-bc69-7394d14e67f3	orthodontics	BDS-I	A dentist tells a child that if they hold still for the injection, they won't use the loud drill. This removal of an unpleasant stimulus to encourage good behavior is:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Omission,"Classical conditioning"}	1	Negative reinforcement is the withdrawal of an unpleasant stimulus to increase the probability of a desired response.	Learning and Behavior	\N
efe59b27-785e-4f7a-8fce-b766400f88f1	orthodontics	BDS-I	If a child who previously associated the dentist with pain has several pain-free visits, their fear response will eventually fade. This process is called:	{Assimilation,Generalization,Extinction,"Positive reinforcement"}	2	Extinction occurs when a conditioned association (white coat = pain) is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus, causing the conditioned response (fear) to fade.	Behavioral Development	\N
4351d21e-53f5-46bb-a712-6c03c9a3ee59	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Erikson, an infant (Birth to 18 months) is navigating which primary psychosocial crisis?	{"Autonomy vs. Shame","Initiative vs. Guilt","Industry vs. Inferiority","Basic Trust vs. Mistrust"}	3	The initial stage involves developing basic trust in the environment and caregivers.	Emotional Development	\N
8dc05888-d1b5-4ba0-a239-09a8c950dde9	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "terrible two's" are characterized by a child's drive to exercise free choice and protect against dependence. This aligns with Erikson's stage of:	{"Basic Trust vs. Mistrust","Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt","Initiative vs. Guilt","Identity vs. Role Confusion"}	1	Between 18 months and 3 years, children are developing autonomy and a sense of individual identity.	Emotional Development	\N
d49f221f-4c69-4795-98c4-d0b86231d9a4	orthodontics	BDS-I	A 9-year-old child's motivation is heavily influenced by competition with others, grades, and a reward system. This represents Erikson's stage of:	{"Autonomy vs. Shame","Initiative vs. Guilt","Industry vs. Inferiority","Integrity vs. Despair"}	2	From 7 to 11 years, children are in the Industry vs. Inferiority stage, where acquiring skills and competing for recognition are paramount.	Emotional Development	\N
8705fe85-6a9f-40f5-92d6-8775e441051b	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the IOTN grading system, a "submerged deciduous tooth" is classified under which severity level?	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	3	Submerged deciduous teeth are considered an Extreme/Need Treatment anomaly, classified as Grade 5s.	Demand for Treatment	\N
2d30c0be-8d8a-4db0-9542-cf55c763bce9	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to NHANES III, what percentage of adults in the U.S. have "ideal alignment" (0 mm of lower incisor irregularity)?	{~10%,~34%,~50%,~80%}	0	Only about 10-15% of adults have perfect 0 mm irregularity, while ~34% have well-aligned (0-1mm) incisors. Crowding is extremely common.	Epidemiology	\N
150f7b9d-3b55-4652-ade3-da0e6b86f62d	oral_path	BDS-I	"Cheatle split"part logic:	{Proximal,"Distal anti-mesenteric",Mesenteric,Mucosal}	1	Cut into distal lumen to align unequal diameters.	Surgical Skills	\N
af95a03b-49db-490a-bf77-02cacdbf6c79	orthodontics	BDS-I	From an evolutionary perspective, the human third molar is frequently missing or impacted because:	{"It is a recent evolutionary addition.","Jaw size has decreased faster than the evolutionary reduction in tooth number.","Modern humans have larger teeth than Neanderthals.","Early humans extracted them routinely."}	1	Evolutionary reduction in jaw size (due to softer diets) has occurred much faster than the genetic loss of teeth, leading to crowding and impaction of the last teeth to erupt.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
b162dd77-6360-411d-b959-d40a75228ac2	orthodontics	BDS-I	In primitive populations, the heavy attrition (wear) of teeth from a coarse diet had what effect on the dental arches?	{"It caused severe anterior crowding.","It eliminated the leeway space prematurely.","It led to a decrease in arch length, preventing crowding.","It stimulated the growth of supernumerary teeth."}	2	Heavy interproximal and occlusal wear reduced the mesiodistal size of the teeth, allowing them to drift mesially, naturally reducing arch length and preventing crowding.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
85ebf025-c8f2-4de4-8c3e-90624ddffcea	orthodontics	BDS-I	While occlusal discrepancies can contribute to TMJ pain, what is currently considered the primary cause of TMD symptoms in the majority of patients?	{"Angle Class I malocclusion","Muscle fatigue and spasm, often related to stress and clenching.","Congenitally missing lateral incisors.","A 1 mm midline diastema."}	1	Muscle fatigue and spasm from clenching/grinding (often stress-induced) is the most frequent cause, rather than the dental occlusion alone.	Function and TMJ	\N
476c6d15-6395-477e-8217-61a0dac490ac	orthodontics	BDS-I	Why is a randomized clinical trial (RCT) considered the gold standard over retrospective case studies in orthodontics?	{"RCTs are cheaper and faster.","RCTs eliminate selection bias by randomly assigning patients before treatment begins.","RCTs allow the clinician to pick the best cases for publication.","RCTs only use expert opinion."}	1	RCTs control for known and unknown variables through random assignment, preventing the bias of selecting only successful cases (which plagues retrospective studies).	Evidence-Based Selection	\N
4ccb5df8-98d6-41ad-a806-73576a3ddb6e	orthodontics	BDS-I	When tracking a child's physical growth on a standard growth chart, what indicates a potential medical problem requiring investigation?	{"The child is at the 75th percentile.","The child's growth curve suddenly crosses multiple percentile lines (e.g., dropping from 50th to 10th).","The child follows the 25th percentile curve perfectly for 5 years.","The child is taller than their peers."}	1	Pattern implies predictability. An unexpected, severe deviation crossing percentile lines indicates an alteration in growth that warrants investigation.	Growth Variability	\N
40cd9e3d-bdb6-4789-87db-7439751749b1	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the surface remodeling of the mandibular ramus, bone is added to the posterior surface. What cellular activity dominates the anterior surface of the ramus to allow for lengthening?	{"Osteoblastic apposition","Chondrocytic hypertrophy","Osteoclastic resorption","Fibroblastic proliferation"}	2	The anterior surface of the ramus undergoes osteoclastic resorption, creating space for the erupting molars as the mandible grows longer.	Cellular Growth	\N
adb91d5d-38b4-49a9-b74b-71e9c17f1020	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the long bones of the extremities, the central calcified shaft is called the:	{Epiphysis,Diaphysis,Synchondrosis,Fontanelle}	1	The central shaft is the diaphysis, and the calcified ends are the epiphyses, separated by the uncalcified epiphyseal plate.	Endochondral Ossification	\N
b128da00-c751-434d-be94-5733dacee41b	orthodontics	BDS-I	The maxilla and mandible both initially develop from the tissues of which embryonic structure?	{"Second pharyngeal arch","First pharyngeal arch","Frontonasal process","Occipital somites"}	1	The first pharyngeal arch gives rise to the maxillary process and the mandibular process.	Embryologic Development	\N
b6104c87-b616-4b50-838c-b5911babf13b	orthodontics	BDS-I	The mandible initially forms as two separate halves. When does the mental symphysis typically fuse in humans?	{"At birth","During the first year of life","At age 6","At puberty"}	1	The mandibular symphysis fuses early in postnatal life (during the first year), making the mandible a single continuous bone thereafter.	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
64ba54c9-9d75-4e54-a8a9-9c6c0e041151	orthodontics	BDS-I	Dental age 8 is clinically characterized primarily by the eruption of which teeth?	{"Maxillary central incisors","Maxillary lateral incisors","Mandibular canines","First permanent molars"}	1	Dental age 8 is characterized by the eruption of the maxillary lateral incisors. (Age 7 is maxillary centrals/mandibular laterals; Age 6 is first molars/mandibular centrals).	Dental Age	\N
1ecdad4d-a155-4bfc-9320-0a0f57216e58	orthodontics	BDS-I	Dental age 11 is clinically characterized by the near-simultaneous eruption of which groups of teeth?	{"Maxillary incisors","Mandibular canines, mandibular first premolars, and maxillary first premolars","Maxillary canines and second molars","Third molars"}	1	Age 11 is marked by the eruption of the mandibular canines and the first premolars in both arches.	Dental Age	\N
49fbbc9d-8f2f-4e08-a0af-86d53282cefe	orthodontics	BDS-I	In "mini-esthetics" (smile analysis), the "smile arc" is considered ideal when the contour of the maxillary incisal edges:	{"Forms a perfectly straight, flat horizontal line.","Curves upward away from the lower lip.","Matches the curvature of the lower lip upon smiling.","Is completely hidden by the upper lip."}	2	An ideal, youthful smile arc occurs when the maxillary incisal edges follow the natural curvature of the lower lip.	Facial and Dental Appearance	\N
4838d4f8-98ae-46f5-adef-9542c99f0e42	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN), a patient with an increased overjet greater than 9 mm is classified under which category?	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	3	An increased overjet greater than 9 mm is classified as Grade 5a, which represents an Extreme/Need Treatment anomaly.	Demand for Treatment	\N
f675fcfb-3e87-49c8-b8e0-a1157a120940	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the IOTN classification, severe contact point displacements greater than 4 mm fall into which grade?	{"Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"}	3	Severe contact point displacements greater than 4 mm are classified as Grade 4d, indicating a Severe Need for treatment.	Demand for Treatment	\N
f45a2bcf-9ac4-47ce-879c-5a356c15f350	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on NHANES III data for the U.S. population, severe or extreme overjet (Class II problem) is most prevalent in which demographic group?	{"Non-Hispanic Whites","Non-Hispanic Blacks",Hispanics,"Asian Americans"}	2	Severe or extreme Class II and Class III problems are noted to be more prevalent in the Hispanic population than in the white or black groups.	Epidemiology of Malocclusion	\N
7350bf2f-6bdc-4e1e-8289-0d1041136098	public_health	BDS-I	"Standard Deviation" measures the:	{"Accuracy of the mean","Dispersion of values around the mean","Total number of subjects","Error in the study"}	1	It describes the spread of individual data points. High SD means the data is widely scattered.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
34593d4d-62a4-4b1e-af33-9270d47c579a	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to NHANES III, what is the prevalence of a midline diastema greater than 2 mm in Black children/youths compared to White or Hispanic counterparts?	{"It is equal across all groups","It is half as prevalent in Blacks","It is more than twice as likely in Blacks","It is virtually non-existent in Blacks"}	2	Blacks are more than twice as likely to have a noticeable midline diastema (>2 mm) than whites or Hispanics, a difference that is highly statistically significant.	Epidemiology of Malocclusion	\N
cf6a2483-cc50-47e1-903f-876d1b814884	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the fundamental difference in the "primary treatment goal" between the traditional Angle Paradigm and the modern Soft Tissue Paradigm?	{"Angle: Soft tissue adaptations; Soft Tissue: Ideal jaw relationships","Angle: Ideal dental occlusion; Soft Tissue: Normal soft tissue proportions","Angle: TMJ function; Soft Tissue: Dental occlusion","Angle: Facial esthetics; Soft Tissue: Cephalometric numbers"}	1	The Angle paradigm's primary goal is ideal dental occlusion, whereas the soft tissue paradigm focuses on normal soft tissue proportions and adaptations.	Modern Treatment Goals	\N
403dbfcd-c5f2-4696-8f29-fc9d2bb9312c	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the hierarchy of clinical effectiveness, which type of study design offers the lowest level of reliability and is considered the weakest form of clinical evidence?	{Meta-analysis,"Randomized clinical trial","Retrospective study","Unsupported opinion of expert"}	3	The unsupported opinion of an expert is the weakest form of clinical evidence at the very bottom of the hierarchy of quality.	Evidence-Based Selection	\N
4766123e-2e94-4329-926b-c679d040b6ea	orthodontics	BDS-I	Anthropological studies (like Corruccini's study on Punjabi youths) suggest that the modern increase in malocclusion is largely due to:	{"An evolutionary increase in tooth size","A transition to softer urban diets requiring less masticatory effort","Higher rates of premature tooth loss from caries","Genetic mutations in urban populations"}	1	The transition from primitive agricultural to modern urbanized societies with softer foods leads to less masticatory effort, causing disuse atrophy and narrower jaws, increasing crowding.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
