6ecbbf9c-d596-403b-af8a-bda3d5af9c5b	orthodontics	BDS-I	The most common supernumerary tooth found in the human dentition is located in the maxillary midline and is called a(n):	{Paramolar,Mesiodens,Distodens,Odontoma}	1	The most common supernumerary tooth appears in the maxillary midline and is called a mesiodens.	Specific Causes of Malocclusion	\N
23d9513c-d0fe-4a92-8e6a-016a0f02855e	orthodontics	BDS-I	Excluding the third molars, which two permanent teeth are most frequently congenitally missing (hypodontia)?	{"Maxillary canines and mandibular first molars","Maxillary central incisors and mandibular canines","Maxillary lateral incisors and mandibular second premolars","Mandibular lateral incisors and maxillary first premolars"}	2	After the third molars, the maxillary lateral incisors and mandibular second premolars are the most commonly missing teeth.	Specific Causes of Malocclusion	\N
76bb3dea-f161-432a-ae32-b6047a2a6cc6	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient taking "Glucagon" in an emergency is being treated for:	{Asthma,"Severe hypoglycemia","Heart attack",Stroke}	1	Glucagon is an antihypoglycemic given when IV access is difficult. [Table 2-2]	Medical Emergencies	\N
868ad2ec-db00-426b-9031-7e311fceea09	orthodontics	BDS-I	A severe distortion of root form, where the root develops at a sharp angle to the clinical crown, is usually the result of mechanical trauma to the primary predecessor. This condition is termed:	{Concrescence,Ankylosis,Dilaceration,Hypercementosis}	2	Trauma can physically displace the developing permanent crown. If the root continues to form, it will form at an angle, resulting in dilaceration.	Traumatic Displacement	\N
3a432796-2b76-41f3-b197-5f8cda396afe	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the equilibrium theory of tooth position, which type of force is most important in determining the final resting position of the teeth?	{"Heavy, intermittent forces (like chewing)","Light, intermittent forces (like swallowing)","Light, continuous resting forces (from lips, cheeks, tongue)","Extreme traumatic forces"}	2	Equilibrium is dictated primarily by the duration of the force. Light pressures of long duration from the resting posture of the lips, cheeks, and tongue are the primary determinants of tooth position.	Equilibrium Considerations	\N
2e8dd1be-f5df-42e1-8b22-a75a2419cfc2	orthodontics	BDS-I	Why do the extremely heavy forces generated during mastication (chewing) fail to permanently move the teeth orthodontically?	{"The tongue instantly provides equal opposing force.","The forces are of extremely short duration (minutes per day).","The periodontal ligament paralyzes osteoclasts.","Teeth are ankylosed during function."}	1	Despite their magnitude, masticatory forces last for a very short duration (only minutes per day total), which is insufficient to trigger the biologic response necessary for tooth movement.	Equilibrium Considerations	\N
ff5e5690-8761-4caf-b3ee-fb01a027b9f9	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child with a prolonged thumb-sucking habit frequently develops a narrow, constricted maxillary arch. What is the primary mechanical reason for this specific transverse constriction?	{"The direct upward pressure of the thumb expands the midpalatal suture.","The tongue is lowered while cheek pressure is increased due to suction, altering the muscular equilibrium.","The habit stimulates excessive condylar growth.","Premature fusion of the palatal shelves."}	1	Thumb sucking lowers the tongue, removing its resting pressure from the palate, while the suction increases pressure from the cheek muscles (buccinators), which physically narrows the maxillary arch.	Sucking and Other Habits	\N
580e8254-61c9-4e90-aae8-0a12b3898bb0	orthodontics	BDS-I	The contemporary view of a "tongue thrust" swallow (placement of the tongue tip forward between the incisors during swallowing) is that it is primarily:	{"The root cause of severe anterior open bites.","The cause of posterior crossbites.","A physiologic adaptation to seal the oral cavity when a pre-existing anterior open bite is present.","A genetic neuromuscular disorder."}	2	Modern understanding views tongue thrust during swallowing primarily as an adaptation to an existing open bite (to create a seal), rather than the cause of the open bite itself.	Tongue Thrusting	\N
8b792f6d-06e4-4d31-bfcf-6e6ee79569f8	orthodontics	BDS-I	Chronic severe nasal obstruction forces a child to become a mouth breather. This altered posture frequently leads to skeletal changes known as "adenoid facies." Which finding is characteristic?	{"Downward and backward rotation of the mandible","Forward rotation of the mandible","Severe mandibular prognathism","A perfectly flat mandibular plane"}	0	To breathe through the mouth, the mandible drops and rotates downward and backward. If maintained, this increases lower face height and leads to an anterior open bite tendency.	Respiratory Pattern	\N
5fbf0e25-ba37-4253-a88d-2b0c3da9fe5d	orthodontics	BDS-I	The core philosophy of the "problem-oriented approach" to orthodontic diagnosis is to:	{"Immediately extract teeth if crowding is severe.","Treat the most obvious dental defect immediately.","Develop a comprehensive database to identify all patient problems before formulating a treatment plan.","Rely solely on cephalometric numbers."}	2	The problem-oriented approach emphasizes developing a comprehensive database to identify all pathologic and developmental problems before synthesizing a prioritized problem list and treatment plan.	Problem-Oriented Approach	\N
724f5988-b551-47b3-9ccf-4c99e958fb47	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the initial patient interview/questionnaire, gathering information is divided into three major areas. Which of the following is NOT one of these primary areas?	{"Medical and dental history","Physical growth status","Motivation, expectations, and social factors","Cephalometric tracing analysis"}	3	The interview focuses on (1) medical and dental history, (2) physical growth status, and (3) motivation and expectations. Cephalometric analysis is part of the objective clinical records.	Questionnaire/Interview	\N
e87e712c-bfdc-4ead-b065-90460fbd5cf6	orthodontics	BDS-I	On a standard lateral cephalometric radiograph, the anterior cranial base, which serves as a stable reference line for superimposition, is traditionally represented by the line connecting:	{"Porion and Orbitale","Gonion and Menton","Sella and Nasion","ANS and PNS"}	2	The line connecting Sella (center of sella turcica) and Nasion (frontonasal suture) classically represents the anterior cranial base.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
2cd59f19-bfd8-4395-a184-029c77710810	orthodontics	BDS-I	A dentist examining an adolescent patient notes severe bleeding upon gentle probing around crowded incisors, with associated gingival recession. What underlying condition must be suspected and controlled before initiating orthodontics?	{"Cleidocranial dysplasia","Aggressive juvenile periodontitis","Primary failure of eruption","Amelogenesis imperfecta"}	1	Bleeding on probing in kids can occasionally indicate aggressive juvenile periodontitis. It is critical to identify and treat this before orthodontic forces accelerate bone loss.	Clinical Evaluation	\N
71c3bcb9-0b3e-4cd5-bc0f-a8d9f621b95d	orthodontics	BDS-I	During a clinical exam, the dentist guides the patient's jaw into centric relation (CR). The incisors touch edge-to-edge, but the patient then slides their jaw forward into a full anterior crossbite in maximum intercuspation (MI). This phenomenon is termed a:	{"True skeletal Class III","Bilateral posterior crossbite","Pseudo-Class III (Functional Shift)","Class II division 2"}	2	A functional shift forward to avoid an incisor interference creates a pseudo-Class III, meaning the true skeletal discrepancy is much less severe than it appears in MI.	Jaw and Occlusal Function	\N
eede45f0-d24e-4e89-a2d0-51a93c66f67b	orthodontics	BDS-I	A pediatric patient has a pronounced lisp and distortion when pronouncing sibilant sounds (e.g., /s/ and /z/). Which underlying malocclusion is most closely associated with this speech difficulty?	{"Skeletal Class III","Anterior open bite","Severe deep bite","Posterior crossbite"}	1	The distortion of sibilant sounds (/s/ and /z/), commonly presenting as a lisp, is most directly related to an anterior open bite.	Speech Difficulties	\N
f1a76747-16ad-4d1e-93f5-4a99ae342dac	orthodontics	BDS-I	Difficulty in producing labiodental fricative sounds (/f/ and /v/) is classically associated with which skeletal discrepancy?	{"Anterior open bite","Maxillary midline diastema","Skeletal Class III (prognathic mandible)","Class II division 1"}	2	Difficulty in pronouncing labiodental fricative sounds is classically associated with a Skeletal Class III malocclusion, where the inverted jaw relationship hinders proper lip-to-incisor contact.	Speech Difficulties	\N
ce75a766-f2cd-4b52-a0c4-df51d1cf15f4	orthodontics	BDS-I	When analyzing facial proportions in the frontal view, the "Rule of Fifths" dictates that the intercanthal distance (the central fifth) should ideally be equal to the:	{"Width of the mouth","Interpupillary distance","Width of the nasal base","Width of the chin"}	2	According to the rule of fifths, the central fifth is defined by the intercanthal distance, which should ideally equal the width of the nose at its base.	Macro-Esthetics	\N
408b6de7-1b7b-49c8-9370-f073455c95ca	orthodontics	BDS-I	In evaluating "mini-esthetics," an ideal "smile arc" is present when the incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth:	{"Form a perfectly straight horizontal line.","Are completely hidden by the upper lip.","Follow the natural contour of the lower lip upon smiling.","Touch the mandibular incisors directly."}	2	An ideal smile arc occurs when the contour of the incisal edges of the maxillary anterior teeth matches the curvature of the lower lip upon smiling.	Mini-Esthetics	\N
40eaad6d-3086-4ec7-bde7-6b6c77df982b	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the "golden proportion" in micro-esthetics, when viewing the maxillary anterior teeth from the direct frontal perspective, the apparent width of the lateral incisor should be what percentage of the central incisor?	{0.5,0.62,0.8,1}	1	The golden proportion suggests a ratio of 1.0 : 0.62 : 0.38. Therefore, the apparent width of the lateral incisor should be 62% of the central incisor.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
f584c24e-abe5-450a-8771-4ecc8043d51e	orthodontics	BDS-I	To achieve ideal gingival esthetics, the gingival zenith (highest point of the tissue contour) of the maxillary lateral incisor should be located:	{"Distal to the long axis of the tooth.","Mesial to the long axis of the tooth.","Coincident with the vertical long axis of the tooth.","Exactly level with the central incisor zenith."}	2	While the zenith for central incisors and canines is slightly distal to the long axis, the zenith for the maxillary lateral incisor should coincide with its vertical long axis.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
2d428675-e172-4cb6-8466-a04c0826a917	orthodontics	BDS-I	An adult patient develops noticeable "black triangles" between their maxillary incisors after their severe crowding is corrected. This is typically due to:	{"Hypertrophy of the gingival papilla","An allergic reaction to brackets","Loss of crestal bone height and reshaping of the interdental embrasure","Excessive flossing"}	2	Short interdental papillae leave an open gingival embrasure (black triangle). This often becomes apparent when crowded/rotated incisors are aligned in adults who have lost some crestal bone height.	Micro-Esthetics	\N
8650e916-538c-4149-9100-f28f967936bd	orthodontics	BDS-I	For a routine initial orthodontic evaluation, which radiographic record is generally preferred over a full mouth series (FMX) because it provides a broader view of the jaws at a significantly lower radiation dose?	{"Lateral cephalogram","Cone Beam CT (Large Field)","Panoramic radiograph","Submental vertex"}	2	A panoramic radiograph is preferred for initial evaluation because it yields a broader view of the teeth, jaws, and TMJs at a much lower radiation dose than an FMX.	Diagnostic Records	\N
bbeac154-f3f7-4974-98ca-6b23dbaddaef	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to modern radiation dose standards, a digital lateral cephalometric radiograph exposes the patient to approximately what effective dose?	{"2 µSv","5.5 µSv","16 µSv","68 µSv"}	1	A digital cephalometric radiograph exposes the patient to an extremely low effective dose of about 5.5 µSv.	Diagnostic Records	\N
874f41ea-3a59-4b82-aeb7-07c46f18e406	orthodontics	BDS-I	The Bolton analysis evaluates tooth size discrepancies. What is the normal approximate ratio of the summed mesiodistal widths of all 12 mandibular teeth to all 12 maxillary teeth (overall ratio)?	{0.62,0.77,0.91,1}	2	The normal overall Bolton ratio (mandibular sum of 12 / maxillary sum of 12) is approximately 91.3%.	Tooth Size Analysis	\N
d5b10f08-3392-4e63-b321-efc4f15061f1	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the Bolton analysis, what is the normal approximate ratio for the anterior teeth (sum of 6 mandibular / sum of 6 maxillary)?	{0.62,0.77,0.91,1}	1	The normal anterior Bolton ratio is approximately 77.2%.	Tooth Size Analysis	\N
8028a5d3-3869-40e1-9d16-ad58f91ceee2	orthodontics	BDS-I	Why are orthodontic diagnostic study casts traditionally trimmed with symmetrical bases (e.g., perpendicular to the midpalatal raphe)?	{"To ensure they fit correctly into the articulator.","To make them balance without support.","To aid the clinician in visually detecting transverse asymmetries within the dental arches.","To measure the exact curve of Spee."}	2	Trimming the casts symmetrically relative to the midline makes it much easier for the observer to visually detect lateral shifts and asymmetries within the dental arches.	Cast Analysis	\N
b070fc42-557d-4834-a9de-258cd1c719d8	medicine	BDS-I	"Koilonychia" is most common in which group?	{Athletes,"Elderly women","Chronic GI bleed patients",Diabetics}	2	Koilonychia is a sign of iron deficiency, often due to chronic GI blood loss.	Blood Disease	\N
133aa7b1-758c-4c50-ae09-a14349f3aa4d	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "mandibular plane," used to evaluate vertical facial proportions on a cephalogram, is classically drawn by connecting which two landmarks?	{"Sella and Nasion","Porion and Orbitale","Gonion and Menton","Articulare and Pogonion"}	2	The mandibular plane is classically drawn connecting Gonion (at the angle of the mandible) and Menton (the lowest point on the symphysis).	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
57f94de7-8ad6-42b4-a960-948dde58d2ca	orthodontics	BDS-I	Under the IOTN, "impeded eruption of teeth (except third molars) due to crowding, displacement, or supernumerary teeth" falls under which grade?	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	3	Impeded eruption is classified under Grade 5i, indicating an Extreme Need for treatment.	Demand for Treatment	\N
bd59f73f-cef5-41cc-b172-3f3ffcb45177	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the IOTN, a reverse overjet greater than 3.5 mm with reported masticatory and speech difficulties is graded as:	{3b,4m,5m,5a}	2	This combination of severe structural discrepancy and functional impairment makes it an Extreme Need, classified specifically as 5m.	Demand for Treatment	\N
33a78000-7bc2-4469-bfb8-dd11f05ae317	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In an anxiety-reduction protocol, why is relaxing background music used?	{"To comply with HIPAA","As a nonpharmacologic means of anxiety control","To mask the sound of the dental drill only","To keep the office staff alert"}	1	Music is a validated nonpharmacologic anxiety reducer. [Box 1-9]	Medical Management	\N
ed37cdf5-bfcd-4c37-819e-cfa9cea4f8be	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the transition from the mixed to the permanent dentition, the total arch length (measured from the midline to the first molars) generally:	{"Increases due to jaw growth","Decreases due to mesial drift of the first molars into the leeway space","Remains completely unchanged","Doubles in size"}	1	Arch length decreases during the transition primarily due to the mesial drift of the permanent first molars into the leeway space left by the exfoliating primary molars.	Space Relationships	\N
d5fb36ae-db41-4742-bf04-d085d915a1ae	orthodontics	BDS-I	Early unilateral loss of a primary mandibular canine is a common cause of:	{"Bilateral posterior crossbite","Severe anterior open bite","A shift of the lower dental midline toward the side of the loss","Premature eruption of the third molars"}	2	Early loss of a primary canine allows the permanent incisors to drift toward that side, causing a noticeable midline shift.	Space Relationships	\N
b62b622b-0110-4bd9-9c3d-61150c37497d	orthodontics	BDS-I	Long-term studies indicate that "late incisor crowding" (crowding of the lower anterior teeth that develops in the late teens/early 20s) is primarily caused by:	{"Pressure from erupting third molars pushing the teeth forward","Continued slow forward growth of the mandible displacing the lower incisors lingually against the lips","Severe periodontal disease","The loss of leeway space"}	1	Late incisor crowding is primarily related to the pattern of late mandibular growth. The mandible continues to grow forward slightly, displacing the lower incisors lingually against the lips.	Maturational Changes	\N
14685749-325c-41fe-a144-b2cf53da2872	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following is true regarding the cartilage of the mandibular condyle compared to an epiphyseal plate of a long bone?	{"It acts as the primary genetic pacemaker of the jaw.","It undergoes intramembranous ossification.","It is an independent secondary cartilage that lacks innate growth potential if transplanted.","It completely calcifies by age 6."}	2	Condylar cartilage is a secondary cartilage that grows appositionally. Transplantation studies demonstrate it lacks the innate, independent growth potential of an epiphyseal plate.	Growth Theories	\N
2ff6ba29-2f96-4a67-9755-149409f87b1b	orthodontics	BDS-I	In classical conditioning, if an unconditioned stimulus (pain) is no longer paired with a conditioned stimulus (white coat), the fear response will eventually fade. This process is called:	{Sensitization,Extinction,Generalization,"Negative reinforcement"}	1	Extinction occurs when a conditioned association is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus, causing the conditioned response to fade.	Behavioral Development	\N
a87590d3-b36d-471c-825f-c597bd4f3a0c	orthodontics	BDS-I	Using "voice control" (speaking sharply or sternly to gain a child's attention) is classified under which behavioral conditioning category?	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Punishment,Omission}	2	Voice control involves presenting an unpleasant stimulus (a sharp voice) to decrease an undesirable behavior, which is a mild form of punishment.	Behavioral Development	\N
0d9541a6-c76c-4bb7-9c47-a798c401bda7	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the IOTN, "impeded eruption of teeth (except third molars) due to crowding, displacement, or supernumerary teeth" is classified under which grade?	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	3	Impeded eruption is classified as Grade 5i, indicating an Extreme/Need Treatment anomaly.	Demand for Treatment	\N
a4233e6c-4001-4c27-b2f1-debfbd0aa11c	orthodontics	BDS-I	Under the IOTN, a patient with less extensive hypodontia requiring prerestorative orthodontics (one tooth per quadrant) is classified as:	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	2	Less extensive hypodontia requiring space closure or prerestorative orthodontics is classified as Grade 4h (Severe Need).	Demand for Treatment	\N
c58d2c63-2404-457d-a887-fdfcd01a3397	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient presents with a reverse overjet of 4.0 mm and reports significant masticatory and speech difficulties. What is the correct IOTN grade?	{"Grade 3b","Grade 4m","Grade 5m","Grade 5a"}	2	Reverse overjet >3.5 mm combined with reported masticatory/speech difficulties is classified as Grade 5m (Extreme Need).	Demand for Treatment	\N
52de5ccd-39a6-4031-81be-ef79a19a8eaa	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which IOTN grade applies to a patient with an increased overjet of 5 mm and incompetent lips?	{"Grade 2a","Grade 3a","Grade 4a","Grade 5a"}	1	Increased overjet >3.5 mm but ≤6 mm with incompetent lips is classified as Grade 3a (Moderate/Borderline Need).	Demand for Treatment	\N
3af9a973-a6ae-4b4d-a998-1e282e3b62c5	orthodontics	BDS-I	Under the modern "Soft Tissue Paradigm," what is the primary goal of orthodontic treatment?	{"Achieving Angle's ideal dental occlusion","Perfecting TMJ function and centric relation","Establishing normal soft tissue proportions and adaptations","Maximizing dental arch width to avoid extractions"}	2	The soft tissue paradigm dictates that establishing ideal soft tissue relationships and adaptations is the primary goal, determining where teeth should be placed.	Modern Treatment Goals	\N
02bf4e55-7f58-4faf-ac36-bc7038bea0a1	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the hierarchy of clinical evidence, which study design provides the highest quality, most reliable data for orthodontic decisions?	{"Retrospective study based on treatment response","Case reports","Meta-analysis of multiple randomized clinical trials","Unsupported opinion of an expert"}	2	A meta-analysis of multiple randomized clinical trials represents the absolute peak of the evidence hierarchy, effectively minimizing bias.	Evidence-Based Selection	\N
ed207aaa-ae32-4703-aa0d-ce229a4a979e	orthodontics	BDS-I	Based on NHANES III data, which demographic group has the highest prevalence of severe Class II and severe Class III malocclusions?	{"Non-Hispanic Whites","Non-Hispanic Blacks",Hispanics,"Asian Americans"}	2	Severe Class II and Class III problems are noted to be more prevalent in the Hispanic population than in White or Black groups.	Epidemiology	\N
56fbe9eb-cf80-4910-bf7c-4d57b0d26ef1	orthodontics	BDS-I	NHANES III data shows that a midline diastema greater than 2 mm is most prevalent in which population?	{Whites,Hispanics,Blacks,"It is equally prevalent in all groups"}	2	Black individuals are more than twice as likely to have a noticeable midline diastema (>2 mm) than White or Hispanic individuals.	Epidemiology	\N
2807a47d-e3da-43f8-bbae-60fcac87ad5e	orthodontics	BDS-I	From an evolutionary perspective, what is the primary reason for the high prevalence of crowding in modern humans?	{"Rapid evolutionary increase in tooth size.","Decrease in jaw size due to softer diets, outstripping the decrease in tooth size.","A genetic mutation specific to the 20th century.","Loss of the primary dentition due to modern refined sugars."}	1	The transition to softer modern diets caused a reduction in jaw size (disuse atrophy) that occurred faster than the evolutionary reduction in tooth size/number.	Etiology in Perspective	\N
7ca8fbee-b853-4637-ad29-bd1d999fcd2e	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Scammon's curves, which tissue type completes its growth earliest, reaching nearly 100% of its adult size by age 6 or 7?	{"General body",Lymphoid,Genital,Neural}	3	Growth of the neural tissues is nearly complete by 6 or 7 years of age, well before other systemic tissues.	Growth Patterns	\N
fb71c4ed-2d4d-4055-95dd-f0770958a395	orthodontics	BDS-I	On Scammon's growth curves, which tissue type proliferates to nearly 200% of adult size before puberty, and then shrinks?	{Neural,Maxillary,Lymphoid,Genital}	2	Lymphoid tissues proliferate far beyond the adult amount in late childhood and then undergo involution (shrinkage) at puberty.	Growth Patterns	\N
4ea68285-e2f6-4577-a363-1f5107c5a866	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "cephalocaudal gradient of growth" dictates that at the third month of fetal development, the head comprises what percentage of total body length?	{0.12,0.3,0.5,0.75}	2	At the third month of intrauterine development, the head makes up almost 50% of the total body length. At birth, it decreases to about 30%.	Cephalocaudal Gradient	\N
fdea7c8e-f2dd-4dd8-99cc-27cfe7769ba7	orthodontics	BDS-I	Due to the cephalocaudal gradient, how does postnatal growth of the mandible compare to the maxilla?	{"The maxilla grows more and later than the mandible.","The mandible grows more and later than the maxilla.","They grow at exactly the same rate.","The mandible stops growing 5 years before the maxilla."}	1	Structures further from the brain grow more and later. Therefore, the mandible grows more and later than the maxilla.	Cephalocaudal Gradient	\N
964e4b4d-c44c-45ff-8a6f-e74d434b244a	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following is a key difference between the growth mechanisms of bone and cartilage?	{"Cartilage grows only by apposition; bone grows interstitially.","Bone grows interstitially; cartilage does not.","Cartilage grows interstitially and appositionally; bone grows only appositionally.","Neither can undergo interstitial growth."}	2	Cartilage can undergo interstitial expansion. Once tissue is mineralized into bone, interstitial growth is impossible; bone only grows via surface apposition.	Nature of Skeletal Growth	\N
0e1ef127-671d-4e36-9989-1478f98aa439	orthodontics	BDS-I	Postnatal growth of the cranial vault (parietal and frontal bones) occurs entirely via:	{"Endochondral ossification at synchondroses","Intramembranous ossification at the sutures","Interstitial expansion","Replacement of Meckel's cartilage"}	1	The cranial vault comprises flat bones formed directly by intramembranous ossification, with postnatal growth occurring primarily at the sutures.	Cranial Vault Growth	\N
3b18b2c0-87c2-43bb-afeb-6950197d5ac5	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which of the following craniofacial structures develops postnatally entirely by intramembranous ossification?	{"Sphenoid bone","Ethmoid bone","Mandibular condyle",Maxilla}	3	The maxilla develops postnatally entirely by intramembranous ossification. There is no cartilage replacement in its postnatal growth.	Maxillary Growth	\N
f828902c-eada-4f14-a6fc-865147807f02	orthodontics	BDS-I	The spheno-occipital synchondrosis is a critical growth center in the cranial base. When does it typically fuse?	{"At birth","By 6-7 years of age","In the early twenties","At age 12"}	2	Unlike the intersphenoid and spheno-ethmoidal synchondroses, the spheno-occipital synchondrosis remains active until early adulthood (early 20s).	Cranial Base Growth	\N
1478b737-69f2-407b-bc0d-53effcb4126d	orthodontics	BDS-I	As the maxilla translates downward and forward during growth, what remodeling process predominantly occurs on its anterior surface?	{"Heavy bone apposition","Bone resorption","Cartilage proliferation","Interstitial expansion"}	1	Paradoxically, as the maxilla is carried downward and forward, its anterior surface actually undergoes bone resorption.	Maxillary Growth	\N
6e201746-04ab-4c09-a4b7-b25c29fdf7e3	orthodontics	BDS-I	In the mandible, how is lengthening of the ramus achieved to accommodate erupting molars?	{"Interstitial growth within the ramus.","Apposition on the anterior surface, resorption on the posterior surface.","Resorption on the anterior surface, apposition on the posterior surface.","Expansion of the mental symphysis."}	2	The mandible grows longer by removing (resorbing) bone from the anterior surface of the ramus and adding (appositing) bone to its posterior surface.	Mandibular Growth	\N
6a325521-ff1b-4b16-84b5-31b49b3178d0	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which theory of craniofacial growth posits that the enlargement of the nasal and oral cavities is the primary determinant of jaw growth?	{"Cartilage-Directed Theory","Sutural Dominance Theory","Functional Matrix Theory","Genetic Theory"}	2	Moss's Functional Matrix Theory states that soft tissue spaces (nasal/oral cavities) grow in response to function, driving passive bone growth.	Theories of Growth Control	\N
a913b63f-c10f-41c9-8fc2-857660f274bf	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which structure was incorrectly hypothesized by the Cartilage-Directed Theory to act as a primary pacemaker "pushing" the maxilla forward?	{"Mandibular condyle","Spheno-occipital synchondrosis","Cartilaginous nasal septum","Midpalatal suture"}	2	Proponents of this theory believed the cartilaginous nasal septum served as a pacemaker pushing the maxilla forward, though later experiments cast doubt on it being the sole determinant.	Theories of Growth Control	\N
655b900d-f519-43b0-8a20-9725074f5fe2	orthodontics	BDS-I	The surgical procedure "distraction osteogenesis" is heavily based on which natural growth principle?	{"Endochondral ossification","Sutural separation and subsequent bone fill","Cartilage interstitial growth","Tooth eruption forces"}	1	Distraction osteogenesis mimics sutural growth by surgically separating bone segments and applying tension, stimulating new bone formation in the resulting gap.	Distraction Osteogenesis	\N
ce4399a0-57d2-42a5-84c1-29e44450d6fa	orthodontics	BDS-I	In behavioral psychology, Classical Conditioning (Pavlovian) is defined as learning by:	{Consequences,Association,Observation,Punishment}	1	Classical conditioning is learning by association, where a neutral stimulus (e.g., a white coat) becomes associated with an unconditioned stimulus (e.g., pain).	Behavioral Development	\N
2212f5df-73ae-43ea-a576-82c2d210de39	orthodontics	BDS-I	Operant Conditioning (Skinnerian) is defined by the principle that behavior is determined by:	{"Genetic instincts","Its consequences","Unconscious fears","Involuntary reflexes"}	1	The basic principle of operant conditioning is that the consequence of a behavior acts as a stimulus that affects the future probability of that behavior.	Behavioral Development	\N
1da85a65-a8b9-4cab-8347-8050d6da4423	orthodontics	BDS-I	A dentist promises a child a toy if they sit still for the filling. This is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Omission,"Classical conditioning"}	0	Positive reinforcement involves presenting a pleasant stimulus following a desired behavior to increase the likelihood of that behavior.	Behavioral Development	\N
6d7668a9-135c-4963-9481-8d243a1bfa62	orthodontics	BDS-I	Telling a patient they don't have to wear their headgear if they maintain excellent oral hygiene is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement",Omission,Punishment}	1	Negative reinforcement involves the withdrawal of an unpleasant stimulus (headgear) after a desired response (good hygiene) to reinforce the behavior.	Behavioral Development	\N
c29896b7-7afe-4877-95d1-bbeb9591fd75	orthodontics	BDS-I	Stopping a child's favorite movie in the dental chair because they kicked the assistant is an example of:	{"Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","Omission (Time-out)",Extinction}	2	Omission involves the removal of a pleasant stimulus after a negative response, aiming to decrease the likelihood of the misbehavior.	Behavioral Development	\N
ec8fe69e-f778-4c71-ad6e-285909bd75f1	orthodontics	BDS-I	If a child associates the dentist with pain, but then has several pain-free visits, their fear will gradually fade. This process is called:	{Assimilation,Sensitization,Extinction,"Negative reinforcement"}	2	Extinction occurs when the conditioned association is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus (pain), causing the conditioned response (fear) to fade.	Behavioral Development	\N
b04bc263-aa58-43dc-92ec-eda63037c8be	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Piaget, a 5-year-old child who believes the dental drill "wants to bite" is displaying:	{Conservation,Egocentrism,Animism,"Formal operations"}	2	Animism is a characteristic of the preoperational stage where a child invests inanimate objects with life and lifelike qualities.	Cognitive Development	\N
21e8171c-79a3-4bbd-9dd9-fcc6d3bfb936	orthodontics	BDS-I	In Erikson's stages, the "terrible twos" (ages 1.5 to 3 years), characterized by a struggle for independence, correspond to the crisis of:	{"Basic Trust vs Mistrust","Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt","Initiative vs Guilt","Industry vs Inferiority"}	1	Between 18 months and 3 years, children are developing autonomy. The struggle for independence often manifests as the "terrible twos".	Emotional Development	\N
ccf05c16-5d16-4e7f-aa20-aa96c2af90b9	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to Erikson, adolescents (ages 12-17) seeking orthodontics for peer acceptance are primarily navigating the crisis of:	{"Trust vs Mistrust","Initiative vs Guilt","Industry vs Inferiority","Identity vs Role Confusion"}	3	Adolescence involves the Development of Personal Identity, heavily relying on peer groups rather than parents, driving motivation for treatment.	Emotional Development	\N
c3f22484-1fd4-43b5-9ccc-bbd34c6f1636	orthodontics	BDS-I	Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS), characterized by central midface deficiency, originates during which embryonic stage?	{"Germ layer formation (Day 17)","Neural tube formation (Days 18-23)","Primary palate formation (Days 28-38)","Secondary palate formation (Days 42-55)"}	0	FAS originates extremely early from exposure during germ layer formation (around Day 17), leading to deficiencies in the neural plate.	Embryologic Development	\N
48f32544-43d5-4915-b16a-814c26930eb9	orthodontics	BDS-I	Treacher Collins syndrome and Hemifacial Microsomia result from disturbances during the migration of which embryonic cells?	{"Ectodermal placodes","Mesodermal somites","Neural crest cells","Endodermal tubes"}	2	These syndromes are caused by problems in the origin, migration, and interaction of neural crest cells (Days 19-28).	Embryologic Development	\N
6224b503-627a-4118-8be4-0bed98fbcb86	orthodontics	BDS-I	A cleft of the primary palate (cleft lip) occurs due to a failure of fusion between which processes?	{"Lateral palatal shelves","Median nasal, lateral nasal, and maxillary processes","Mandibular processes","Frontonasal and medial nasal processes"}	1	Cleft lip occurs due to a failure of fusion between the median and lateral nasal processes and the maxillary prominence (Days 28-38).	Embryologic Development	\N
b4f7b7e2-a67f-463b-a1ad-12d6fb151201	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which specific teratogen is classically associated with anencephaly?	{Thalidomide,Accutane,Aminopterin,"Ethyl alcohol"}	2	Aminopterin is a known teratogen that affects early embryonic development, resulting in anencephaly.	Etiology	\N
8ce85f26-2af5-4590-b7e7-d8abbbdc868d	orthodontics	BDS-I	13-cis Retinoic acid (Accutane) is a teratogen known to cause malformations similar to:	{"Fetal Alcohol Syndrome","Treacher Collins syndrome",Anencephaly,Craniosynostosis}	1	Accutane affects neural crest cell migration, causing severe malformations akin to craniofacial microsomia and Treacher Collins syndrome.	Etiology	\N
41796bde-c4f7-4775-b14b-56cc1ee41213	orthodontics	BDS-I	In Cleidocranial Dysplasia, permanent teeth fail to erupt primarily because of:	{"Absence of the tooth follicle","Premature fusion of root apices","A generalized defect in bone resorption","Ankylosis of primary teeth"}	2	In cleidocranial dysplasia, succedaneous teeth have normal eruptive potential, but the overlying bone is not resorbed due to a systemic defect.	Eruption Disturbances	\N
201b4991-ae48-4d4a-add6-88bdcd88fa39	orthodontics	BDS-I	Primary Failure of Eruption (PFE) is a genetic condition where posterior teeth fail to erupt despite a cleared path. It is linked to a mutation in which gene?	{MSX1,RUNX2,PTHR1,SHH}	2	PFE has been linked to a mutation in the parathyroid hormone receptor gene (PTHR1), affecting the propulsive mechanism of eruption.	Eruption Disturbances	\N
c3a8567a-e938-4803-a10e-1178882b4dbc	orthodontics	BDS-I	During the "post-emergent spurt," rapid tooth eruption continues until what specific event occurs?	{"The root apex completely closes.","The tooth emerges through the gingiva.","The tooth approaches the occlusal level and is subjected to masticatory forces.","The patient reaches 18 years of age."}	2	Once a tooth emerges, it erupts rapidly until it approaches the occlusal level and is subjected to the heavy forces of mastication, which halts the spurt.	Post-emergent Eruption	\N
