165e6917-2051-48b6-915a-af76501d7dcc	orthodontics	BDS-I	"Late incisor crowding" (crowding of the lower anterior teeth developing in the late teens/early 20s) is primarily caused by:	{"Pressure from erupting third molars.","Continued late forward growth of the mandible, displacing lower incisors lingually.","Premature loss of leeway space.","Severe periodontal disease."}	1	Late incisor crowding is primarily related to the pattern of late mandibular growth. The mandible continues to grow forward slightly, displacing the lower incisors lingually against the lips.	Maturational Changes	\N
c67c8060-2165-4c32-a593-62fd82f27fe8	orthodontics	BDS-I	As adults age from their 20s into their 50s, a predictable change in facial soft tissues occurs, resulting in:	{"The lips sagging downward, decreasing maxillary incisor display and increasing mandibular incisor display.","The upper lip shortening, causing a gummy smile.","The vermilion border becoming much thicker.","The intercanthal distance decreasing."}	0	With aging, the soft tissues sag downward. This decreases the exposure of the upper incisors and increases the exposure of the lower incisors at rest and smiling.	Aging Changes	\N
