c19d4617-74cf-41a0-9380-e98e9bd4ab5b	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the primary physiologic reason the pulp chambers of permanent teeth become significantly smaller as a person ages?	{"Pathologic internal resorption","Calcification of the periodontal ligament","Slow, continuous deposition of secondary dentin","Shrinkage of the enamel"}	2	As time passes, additional dentin (secondary dentin) is slowly deposited on the inside of the tooth, causing the pulp chamber to gradually shrink.	Aging Changes in Teeth	\N
b154784b-fd54-4c43-83d7-2c3a47108c00	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the equilibrium theory of tooth position, which factor is most critical in determining the final position of the teeth?	{"The heavy, intermittent forces of mastication (chewing).","The heavy, intermittent forces of swallowing.","The light, long-lasting resting pressures from the lips, cheeks, and tongue.","Intermittent traumatic forces."}	2	Equilibrium is dictated by duration, not just magnitude. Light pressures of long duration from resting posture are the primary determinants of tooth position.	Equilibrium Theory	\N
58b6c2a5-c34a-4182-8f73-d3df4db09bef	orthodontics	BDS-I	A child with a chronic thumb-sucking habit frequently develops a constricted, narrow maxillary arch. What is the primary mechanical reason for this?	{"Upward pressure of the thumb expands the midpalatal suture.","The tongue is lowered while cheek pressure is increased due to suction, altering muscular equilibrium.","The habit stimulates excessive condylar growth.","Thumb-sucking causes premature fusion of the palatal shelves."}	1	Thumb sucking lowers the tongue, removing its resting pressure from the palate, while the suction increases pressure from the cheek muscles, physically narrowing the arch.	Sucking Habits	\N
2b6adb59-3ec9-4db1-8963-68006a3f68ae	orthodontics	BDS-I	The contemporary view of a "tongue thrust" swallow is that it is primarily:	{"The primary root cause of severe anterior open bites.","The main cause of posterior crossbites.","A physiologic adaptation to seal the oral cavity when a pre-existing anterior open bite is present.","A severe genetic neuromuscular disorder."}	2	Modern understanding views tongue thrust during swallowing primarily as an adaptation to an existing open bite (to create a seal), rather than the root cause of the open bite.	Tongue Thrust	\N
f494aa31-fcb6-4c84-ad6b-18394180d421	orthodontics	BDS-I	Chronic mouth breathing frequently leads to a specific altered posture and skeletal changes termed "adenoid facies." Which finding is characteristic?	{"Forward rotation of the mandible","Downward and backward rotation of the mandible","Severe mandibular prognathism","Flattening of the mandibular plane"}	1	To breathe through the mouth, the mandible drops and rotates downward and backward. Maintained long-term, this increases lower face height and leads to an anterior open bite tendency.	Respiratory Pattern	\N
