aeeb4d39-16f6-438c-98c4-4e704d686004	orthodontics	BDS-I	The most common supernumerary tooth found in the human dentition is located in the maxillary midline and is known as a(n):	{Paramolar,Mesiodens,Odontoma,Distodens}	1	A mesiodens in the maxillary midline is the most common supernumerary tooth and frequently interferes with the eruption of the central incisors.	Specific Causes of Malocclusion	\N
dc89a58c-add4-4099-b9c7-820f0edf9ca7	orthodontics	BDS-I	Excluding the third molars, which permanent teeth are the most frequently congenitally missing (hypodontia)?	{"Maxillary central incisors and mandibular canines","Maxillary lateral incisors and mandibular second premolars","Mandibular lateral incisors and maxillary first premolars","Maxillary canines and mandibular first molars"}	1	After the third molars, the maxillary lateral incisors and mandibular second premolars are the most commonly missing teeth.	Specific Causes of Malocclusion	\N
ac1516d9-14b2-4f5a-abd7-66b095492680	orthodontics	BDS-I	A severe distortion of root form, where the root develops at a sharp angle to the clinical crown, is usually the result of mechanical trauma to the primary predecessor. This is called:	{Concrescence,Ankylosis,Dilaceration,Hypercementosis}	2	Trauma can physically displace the developing permanent crown. If the root continues to form, it will form at an angle, resulting in a dilaceration.	Traumatic Displacement	\N
46ad0f52-6070-409b-91b9-03dfb597c0fe	orthodontics	BDS-I	The "neonatal line" is a distinct histologic mark of interrupted enamel formation caused by the physiologic shock of birth. Which permanent teeth typically exhibit this line?	{"Central incisors","Lateral incisors",Canines,"First molars"}	3	Because their calcification begins before birth, all primary teeth and the permanent first molars exhibit the neonatal line.	Early Stages of Development	\N
f8bfdc31-34d8-418e-a0f2-dda7f1814c2f	orthodontics	BDS-I	During a clinical examination of jaw function, what is the expected normal range of maximum opening for an adult patient?	{"20-30 mm","35-40 mm","50-60 mm","75-85 mm"}	2	A normal maximum opening for an adult is around 50 mm. Significant restriction may indicate TMJ or muscle dysfunction.	Clinical Evaluation	\N
8916e7b5-26d9-4a4b-b8cf-3cb0be76776d	orthodontics	BDS-I	Dental age 11 is clinically defined by the near-simultaneous eruption of which specific groups of teeth?	{"Maxillary central and lateral incisors","Mandibular canines, mandibular first premolars, and maxillary first premolars","Maxillary canines and second molars","Third molars"}	1	Dental age 11 is characterized by the eruption of the mandibular canines, mandibular first premolars, and maxillary first premolars.	Dental Age	\N
beebb505-afbf-43ca-8823-859d2acac2d1	orthodontics	BDS-I	Which diagnostic cephalometric plane is used to evaluate the "cant of the occlusal plane"?	{"The line connecting Sella and Nasion","The line through the overlapping cusps of the first premolars and first molars","The line connecting Porion and Orbitale","The line tangent to the lower border of the mandible"}	1	The occlusal plane is typically drawn through the overlapping cusps of the first premolars and first molars.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
fa14a11f-d3f2-47e2-8c79-3fb7481aa7f7	oral_surgery	BDS-I	The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) primarily focuses on:	{"Low-cost dentistry","Confidentiality of patient information","Mandatory oral surgery","Free radiographs"}	1	HIPAA ensures the privacy and security of health data. [Chapter 1, Medical History]	Medicolegal	\N
8e8848b6-066d-42f6-aad0-e833a90a4e3a	orthodontics	BDS-I	A patient is classified as having a Class II division 2 malocclusion. What classic dental feature distinguishes this from a Class II division 1?	{"Flared maxillary central incisors with large overjet.","Retroclined maxillary central incisors with a deep overbite.","A unilateral posterior crossbite.","A severe anterior open bite."}	1	Class II division 2 is characterized by retroclined (upright) maxillary central incisors and a deep bite, unlike the flared incisors of division 1.	Orthodontic Classification	\N
4c25aee2-36a8-4a75-bb7b-9905ba41c0a5	orthodontics	BDS-I	What evolutionary trend in human dentition is most directly correlated with the modern prevalence of malocclusion?	{"A genetic mutation specific to agricultural societies.","An evolutionary decrease in jaw size linked to a softer diet, outstripping the decrease in tooth size.","An increase in the prevalence of dental caries.","The introduction of refined sugars into the diet."}	1	The transition to softer diets led to disuse atrophy and a reduction in jaw size that happened faster than the evolutionary reduction in tooth size, causing crowding.	Etiology	\N
fa7d7af0-5875-4726-87ac-fad9ce257f07	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to the IOTN aesthetic component, treatment need is assessed using:	{"A cephalometric tracing analysis","A scale of 10 standard photographs showing varying degrees of dental attractiveness","A psychological questionnaire","Exact millimeter measurements of overjet"}	1	The aesthetic component of the IOTN uses 10 standard photographs representing a range of dental attractiveness to grade need.	Demand for Treatment	\N
8fd3d12a-216d-47d2-b402-b961ee5eaa51	orthodontics	BDS-I	During embryonic development, what structure gives rise to the conductive ossicles of the middle ear (malleus and incus)?	{"The frontonasal process","Meckel's cartilage","The primary palate","The palatal shelves"}	1	Remnants of Meckel's cartilage (the cartilage of the first pharyngeal arch) transform into the malleus and incus of the middle ear.	Sites and Types of Growth	\N
d44ed278-7813-4b7e-9860-2bf45e664cff	orthodontics	BDS-I	"Submerged deciduous teeth" are given what grade in the Index of Treatment Needs (IOTN)?	{"Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"}	3	Submerged deciduous teeth are considered an Extreme/Need Treatment anomaly, classified as Grade 5s.	Demand for Treatment	\N
b43a041c-e3b4-4f8a-bade-1f4642812bfe	orthodontics	BDS-I	A "mesial step" relationship of the primary second molars most frequently transitions into which Angle molar relationship in the permanent dentition?	{"Class II","Class I","Class III",End-to-end}	1	A mesial step is the primary dentition equivalent of Angle's Class I and correlates with a normal Class I permanent relationship.	Space Relationships	\N
b044f1f4-8e71-4b09-9f92-ce1201efbe6c	orthodontics	BDS-I	A "distal step" relationship of the primary second molars inevitably leads to which permanent molar relationship?	{"Class I","Class II","Class III",Edge-to-edge}	1	A distal step in the primary dentition is the equivalent of a permanent Class II relationship and inevitably leads to a Class II malocclusion.	Space Relationships	\N
d0bc54e0-b840-4536-9b99-df8bca6cea79	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the average amount of leeway space available per side in the maxillary arch?	{"0.5 mm","1.5 mm","2.5 mm","3.5 mm"}	1	The leeway space averages about 1.5 mm per side in the maxillary arch.	Space Relationships	\N
1e1350ba-4d4a-41ba-875e-c3b7198498a9	orthodontics	BDS-I	What is the average amount of leeway space available per side in the mandibular arch?	{"0.5 mm","1.5 mm","2.5 mm","3.5 mm"}	2	Because the primary second molars are significantly larger than the second premolars, the leeway space averages about 2.5 mm per side in the mandibular arch.	Space Relationships	\N
23d78522-9330-422b-930e-91fd4168577c	orthodontics	BDS-I	During pre-emergent tooth eruption, the extremely slow labial or buccal movement of the tooth follicle within the bone is referred to as:	{"Active eruption",Translation,"Pre-emergent drift",Translocation}	2	Pre-emergent drift is the slow movement of the follicle within the bone before the tooth erupts into the oral cavity.	Eruption Mechanisms	\N
c2e8bd66-081d-473e-960a-ec0135fb1be6	orthodontics	BDS-I	In cephalometric analysis, the phenomenon where a tooth moves through space strictly because the jaw bone it is embedded in is moving is called:	{"Active eruption",Translation,Translocation,"Interstitial growth"}	1	Movement of teeth relative to the cranial base is produced by a combination of translation (moving with the jaw) and true eruption (movement within the jaw).	Jaw Rotation	\N
1a257e13-bec3-491c-ae6d-475dbe02f0cd	orthodontics	BDS-I	The cascade of endocrine events initiating the adolescent growth spurt begins with the secretion of releasing factors from the:	{"Anterior pituitary","Adrenal cortex",Hypothalamus,"Thyroid gland"}	2	The process begins when neurons in the hypothalamus secrete releasing factors into the portal circulation to the pituitary gland.	Initiation of Adolescence	\N
90c34c1f-7d16-4480-adec-9846fda97a1d	orthodontics	BDS-I	In clinical facial profile analysis, the "facial convexity" is determined by looking at the angle formed by which three points?	{"Nasion, Subnasale, Pogonion","Sella, Nasion, Point A","Porion, Orbitale, Pogonion","Gonion, Gnathion, Menton"}	0	The angle of facial convexity is assessed by looking at the profile curve from the bridge of the nose (Nasion) to the base of the upper lip (Subnasale) to the chin (Pogonion).	Facial Proportions	\N
cbbe1c5a-84e7-4249-984d-755b751760fa	orthodontics	BDS-I	An anterior crossbite where the lower incisors are positioned horizontally ahead of the upper incisors is clinically termed:	{"Excessive overjet","Reverse overjet","Deep bite","Open bite"}	1	Reverse overjet (anterior crossbite) occurs when the lower incisors are labial to the upper incisors.	Demand for Treatment	\N
2478f79a-091c-4d2a-80df-60dda5a6261f	orthodontics	BDS-I	Vertical maxillary excess (excessive downward growth of the maxilla) typically results in which clinical appearance?	{"A \\"short face\\" with a deep bite.","A \\"gummy smile\\" and a long anterior lower face.","Severe mandibular prognathism.","An ideal smile arc with no gingival display."}	1	Vertical maxillary excess causes a long lower face and excessive display of maxillary gingiva upon smiling (gummy smile).	Facial Proportions	\N
fdd9efbe-a7c7-45ef-a3b7-9d6a1ddc1716	orthodontics	BDS-I	A cephalogram reveals a very steep mandibular plane angle. What vertical facial characteristic is this strongly correlated with?	{"Short anterior lower face height and deep bite","Long anterior lower face height and tendency toward open bite","A perfectly horizontal smile arc","Severe maxillary retrognathia"}	1	A steep mandibular plane angle usually means the mandible has grown primarily in a downward direction (backward rotation), correlating with a long lower face.	Cephalometric Analysis	\N
66a3ea2d-19ac-41e7-8fd1-2955948c8c21	orthodontics	BDS-I	According to NHANES III data, what percentage of adults in the U.S. have "ideal alignment" (0 mm of lower incisor irregularity)?	{~10-15%,~34%,~50%,~80%}	0	Only about 10-15% of adults have perfect 0 mm irregularity, while ~34% have well-aligned (0-1mm) incisors. Crowding is extremely common.	Epidemiology	\N
65bee9a0-4f1e-4374-84cb-34b5d08c2a0d	public_health	BDS-I	According to the textbook's organizational flow, which chapter immediately precedes "Planning and Evaluation"?	{"Survey Procedures in Dentistry","Indices in Dental Epidemiology","Research Methodology and Biostatistics","Dental Manpower"}	2	As per the Table of Contents, Chapter 14 (Research Methodology and Biostatistics, p. 360) directly precedes Chapter 15 (Planning and Evaluation, p. 400).	Textbook Architecture	\N
867329bb-366e-4df1-8c03-0803f0d94e72	public_health	BDS-I	According to the Soben Peter textbook structure, all of the following are categorized as specific disease "Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention" chapters EXCEPT:	{"Dental Caries",Malocclusion,"Oral Cancer","Survey Procedures in Dentistry"}	3	Chapters 4, 5, 6, and 7 are dedicated to specific disease epidemiology; Chapter 12 (Survey Procedures) is categorized under methodological procedures.	Epidemiological Categorization	\N
c91fd544-9b63-4810-94a8-8b20272661ef	public_health	BDS-I	In the methodological hierarchy established by the author, which chapter must a postgraduate student master immediately before progressing to "Indices in Dental Epidemiology" (Chapter 13)?	{"General Epidemiology","Survey Procedures in Dentistry","Research Methodology and Biostatistics","Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry"}	1	The source sequence dictates that Chapter 12 (Survey Procedures, p. 282) provides the procedural framework required before the application of Chapter 13 (Indices, p. 311).	Research Methodology	\N
3c3bbb64-9ef4-48b2-991c-67c93011b0eb	public_health	BDS-I	Following the sequence of clinical preventive interventions, which topic is discussed immediately after "Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests"?	{"Pit and Fissure Sealants","Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)","Finance in Dental Care","Dental Manpower"}	0	Chapter 18 covers Caries Activity/Susceptibility Tests (p. 431), followed immediately by Chapter 19, Pit and Fissure Sealants (p. 440).	Preventive Interventions	\N
320cf95a-7713-4299-9fef-4e27e275dcc6	public_health	BDS-I	Dr. Mahesh C. P., a key contributor to the text, is a Professor within which clinical specialty?	{Pedodontics,Periodontology,Orthodontics,"Community Dentistry"}	1	The contributor list identifies Dr. Mahesh C. P. as a Professor in the Department of Periodontology at Manipal College of Dental Sciences.	Contributor Metadata	\N
8f9561b3-71bf-4bd7-9a3c-94f858eebfae	public_health	BDS-I	Which clinical intervention topic represents the first chapter to appear immediately after the administrative section comprising "Dental Manpower" and "Finance in Dental Care"?	{"Planning and Evaluation","Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)","Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests","Pit and Fissure Sealants"}	2	The administrative chapters 16 and 17 (starting p. 411 and 421) are followed immediately by Chapter 18, Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests (p. 431).	Textbook Architecture	\N
d895c351-f89b-433f-b69e-28472327e067	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher seeking information specifically on the "Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Periodontal Disease" must consult which chapter and page?	{"Chapter 4; Page 83","Chapter 5; Page 110","Chapter 6; Page 133","Chapter 7; Page 158"}	1	The Table of Contents lists Chapter 5, Periodontal Disease, as beginning on page 110.	Disease Epidemiology	\N
80c6d104-cce7-4047-b8ee-4e05f675da4b	public_health	BDS-I	Which contributor is affiliated with the Department of Community Dentistry at Yenepoya Dental College & Hospital, Mangalore?	{"Dr. Abi M. Thomas","Dr. Rekha P. Shenoy","Dr. Balagopal Varma","Dr. Seema lyyer Bhalajhi"}	1	Dr. Rekha P. Shenoy is identified as an Assistant Professor in the Department of Community Dentistry at Yenepoya.	Contributor Metadata	\N
55eb2e12-3e0f-409c-aac7-01067af4fcd4	public_health	BDS-I	The textbook structure places "Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry" (Chapter 8) immediately after which disease-specific epidemiological chapter?	{"Oral Cancer",Malocclusion,"Dental Caries","Periodontal Disease"}	1	Chapter 7 (Malocclusion, p. 158) is followed immediately by Chapter 8 (Behavioral Sciences, p. 180).	Textbook Architecture	\N
272fec67-6073-4b26-a3d4-30080380b2f9	public_health	BDS-I	According to the logical progression of the source, "Indices in Dental Epidemiology" (Chapter 13) serves as the conceptual bridge between which two chapters?	{"General Epidemiology and Dental Caries","Survey Procedures and Research Methodology","Planning and Evaluation and Dental Manpower","Finance and Caries Activity Tests"}	1	The Table of Contents lists Chapter 13 (p. 311) between Chapter 12 (Survey Procedures, p. 282) and Chapter 14 (Research Methodology, p. 360).	Research Methodology	\N
af6f9960-ba48-4b1f-b159-2e5bdc9388fd	public_health	BDS-I	Which clinical expert is recognized with "Special Thanks" in the front matter and carries the designation of "Senior Periodontologist, Mumbai"?	{"Dr. Mahesh C. P.","Dr. A. Kumaraswamy","Dr. S. I. Bhalajhi","Dr. Arathi Rao"}	1	The contributor page includes a "Special Thanks to: Dr. A. Kumaraswamy, M.D.S., Senior Periodontologist, Mumbai."	Contributor Metadata	\N
88e8e623-0a66-4117-ba0b-a080b8089c97	public_health	BDS-I	Contrast the placement of specialized preventive treatments: Which chapter concludes the textbook's clinical intervention section, appearing after "Pit and Fissure Sealants"?	{"Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests","Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)","Finance in Dental Care","School Dental Health Program"}	1	Chapter 20, Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART), is the final chapter of the text (p. 447), succeeding Chapter 19, Pit and Fissure Sealants (p. 440).	Clinical Public Health	\N
ca5e9c6b-a571-425c-bff2-f2c9b6764eae	public_health	BDS-I	"General Epidemiology" (Chapter 3) begins on page 42. Which chapter concludes the introductory section of the book immediately prior to the start of Chapter 3?	{"Introduction to Public Health","Public Health Dentistry","Behavioral Sciences",Indices}	1	The sequence in the Table of Contents is Chapter 1 (Introduction to Public Health, p. 1), Chapter 2 (Public Health Dentistry, p. 22), and then Chapter 3 (General Epidemiology, p. 42).	Textbook Architecture	\N
9ce618e2-497b-4a78-83db-5f59861fc17a	public_health	BDS-I	Dr. S. I. Bhalajhi and Dr. Seema lyyer Bhalajhi, contributors to the orthodontic perspective of the text, are affiliated with which organization?	{"Manipal College of Dental Sciences","Ministry of Health, Kuwait","Amrita College of Dentistry","Christian Dental College, Ludhiana"}	1	Both contributors are listed as belonging to the Department of Orthodontics, Ministry of Health, Kuwait.	Contributor Metadata	\N
71ca07c4-f618-42dd-ba65-cec7cb566d08	public_health	BDS-I	A public health dentist advises a local government to ban the advertisement of high-sugar snacks targeting children under 12. This action represents:	{"Primordial Prevention","Primary Prevention","Secondary Prevention","Tertiary Prevention"}	0	Primordial prevention targets the social and environmental conditions that lead to the emergence of risk factors. Unlike primary prevention, which manages established risk factors (like topical fluoride), this strategy prevents the risk factor (sugar consumption habit) from developing.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
ede8ae5a-47de-471c-bb74-8bb2d1535c0c	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher follows 500 individuals with high plaque scores and 500 with low plaque scores over 5 years to see who develops periodontitis. This study design is a:	{"Case-Control Study","Retrospective Cohort","Prospective Cohort","Cross-sectional Study"}	2	The study starts with the exposure (plaque status) and moves forward in time to observe the outcome (periodontitis), defining a prospective cohort. Case-control studies are inefficient here as they start with the disease and look backward at exposure.	General Epidemiology	\N
12410886-9ff8-4524-aa01-23bbcf9fdbf7	public_health	BDS-I	All of the following are components of the "Epidemiological Triad" except:	{"Host factors","Agent factors","Environmental factors","Time factors"}	3	The classic triad consists of Agent, Host, and Environment. While "Time" is often considered the fourth dimension in advanced models (the "Epidemiological Tetrad"), it is not part of the primary triad described in Soben Peter.	General Epidemiology	\N
a82e80d3-e32f-4cf7-a164-7fe59f154c9a	public_health	BDS-I	In the natural history of dental caries, "Screening for early white spot lesions" falls under which level of prevention?	{Primordial,Primary,Secondary,Tertiary}	2	Secondary prevention involves early diagnosis and prompt treatment to halt disease progression. Identifying white spot lesions occurs after the disease process has initiated but before cavitation, marking it as secondary.	General Epidemiology	\N
ffe248c2-d850-4608-a644-bfa7cb0f97d9	public_health	BDS-I	A patient presents with a deep carious lesion requiring a root canal and crown. This intervention is classified as:	{"Disability Limitation","Specific Protection","Health Promotion","Primordial Prevention"}	0	Disability limitation is a sub-type of tertiary prevention aimed at preventing further complications from established disease. Unlike rehabilitation (the other tertiary level), it focuses on stopping the current destruction.	General Epidemiology	\N
feb4e503-6a8d-4d9b-9e5b-dece01b8a0b0	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following statements regarding "Double Blinding" in a clinical trial is incorrect?	{"It eliminates investigator bias","It eliminates subject bias","It is always ethically feasible","It requires a third party for code breaking"}	2	While double blinding is the gold standard for reducing bias, it is not always ethically or practically feasible (e.g., in surgical trials where a "sham" surgery might be harmful). Blinding focuses on objectivity, not ethics.	General Epidemiology	\N
a978e160-e0b9-45c9-b142-1f5bf81f7844	public_health	BDS-I	A diagnostic test for oral cancer has high "Sensitivity." This specifically means the test:	{"Minimizes False Positives","Minimizes False Negatives","Is highly reproducible","Measures what it claims to measure"}	1	Sensitivity is the ability of a test to correctly identify those with the disease (True Positives); therefore, high sensitivity results in fewer False Negatives. High "Specificity" would minimize False Positives.	General Epidemiology	\N
26704a06-7939-4819-9b8d-5e807f269ecd	public_health	BDS-I	John Snow’s investigation of the 1854 Cholera outbreak is a classic example of:	{"Experimental Epidemiology","Analytical Epidemiology","Descriptive Epidemiology","Theoretical Epidemiology"}	2	Snow described the distribution of cases by "Place" (the Broad Street Pump), which is the hallmark of descriptive epidemiology. Analytical epidemiology would involve testing a formal hypothesis with a control group.	General Epidemiology	\N
6a9199c4-600c-47ea-86bb-39eb0b5fcb77	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is considered an "Agent" factor in the epidemiology of dental caries?	{"Salivary flow rate","Dietary sucrose","Tooth morphology","Socioeconomic status"}	1	Sucrose is the external substrate (agent) that the bacteria (also an agent) utilize to cause disease. Salivary flow and tooth morphology are host factors, while SES is an environmental factor.	General Epidemiology	\N
2e1a8f35-e2fa-4bd8-8f0f-05107db60b91	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Randomized Controlled Trial" (RCT), the process of "Randomization" primarily ensures:	{"Sample representativeness","Comparability of groups","Elimination of attrition bias","Increase in study duration"}	1	Randomization is used to ensure that known and unknown confounding variables are distributed equally between the control and experimental groups, making them comparable. Representativeness is achieved through sampling, not randomization.	General Epidemiology	\N
44799e65-f89b-4d17-ac78-bcc09a154aa0	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following describes "Herd Immunity"?	{"Immunity of an individual after vaccination","Resistance of a group to an invasion of an infectious agent","The genetic resistance of a population","Immunity passed from mother to child"}	1	Herd immunity occurs when a large proportion of a community is immune, making the spread of disease from person to person unlikely. It protects even those who are not immune by breaking the chain of transmission.	General Epidemiology	\N
9e535ea4-dcad-4261-80a3-56dc73695f24	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher studies the prevalence of tobacco use and oral lesions at a single point in time across different age groups. This is a:	{"Longitudinal study","Cross-sectional study","Cohort study","Randomized trial"}	1	Cross-sectional studies provide a "snapshot" of a population, measuring both exposure and outcome simultaneously. They are ideal for prevalence but cannot establish a temporal sequence (cause-effect).	General Epidemiology	\N
9c603ee6-012b-47f1-a82f-d54ab0ac94c8	public_health	BDS-I	Which level of prevention is "Water Fluoridation"?	{Primordial,Primary,Secondary,Tertiary}	1	Primary prevention involves action taken prior to the onset of disease, which removes the possibility that a disease will ever occur. Water fluoridation is "Specific Protection," a key phase of primary prevention.	Public Health Dentistry	\N
682c38b8-fa33-4e0f-8d3d-3aab6edd134c	public_health	BDS-I	The "Web of Causation" model is most useful for studying:	{"Single-pathogen infections","Chronic multi-factorial diseases","Acute trauma","Genetic disorders"}	1	For diseases like oral cancer or periodontitis, where multiple factors (tobacco, alcohol, genetics, bacteria) interact, the web of causation explains the complex interrelationships better than a simple triad.	General Epidemiology	\N
a0b32ab0-1b66-44ed-9029-7cc918a43f1b	public_health	BDS-I	Which historical figure wrote the "Treatise on the Teeth" and is the "Father of Modern Dentistry"?	{"Pierre Fauchard","Willoughby D. Miller","G.V. Black","Alfred Fones"}	0	Pierre Fauchard is credited with professionalizing dentistry and moving it away from the "tooth-puller" trade. W.D. Miller is known for the chemico-parasitic theory.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
0a14bc3e-3481-4ff0-b9c3-aa93f0200120	public_health	BDS-I	A 45-year-old smoker presents with 5mm pockets, 2mm of gingival recession, and radiographic bone loss. According to the current understanding of risk, smoking is a:	{"Risk Predictor","Risk Indicator","Risk Factor","Risk Determinant"}	2	A risk factor is a confirmed factor (usually through longitudinal studies) that increases the probability of disease. Smoking has a proven temporal and causal relationship with periodontal disease.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Periodontal Disease	\N
6d22d25d-221b-412f-80fe-9a1bc0889522	public_health	BDS-I	All of the following are "Modifiable" risk factors for dental caries except:	{"Frequency of sugar intake","Salivary buffer capacity","Oral hygiene status","Deep pits and fissures"}	3	Pits and fissures are anatomical/genetic "Host" factors and are non-modifiable by the patient's behavior. While they can be treated (sealants), the factor itself is inherent to the tooth.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
96d107f6-eab1-4694-ac25-de08de52fbb1	public_health	BDS-I	The "Critical pH" at which demineralization exceeds remineralization in the oral environment is approximately:	{4.5,5.5,6.5,7}	1	Enamel hydroxyapatite begins to dissolve when the local pH drops below 5.5. For fluoroapatite, the critical pH is lower, around 4.5, explaining why fluoridated enamel is more resistant.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
a48a93a8-6f53-4ec8-936b-9f62c2d6fab7	public_health	BDS-I	A patient from rural India presents with a firm, white, non-scrapable patch on the buccal mucosa. He has a 20-year history of betel quid use. The most likely diagnosis is:	{"Oral Candidiasis","Oral Leukoplakia","Lichen Planus",Erythroplakia}	1	Leukoplakia is a clinical term for a white patch that cannot be characterized as any other disease. Its association with betel quid and non-scrapable nature makes it the primary clinical suspicion for a precancerous lesion.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Oral Cancer	\N
92ff02de-1f04-4405-bc4b-2b82b5627ef0	public_health	BDS-I	Regarding the "Chemico-Parasitic Theory" by W.D. Miller, which statement is incorrect?	{"It requires a host (tooth)","It requires bacteria (parasites)","It requires a protein-rich substrate","It requires an acidic environment"}	2	Miller's theory proved that bacteria act on carbohydrates (not proteins) to produce acid, which then dissolves the tooth's mineral component.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
cd0880ea-cc88-40d5-a37b-68fe35e17bee	public_health	BDS-I	"Specific Protection" against malocclusion includes:	{"Space maintainers","Fixed multi-bracket therapy","Orthognathic surgery","Serial extraction"}	0	Space maintainers are a form of primary prevention (Specific Protection) used to prevent the drifting of teeth after premature loss of deciduous teeth, thus preventing malocclusion.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Malocclusion	\N
813b261d-28e5-4d6b-a419-f382e6a010e8	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is the most common site for oral cancer globally?	{"Hard palate","Dorsum of the tongue","Lateral border of the tongue",Gingiva}	2	The lateral border of the tongue and the floor of the mouth are the most common sites globally due to the thinness of the epithelium and pooling of carcinogens like alcohol and tobacco.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Oral Cancer	\N
dddfdb3f-4bd2-4c8c-8b1f-310294aa1644	public_health	BDS-I	A child presents with "circular" caries on the labial and lingual surfaces of the maxillary incisors. This pattern is characteristic of:	{"Chronic Caries","Early Childhood Caries (Nursing Bottle)","Arrested Caries","Recurrent Caries"}	1	ECC typically involves the maxillary incisors first because they are bathed in fermentable liquids during sleep while the mandibular teeth are protected by the tongue.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
642cbaee-d2d8-450e-b981-9118e74b7f4b	public_health	BDS-I	In the TNM staging system for oral cancer, "T2" refers to a tumor that is:	{"< 2 cm in diameter","> 2 cm but < 4 cm","> 4 cm","Any size with bone invasion"}	1	T1 is ≤ 2cm; T2 is > 2cm but ≤ 4cm; T3 is > 4cm. T4 involves invasion into deep structures like bone or skin.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Oral Cancer	\N
31ea7cc5-9eed-4802-9793-b739008563ec	public_health	BDS-I	Interceptive orthodontics (Secondary Prevention) differs from Preventive orthodontics because:	{"It happens before any signs appear","It deals with an existing malocclusion in its early stages","It only uses fixed appliances","It is used only in adults"}	1	Preventive orthodontics acts to prevent malocclusion (e.g., space maintainers), whereas interceptive orthodontics acts when the problem has already started (e.g., correcting a developing crossbite).	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Malocclusion	\N
4c11bee9-8892-4e3d-ad9a-f7f8e7e4da5d	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following bacteria is considered the "primary colonizer" in dental plaque formation?	{"S. mutans","S. sanguis","P. gingivalis","F. nucleatum"}	1	Streptococci like S. sanguis and S. mitis are initial colonizers that bind to the acquired pellicle. S. mutans and anaerobic rods like P. gingivalis appear later as the plaque matures.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
4cf985ea-4dd9-4572-a178-ab1e3e51d897	public_health	BDS-I	A patient with a 6mm periodontal pocket and no mobility is categorized under which level of prevention for a deep scaling and root planing procedure?	{Primary,Secondary,Tertiary,Primordial}	1	Deep scaling and root planing are "Prompt Treatment" for an established disease state, which is the definition of secondary prevention. Tertiary prevention would involve a flap surgery or implant.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Periodontal Disease	\N
d9952d1c-b734-496a-92f3-e70f963e56de	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is an "Ecological" factor in the etiology of malocclusion?	{"Thumb sucking","Mouth breathing","Tongue thrusting","All of the above"}	3	Ecological or environmental factors include various oral habits that exert abnormal pressure on the developing dental arches, leading to structural changes.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Malocclusion	\N
6943dbc2-e9ee-4b90-9131-80550ca42179	public_health	BDS-I	The "Stephan Curve" demonstrates that after a sugar rinse, the plaque pH takes how long to return to the resting level?	{"5 minutes","10 minutes","30-60 minutes","2 hours"}	2	While the pH drop is rapid (within minutes), the recovery phase (remineralization) takes significantly longer—usually 30 to 60 minutes—due to the buffering capacity of saliva.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
492dc22b-cd96-4e87-ac97-f9a96a9a2a52	public_health	BDS-I	All the following are true regarding the "Pre-pathogenesis" period of a disease except:	{"It occurs in the environment","The agent and host have not interacted","The disease is clinically detectable","Risk factors may be present"}	2	Pre-pathogenesis is the period before the agent enters the host; therefore, there is no disease to detect. Detection only happens during the "Pathogenesis" phase.	General Epidemiology	\N
d3eff493-83e4-47da-8da1-030d848561ee	public_health	BDS-I	"Incidence" of dental caries is best measured using:	{"DMFT Index at one point in time","The change in DMFT over a specific period","OHI-S scores","CPITN scores"}	1	Incidence refers to the number of new cases/lesions. To measure it, one must record the status at baseline and follow up over time to see the "increment."	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
152186b2-3e33-49a9-9861-30bf3a9667f4	public_health	BDS-I	Which type of oral cancer is most common?	{Adenocarcinoma,"Squamous Cell Carcinoma","Basal Cell Carcinoma",Melanoma}	1	Over 90% of oral malignancies are Squamous Cell Carcinomas (SCC), originating from the oral epithelium.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Oral Cancer	\N
11e7dbd9-2170-4fa3-9965-7edf440d1c08	public_health	BDS-I	"Primary Prevention" of oral cancer includes:	{"Biopsy of a suspicious lesion","Surgical excision","Tobacco cessation counseling","Radiation therapy"}	2	Tobacco cessation targets the cause of the disease before it manifests, making it a primary preventive measure. Biopsy and surgery are secondary and tertiary, respectively.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Oral Cancer	\N
ebc64463-8e43-4e3f-af5a-d0a2c61111af	public_health	BDS-I	"Vipeholm Study" was significant because it proved:	{"Fluoride reduces caries","Frequency of sugar intake is more important than total amount","S. mutans causes caries","Brushing reduces gingivitis"}	1	This 1940s study on institutionalized patients demonstrated that sugar consumed between meals (sticky form) was far more cariogenic than sugar consumed with meals.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
9ec93ebf-6178-4147-905d-340607b107e3	public_health	BDS-I	A patient presents with generalized gingival bleeding but no pocketing or attachment loss. This condition is:	{"Reversible and represents Secondary Prevention if treated","Irreversible and represents Tertiary Prevention","Reversible and represents Primordial Prevention","Irreversible and represents Primary Prevention"}	0	Gingivitis is a reversible disease. Treating it (Early Diagnosis and Prompt Treatment) is a classic secondary prevention strategy to prevent progression to periodontitis.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Periodontal Disease	\N
cadd286f-0dae-4f32-b859-ee345f7b85a0	public_health	BDS-I	Systemic fluoride delivery is primarily categorized as:	{"Topical effect","Pre-eruptive benefit","Post-eruptive benefit","Only effective for adults"}	1	Systemic fluoride is ingested and incorporated into the developing enamel crystals (hydroxyapatite to fluoroapatite) before the tooth erupts.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
2fdf94e4-e355-4a90-89ee-eaf137e65076	public_health	BDS-I	The "Certainly Lethal Dose" (CLD) of fluoride for a 70kg adult is:	{"32-64 mg F/kg","5-10 mg F/kg","1 mg F/kg","500 mg Total"}	0	The CLD is estimated at 32-64 mg of fluoride per kilogram of body weight. For an average adult, this is roughly 2.5 to 5.0 grams.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
a3098415-e5af-4ec4-8afe-ef65e9d332ac	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is NOT a mechanism of action for topical fluoride?	{"Inhibition of enolase enzyme","Promotion of remineralization","Decreasing surface energy of enamel","Systemic incorporation into the pulp"}	3	Topical fluoride acts on the surface of the erupted tooth. It does not enter the systemic circulation to any degree that would affect the pulp or developing teeth.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
ade7c273-47ea-4280-99a5-000f933e966f	public_health	BDS-I	A 6-year-old child in a community with 0.2 ppm fluoride in the water should be prescribed how much supplemental fluoride?	{None,"0.25 mg/day","0.50 mg/day","1.00 mg/day"}	3	According to ADA/AAP guidelines, children aged 6-16 in areas with <0.3 ppm fluoride require 1.0 mg/day to compensate for the deficiency.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
33ba2c66-2b9a-454c-8b4a-cca2fa653464	public_health	BDS-I	Acidulated Phosphate Fluoride (APF) is contraindicated in patients with:	{"Composite and Porcelain restorations","High caries risk","Deep pits and fissures","Orthodontic brackets"}	0	The 1.23% APF has an acidic pH (3.0-3.5) which can etch and roughen the surface of porcelain and resin-based composite restorations.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
068d7e48-1340-488e-9869-280b72920137	public_health	BDS-I	The concentration of fluoride in 1.23% APF gel is:	{"12,300 ppm","1,230 ppm","22,600 ppm","5,000 ppm"}	0	1.23% weight/volume translates to 12.3 mg per ml, which is 12,300 parts per million (ppm).	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
f1511386-2441-44fd-bd77-3927c1aeef62	public_health	BDS-I	The "Nalgonda Technique" for defluoridation uses which chemicals?	{"Aluminum salts and Lime","Chlorine and Iodine","Silver nitrate and Fluoride","Calcium phosphate and Alum"}	0	This technique involves the addition of Alum (for flocculation) and Lime (for pH adjustment and settling) to remove excess fluoride from water at the community level.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
b7816983-fe1b-482b-a8d7-ca4131684560	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is the first sign of "Acute" fluoride toxicity?	{"Dental fluorosis",Osteosclerosis,"Nausea and vomiting","Calcification of ligaments"}	2	Acute toxicity causes hydrofluoric acid to form in the stomach, leading to immediate gastrointestinal distress. Fluorosis and osteosclerosis are signs of chronic toxicity.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
8ecec853-3c24-4846-a703-b6593e64fabf	public_health	BDS-I	"Dental Fluorosis" occurs during which stage of tooth development?	{Initiation,Morphodifferentiation,"Apposition and Calcification",Eruption}	2	Fluorosis is a permanent hypomineralization caused by excess fluoride interfering with ameloblastic activity during the calcification and maturation stages of enamel.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
6cd9cfce-65e9-4249-9b75-e1e1d56fe8f7	public_health	BDS-I	In "Knutson’s Technique" for 2% Sodium Fluoride application, the recommended schedule is:	{"Once a month","Four applications at weekly intervals","Once every six months","Once a year"}	1	Knutson's technique involves a series of four applications given one week apart, typically at ages 3, 7, 10, and 13 to coincide with tooth eruption patterns.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
15ae6ea1-5f14-4bff-894c-5d33615a203c	public_health	BDS-I	"Snyder’s Test" is a caries activity test that measures:	{"Acidogenesis by oral bacteria","The number of S. mutans colonies","Salivary flow rate","Salivary buffering capacity"}	0	Snyder's test is a colorimetric method where a change from blue-green to yellow in the agar indicates the rate of acid production by the patient’s oral flora.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
e4a2a1b1-791d-4582-a40e-7fe4632342d2	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is a disadvantage of Stannous Fluoride (SnF2)?	{"Poor fluoride uptake","Metallic taste and extrinsic staining","High cost","Requires special lights for activation"}	1	Stannous fluoride can cause a brown tin-sulfide stain on teeth and has an unpleasant metallic taste, which led to the preference for NaF and APF.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
cb5ce839-80e1-42c2-bb60-8fea6f33b89a	public_health	BDS-I	The "Safely Tolerated Dose" (STD) of fluoride is defined as:	{"1/2 of the CLD","1/4 of the CLD","5 mg/kg body weight","1 ppm"}	1	The STD is exactly one-fourth of the Certainly Lethal Dose. It represents the amount that can be ingested without needing immediate emergency treatment.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
ac01f244-3db1-471e-b294-6ea585be610a	public_health	BDS-I	"Atraumatic Restorative Treatment" (ART) was specifically developed for:	{"High-tech private clinics","Re-treatment of failed composites","Underserved communities with no electricity","Pediatric patients under general anesthesia"}	2	ART uses only hand instruments to remove decay and GIC to restore, making it ideal for rural settings where power-driven drills are unavailable.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
c8c61e54-1428-4dce-ada3-808f1d5f45bd	public_health	BDS-I	Which material is most commonly used as a "Pit and Fissure Sealant"?	{Amalgam,"Bis-GMA resin","Zinc Phosphate","Polycarboxylate cement"}	1	Bis-GMA (Bisphenol A-glycidyl methacrylate) is the standard resin used for sealants due to its ability to flow into deep pits and bond to etched enamel.	Pit and Fissure Sealants	\N
4e73496a-e28b-4381-bc87-8d7186f732cd	public_health	BDS-I	The "Press-finger" technique in ART is used to:	{"Check for pulpal exposure","Compact GIC into pits and fissures","Anesthetize the gingiva","Test the hardness of dentin"}	1	After placing GIC, a gloved, petroleum-jelly-coated finger is used to press the material into the fissures, ensuring a void-free seal.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
b7d7528d-b2c1-4786-870c-4ebe8d8706a0	public_health	BDS-I	All the following are true regarding "School Water Fluoridation" except:	{"It uses 4.5 times the optimal fluoride concentration","It reaches children only during school hours","It is the most effective way to prevent fluorosis","It is a \\"passive\\" preventive measure"}	2	Because children only drink school water for part of the day/year, the concentration is higher (4.5x), but it cannot prevent fluorosis; it is designed to prevent caries.	School Dental Health Program	\N
fb758149-4ee3-472b-84b6-9a3f35ba6730	public_health	BDS-I	Which caries activity test is most specific for counting Lactobacillus?	{"Snyder’s Test","Rogosa’s SLB Agar Test","Albustix Test","Fosdick Calcium Dissolution Test"}	1	Rogosa’s agar is a selective medium that supports the growth of Lactobacilli while inhibiting other oral flora.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
aed1a87e-cbe0-44b5-aa50-cc561d04328f	public_health	BDS-I	The main reason GIC is used in ART is its:	{"High aesthetic value","Chemical bond to tooth and fluoride release","High compressive strength compared to amalgam","Rapid setting time in saliva"}	1	GIC is the material of choice for ART because it doesn't require moisture-sensitive etching (it bonds chemically) and releases fluoride to help arrest remaining decay.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
635e4e63-5f2a-44f1-bb2e-c6e2116e2dcc	public_health	BDS-I	"Varnish" fluoride (5% NaF) is particularly advantageous for:	{"Large community water tanks","Infants and toddlers (high safety)","Adult root canal therapy","Sterilizing instruments"}	1	Varnish is applied in very small amounts and sets quickly, minimizing the risk of the child swallowing large amounts of fluoride gel.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
3ca30ed9-1e25-430f-97fe-ad577d63a1bf	public_health	BDS-I	A school-based weekly fluoride mouth-rinsing program typically uses:	{"0.05% NaF","0.2% NaF","1.23% APF","5% Varnish"}	1	Daily rinsing uses 0.05% NaF, whereas weekly rinsing (common in schools) uses the higher 0.2% NaF concentration.	School Dental Health Program	\N
d7bfa9b0-14c9-4965-83ba-16630879e04a	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is the "best" predictor of future caries in a child?	{"Lactobacilli count","Past caries experience","Salivary buffer capacity","Snyder’s test"}	1	Extensive epidemiological evidence shows that a history of caries (DMFT/deft) is the strongest clinical predictor of future lesions.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
480bc2c5-9756-43b1-8076-93224941631e	public_health	BDS-I	The primary goal of a "School Dental Health Program" is:	{"To provide free orthodontics","To instill lifelong positive oral health behaviors","To replace private dental practices","To conduct genetic research"}	1	While treatment is provided, the pedagogical goal is health education and the establishment of self-care habits during the formative years.	School Dental Health Program	\N
0160b8aa-e780-4298-b100-5483abb97a0d	public_health	BDS-I	The "Halo Effect" of water fluoridation describes:	{"The appearance of white spots on teeth","Benefits to non-fluoridated areas from processed food/drinks","The reduction in systemic diseases","The cost-benefit ratio of fluoride"}	1	People in non-fluoridated areas consume drinks and foods manufactured in fluoridated cities, thereby receiving a "halo" of protection.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
e93a8799-79df-427d-8a15-294f8280c755	public_health	BDS-I	Which fluoride is "self-applied" by the patient?	{"2% NaF solution","5% Fluoride Varnish","0.4% Stannous Fluoride gel","1.23% APF"}	2	0.4% SnF2 and 0.05% NaF rinses are designed for home use. 2% NaF and APF gels are "professionally applied" due to their high concentrations.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
76046359-7fb7-4802-ad6b-a91dcf088ebc	public_health	BDS-I	In the WHO DMFT index, a tooth with a temporary restoration and a new carious lesion should be coded as:	{"Filled (F)","Decayed (D)","Missing (M)",Excluded}	1	According to WHO criteria, if a tooth has both a filling (temporary or permanent) and a carious lesion, the "Decayed" (D) status takes precedence.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
a79482bb-a072-47c1-9c3f-2d0d5f41848c	public_health	BDS-I	A patient has the following CPITN scores for 6 sextants: 0, 1, 2, 3, 2, 0. What is the treatment need (TN)?	{"TN 0","TN 1","TN 2","TN 3"}	2	The highest score is 3 (pocket 4-5mm). According to CPITN, a score of 2 or 3 requires "Oral hygiene instruction + Scaling," which is categorized as Treatment Need 2.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
9d7f1108-0db8-4586-a40a-acb17364f4d7	public_health	BDS-I	A study reports a p-value of 0.04. This means:	{"The results are 4% accurate","There is a 4% chance the null hypothesis was rejected when it was actually true","The effect size is 0.04","The results are not significant"}	1	A p-value of 0.04 is less than the alpha of 0.05, meaning it is statistically significant. It represents a 4% probability of making a Type I Error.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
bff00c77-6b46-43c4-92d1-545286e10ead	public_health	BDS-I	All the following are "Probability" sampling methods except:	{"Simple Random Sampling","Stratified Random Sampling","Quota Sampling","Cluster Sampling"}	2	Quota sampling is a non-probability method where the researcher selects a specific number of people from various strata, but selection is not random.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
d1a0e290-c44e-437c-bd75-8b6a80ccdd77	public_health	BDS-I	"Berkson's Bias" is a type of selection bias that typically occurs in:	{"Community surveys","Hospital-based studies","Random clinical trials","Longitudinal cohorts"}	1	This occurs when the sample is taken from a hospital population, which is not representative of the general community because hospitalized patients may have multiple co-morbidities.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
7e672bcf-1670-4464-850f-24da20910c54	public_health	BDS-I	A dataset of DMFT scores is: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 16. What is the "Median" score?	{1,2,4,2.5}	1	Arranging in order, the middle value in this set of 7 numbers is the 4th value, which is 2. The mean would be significantly higher due to the outlier (16).	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
66b3dbf7-f7be-4008-956f-ca978a113a31	public_health	BDS-I	Which index was specifically designed to measure the "Prevalence and Severity" of fluorosis?	{CPITN,OHI-S,"Dean’s Fluorosis Index","GI (Loe and Silness)"}	2	Dean's index uses a 6-point ordinal scale (Normal to Severe) to categorize the clinical appearance of fluorosis based on the two most affected teeth.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
d5ac7b18-1716-488f-92e7-ad4de343aaf0	public_health	BDS-I	The "Ramfjord Teeth" (16, 21, 24, 36, 41, 44) are used in which index?	{OHI-S,"PDI (Periodontal Disease Index)",CPITN,DMFS}	1	These teeth represent the whole mouth in the PDI. Option A uses different index teeth.	Periodontal Indices	\N
91fece83-09d1-4fe8-a23c-0a6c0b2ce72a	public_health	BDS-I	A "Pathfinder Survey" as described by WHO is:	{"A comprehensive census of a whole country","A survey using index ages and specific localities to assess needs","A search for new dental materials","A survey of dental schools only"}	1	Pathfinder surveys are practical, providing a reliable overview of oral health with minimum cost by focusing on specific age groups (e.g., 5, 12, 35-44 years).	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
56cdc6e4-b222-4bc2-9ba8-baabb87eb6bc	public_health	BDS-I	In the OHI-S (Simplified Oral Hygiene Index), which teeth are typically examined?	{"All 32 teeth","4 molars and 2 incisors","12 specific surfaces","28 teeth"}	1	OHI-S examines 6 index teeth (16, 11, 26, 36, 31, 46) to represent the debris and calculus status of the entire mouth.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
d6ab345a-607b-4d6b-b13f-8505d6ce058f	public_health	BDS-I	"Type II Error" (Beta) occurs when:	{"We reject a true null hypothesis","We fail to reject a false null hypothesis","The sample size is too large","The p-value is 0.01"}	1	Type II error is a "False Negative"—concluding there is no difference or effect when one actually exists in the population.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
f6416ac7-06cc-4661-94ba-70ad58daa330	public_health	BDS-I	The "Chi-square test" would be most appropriate for comparing:	{"The mean DMFT of two schools","The proportion of smokers with and without oral cancer","The salivary flow rates of two groups","The average age of dental students"}	1	Chi-square is used for "Categorical" or nominal data (proportions/frequencies). For means of continuous data, a t-test or ANOVA would be used.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
9b17a97e-da07-4a75-9743-4737507da043	public_health	BDS-I	"Standard Deviation" is a measure of:	{"Central tendency","Dispersion (Variation)",Symmetry,Correlation}	1	It quantifies how much individual data points vary from the mean. A low SD means data points are close to the mean.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
ce2b8a45-2eda-4a57-8821-0cff3b302830	public_health	BDS-I	To calculate the "Degrees of Freedom" for a 3x3 contingency table in a Chi-square test, the value is:	{9,6,4,2}	2	Degrees of Freedom (df) = (Rows - 1) x (Columns - 1). Here, (3-1) x (3-1) = 2 x 2 = 4.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
c680e034-402b-4fd2-8fc5-cb47abfbda67	public_health	BDS-I	Which index is "Reversible"?	{DMFT,"Dean’s Index","Gingival Index (GI)",deft}	2	Reversible indices measure conditions that can return to health (like gingival inflammation). DMFT and Fluorosis index are irreversible as they measure permanent structural changes.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
00bcd288-abd0-4cea-b4c3-02b39b5074ef	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Negatively Skewed" distribution:	{"Mean > Median > Mode","Mode > Median > Mean","Mean = Median = Mode","The tail is on the right"}	1	In a negative skew, the long tail is on the left (lower values), which pulls the mean down below the median and mode.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
ce5fbe7b-6edd-4b32-b667-511e931ab164	public_health	BDS-I	The "Russell’s Periodontal Index" (PI) was unique because it:	{"Measured only gingival bleeding","Included a \\"Rule of Precedence\\"","Used both clinical and radiographic findings","Was only used for children"}	2	PI was a composite index that looked at both inflammation and advanced destruction (bone loss), assigning a higher score (8) to radiographic bone loss.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
c82b2832-c401-432a-958c-b0542a96789e	public_health	BDS-I	A "Correlation Coefficient" (r) of +0.1 indicates:	{"A strong positive relationship","A weak positive relationship","No relationship","A perfect positive relationship"}	1	Values of 'r' range from -1 to +1. 0.1 is close to zero, indicating that while there is a positive direction, the relationship is very weak.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
146b6247-3f54-432b-9ddd-b8ee76973410	public_health	BDS-I	"Intra-examiner Reliability" refers to:	{"Consistency between two different examiners","Consistency of the same examiner over time","The accuracy of the tool compared to a gold standard","The number of participants in a survey"}	1	Reliability is about consistency. "Intra-" means the same examiner should give the same score to the same patient when examined twice.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
4866b5d2-eb28-4706-be7e-5cc5f10da1e2	public_health	BDS-I	Which sampling method is best for a population that is divided into natural groups like "Villages" or "Schools"?	{"Stratified Random Sampling","Cluster Sampling","Systematic Sampling","Convenience Sampling"}	1	Cluster sampling is used when the population is naturally grouped; it is more cost-effective than simple random sampling for large geographic areas.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
9c221127-d202-41da-be1d-bc8f3e2f077a	public_health	BDS-I	All the following are "Measures of Central Tendency" except:	{Mean,Median,Range,Mode}	2	Range is a measure of "Dispersion" or spread, describing the difference between the maximum and minimum values, not the center.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
f14f3567-6669-41f9-9146-e7dc0afb6dee	public_health	BDS-I	A 5-year-old has 4 decayed primary molars and 1 extracted primary molar due to caries. The 'deft' score is:	{4,5,1}	1	In 'deft', d=decayed, e=extracted (due to caries), t=filled. Here, d=4 and e=1, so deft = 5.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
a8cc35a1-421e-4ac1-94ac-a845a61443b3	public_health	BDS-I	"Calibration" of examiners before a survey is done to:	{"Reduce sample size","Minimize measurement bias and ensure uniformity","Increase the speed of examination","Make the survey more expensive"}	1	Calibration ensures all examiners understand and apply the diagnostic criteria identically, reducing inter-examiner variability.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
9059f844-fd6c-4437-9e07-2ad9c877a217	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is a "Continuous" variable?	{"Number of decayed teeth",Gender,"Blood pressure","Social class"}	2	Continuous variables can take any value within a range (e.g., 120.5 mmHg). "Number of teeth" is a discrete variable (you can't have 2.5 teeth).	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
82a39c21-4f65-444e-b67c-f0ce9cc590d3	public_health	BDS-I	In Biostatistics, "Standard Error of Mean" (SEM) measures:	{"The error made by the researcher","The variability of the sample mean from the true population mean","The difference between the mean and median","The probability of a Type I error"}	1	SEM indicates how much the sample mean is likely to fluctuate if multiple different samples were taken from the same population.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
eb7938cc-11fe-4e7c-bea5-dea0bb584793	public_health	BDS-I	"Prevalence" is a measure of:	{"The speed of disease spread","The total burden of disease in a population at a time","The number of new cases per year","The lethality of a disease"}	1	Prevalence includes both old and new cases, providing a snapshot of the "pool" of disease existing in the population.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
2790c9b8-e5f4-4c99-a65b-f102a2356150	oral_path	BDS-I	"Leonine facies"predominant cell logic:	{Neutrophil,"Macrophage + bacilli",Lymphocyte,"Giant cell"}	1	Characterizes lepromatous leprosy.	Infections	\N
c70074b7-9b8e-42c8-8baa-c9ade113288b	public_health	BDS-I	According to the "Health Belief Model," a person's decision to get a dental checkup is most influenced by:	{"The cost of the appointment","Perceived susceptibility to and severity of dental disease","The distance to the clinic","The brand of toothpaste they use"}	1	This model focuses on the patient’s psychological readiness, specifically whether they believe they are at risk (susceptibility) and that the disease has serious consequences (severity).	Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry	\N
cd5a21e0-82fc-4fa6-a98a-91a0cfe92faf	public_health	BDS-I	All the following are barriers to "Effective Communication" except:	{"Using dental terminology (jargon)","Active listening","Selective perception","Emotional distress"}	1	Active listening is a facilitator of communication. Jargon, emotional state, and selective hearing are all barriers that prevent the message from being received correctly.	Oral Health Education and Health Promotion	\N
c26e85ee-8fbb-486e-800d-81bb741f2615	public_health	BDS-I	The recommended "Dentist-to-Population Ratio" by WHO for basic dental care in developing countries is:	{0.1111111111111111,"1:7,500","1:50,000","1:1,000,000"}	1	While ratios in developed nations are often 1:2000, WHO suggests a target of 1:7,500 for developing countries to ensure a minimum level of service.	Dental Manpower	\N
1de76294-0b76-41aa-93fb-ca50a6eb1a76	public_health	BDS-I	A "Capitation" payment system is one where:	{"The dentist is paid for each filling","The dentist is paid a fixed monthly fee per enrolled patient","The government pays all fees","The patient pays a percentage of the bill"}	1	Capitation is a prospective payment method. It shifts the risk to the provider and encourages preventive care to keep the patient healthy (and thus more profitable).	Finance in Dental Care	\N
867d3e89-09bc-4b00-a7ad-8c47fb8bf3fa	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is an example of "Non-Verbal" communication?	{"Speaking slowly","Writing a prescription","Eye contact and posture","Giving a lecture"}	2	Non-verbal communication involves body language, facial expressions, and proximity. Writing and speaking are forms of verbal/linguistic communication.	Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry	\N
