fb5915a0-25d2-457a-bd56-1d2d7460c6e9	public_health	BDS-I	All the following are "Principles of Primary Health Care" except:	{"Equitable distribution","Community participation","Specialized tertiary treatment","Multi-sectoral coordination"}	2	Primary health care (Alma-Ata) focuses on basic, accessible care. Specialized/high-tech (Tertiary) care is not a principle of the primary care level.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
3e6242e2-5798-4b68-98d5-9f5ed73aeaa5	public_health	BDS-I	The "Motivation" of a patient is best defined as:	{"The dentist's ability to talk","An internal state that activates behavior","Giving rewards for good brushing","Threatening the patient with tooth loss"}	1	True motivation is an internal drive. External rewards or threats (extrinsic) are usually less effective for long-term health behavior change.	Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry	\N
deed4d99-897e-4de4-9a7f-37b629acb1b4	public_health	BDS-I	"Direct Reimbursement" is a dental plan where:	{"The insurance pays the dentist directly","The employer pays the employee back for dental expenses","The government pays the dentist","No money is exchanged"}	1	This is a self-funded plan where the employer bypasses insurance companies and reimburses the employee a portion of their actual dental bill.	Finance in Dental Care	\N
8066546b-f3b5-4b69-bb51-ef9eb81eda17	public_health	BDS-I	The most effective way to change "Attitudes" in a community health program is through:	{"Distributing pamphlets","One-way lectures","Group discussions and active participation","Radio advertisements"}	2	Active participation and two-way communication (group discussions) allow people to voice concerns and be persuaded, making it more effective for attitude change than passive media.	Oral Health Education and Health Promotion	\N
e658a369-1e21-49fe-9828-b39d5284f95d	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher identifies 100 individuals with oral submucous fibrosis and 100 healthy individuals, then investigates their past history of areca nut chewing. This study design is:	{"Prospective Cohort",Case-control,"Randomized Controlled Trial","Ecological study"}	1	This is a retrospective case-control study. We start with the effect (disease) and move backward to the cause (exposure). According to Soben Peter, this is ideal for diseases with long induction periods.	General Epidemiology	\N
f5efbdeb-dc4d-4e00-acaf-397f550883a1	public_health	BDS-I	In a community of 5,000, 200 people have periodontitis at the start of 2023. By the end of the year, 50 more develop the condition. The incidence rate for 2023 is:	{0.05,0.04,0.01,0.0104}	2	Incidence refers ONLY to new cases. 50 new cases / 5,000 population = 0.01 or 1%. Prevalence would include the existing 200 cases.	General Epidemiology	\N
8bd2ac08-d695-48d7-add5-3f3091181be5	public_health	BDS-I	A diagnostic test for oral cancer yields a high number of "False Negatives." This indicates that the test has low:	{Specificity,Sensitivity,Reliability,Repeatability}	1	Sensitivity is the ability of a test to correctly identify those WITH the disease. High false negatives mean the test is missing actual cases, indicating low sensitivity.	General Epidemiology	\N
7cbe1fd3-63df-4a0b-afc5-5984b97dfb8a	public_health	BDS-I	In the "Natural History of Disease," a patient undergoing a deep scaling and root planing procedure to prevent further bone loss is at which level of prevention?	{Primordial,Primary,Secondary,Tertiary}	2	Secondary prevention involves early diagnosis and prompt treatment. Dr. Mahesh C.P. (Periodontology) emphasizes that arresting disease progression via scaling falls under secondary prevention.	General Epidemiology	\N
