2bd3bfd9-780a-42c1-8734-ef80fef9975b	public_health	BDS-I	Which measure is most appropriate to determine the "Public Health Impact" of stopping tobacco use on the incidence of oral cancer?	{"Relative Risk","Odds Ratio","Attributable Risk",Prevalence}	2	Attributable Risk (AR) indicates how much of the disease can be reduced if the risk factor is eliminated, making it the primary measure for public health planning.	General Epidemiology	\N
cce662f5-defb-4fc5-869d-19d7c3cd4072	public_health	BDS-I	An investigator follows a group of 1,000 smokers and 1,000 non-smokers for 15 years to calculate the Relative Risk of developing leukoplakia. This study is:	{Retrospective,Cross-sectional,Prospective,Descriptive}	2	Following a defined "exposed" group forward in time to see who develops the disease is the hallmark of a prospective cohort study.	General Epidemiology	\N
3c7a3c17-d486-497a-a843-6a20739dc882	public_health	BDS-I	According to the "Vipeholm Study," which factor is most critical for the development of dental caries?	{"Total amount of sugar","Frequency of intake","Type of sugar (liquid vs sticky)","Both B and C"}	3	The Vipeholm study established that the risk of caries is greatest if sugar is consumed in a sticky form and with high frequency between meals.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
d63f36aa-5824-4862-b582-5f82fcffa5b9	public_health	BDS-I	In the etiology of periodontal disease, a "Risk Factor" that cannot be modified is:	{Smoking,"Diabetes mellitus","Genetic polymorphism","Poor oral hygiene"}	2	Risk factors like smoking and plaque are modifiable. Genetic factors are "Risk Determinants" or non-modifiable factors.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Periodontal Disease	\N
5a74fce9-a6fc-4f15-8d4c-88b44ed0ebde	public_health	BDS-I	In a case-based scenario, a patient presents with generalized gingival bleeding and 4mm pockets. According to Dr. Mahesh C.P., the "Primary Prevention" for this patient's future siblings would be:	{"Oral hygiene instruction","Flap surgery",Splinting,"Maintenance therapy"}	0	Primary prevention aims to prevent the onset of disease through health promotion (OHI) and specific protection.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Periodontal Disease	\N
0fac6fa1-0f20-4227-b483-568d45fc94f9	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is considered a "Host factor" in the epidemiology of malocclusion, as per Dr. S.I. Bhalajhi’s orthodontic principles?	{"Mouth breathing habit","Thumb sucking","Abnormal jaw size (Skeletal)","Tongue thrusting"}	2	Skeletal patterns and genetic inheritance are Host factors. Habits like mouth breathing are considered Environmental factors.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Malocclusion	\N
cf8c4dd3-20d6-404b-a80f-9808d7b65103	public_health	BDS-I	The "Sentinel Surveillance" system is primarily used to:	{"Screen 100% of the population","Calculate exact prevalence","Identify changes in disease trends at specialized sites","Provide free treatment"}	2	Sentinel surveillance involves a small group of high-quality reporting sites (e.g., specific dental colleges) to monitor trends rather than universal screening.	General Epidemiology	\N
6500fd6a-944c-417d-927f-9f72a443b923	public_health	BDS-I	A "Cross-sectional study" provides a "snapshot" of a population. Its main limitation is that it cannot:	{"Measure prevalence","Establish a temporal relationship","Calculate the mean DMFT","Be used for surveys"}	1	Because exposure and disease are measured simultaneously, we cannot tell if the cause preceded the effect.	General Epidemiology	\N
81fe728f-b7a9-4b8c-ae95-be8ff4d5d05a	public_health	BDS-I	In oral cancer epidemiology, the "Field Cancerization" concept implies that:	{"Only one tumor exists","The entire mucosal area exposed to carcinogens is at risk","Cancer only spreads via lymph nodes","Surgery is always curative"}	1	This concept suggests that repeated exposure to carcinogens (like tobacco) creates a "field" of premalignant changes across the entire oral mucosa.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Oral Cancer	\N
761d4e06-34b5-48ec-9329-8a8102cd8ddd	public_health	BDS-I	Which study design is most efficient for investigating a very rare condition like Amelogenesis Imperfecta?	{"Cohort study","Case-control study","Randomized Controlled Trial","Descriptive survey"}	1	Case-control studies are the design of choice for rare diseases because they begin with people who already have the condition.	General Epidemiology	\N
