db35d729-85bf-46c5-8e92-3df5eff57f27	public_health	BDS-I	Assertion: A Chi-square test cannot be used if any cell frequency is less than 5. Reasoning: Small cell frequencies make the Chi-square distribution unstable.	{"Both A and R are true; R explains A","Both A and R are true; R does not explain A","A is true; R is false","Both A and R are false"}	0	This is "Yates' Correction" or "Fisher's Exact Test" territory.	Tests of Significance	\N
2e89bb49-b328-4875-8d79-67a9cff7eda6	public_health	BDS-I	"Body Temperature" in degrees Celsius is an example of which scale?	{Nominal,Ordinal,Interval,Ratio}	2	It has equal intervals but no "true zero" (0°C is not the absence of temperature). Option D requires a true zero.	Data Types	\N
72d0dd3e-0da1-4a49-9f19-cb6a5246808f	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher wants to study a rare condition like "Gingival Fibromatosis." Which sampling is most feasible?	{Random,Systematic,Convenience/Purposive,Cluster}	2	Rare diseases often require purposive sampling of known cases.	Sampling Techniques	\N
4305b1bf-368a-46a1-a263-1a5c362006a7	public_health	BDS-I	The "Standard Deviation" is the square root of:	{Range,Mean,Variance,"Standard Error"}	2	Variance is SD squared.	Measures of Dispersion	\N
063ee1e0-6216-4467-a8ef-87aaa329af8d	public_health	BDS-I	If two variables move in opposite directions (e.g., as Fluoride increases, Caries decreases), the correlation is:	{Positive,Zero,Negative,Perfect}	2	This is an inverse relationship. Option A would mean both increase together.	Correlation	\N
8a0056ed-73d1-4ecf-b669-6be0b6d47349	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is a "Non-parametric" equivalent of the unpaired t-test?	{"Wilcoxon Signed Rank","Mann-Whitney U",Kruskal-Wallis,Friedman}	1	Mann-Whitney U compares two independent groups for non-normal data. Option A is for paired data.	Tests of Significance	\N
4001b71c-f912-4f2a-912f-f40924f49a40	public_health	BDS-I	The "Bell-shaped" curve is characteristic of:	{"Chi-square distribution","Binomial distribution","Normal distribution","Poisson distribution"}	2	Also called Gaussian distribution.	Research Methodology	\N
2fc2a995-b6f5-4486-823c-3f2f6ba107cf	public_health	BDS-I	"Incidence Rate" is a type of:	{Ratio,Proportion,Rate,Mean}	2	It includes a time dimension, which defines a rate.	Basic Measurements	\N
4bbf32f4-1edc-4fd0-8618-b796bd0a88ea	public_health	BDS-I	Which graph is best for representing the "Trend" of dental caries over 10 years?	{"Bar chart","Pie chart","Line diagram",Histogram}	2	Line diagrams are the standard for showing changes over time. Option B is for parts of a whole.	Data Presentation	\N
7d5cf307-ed73-409a-84aa-d0a3691db962	public_health	BDS-I	The primary purpose of "Randomization" is to:	{"Make the study cheaper","Ensure groups are comparable and reduce bias","Increase the sample size","Guarantee significant results"}	1	It ensures that both known and unknown confounding factors are distributed equally.	Experimental Epidemiology	\N
c7bcda98-c0e1-45ce-9f57-695d596ab3e4	public_health	BDS-I	According to C.E.A. Winslow's definitive 1920 description of public health, which of the following is NOT categorized as a primary objective achieved through organized community effort?	{"Prevention of disease","Prolonging life","Promotion of physical and mental health","Provision of tertiary specialized surgical cure"}	3	Winslow’s definition emphasizes the "science and art of preventing disease" and health promotion through community efforts; tertiary clinical cure is a medical function, not a defining goal of the public health movement.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
b4d7c637-e6e0-480f-a1fb-32cd3c8574d0	public_health	BDS-I	A 45-year-old patient with high caries risk is prescribed 1.23% Acidulated Phosphate Fluoride (APF) gel application. According to the Leavell and Clark levels of prevention, this intervention is classified as:	{"Health promotion","Specific protection","Early diagnosis and treatment","Disability limitation"}	1	Specific protection involves measures aimed at preventing a specific disease entity before its onset, such as immunizations or topical fluoride application to prevent dental caries.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
e6eee017-dc36-4231-ae1f-5debcdd266d8	public_health	BDS-I	In the epidemiological triad of dental caries, a sudden decrease in salivary flow (xerostomia) due to medication represents a change in which component?	{Agent,Host,Environment,Vector}	1	Saliva is a host factor; changes in its flow or composition modify the host's resistance or susceptibility to the cariogenic challenge.	General Epidemiology	\N
a6cf6910-12aa-4343-8c1e-ddf0d4f3be57	public_health	BDS-I	An epidemiological study identifies that the prevalence of oral submucous fibrosis (OSMF) is significantly higher in a specific coastal village compared to the rest of the district. This observation represents:	{Endemic,Epidemic,Pandemic,Sporadic}	0	Endemic refers to the constant presence of a disease or infectious agent within a given geographic area or population group.	General Epidemiology	\N
201619b9-34e1-4782-9c9a-5efd74ea0ecf	public_health	BDS-I	The first step in the "Health Planning" process is:	{"Setting goals","Situation Analysis","Resource allocation",Evaluation}	1	Planning must begin with an assessment of the current state of affairs, including needs, problems, and resources.	Planning and Evaluation	\N
29592b68-4cbb-4c6b-a080-7bea548539d7	public_health	BDS-I	A diagnostic test for oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) has a sensitivity of 95% and a specificity of 60%. If used in a mass screening program, what is the most likely clinical consequence?	{"High rate of false negatives","High rate of false positives","High predictive value of a positive test","Low overall diagnostic yield"}	1	High sensitivity ensures most diseased cases are caught, but low specificity results in many healthy individuals testing positive (false positives), leading to unnecessary anxiety and further diagnostic costs.	General Epidemiology	\N
59786b94-7e0a-4cea-b5ea-b4294e997de6	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher follows a group of 500 individuals with high tobacco consumption and 500 individuals with no tobacco history for 15 years to observe the development of leukoplakia. This study design is:	{"Case-control study","Prospective cohort study","Cross-sectional survey","Retrospective cohort study"}	1	Cohort studies start with an exposure-based group and follow them forward in time to observe the incidence of the outcome.	General Epidemiology	\N
465420a9-b941-4515-9781-6a2dbf0f56dc	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is considered the "Pathognomonic" sign of a successful primary prevention strategy at the primordial level?	{"Mass distribution of toothbrushes","Legislation banning the sale of tobacco to minors","Early restoration of incipient lesions","Specialized surgical rehabilitation"}	1	Primordial prevention targets the social and environmental conditions that lead to the development of risk factors; legislation is a classic example.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
5a55fdd1-62fd-433a-8b76-f8f9320f6128	public_health	BDS-I	In the "Web of Causation" model for Chronic Periodontitis, which of the following represents a "synergistic" environmental factor?	{"Porphyromonas gingivalis","Host immune response","Poor oral hygiene combined with smoking","Genetic polymorphism"}	2	The web of causation accounts for the multifactorial nature of disease where factors like smoking and plaque interact synergistically to increase disease severity.	General Epidemiology	\N
7e3ae15c-d403-4bd5-97b2-2a6f0655a6e8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which of the following is an "Antihypoglycemic" given parenterally?	{Glucagon,Insulin,Epinephrine,Atropine}	0	50% Dextrose or Glucagon is used to raise blood sugar. [Table 2-2]	Medical Emergencies	\N
86787af4-3931-4de7-a24c-d160a326d8d0	public_health	BDS-I	During a 12-month period, 50 new cases of dental fluorosis were identified in a town of 10,000 people. If 200 people already had the condition at the start of the year, what is the incidence rate?	{0.02,0.005,0.025,0.05}	1	Incidence is the number of NEW cases (50) divided by the population at risk (10,000) during a specific period.	General Epidemiology	\N
601e45af-83c1-433d-aa24-244e44a5a474	public_health	BDS-I	The concept of "Social Medicine" as described by Neuman and Virchow implies that:	{"Medicine is a social science and politics is medicine on a large scale","All dental clinics should be state-funded","Doctors should only treat social workers","Disease is purely a biological phenomenon"}	0	Social medicine posits that health and disease are influenced by the social, economic, and political structure of society.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
8d985881-ba51-4eb8-965d-b66d0017fce2	public_health	BDS-I	If the Relative Risk (RR) for oral cancer among alcoholics is 4.0, it indicates:	{"Alcoholism causes 4% of cancers","Alcoholics are 4 times more likely to develop cancer than non-alcoholics","4 out of 10 alcoholics get cancer","There is a 40% reduction in cancer risk"}	1	RR measures the strength of association; a value of 4.0 means the risk in the exposed group is four times that of the unexposed.	General Epidemiology	\N
97ba60ea-31df-4e63-b5b5-5e575e200e7d	public_health	BDS-I	The "Iceberg Phenomenon" of disease suggests that the clinician usually sees only:	{"The most prevalent diseases","Symptomatic and clinical cases","The preclinical stage of disease","The entire community burden"}	1	The tip of the iceberg represents visible, diagnosed disease, while the submerged portion represents undiagnosed or asymptomatic cases in the community.	Public Health Dentistry	\N
10fb775a-1918-4ba3-a378-7d4587eeeb43	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is NOT a pillar of Primary Health Care (PHC) as defined by the Alma-Ata Declaration?	{"Equitable distribution","Community participation","Specialized tertiary care","Multi-sectoral coordination"}	2	PHC focuses on essential, accessible, and community-based care; tertiary care is the top tier of the referral pyramid, not a core pillar of PHC.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
ca485228-babf-4afa-978e-f33e5d33e032	public_health	BDS-I	In John Hanlon’s "Basic Priority Rating" (BPR) formula (A+B)C/3 \\times D, which factor carries the most weight in determining whether a program should be implemented?	{"Size of the problem (A)","Seriousness of the problem (B)","Effectiveness of the intervention (C)","PEARL factors (D)"}	3	Even if a problem is large and the treatment is effective, if the PEARL factors (Propriety, Economics, Acceptability, Resources, Legality) are zero, the entire BPR becomes zero.	Planning and Evaluation	\N
4dd849e4-cff1-4971-9996-f0aeeb10664b	public_health	BDS-I	A patient presents with a deep carious lesion on the mandibular first molar with reversible pulpitis. Performing a pulp capping procedure represents which level of prevention?	{"Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention"}	1	Secondary prevention involves early diagnosis and prompt treatment to arrest the disease process and prevent further sequelae.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
a6748fcb-3a6a-49b7-bca7-bc4917a2b924	public_health	BDS-I	The Vipeholm study, conducted in Sweden, was unique because it demonstrated that:	{"Sugar amount is more important than frequency","Starchy foods are as cariogenic as sucrose","Frequency and the \\"sticky\\" nature of sugar intake are more critical for caries than total amount","Fluoride in water completely neutralizes sugar intake"}	2	Vipeholm provided evidence that sugar consumed between meals in a retentive (sticky) form caused the highest increase in caries activity.	Epidemiology of Dental Caries	\N
a6a05aec-b0c0-45ba-b1db-3bcbc1656e36	public_health	BDS-I	Based on the "Stephens Curve," the critical pH at which enamel demineralization is initiated is approximately 5.5. For a patient with significant gingival recession, at what pH would root surface (cementum) demineralization begin?	{5.5,4.5,"6.2 - 6.7",7}	2	Cementum and dentin have a higher critical pH (6.2-6.7) than enamel, making root surfaces more susceptible to decay in acidic environments.	Epidemiology of Dental Caries	\N
bc0040f4-21ee-4bc9-9f05-f22fab8090b5	public_health	BDS-I	The "Ecological Plaque Hypothesis" suggests that dental caries is a result of:	{"A single specific pathogen like S. mutans","Any bacteria found in the oral cavity","A shift in the resident flora balance due to an environmental change (e.g., low pH)","Genetic mutations in the host's saliva"}	2	Unlike the specific plaque hypothesis, this model focuses on how changes in the local environment (acid) favor the overgrowth of cariogenic species.	Epidemiology of Dental Caries	\N
b0928b21-302b-4032-9584-a06fa6e80132	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During the "Routine Review of Head and Neck," questions about "Tinnitus" belong to which sub-section?	{Eyes,Ears,"Nose and Sinuses",Oral}	1	Tinnitus (ringing) is part of the Ears review (Box 1-4).	Review of Systems	\N
28b0c29b-7485-4527-9ac4-3ff248402b85	public_health	BDS-I	The Hopewood House study provided evidence for the role of diet in caries by showing that:	{"Children on a restricted lacto-vegetarian diet with minimal refined sugar had significantly lower caries scores","Fluoride supplements are as effective as water fluoridation","Caries is strictly a genetic disorder","Institutionalized children always have higher caries due to stress"}	0	Even with poor oral hygiene, the restricted diet resulted in a DMFT of 1.1 compared to 9.5 in the general population.	Epidemiology of Dental Caries	\N
4aa8c306-9b54-472d-887d-541e9b13ca41	public_health	BDS-I	In a community periodontal survey using the CPITN probe, a 30-year-old patient shows the black band of the probe partially visible in the deepest pocket of a sextant. What is the assigned code?	{"Code 1","Code 2","Code 3","Code 4"}	2	Code 3 indicates a pathological pocket of 4–5 mm (black band partially buried). Code 4 would mean the black band is completely invisible (>6mm).	Epidemiology of Periodontal Disease	\N
0d1e269a-7c2f-4661-a7cf-1da9c71312ca	public_health	BDS-I	Which index is most appropriate for a researcher wishing to assess the "Total Caries Experience" including untreated decay, missing teeth, and restored teeth in a 12-year-old population?	{OHI-S,CPITN,DMFT,"Dean's Index"}	2	DMFT (Decayed, Missing, Filled Teeth) is the standard index for quantifying lifetime caries experience in permanent teeth.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
1cc177ca-87eb-40d3-8b79-a4f9642581cd	public_health	BDS-I	A dental survey of a 15-year-old reveals: Tooth 18 (Impacted), 16 (Deep occlusal caries), 26 (Filling with secondary caries), 36 (Extracted due to caries), 46 (Missing due to trauma). Calculate the DMFT:	{5,4,3,2}	2	D=2 (16 and 26; 26 is counted as D if secondary caries is present), M=1 (36; 46 is excluded as it was trauma), F=0. Tooth 18 (3rd molar) is excluded per WHO 97. Total = 3.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
d13647b7-2196-450f-ad60-de9ec0c7d7bd	public_health	BDS-I	In the TNM staging system for oral cancer according to Soben Peter, a T2 lesion is surgically defined as a tumor that is:	{"Not exceeding 2 cm in greatest dimension","Greater than 2 cm but not exceeding 4 cm in greatest dimension","Greater than 4 cm in greatest dimension","Invading the cortical bone or deep tongue muscles"}	1	Precise TNM staging: T1 is ≤2cm, T2 is >2cm but ≤4cm, T3 is >4cm, and T4 involves adjacent structures.	Epidemiology of Oral Cancer	\N
529dfc90-c818-435c-840e-f6338da415c4	public_health	BDS-I	The "Synergistic Effect" in the etiology of oral cancer refers to the interaction between:	{"Poor oral hygiene and malocclusion","Tobacco use and alcohol consumption","Vitamin A deficiency and UV radiation","Fluoride and sugar"}	1	When tobacco and alcohol are used together, the risk of oral cancer increases exponentially, far exceeding the sum of their individual risks.	Epidemiology of Oral Cancer	\N
2956d83d-91f3-45ec-81c8-645127cc5b67	public_health	BDS-I	Malocclusion is defined as a "Public Health Problem" rather than a disease because:	{"It is caused by a specific virus","It is life-threatening in all cases","It has high prevalence and impacts social and psychological well-being","It is only found in developing nations"}	2	Public health concerns itself with conditions that affect a large portion of the population and impact the quality of life (sociodental impact).	Epidemiology of Malocclusion	\N
1e09afa1-b205-408b-987e-0f3ec2137710	public_health	BDS-I	During a Stephens Curve assessment, the plaque pH drops rapidly after a glucose rinse. The most critical factor determining the "recovery time" to neutral pH is:	{"The type of toothbrush used","Salivary buffering capacity and flow rate","The age of the patient","The concentration of fluoride in the enamel"}	1	Saliva is the primary mechanism for neutralizing plaque acid; reduced flow significantly prolongs the acid challenge.	Epidemiology of Dental Caries	\N
e331a9d0-be28-4909-933e-405f3f67a924	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is NOT included in the "Epidemiological Triad" of Periodontal Disease?	{"Bacterial biofilm (Agent)","Host immune response (Host)","Professional dental scaling (Intervention)","Socioeconomic status (Environment)"}	2	Treatment or scaling is a "Level of Prevention/Intervention," not a part of the original Triad which describes the natural history of disease.	Epidemiology of Periodontal Disease	\N
692774c2-e8e8-4864-9761-ba5f7337f4f4	public_health	BDS-I	In a community survey using CPITN, a sextant receives a Code 4. What is the designated Treatment Need (TN)?	{"TN 0 (No treatment)","TN I (Oral hygiene instruction)","TN II (OHI + Scaling)","TN III (OHI + Scaling + Complex Periodontal Therapy)"}	3	Code 4 (pockets >6mm) necessitates complex therapy (TN III), which always includes the components of TN I and TN II.	Epidemiology of Periodontal Disease	\N
565596ea-0018-4775-80b7-8caa351fd5b9	public_health	BDS-I	Which clinical feature distinguishes "Aggressive Periodontitis" in a population survey from "Chronic Periodontitis"?	{"Presence of heavy local factors like calculus","Slow rate of progression","Rapid bone loss in a systemically healthy, younger individual","Association with systemic diseases like Diabetes"}	2	Aggressive periodontitis is characterized by rapid attachment loss in patients who often show minimal plaque/calculus.	Epidemiology of Periodontal Disease	\N
cc7e5475-9611-41cd-9137-d9c605691967	public_health	BDS-I	The "Oral Health Impact Profile" (OHIP) differs from clinical indices like DMFT because it:	{"Measures the number of missing teeth","Assesses the quality of life and subjective functional impact","Is only used for children","Requires a microscope for evaluation"}	1	OHIP is a sociodental index that captures how oral conditions affect daily functioning and psychological health.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
b6442fdb-b840-45b2-8e75-9de6b416503a	public_health	BDS-I	Providing space maintainers to a child following the premature loss of a primary molar is an example of:	{"Health promotion","Primary prevention of malocclusion","Secondary prevention of malocclusion","Tertiary prevention of malocclusion"}	1	Space maintainers prevent the shift of teeth and future crowding (malocclusion), which is primary prevention (specific protection).	Epidemiology of Malocclusion	\N
3a02008f-c91e-4ff8-a764-b28b1b3df7ec	public_health	BDS-I	The gangrenous condition "Noma" (Cancrum Oris) is most closely associated with which "Environment" factor?	{"High fluoride intake","Excessive sugar consumption","Extreme poverty and severe malnutrition","Use of smokeless tobacco"}	2	Noma is a disease of poverty, often triggered by malnutrition and infections like measles in immunocompromised children.	Epidemiology of Oral Cancer	\N
08ab1006-f4fb-40b4-871c-b2ca95e4638b	public_health	BDS-I	According to the "Rule of Three" in the Community Periodontal Index (CPI), if only one index tooth (e.g., 16) is present in a sextant:	{"The sextant is excluded","All remaining teeth in that sextant are examined","The single tooth is used to represent the sextant","Only bleeding is recorded"}	1	In CPI/CPITN, if the designated index teeth are missing, all remaining teeth in that sextant are examined and the highest score is recorded.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
3d535f5e-2c0a-4c95-8364-8c38c7b2c5a1	public_health	BDS-I	A community health worker identifies a "white spot lesion" on a child's tooth and refers them for immediate remineralization therapy. This represents:	{"Primordial prevention","Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention"}	2	White spot lesions are the earliest clinical signs of caries; treating them to prevent cavitation is "Early Diagnosis and Treatment" (Secondary).	Epidemiology of Dental Caries	\N
4859d764-0e04-4419-8496-cf13183e8322	public_health	BDS-I	The Community Periodontal Index of Treatment Needs (CPITN) was specifically designed by WHO to:	{"Replace the OHI-S","Quantify bone loss","Facilitate the planning of periodontal manpower and resources","Measure the amount of plaque"}	2	CPITN was unique because it linked clinical status directly to the required treatment intervention for population planning.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
e15ddd5a-c904-46d4-8bac-d173c2915997	public_health	BDS-I	A community in a region with a mean maximum daily air temperature of 28.5°C requires water fluoridation. Using the Galagan and Vermillion formula, the optimal fluoride concentration would likely be:	{"0.5 ppm","0.7 ppm","1.0 ppm","1.2 ppm"}	1	The formula relates water intake to temperature. In hotter climates (higher temp), people drink more water, so the fluoride concentration must be lower (0.7 ppm) compared to temperate zones (1.0 ppm).	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
ef0f8fc2-68fc-4756-a2e9-08cd6206a264	public_health	BDS-I	The "Post-eruptive" mechanism of fluoride in caries prevention primarily involves:	{"Making the tooth harder during development","Inhibiting bacterial glycolysis and enhancing remineralization","Changing the shape of the crown","Increasing the flow of saliva"}	1	While fluoride has pre-eruptive benefits (fluorapatite), its most significant effect is post-eruptive: promoting remineralization and interfering with bacterial enzymes.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
6f3fd12a-ff2e-4f89-ae1d-8364e988dd9c	public_health	BDS-I	Which method of systemic fluoride delivery is considered the most "socially equitable" and cost-effective for a developing nation with centralized water systems?	{"Fluoride tablets","School water fluoridation","Community water fluoridation","Fluoridated salt"}	2	Water fluoridation is the most equitable as it requires no individual effort, reaching all socioeconomic strata at a very low per-capita cost.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
4d677602-198f-4522-8f0c-d11da5f97aa5	public_health	BDS-I	The strategy of "Milk Fluoridation" was first pioneered in the 1950s in:	{"Zurich, Switzerland","New York, USA","Birmingham, UK","New Delhi, India"}	0	Switzerland was the first to implement milk fluoridation, often used where water fluoridation is not feasible.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
6e71ec45-42ea-4322-85b3-7f4ee7372e24	public_health	BDS-I	The "Probably Toxic Dose" (PTD) of fluoride, which requires immediate emergency management, is:	{"1 mg/kg body weight","5 mg/kg body weight","15 mg/kg body weight","32 mg/kg body weight"}	1	5 mg/kg is the threshold for toxicity where clinical symptoms are likely and therapeutic intervention is mandatory.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
b0dab1ae-8bdc-4e1e-bdc3-55dc4ae8254c	public_health	BDS-I	A 4-year-old child lives in an area where the water fluoride level is 0.4 ppm. According to standard supplemental dosage schedules, how much fluoride should be prescribed?	{None,"0.25 mg/day","0.50 mg/day","1.00 mg/day"}	1	For children aged 3–6 years in areas with 0.3–0.6 ppm fluoride, the recommended supplement is 0.25 mg/day. Option A is for >0.6 ppm; Option C is for <0.3 ppm.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
4f3caeda-7527-4239-9fc4-1ae072c284e2	public_health	BDS-I	Which property of Acidulated Phosphate Fluoride (APF) makes it contraindicated for patients with porcelain or composite restorations?	{"High pH","Low pH (acidic)","Presence of sodium","Bad taste"}	1	APF has an acidic pH (~3.0) which can etch and roughen the surface of porcelain and composite resin materials.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
1d6167a8-d5b4-4f84-9867-6b8dd495cdda	public_health	BDS-I	Which fluoride compound is the "gold standard" for community water fluoridation due to its high solubility and cost-effectiveness in large-scale automated feeders?	{"Sodium Fluoride (NaF)","Stannous Fluoride (SnF2)","Hydrofluosilicic acid","Calcium Fluoride"}	2	Hydrofluosilicic acid (or its salt, sodium silicofluoride) is most commonly used in large systems because it is a liquid and easier to dose accurately than solid NaF.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
eafc4887-ba99-411e-8f02-e90c518633e5	public_health	BDS-I	"Defluoridation" using the Nalgonda technique is indicated when the water fluoride levels consistently exceed:	{"0.5 ppm","1.0 ppm","1.5 ppm","0.1 ppm"}	2	1.5 ppm is the WHO upper limit for fluoride in drinking water to prevent the risk of skeletal fluorosis.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
10e442e5-116b-4f91-8e95-5882231f4505	public_health	BDS-I	The "Nalgonda Technique," developed by the NEERI in India, employs which chemical sequence for defluoridation?	{"Alum and Lime","Fluorine and Chlorine","Charcoal and Sand","Silver and Gold"}	0	This technique involves the addition of Alum (for flocculation) and Lime (for pH adjustment and settling), which are widely available and cheap.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
ee563b38-3e24-450c-9617-941280582052	public_health	BDS-I	Which topical fluoride application involves a 4-visit procedure at ages 3, 7, 10, and 13?	{"Knutson’s Technique","Muhler’s Technique","Szabo’s Technique","Dhillon’s Technique"}	0	Knutson’s technique uses 2% NaF applied in a series of four visits a week apart to coincide with the eruption of new tooth groups.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
a93cacd6-2498-4491-a8d6-a63502bcaee9	public_health	BDS-I	A major clinical disadvantage of using 8% Stannous Fluoride (SnF2) as a topical agent is:	{"Lack of efficacy","Etching of porcelain","Gingival irritation and extrinsic staining","High cost"}	2	SnF2 is highly effective but can cause a metallic taste, gingival "blanching," and brown staining of teeth due to the tin (stannous) ion.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
42b65141-f7ca-4720-b155-79a6ee16e7ad	public_health	BDS-I	In a "School Water Fluoridation" program, why is the fluoride concentration maintained at 4.5 times the optimal community level?	{"To make it taste better","Because children are only in school for part of the day","To compensate for the lack of toothpaste","Because school water is safer"}	1	Since children consume only a fraction of their daily water at school, the concentration is elevated to ensure the total daily intake is therapeutic.	School Dental Health Program	\N
80709b0d-ae5b-491a-96f6-7a611022f5d4	public_health	BDS-I	The "Atraumatic Restorative Treatment" (ART) approach is based on:	{"Drilling as deep as possible","Selective removal of infected dentin using only hand instruments","Using high-speed air rotors with water spray","Only treating teeth that are about to fall out"}	1	ART involves removing only the soft, infected dentin with hand instruments and sealing the cavity with GIC to preserve "affected" dentin which can remineralize.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
4fee2b76-f5e3-40a0-9cd5-586ff0041d2b	public_health	BDS-I	Which clinical condition is an absolute contraindication for performing an ART procedure on a molar?	{"A single-surface occlusal cavity","The presence of a periapical abscess or fistula","The absence of electricity in the village","A child who is slightly uncooperative"}	1	ART is strictly for cavitated lesions without pulpal involvement; an abscess indicates a need for endodontic care or extraction.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
4bef2773-371e-4214-8c4a-6e37c4705f59	public_health	BDS-I	The most common cause for the failure of pit and fissure sealants in a community program is:	{"Poor material strength","Salivary contamination during the etching process","Patient eating sticky candy","Lack of fluoride in the water"}	1	Moisture control is the most critical step; saliva prevents the resin from flowing into the etched enamel, leading to poor retention. Distractors like material strength are less significant than technique error.	Pit and Fissure Sealants	\N
bed92a9a-e933-47e5-9b78-8e9505d13397	public_health	BDS-I	Which "Generation" of dental sealants is characterized by autopolymerization (chemical cure)?	{"First Generation","Second Generation","Third Generation","Fourth Generation"}	1	First was UV light-cured; Second was self-cured (base + catalyst); Third is visible light-cured (blue light). Distractors are incorrect based on the historical evolution of the material.	Pit and Fissure Sealants	\N
8044f31d-70c7-4d71-be56-6c2e97421da5	public_health	BDS-I	The "Tattle-Tooth" health education program is designed to:	{"Catch children who don't brush","Provide a comprehensive dental health curriculum for schools","Screen for oral cancer in the elderly","Distribute free fluoride tablets"}	1	It is a well-researched, teacher-led curriculum. Option A is a literal interpretation of the name, which is incorrect in a professional context.	School Dental Health Program	\N
49182a5d-969f-4219-8be8-ff1ffd1b114a	public_health	BDS-I	A "Comprehensive School Dental Health Program" must include all the following EXCEPT:	{"Health Education","Specific Protection (Fluorides/Sealants)","Mandatory orthodontic treatment for all students","Emergency dental care"}	2	Orthodontics is a specialized service, not part of a "comprehensive" basic school health package. Options A, B, and D are standard pillars.	School Dental Health Program	\N
6e5f55aa-4cc4-47ab-ad83-111d7bdf4c9d	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Blanket Referral" system used in school screenings:	{"Only children with 5+ cavities are referred","Every child is given a card to see a dentist for a professional exam","No referrals are made to private clinics","Only children from poor families are referred"}	1	This system ensures no pathology is missed by referring everyone for a detailed exam. Distractor A is a "selective" referral approach.	School Dental Health Program	\N
6ba87b85-c5fb-432b-b6d1-7b3a2f897b61	public_health	BDS-I	Providing custom-made mouthguards to a school’s football team represents:	{"Health promotion","Specific protection","Early diagnosis",Rehabilitation}	1	Mouthguards prevent a specific injury (trauma) to a specific group, making it specific protection. Option A is too broad; health promotion refers to lifestyle and general well-being.	School Dental Health Program	\N
0ff09761-1b52-41f6-a62b-0c6cb9d4e7e9	public_health	BDS-I	Which property of Glass Ionomer Cement (GIC) makes it the "material of choice" for ART?	{"It is the cheapest material available","It has a chemical bond to tooth structure and releases fluoride","It looks exactly like natural enamel","It sets within 5 seconds"}	1	GIC’s chemical adhesion and fluoride release are its technical advantages. Distractor A is a reason for choice in public health, but B is the clinical reason.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
a1656884-ea40-4108-b8e8-59989bad2074	public_health	BDS-I	Fluoride varnishes (like 5% Sodium Fluoride) are advantageous in community settings because:	{"They require a dry field for 30 minutes","They set rapidly on contact with saliva and have a long contact time","They are used as a permanent filling","They are only for adults"}	1	Varnish "sets" in a wet environment, making it ideal for young children. Distractor A is a disadvantage of other topical agents like APF gel.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
8211a932-c959-42ab-a692-fd23170f569d	public_health	BDS-I	For a school-based "Fluoride Mouthrinse" program using 0.2% Sodium Fluoride (NaF), how often should the children rinse?	{Daily,Weekly,Monthly,Yearly}	1	0.2% NaF is for weekly/fortnightly use; 0.05% NaF is for daily use. This is a common point of confusion in MDS exams.	School Dental Health Program	\N
62c716e7-cc41-4884-ac0c-68fdce551f59	public_health	BDS-I	"Salt Fluoridation" has been highly successful in Switzerland because:	{"People drink no water","It allows for individual \\"consumer choice\\" while reaching a mass audience","Salt is more effective than water","Only dentists are allowed to sell salt"}	1	Salt fluoridation is an alternative to water fluoridation that respects "freedom of choice" while providing systemic benefits.	Fluorides in Preventive Dentistry	\N
e8cf8bb2-7aa6-46b4-ae7e-074b5c861718	public_health	BDS-I	A "Pathfinder Survey" is a WHO-recommended method that utilizes:	{"Only the most diseased individuals in a city","Specific index age groups and clusters to get a representative profile","Every single person in the population","Only people living in rural areas"}	1	Pathfinder surveys use index ages (5, 12, 15, 35–44, 65–74) to efficiently profile a population. Option C describes a census, not a survey.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
185b6d49-4bb9-485e-bb08-86bac336c609	public_health	BDS-I	"Calibration" of examiners in a dental survey is primarily performed to:	{"Increase the speed of the survey","Ensure the results are statistically significant","Reduce inter-examiner and intra-examiner variability","Hire fewer people"}	2	Calibration ensures all examiners use the criteria the same way. Distractor B is a result of data analysis, not examiner training.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
ee2c59ef-f563-4f93-9dad-8e0d3385b4d8	public_health	BDS-I	In a survey, the "Kappa Statistic" is used to evaluate:	{"The prevalence of caries","The degree of agreement between examiners beyond chance","The sample size required","The p-value of the study"}	1	Kappa is the gold standard for measuring reliability. Distractor A is measured by DMFT/indices; distractor D is for hypothesis testing.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
790dfac6-a122-4236-b810-2b016fe854f2	public_health	BDS-I	A researcher wants to study the gingival health of pregnant women. The town is divided into 5 wards, and the researcher randomly selects 2 wards and examines every pregnant woman there. This is:	{"Simple random sampling","Stratified sampling","Cluster sampling","Quota sampling"}	2	The ward acts as a "cluster." In stratified sampling (Distractor B), you would take a few women from all 5 wards.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
a3bf9ba0-29c4-406f-9277-cb52a79d7e04	oral_surgery	BDS-I	You press a finger into a patient's swollen ankle and it leaves an indentation. This confirms:	{Lymphedema,"Pedal Edema",Cellulitis,Myxedema}	1	Pitting indentation is the clinical marker for pedal edema.	CHF Management	\N
2e990902-138b-4e0a-b6ac-d6a71ff9e20a	public_health	BDS-I	To compare the Mean OHI-S scores of 100 vegetarians and 100 non-vegetarians, the most appropriate statistical test is:	{"Chi-square test","Unpaired t-test","Paired t-test",ANOVA}	1	Comparing the means of two independent (different) groups requires an unpaired (independent) t-test. Chi-square (A) is for proportions/counts.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
cc427a9f-71bf-44d6-832e-247e2165c2c6	public_health	BDS-I	The "Median" is preferred over the "Mean" as a measure of central tendency when:	{"The data is normally distributed","The sample size is very large","The data contains extreme outliers or is highly skewed","All the numbers are the same"}	2	The mean is sensitive to outliers, whereas the median remains stable. Option A is when the mean and median are equal.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
6c5abfbc-cfe7-4baa-84ec-16df451e8c0b	public_health	BDS-I	If a study reports a P-value of 0.001, it means:	{"The result is definitely wrong","The result is statistically highly significant","There is a 10% chance the null hypothesis is true","The sample size was too small"}	1	A p-value <0.05 is significant; <0.01 is highly significant. Distractor C is incorrect because 0.001 represents a 0.1% chance.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
a773d763-7e93-4703-91b6-e34571d9dabb	public_health	BDS-I	A "Type II Error" (Beta) in a clinical trial occurs when the researcher:	{"Rejects a true null hypothesis","Fails to reject a false null hypothesis","Uses a sample size that is too large","Collects data in the wrong language"}	1	Type II is a "False Negative" (missing a real difference). Option A is a Type I error (False Positive).	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
c0aae1dd-d600-4e43-9f86-5cfacc5ec919	public_health	BDS-I	A public health dentist compares the DMFT scores of a group of children before and after a 6-month fluoride program. The appropriate test is:	{"Unpaired t-test","Paired t-test","Chi-square test","Correlation coefficient"}	1	Since the same group of children is being measured twice, the data is "related" or "paired."	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
644d39f7-e20e-47b5-a819-d06974bdaa30	public_health	BDS-I	To determine if there is a significant association between "Tobacco Chewing" (Yes/No) and "Leukoplakia" (Yes/No), which test is used?	{t-test,ANOVA,"Chi-square test",Z-test}	2	Chi-square is used for categorical data (Yes/No) to see if two variables are associated. t-tests (A) are for continuous means.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
76ee9e6d-7ca7-498c-ba72-08c18130a617	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Normal Distribution Curve" (Gaussian), what percentage of the population falls between the Mean and +2 Standard Deviations?	{0.68,0.95,0.475,0.99}	2	95% of the data falls within +/- 2 SD. Therefore, between the Mean and +2 SD (half of the range), it is 47.5%. Distractor B is the total range (-2 to +2).	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
74775c70-f4e9-4ff1-ab6f-e355cdbde45a	public_health	BDS-I	"Standard Error of the Mean" (SEM) is a measure of:	{"The error made by the dentist during examination","The spread of data within a sample","How much the sample mean deviates from the true population mean","The difference between the mean and mode"}	2	SEM indicates the precision of the sample mean as an estimate of the population. Distractor B is the definition of Standard Deviation.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
e071cf85-5c58-4f9f-b6c4-38d537e14c9e	public_health	BDS-I	Which index uses a mouth mirror and explorer but specifically EXCLUDES the use of a periodontal probe?	{CPI,OHI-S,"Gingival Index (Loe and Silness)","Russell's Periodontal Index"}	1	OHI-S uses a mirror and explorer to check for debris/calculus on surfaces. CPI (A) and Russell's (D) require a probe for pockets/assessment.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
a18895bb-f26e-4307-a306-ee0fc384d6d0	public_health	BDS-I	In the OHI-S, a "Calculus Index" (CI-S) score of 3 indicates:	{"No calculus present","Calculus covering < 1/3 of the tooth","Calculus covering 1/3 to 2/3 of the tooth","Calculus covering > 2/3 of the tooth OR a continuous heavy band of subgingival calculus"}	3	Option C is a score of 2. Option B is a score of 1.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
f7f6b50c-a095-467e-803b-ae5831c68478	public_health	BDS-I	According to WHO 1997 criteria, a tooth with a temporary filling (like ZOE) should be coded as:	{"Decayed (D)","Filled (F)","Missing (M)",Excluded}	0	Temporary fillings are not permanent; therefore, the tooth is still considered to have active decay. Distractor B is for permanent restorations.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
1c760f97-6889-47b6-aeb5-07518aa67386	public_health	BDS-I	For a survey of 5-year-old children, the appropriate index to record caries in the primary dentition is:	{DMFT,dmft,CPITN,OHI-S}	1	Lower case 'dmf' is used for primary teeth. DMFT (A) is for permanent teeth.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
617e3fc6-1aa9-4a35-9d10-2de7809028a4	public_health	BDS-I	In "Systematic Random Sampling," if the population is 1000 and you need a sample of 50, the "Sampling Interval" (k) is:	{5,20,50,100}	1	k = N / n (1000 / 50 = 20). Every 20th person is selected.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
02a1cbc7-2aa2-4b5e-bfea-f941ca901247	public_health	BDS-I	A "Negative Correlation" (r = -0.85) between water fluoride and DMFT indicates:	{"Fluoride causes caries","As fluoride levels increase, DMFT scores decrease significantly","There is no relationship","Fluoride has no effect on teeth"}	1	Negative correlation means the variables move in opposite directions. It does not "prove" causation (A) but shows a strong inverse association.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
4b6e9b81-3d14-4f66-85cf-b412d8802ceb	public_health	BDS-I	"Standard Deviation" is mathematically defined as the:	{"Square of the variance","Square root of the variance","Mean of the variance","Difference between highest and lowest"}	1	This is a fundamental biostatistics definition. Option D is the Range.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
c098580e-13ff-4d82-bbde-3961a8959d26	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is a "Continuous" quantitative variable?	{Gender,"Number of teeth","Blood pressure","DMF score"}	2	Blood pressure can take any value (e.g., 120.5). Teeth (B) and DMF (D) are "discrete" (you can't have 2.5 teeth). Gender (A) is qualitative.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
e96d5483-44c9-41d0-bdb0-22a9bfd48e26	public_health	BDS-I	"Content Validity" of a questionnaire refers to:	{"Whether the font is easy to read","Whether the questions cover the entire range of the topic being studied","Whether the test gives the same result twice","Whether the participants liked the questions"}	1	Validity is about accuracy. Option C is the definition of Reliability.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
acdad01b-855d-4cc3-a038-0b3f6066138b	public_health	BDS-I	A "Snapshot" study that measures the prevalence of dental caries and sugar consumption simultaneously in a population is a:	{"Cohort Study","Case-control Study","Cross-sectional Study","Experimental Study"}	2	Cross-sectional studies measure the status of disease and exposure at a single point in time to generate hypotheses.	General Epidemiology	\N
14533dca-a5c2-4cf5-93d6-c27a601c30fb	public_health	BDS-I	In the 1997 update of the WHO Basic Oral Health Methods, the CPITN was renamed the "CPI" primarily because:	{"The index teeth were changed","The \\"Treatment Needs\\" (TN) component was removed from the clinical score","It was no longer used for adults","It used a different probe"}	1	The CPI (1997) focus is on the clinical status; the treatment planning was separated to simplify the survey process.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
548cb6c5-df8a-4341-8e4a-f46848d119b0	public_health	BDS-I	A "Pilot Study" is often conducted before a large survey to:	{"Get the results published faster","Test the feasibility of the survey and train examiners","Replace the main survey","Save money by not doing the main survey"}	1	Pilot studies are "mini-surveys" used to find flaws in the protocol.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
40501cea-e1a9-43d4-80eb-c043d4b66a58	public_health	BDS-I	"Non-response Bias" is most likely to affect the results of:	{"A clinical trial","A mailed questionnaire survey","A laboratory experiment","A single-case report"}	1	If people who don't mail back the survey differ from those who do, the results are biased. Distractor A is more likely to have "attrition bias."	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
707c893c-df56-4ffa-be9e-2f8c28770052	public_health	BDS-I	In a frequency distribution, if the Mean = Median = Mode, the curve is:	{"Positively skewed","Negatively skewed","Symmetrical (Normal)",Bimodal}	2	This is the hallmark of the Gaussian/Normal distribution. Skewed curves (A, B) have these values at different points.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
00d8815e-d4bf-49f6-939e-ebf827c940ac	public_health	BDS-I	In the "Transtheoretical Model" (Stages of Change), a patient who is thinking about quitting tobacco but hasn't made a plan yet is in:	{Pre-contemplation,Contemplation,Action,Maintenance}	1	Contemplation is the "thinking about it" stage. Pre-contemplation (A) is when they don't even see it as a problem.	Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry	\N
df37c757-2211-4344-aad1-0abe94363ecb	public_health	BDS-I	A patient with an "External Locus of Control" is most likely to believe:	{"\\"My teeth are bad because I don't brush.\\"","\\"My teeth are bad because of my bad luck/fate.\\"","\\"I can prevent caries by using fluoride.\\"","\\"I will learn the proper flossing technique.\\""}	1	External LOC attributes outcomes to fate or others. Options A, C, and D reflect an Internal LOC.	Behavioral Sciences in Dentistry	\N
f6c324ff-3b4b-4f8b-bb79-c16e76192002	public_health	BDS-I	The "Snyder Test" is a caries activity test that measures:	{"The number of S. mutans directly","The rate of acid production by oral bacteria in a glucose medium","The thickness of plaque","The amount of fluoride in saliva"}	1	Snyder test uses Bromocresol green indicator to show pH drop (acid production). Distractor A is for Lactobacillus/specific counts.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
8d9565b2-e29d-444e-9184-fa68cb4d92a2	public_health	BDS-I	A "Lactobacillus Count" of >100,000 colony-forming units (CFU) per ml of saliva typically indicates:	{"High resistance to caries","Low sugar intake","High current intake of fermentable carbohydrates","Excellent oral hygiene"}	2	Lactobacillus is an "indicator" organism for carbohydrate consumption. Distractor A is the opposite of the truth.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
ff054bab-194b-4b9c-b5ba-698d62f7be56	public_health	BDS-I	In dental manpower planning for India, the targeted Dentist-to-Population ratio in urban/semi-urban areas is generally cited as:	{"1:1,000","1:7,500","1:50,000","1:200,000"}	1	1:7,500 is the recommended ratio for urban dental planning in the Indian context. Distractor A is common in very high-income nations.	Dental Manpower	\N
714e3eec-ec12-4766-8d5e-45800b68bcbb	public_health	BDS-I	"Incremental Dental Care" is a strategy where:	{"All people are treated at once","Specific age groups (e.g., 6-year-olds) are treated first, adding a new group each year","Only the rich get care","Only emergency care is provided"}	1	This allows a system to build a "healthy generation" by maintaining them and adding new cohorts annually.	Public Health Dentistry	\N
6a3452a7-3862-44f8-a4c3-ff78f0061aff	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Third Party Payment" system, the "First Party" is:	{"The Dentist","The Patient","The Insurance Company","The Government"}	1	First Party = Patient; Second Party = Dentist; Third Party = Insurer/Payer. This is a common terminology trap.	Finance in Dental Care	\N
9f496415-3481-416f-9a24-4fc442fabde2	public_health	BDS-I	A payment method where the dentist is paid a fixed, per-capita fee for each person enrolled, regardless of how much treatment is provided, is:	{Fee-for-service,Capitation,Salary,Post-payment}	1	Capitation shifts the risk to the dentist, encouraging prevention. Option A is the opposite (payment per procedure).	Finance in Dental Care	\N
27ece1c9-aba8-4618-85b1-7363cd186546	public_health	BDS-I	"Post-payment Plans" (Dental Budget Plans) are primarily used to:	{"Pay for school checkups","Help patients pay for expensive treatments over time through loans/installments","Provide free care","Pay the dentist in advance"}	1	These are financial tools for expensive rehabilitative care. Option D is "Pre-payment."	Finance in Dental Care	\N
62eb1571-7d47-4530-9389-2a924be589fa	public_health	BDS-I	In the "Health Planning Cycle," which step must be completed immediately after the "Situation Analysis"?	{Implementation,Evaluation,"Establishing Priorities and Goals",Monitoring}	2	You must decide what to do (Priorities) before you can actually do it (Implementation).	Planning and Evaluation	\N
1ab2bb19-d8e0-4929-9fee-b3e9b139ee97	public_health	BDS-I	"Formative Evaluation" is a type of evaluation that occurs:	{"At the very end of the program","During the program's operation to allow for corrections","Before the program starts","Only when the program fails"}	1	Formative = "forming" the program while it runs. Option A is "Summative" evaluation.	Planning and Evaluation	\N
d9e8dae9-bbfb-46b0-aace-8367322861ce	public_health	BDS-I	Which caries activity test relies on the metabolic color change of a dye (like Resazurin) by salivary enzymes?	{"Albans Test","Reductase Test","Salivary Buffer Test","Swab Test"}	1	The speed of the color change reflects the metabolic activity of the bacteria. Distractor A is a simplified Snyder test.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
bb79c79e-1897-4f38-b360-e343b82f07de	public_health	BDS-I	A "Dental Hygienist" is classified as which type of dental auxiliary?	{"Non-operating auxiliary","Operating auxiliary","Tertiary auxiliary","Administrative auxiliary"}	1	Operating auxiliaries (like hygienists or therapists) work directly in the oral cavity. Distractors A (like lab techs) do not.	Dental Manpower	\N
86e63a01-01b7-428a-84b8-2285a3d8cf30	public_health	BDS-I	The "PEARL" acronym used in feasibility analysis stands for Propriety, Economics, Acceptability, Resources, and:	{Purpose,Legality,People,Planning}	1	Legality is the 'L'. Distractor A and C are common "logical-sounding" but incorrect terms in this formula.	Planning and Evaluation	\N
850b7ae0-48b2-4686-b471-27cb5d406ad1	public_health	BDS-I	The use of "Hand Instruments" in ART is technically superior to high-speed drills in rural community settings because:	{"They are more expensive","They selectively remove infected dentin while preserving remineralizable affected dentin","They don't require water","They are faster"}	1	Hand instruments allow for more tactile sensitivity to preserve tooth structure. Distractor C is a benefit of ART, but B is the clinical/biological advantage.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
faa17264-e2dd-446e-aba1-b843e1f9adb4	public_health	BDS-I	According to Winslow’s definition of Public Health, which of the following is NOT classified under the "organized community efforts" used to achieve health goals?	{"Sanitation of the environment","Control of community infections","Personal clinical curative services","Education in personal hygiene"}	2	Winslow’s definition emphasizes social machinery and community organization; while clinical services are vital, the definition prioritizes environmental control and social reform over individual curative interventions.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
19538bf8-96c3-476a-8374-560375a5787f	public_health	BDS-I	In the transition from the "Era of Disease Control" to the "Era of Health Promotion," the primary paradigm shift in public health was:	{"Focus on mortality rates only","Moving from survival to Quality of Life","Implementing mandatory quarantine","Shifting from prevention to cure"}	1	Soben Peter notes that the Era of Health Promotion (1974-present) moved beyond mere disease prevention toward achieving a positive state of physical and mental well-being.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
dc203f22-178f-4ce3-b800-ab71e4a60c7a	public_health	BDS-I	A dental condition is deemed a "Public Health Problem" if it meets specific criteria. Which of the following would DISQUALIFY a condition from this status?	{"High prevalence in a specific group","The condition is not perceived as a threat by the public","Existence of cost-effective prevention","It causes a significant community burden"}	1	For a condition to be a public health problem, there must be a public perception that the condition is a threat, in addition to high prevalence and burden.	Public Health dentistry	\N
d539817d-5051-45c1-94df-9a05157e98c7	public_health	BDS-I	When comparing the "Private Practitioner" to the "Public Health Dentist," which public health procedural step is the functional equivalent of "Treatment Planning"?	{Analysis,"Program Operation","Program Planning",Survey}	2	In the established parallel logic, the Survey is History Taking, Analysis is Diagnosis, and Program Planning is the community’s Treatment Plan.	Public Health dentistry	\N
d7ce7a75-e0d8-4234-af99-730175045ce7	public_health	BDS-I	In the community health cycle, "Analysis" of data corresponds most closely to which clinical phase in a private dental office?	{"Clinical Examination",Diagnosis,"Program Evaluation","Differential Diagnosis"}	1	Analysis involves the synthesis of survey data to reach a community diagnosis, determining the nature and extent of the problem.	Public Health dentistry	\N
f5e681ce-e6f8-4f52-bc72-09925e35b797	public_health	BDS-I	A community health worker provides specific nutritional counseling and plaque control instructions to a patient exhibiting reversible white spot lesions. This is:	{"Primordial Prevention","Primary Prevention","Secondary Prevention","Tertiary Prevention"}	1	Since the intervention involves health promotion and specific protection to prevent the onset/progression of disease at a reversible stage, it is Primary Prevention.	Public Health dentistry	\N
8212adfc-4ab1-4995-a71f-148f08c6be43	public_health	BDS-I	The "Social Engineering Phase" (1960-1974) in the history of public health was primarily characterized by:	{"Focus on basic sanitation","Shift toward chronic disease management","Focus on the germ theory of disease","Implementation of the Nalgonda technique"}	1	Following the Era of Medicine, Social Engineering focused on the social and behavioral aspects of chronic conditions like cancer and diabetes.	Introduction to Public Health	\N
6a8961a5-2c90-400e-ad14-daeaa5da1b17	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is a "Procedural Step" in Dental Public Health that aligns with "Payment for Services" in a private clinical setting?	{"Program Operation","Program Funding","Program Appraisal","Program Planning"}	1	Program Funding (via taxes or grants) is the administrative equivalent of the financial arrangement or payment for services in a clinical setting.	Public Health dentistry	\N
