1143e5d9-6056-4038-be7e-c6e6d77cc6d0	public_health	BDS-I	In the "deft" index for primary teeth, the 'e' component stands for:	{Erupted,"Extracted due to caries",Exfoliated,"Evidence of trauma"}	1	e' represents teeth that are currently present but indicated for extraction because of extensive caries.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
808992d4-0904-406a-8a08-196410fcc4e1	public_health	BDS-I	A debris score of "2" in the OHI-S index means:	{"No debris present","Soft debris covering up to 1/3 of the surface","Soft debris covering 1/3 to 2/3 of the surface","Soft debris covering more than 2/3 of the surface"}	2	Score 1 is <1/3; Score 2 is 1/3 to 2/3; Score 3 is >2/3.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
45cb2a06-4757-46a0-8462-b512c6d4241d	public_health	BDS-I	The PMA Index (Papillary, Marginal, Attached) was primarily designed to:	{"Assess the need for surgery","Measure the extent of gingivitis in children","Determine the depth of periodontal pockets","Assess plaque thickness"}	1	Schour and Massler developed this index to quantify gingival inflammation in epidemiological studies of children.	Indices in Dental Epidemiology	\N
0a4603ec-e998-4c67-b148-99edbeb9815d	public_health	BDS-I	In WHO survey methodology, "Reliability" refers to:	{"The truthfulness of the results","The reproducibility of the results by the same or different examiners","The speed of data collection","The clinical relevance of the index"}	1	Reliability (consistency) is achieved through calibration and standardization of the examination process.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
0f884a7d-a98e-42e5-9368-9f1c072536dc	public_health	BDS-I	A "Type III" clinical examination, often used in public health surveys, is performed using:	{"Complete radiographs and study models","Mouth mirror and explorer under good illumination","Tongue depressor only","Visual inspection under ambient light"}	1	Type III is an inspection using a mirror and explorer; Type IV is a screening using only a tongue depressor or mirror.	Survey Procedures in Dentistry	\N
ed273dd3-c509-4a80-9ad4-fabd91c7b67f	public_health	BDS-I	In a highly skewed distribution of DMFT scores where a few individuals have extremely high caries, the most representative measure of central tendency is:	{Mean,Median,Mode,"Standard Deviation"}	1	The Mean is sensitive to extreme outliers; the Median provides a more accurate "middle" value in non-normal distributions.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
0de9eece-c6bd-44ed-b65a-1ccd20e32080	public_health	BDS-I	A study reports a p-value of 0.03. How should this be interpreted regarding the Null Hypothesis (H0)?	{"The H0 is definitely true","There is a 3% probability that the result is due to chance; reject H0","There is a 3% probability the treatment worked","The study is clinically insignificant"}	1	A p-value <0.05 is statistically significant, meaning we reject the null hypothesis because the chance of the result being random is low.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
23de4b01-9a2d-4b67-9f92-c3b0711e7c99	public_health	BDS-I	To compare the "Mean" DMFT scores of two different schools, which statistical test is most appropriate?	{"Chi-square test","Student’s t-test","Correlation Coefficient",ANOVA}	1	The Student’s t-test is used to determine if there is a significant difference between the means of two independent groups.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
fe6bb3cd-2c72-4cbf-b2b2-854d46fb7f4d	public_health	BDS-I	Which test should be used to analyze the association between "Gender" (Male/Female) and "Smoking Habit" (Yes/No)?	{"Paired t-test",ANOVA,"Chi-square test","Regression analysis"}	2	The Chi-square test is used for categorical (nominal) data to determine if there is a significant association between variables.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
f5757e3e-bc9a-436d-ba5b-290d9f38d5c0	public_health	BDS-I	The "Standard Deviation" (SD) provides information about:	{"The average value","The degree of spread or variation in the data","The middle-most value","The probability of error"}	1	SD measures how much the individual data points deviate from the mean; a small SD means the data is clustered close to the mean.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
cf90cbdb-f97a-4168-8fae-5c2cabd0760d	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Normal Distribution," what percentage of observations fall between the Mean and +2 Standard Deviations?	{0.34,0.475,0.68,0.95}	1	68% of data falls within +/- 1 SD (34% on each side). 95% falls within +/- 2 SD (47.5% on each side).	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
808540a7-fe3e-49ea-8850-2a534883b498	public_health	BDS-I	Which sampling method ensures that every individual in the population has an "equal and independent" chance of selection?	{"Quota sampling","Convenience sampling","Simple Random sampling","Stratified sampling"}	2	Randomization is the defining characteristic of simple random sampling, minimizing selection bias.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
2f8f963b-287c-42a2-aa08-88b890403829	public_health	BDS-I	The "Null Hypothesis" (H0) essentially assumes:	{"The new treatment is superior","There is no difference between the groups being compared","The sample size is too small","The data is skewed"}	1	H0 is the hypothesis of "no difference," which the researcher tries to disprove.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
344595a8-b01d-446f-9b65-71e33bf8697a	public_health	BDS-I	Data such as "Gingival Index scores (0, 1, 2, 3)" or "Disease Severity (Mild, Moderate, Severe)" are examples of:	{"Nominal data","Ordinal data","Interval data","Ratio data"}	1	Ordinal data has a clear, logical ranking or order, but the distance between the ranks is not necessarily equal.	Research Methodology and Biostatistics	\N
ed091af5-a4b3-4b89-99d8-43125487b1df	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Tourniquet" is used in a dental emergency kit to:	{"Stop arterial bleeding","Assist in establishing IV access","Fix a fractured jaw","Stabilize a patient's head"}	1	It makes veins more prominent for catheter insertion. [Table 2-1]	Medical Emergencies	\N
c383fe44-13ae-440a-9292-8d6984f19dc3	public_health	BDS-I	Estimating the number of dentists required based on a ratio of 1 dentist per 5,000 people is the:	{"Health Need method","Health Demand method","Manpower-Population Ratio method","Service-Target method"}	2	This is a traditional method for manpower planning, though it does not account for actual disease levels or demand.	Dental Manpower	\N
47904d7c-ff4f-407a-8227-01057b18a17e	public_health	BDS-I	In a "Third Party" payment system, who is the "Second Party"?	{"The Patient","The Dentist","The Insurance Company","The Government"}	1	1st Party = Patient; 2nd Party = Dentist/Provider; 3rd Party = Payer (Insurer).	Finance in Dental Care	\N
2686ebd5-393d-45f6-be85-1f507130a955	public_health	BDS-I	Which dental payment system involves the dentist being paid a fixed amount per month for each patient enrolled, regardless of treatment provided?	{Fee-for-service,Capitation,Salary,"Post-payment plan"}	1	Capitation shifts the financial risk to the dentist and emphasizes prevention to keep the patient healthy.	Finance in Dental Care	\N
22f21e75-ec97-4ddc-bbf5-9d9329e76b34	public_health	BDS-I	"Summative Evaluation" of a community health program is conducted:	{"During the program to make changes","Before the program starts","At the end of the program to measure its impact","Only if the program fails"}	2	Summative evaluation assesses the final outcome and whether the objectives were met.	Planning and Evaluation	\N
4a47785f-3f8b-4f17-afb3-691b074aaad9	public_health	BDS-I	The Snyder Test measures caries activity by assessing the acid-producing capacity of:	{"S. mutans",Lactobacilli,Actinomyces,Yeast}	1	The Snyder test uses a glucose-based medium and bromocresol green to detect acid production, primarily by Lactobacilli.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
ee6b7b0e-e450-473f-bd31-301f2926c283	public_health	BDS-I	A "Direct" caries activity test measures:	{"Salivary buffering capacity","Salivary flow rate","The actual number of cariogenic bacteria present","The pH of the plaque"}	2	Direct tests (like Lactobacillus count or S. mutans dip-slides) quantify the pathogen rather than measuring the environment.	Caries Activity / Susceptibility Tests	\N
976f3b14-d127-47a4-b22e-f0f3c6b64ac2	public_health	BDS-I	Pit and fissure sealants are most effective when applied to:	{"Partially erupted teeth with gingival flaps","Newly erupted teeth with deep, sound fissures","Carious teeth with small cavities","Smooth surfaces of the teeth"}	1	Sealants are most effective on sound, highly susceptible anatomy soon after eruption to prevent the first onset of decay.	Pit and Fissure Sealants	\N
bdc96e43-42d6-48ba-b8c2-2122f394d473	public_health	BDS-I	The "Acid Etching" phase in sealant application (37% Phosphoric acid) functions by:	{"Chemically bonding the resin to enamel","Creating microporosities for mechanical interlocking","Neutralizing the tooth surface","Releasing fluoride"}	1	Etching increases surface area and energy, allowing the resin to flow into micropores and create a strong mechanical bond.	Pit and Fissure Sealants	\N
087ab43f-890c-4830-92a0-e83e5236797d	public_health	BDS-I	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART) is a "minimal intervention" approach that relies on:	{"High-speed handpieces","Hand instruments and adhesive materials","General anesthesia","Full-mouth rehabilitation"}	1	ART was developed for use in areas without electricity or complex equipment, utilizing hand instruments to remove softened dentin.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
235fb45a-2067-41ff-ad94-17626f1db0ca	public_health	BDS-I	The material of choice for ART is Glass Ionomer Cement (GIC) because it:	{"Is harder than amalgam","Bonds chemically to the tooth and releases fluoride","Is the cheapest material available","Can be used without any instruments"}	1	GIC’s chemical bond and fluoride release (remineralization) are essential for the success of ART in community settings.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
239dd75e-dcef-4969-aba4-743dee369ba2	public_health	BDS-I	In the ART technique, which layer of dentin is intentionally left behind?	{"Infected dentin","Affected (demineralized but not infected) dentin","Carious enamel","Necrotic pulp"}	1	ART removes the soft, bacteria-laden "infected" dentin but preserves the "affected" dentin which is capable of remineralization.	Atraumatic Restorative Treatment (ART)	\N
59ac12f8-b188-4922-90b5-e1d8172c13fc	public_health	BDS-I	Which of the following is considered a "Clinical" preventive measure?	{"Fluoridation of the city water supply","Professional application of 5% NaF Varnish","Mass media campaign for oral hygiene","Legislation to tax sugary drinks"}	1	Clinical measures are those performed by a dental professional on a specific patient in a clinical environment.	Clinical Preventive Measures	\N
87d00704-0446-415c-9b92-1b99d14e31c2	public_health	BDS-I	"Recurrent Caries" (Secondary Caries) is most accurately described as:	{"Caries in the second molars","Caries occurring at the interface of a restoration and the tooth","Caries that occurs only in primary teeth","Caries caused by a second exposure to sugar"}	1	This type of caries indicates a failure of the restorative margin or poor oral hygiene post-treatment.	Epidemiology, Etiology and Prevention of Dental Caries	\N
81df0862-121c-453d-bafa-fa0f80860fff	public_health	BDS-I	The "ultimate goal" of Public Health Dentistry is:	{"To provide expensive care to the wealthy","To ensure every individual has zero teeth missing","To achieve the greatest good for the greatest number of people in a community","To eliminate the need for private dentists"}	2	The community is the "patient," and the focus is on efficient, equitable, and widespread health improvement.	Public Health dentistry	\N
5ccc3e15-103e-4443-9c34-eead47477cbf	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 55-year-old male provides inconsistent dates regarding his previous heart surgery and appears confused during the interview. According to Hupp, what is the clinician's primary concern regarding the validity of this history?	{"Accuracy of physician notes","Patient reliability as a historian","Physical exam findings","Laboratory data"}	1	Hupp states that the validity of a medical history depends primarily on the patient’s reliability as a historian, assessed via biographic data and interview consistency.	Biographic Data	\N
52bdfcf1-f1b8-4ce2-879e-2a5b1fd20874	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with well-controlled Type II Diabetes and a history of Myocardial Infarction 4 months ago. What is the correct ASA Physical Status Classification?	{"ASA II","ASA III","ASA IV","ASA V"}	1	A patient with a recent MI (<6 months) or multiple systemic diseases (even if controlled) is classified as ASA III. ASA II is for mild, single-system disease.	ASA Classification	\N
e2dffe81-ef32-417a-b2c1-c27b1be08da3	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with "orthopnea," requiring four pillows to sleep, and exhibits symptoms of Congestive Heart Failure that are a "constant threat to life." This patient is:	{"ASA II","ASA III","ASA IV","ASA V"}	2	ASA IV is defined by severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life, such as symptomatic CHF/orthopnea.	ASA Classification	\N
d639d0d6-9331-46f2-8b5a-4fd0557b2e2f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During a physical exam, a clinician uses the tips of the middle and index fingers to palpate the radial artery for 30 seconds. If a weak or irregular rhythm is discovered, what is the next mandatory step?	{"Multiply by 2 and record","Palpate the carotid artery","Auscultate the heart directly","Take blood pressure immediately"}	2	Per Hupp, if a weak pulse or irregular rhythm is discovered during radial palpation, the heart should be auscultated directly to determine rate and rhythm.	Vital Signs	\N
c8c6e84e-146a-43c2-9b97-35445fa21e36	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Using a blood pressure cuff that is too small for an obese patient’s arm will result in which clinical error?	{"Falsely low reading","Falsely high reading","Accurate reading","No reading"}	1	Too small a cuff causes readings to be falsely high; too large a cuff causes artificially low readings due to the physics of pressure distribution.	Vital Signs	\N
c04697f2-eb31-4fe1-9453-e32eca5a0d8c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 30-year-old patient who is declared brain-dead and is undergoing organ procurement is classified as:	{"ASA IV","ASA V","ASA VI","ASA E"}	2	ASA VI is specifically reserved for brain-dead patients whose organs are being removed for donor purposes.	ASA Classification	\N
e6c1f4bd-7c8d-402b-8218-8b32ccfaa240	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which of the following findings in the Review of Systems (ROS) is categorized under the "Cardiovascular" review rather than the "Routine Head and Neck" review?	{Tinnitus,Epistaxis,"Ankle Edema",Rhinorrhea}	2	Ankle edema is a sign of right-sided heart failure and is reviewed under the Cardiovascular system (Box 1-5).	Review of Systems	\N
cad31118-1ce9-4678-822d-6ab64cff3b3a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	While examining a swelling in the floor of the mouth, the clinician notes "induration." Which physical examination technique was used to reach this finding?	{Inspection,Palpation,Percussion,Auscultation}	1	Palpation is used to determine induration (hardness), fluctuance, and tenderness in soft tissues.	Physical Examination	\N
7661b143-920f-4b38-81eb-aae57eaec4d3	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient reports a "choking sensation" and pressure in the substernal region that radiates to the left mandible during exertion. What is the Greek origin of the term describing this symptom?	{"Heart Pain","Choking Sensation","Blood Clot","Shortness of Breath"}	1	The term "Angina" is derived from the ancient Greek word for "a choking sensation."	Ischemic Heart Disease	\N
164031bf-9f6b-48ce-8374-3d98f2de0dae	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "moribund patient who is not expected to survive without the operation" is classified as:	{"ASA III","ASA IV","ASA V","ASA VI"}	2	ASA V describes a moribund patient whose survival depends on the surgical intervention.	ASA Classification	\N
9a9d8769-4f56-4587-bef5-1c32e3063038	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For a NEET MDS aspirant, the "Chief Complaint" should ideally be recorded in the dental record using:	{"Medical terminology","Verbatim (patient's own words)","After the physical exam","Physician's diagnosis"}	1	Recording the complaint verbatim helps establish priorities and clarifies the patient’s actual desires/reasons for seeking care.	Chief Complaint	\N
9545f245-469d-4f41-8846-ca576dd9ff1f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which technique is used to evaluate TMJ clicks or carotid bruits?	{Inspection,Palpation,Percussion,Auscultation}	3	Auscultation is the primary means of evaluating TMJ sounds (clicks/crepitus) and carotid bruits in the neck.	Physical Examination	\N
aa961363-2c51-4035-8d3c-09a4ce60f06e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient who cannot assume a supine position without gasping for air and feeling pulmonary distress is suffering from:	{"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea",Orthopnea,Apnea,Tachypnea}	1	Orthopnea is dyspnea specifically induced by the supine position, often requiring pillows for sleep.	CHF Assessment	\N
06349a49-bb4f-4c20-a260-081d43297f95	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During the respiratory review, a "crowing sound" (stridor) is noted. This primarily indicates narrowing of which anatomical structure?	{"Small airways",Bronchioles,Larynx,Alveoli}	2	Stridor indicates a narrowing of the larger air passages, specifically the larynx or vocal cords.	Physical Examination	\N
d12c403b-4358-4abe-8997-86bce5a0e7f6	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which finding during inspection of the neck suggests the need for immediate cardiovascular evaluation?	{"Thyroid enlargement","Jugular venous distention","Tracheal deviation","Cervical lymphadenopathy"}	1	Jugular venous distention is a physical sign of central venous pressure elevation, often associated with CHF.	Cardiovascular Review	\N
573dbfc0-1ebf-48d7-afb4-d9f373714154	oral_surgery	BDS-I	To examine the submandibular gland, Hupp recommends which specific technique?	{"Simple inspection","Indirect percussion","Bimanual palpation","Direct auscultation"}	2	The submandibular gland is examined bimanually through the floor of the mouth and the skin under the floor.	Physical Examination	\N
2969e339-00db-4be1-8df1-becc59513734	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the significance of "induration" when palpating a mucosal lesion?	{"It suggests inflammation","It suggests malignancy or chronic scar","It suggests fluid collection","It suggests nerve damage"}	1	Induration (firmness) in soft tissues often indicates malignancy or significant chronic fibrosis/scarring.	Physical Examination	\N
f5768df8-f33b-4f06-a281-bf91dbcaf609	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient had a Myocardial Infarction 3 months ago. Elective oral surgery should be deferred because:	{"Risk of bleeding is high","Reinfarction risk is highest <6 months","Local anesthesia won't work","Patient is ASA II"}	1	Statistical evidence in Hupp suggests reinfarction risk drops to its lowest about 6 months post-MI.	Ischemic Heart Disease	\N
d9f65fc5-b8f4-4189-8c9f-9fde2fabc9e2	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Inspection of the eyes during a maxillofacial exam includes checking for "conjunctival color." Pale conjunctiva primarily suggests:	{Jaundice,Anemia,Hypertension,Glaucoma}	1	Conjunctival color is inspected to look for signs of anemia (pallor) or jaundice (icterus).	Physical Examination	\N
32ecf95f-5a72-4543-bd1b-2b6fb258cc1c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In the ASA system, a patient with "severe systemic disease that is not incapacitating" is:	{"ASA I","ASA II","ASA III","ASA IV"}	2	ASA III is defined as severe systemic disease (e.g., stable angina, controlled DM with complications) that is not incapacitating.	ASA Classification	\N
2a14866f-955e-4bd2-88cd-0a5bc28671c4	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which part of the medical history database includes "History of ethanol, tobacco, and illicit drug use"?	{"Biographic Data","Chief Complaint","Health-related habits","Review of Systems"}	2	These are categorized as health-related habits/addictions (Box 1-2).	Medical History	\N
c185e2f7-ec18-462a-b585-7b397526bdb8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Rhytidectomy" is more commonly known as:	{"Nose job","Eyelid surgery","Face lift","Chin tuck"}	2	Rhytidectomy involves tightening the skin and SMAS. [Preface]	Cosmetic Surgery	\N
25fc7bbc-3298-4fd1-bbe7-5179130b3b3f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Palpation of the neck during a routine dental exam primarily targets which two structures?	{"Paranasal sinuses and uvula","Lymph nodes and thyroid","Uvula and tonsils","Teeth and gingiva"}	1	The neck is palpated for lymph nodes and thyroid gland size/consistency.	Physical Examination	\N
eef1172b-8f21-4f12-9a7b-8d03f66a785f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient who reports waking up 2 hours after falling asleep with sudden shortness of breath is describing:	{Orthopnea,"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea","Sleep Apnea",Asthma}	1	PND occurs when pooled blood/fluid from the legs is redistributed centrally after 1-2 hours of lying down.	CHF Assessment	\N
e09de98b-e970-40e0-9360-4d58f49da6a5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In assessing a "Chief Complaint" regarding pain, which factor is NOT typically required for the history of the complaint?	{Radiation,Duration,"Cost of previous treatment",Location}	2	Pain history focuses on onset, intensity, duration, location, radiation, and mitigating factors.	Chief Complaint	\N
236bb952-8e12-4e22-8ab3-b783bfa041f4	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Elective oral surgery is deferred for 6 months post-MI. If surgery is required earlier, what is the mandatory first step?	{"Use 100% Oxygen","Medical consultation","Administer prophylactic Nitroglycerin","Perform in a hospital only"}	1	Any surgery <6 months post-MI requires medical consultation to assess stability and recovery.	Ischemic Heart Disease	\N
74bbae05-496e-42a3-a1d0-45be6cfa449e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A cardiac patient requires local anesthesia. You have 2% Lidocaine with 1:100,000 Epinephrine. What is the maximum volume you can safely administer?	{"2 mL","4 mL","8.8 mL","10 mL"}	1	0.04 mg is the limit. Since 1:100k epi is 0.01 mg/mL, the limit is 4 mL (approx. 2 cartridges).	Epinephrine Limits	\N
05f59e13-674c-459c-8081-9d68ce8eef36	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with COPD presents with a "barrel-shaped chest" and "pursed-lip breathing." What is the primary respiratory stimulant in this patient?	{"High CO2 levels","Low O2 levels (Hypoxia)","Low pH","High O2 levels"}	1	Severe COPD patients become acclimated to high CO2 and depend on "hypoxic drive" (low O2) to stimulate breathing.	COPD	\N
fc611a44-a681-4912-9a60-869092cf2145	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Why is high-concentration oxygen (e.g., 10 L/min) contraindicated in a patient with severe COPD?	{"It causes alkalosis","It removes the hypoxic drive","It causes immediate bronchospasm","It is toxic to the alveoli"}	1	Elevating O2 levels removes the hypoxia-based stimulus, which can cause the respiratory rate to critically slow or stop.	COPD	\N
3781b55d-f5c8-4a66-87c6-eea65de5fc6b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with angina that occurs at rest and has increased in frequency over the last week. Management is:	{"Proceed with 0.04mg Epi","Postpone elective surgery","Use Nitrous Oxide only","Perform in hospital today"}	1	Unstable angina (at rest/increasing severity) requires postponement of elective surgery.	Ischemic Heart Disease	\N
e034d3aa-8bea-4789-b7d2-4583cd500237	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a history of asthma should be specifically questioned about allergies to which group of dental medications?	{Penicillins,NSAIDs/Aspirin,"Local Anesthetics","Sulfa drugs"}	1	There is a high frequency of NSAID/Aspirin allergy in asthmatic patients, which can trigger attacks.	Asthma	\N
8c74d5d0-c8ac-4725-9e49-fd55125c6ccf	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For a patient with a history of Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG), what is the typical wait period for routine elective surgery?	{"1 week","3 months","6 months","1 year"}	1	Major elective surgery waits 3 months; routine office surgery can be done sooner if recovery is uncomplicated.	Ischemic Heart Disease	\N
9240761a-8f84-435a-9edd-af28a3ba4ba8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	How should a patient with symptomatic CHF and orthopnea be positioned in the dental chair?	{"Fully supine",Trendelenburg,"Upright or semi-upright",Prone}	2	Supine positioning redistributes fluid centrally, inducing pulmonary edema in CHF patients.	CHF Management	\N
cdbaf793-3880-45aa-919b-41b25f8e03f5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which gas is safe and specifically indicated for anxiety-triggered asthma in the dental chair?	{"Carbon Dioxide","Nitrous Oxide","Pure Oxygen",Helium}	1	Nitrous oxide reduces anxiety (a common trigger) and is safe for asthmatics.	Asthma	\N
2b56e074-8940-4093-9937-32edf3ee54e4	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a cardiac pacemaker requires a surgical extraction. Regarding antibiotic prophylaxis:	{"It is mandatory","It is only for old pacemakers","No evidence supports the need","Only for general anesthesia"}	2	There is no evidence showing the need for antibiotic prophylaxis specifically for pacemakers.	Dysrhythmias	\N
62143273-9088-4f1f-82eb-ddb2b3f13cd9	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which piece of dental equipment should NOT be used near a patient with an older-generation cardiac pacemaker?	{"High-volume suction",Electrocautery,"Digital X-ray sensor","Pulse oximeter"}	1	Electrical equipment like electrocautery can interfere with pacemaker function.	Dysrhythmias	\N
