7516f543-39b7-49b0-8311-9fb22e0212ee	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A tonsil-type (Yankauer) suction tip is critical in an emergency to:	{"Provide oxygen","Clear large volumes of fluids","Intubate the patient","Measure pulse"}	1	Large tips are needed to rapidly clear vomit or blood from the airway (Figure 2-1).	Emergency Equipment	\N
dc45a3ff-04cc-4e0a-adb9-3cec53a7fd19	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Why must an emergency kit be checked for expiration dates frequently?	{"To save money","Drugs lose potency or become toxic","To satisfy the landlord","To prevent theft"}	1	Expired drugs (like Nitroglycerin or Epinephrine) may fail to work during a life-threatening crisis.	Emergency Kit	\N
4bf81397-4db2-46c9-aa36-e2fb6de439cd	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 45-year-old male requires an immediate implant after the extraction of a fractured #8. The clinician notes 4mm of bone apical to the extraction socket. What is the most critical biological requirement to proceed with immediate placement?	{"Presence of thick gingival biotype","Achievement of primary stability in apical bone","Use of a tapered implant design","Grafting the \\"jumping distance\\" gap"}	1	According to Chapter 15, immediate placement is viable only if the implant can engage sufficient bone apical to the socket (typically 3-5mm) to achieve primary stability, which is the mechanical prerequisite for osseointegration.	Advanced Implantology	\N
f8b6b0a9-5bc3-4423-b0b4-e6d0869152f3	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with a residual bone height (RBH) of 3mm in the posterior maxilla. During a lateral window sinus lift, what is the surgical objective regarding the Schneiderian membrane?	{"Perforating it to allow drainage","Elevating it to create space for a bone graft","Removing it to allow direct bone contact","Suturing it to the lateral wall"}	1	In advanced cases where RBH is <5mm, a lateral window is required to carefully elevate the Schneiderian membrane, creating a subantral space for grafting materials to increase bone volume for future implants.	Advanced Implantology	\N
b2fea924-1d10-4de0-bb6a-2fd411c00332	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A clinician is deciding between a standard implant and a "platform switched" system for a patient with a thin crestal ridge. How does the "platform switching" design help manage the risk of crestal bone resorption?	{"By shifting the microgap horizontally away from the bone","By increasing the surface area for osseointegration","By eliminating the need for a second-stage surgery","By reducing the cost of the prosthetic components"}	0	Hupp's advanced concepts explain that platform switching moves the implant-abutment interface (and the associated bacterial microgap) inward, away from the bone-implant crestal interface, thereby mitigating biological bone remodeling.	Advanced Implantology	\N
45949a0c-421e-4451-97bf-82cfa9bf0ce1	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During 3D virtual computerized planning for a mandibular implant, the software indicates the inferior alveolar nerve (IAN) is 7mm from the crest. Applying Hupp’s safety protocols, what is the maximum drill depth allowed?	{"5.0 mm","6.0 mm","6.5 mm","7.0 mm"}	0	To prevent neurovascular damage, a 2mm safety margin must be maintained between the osteotomy and vital structures like the IAN, as dictated by the 6th Edition’s planning principles.	Advanced Implantology	\N
955979b3-2371-49d5-8c98-e27ef83b6385	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient requires a "delayed-immediate" implant (Type 2 placement). What is the primary biological advantage of waiting 6 to 8 weeks post-extraction before placement?	{"To allow for complete bone fill of the socket","To obtain complete soft tissue coverage for graft containment","To allow the sinus to pneumatize further","To increase the density of the surrounding cortical bone"}	1	Waiting 6-8 weeks allows for soft tissue healing, providing the surgeon with more substantial keratinized tissue for tension-free flap closure over potential bone grafts.	Advanced Implantology	\N
b40654f3-702c-4568-aa60-63908963409f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In a case involving severe horizontal ridge deficiency, the surgeon chooses a "ridge split" technique over block grafting. What is the essential anatomical requirement for a successful ridge split?	{"Presence of a thick, dense cortical plate","Presence of an intervening cancellous bone layer","Patient must be under 30 years old","At least 15mm of vertical bone height"}	1	Ridge splitting (expansion) requires an intervening layer of medullary bone between the buccal and lingual cortical plates to allow the plates to be expanded without causing an "off-stick" fracture.	Advanced Implantology	\N
1e2665ad-8fb7-4396-b187-c0e4df3cc418	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a high smile line and requires an implant at site #9. The surgeon opts for "restoration-driven" surgery. How does the 3D virtual planning dictate the surgical site preparation?	{"Based on the thickest area of bone available","Based on the ideal position of the final prosthetic crown","Based on the proximity to the nasopalatine foramen","By prioritizing the longest possible implant"}	1	Advanced implantology (Preface/Ch 26) emphasizes that the final restoration's 3D position dictates the implant's placement (restoration-driven) to ensure aesthetic and functional success.	Advanced Implantology	\N
6d9bd0ea-9a5e-4350-bd4a-2413983c9684	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During an "advanced" sinus augmentation, the surgeon observes a 3mm perforation in the Schneiderian membrane. According to the 6th Edition, how should this be managed intraoperatively?	{"Proceed with grafting without intervention","Abort the procedure and wait 4 months","Repair using a resorbable collagen membrane","Use non-resorbable mesh to seal the defect"}	2	Small perforations can be managed by folding the membrane on itself or covering the site with a resorbable collagen membrane (e.g., Bio-Gide) to maintain the integrity of the graft.	Advanced Implantology	\N
5e0a1f66-f32e-43db-9313-f1bac34c9582	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the primary biological rationale for utilizing Bone Morphogenetic Protein (BMP-2) in complex alveolar reconstruction?	{"It acts as a passive scaffold for bone growth","It stimulates mesenchymal cells to differentiate into osteoblasts","It provides high mechanical strength to the site","It reduces the need for local anesthesia"}	1	BMPs are osteoinductive; they signal undifferentiated mesenchymal stem cells to become bone-forming cells, which is critical for reconstructing large defects (Ch 29/Preface).	Advanced Implantology	\N
1ba9fae5-1f89-46b0-89eb-5131a10de05e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with a flat mandibular ridge and requests a "fixed" solution. The surgeon recommends an implant-supported overdenture with "Advanced" ridge augmentation. Which graft type is the "gold standard" for volume and vitality?	{"Bovine-derived xenograft","Autogenous block graft","Synthetic hydroxyapatite","Allogeneic demineralized bone"}	1	Autogenous bone remains the gold standard because it provides living osteogenic cells, osteoinductive factors, and an osteoconductive scaffold.	Advanced Implantology	\N
9ca97c83-8cb0-4ff2-9f57-ad11b8ce8127	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For immediate implant placement in the "aesthetic zone," where should the implant platform be positioned relative to the facial bone crest?	{"Exactly at the level of the facial crest","1-2 mm coronal to the facial crest","1-2 mm apical to the facial crest","5 mm apical to the gingival margin"}	2	Placing the platform 1-2mm apical to the facial bone crest compensates for the inevitable resorption of the "bundle bone" following extraction, preventing aesthetic failure.	Advanced Implantology	\N
87389f8a-742a-4fe1-a75b-bed84f2f03bb	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In cases of extreme maxillary atrophy, "Zygomatic Implants" are considered. According to advanced protocols, these implants engage which bone for stability?	{"The palatine bone","The malar (zygomatic) bone","The pterygoid plates","The nasal septum"}	1	Zygomatic implants bypass the atrophic maxilla to gain primary stability from the dense cortical bone of the zygomatic arch/body.	Advanced Implantology	\N
1c52260c-f27e-4b44-bb43-dfb2ea43bec8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which clinical factor most significantly increases the risk of "Retrograde Peri-implantitis" in immediate placement?	{"Smoking 10 cigarettes a day","Presence of an untreated periapical lesion on the extracted tooth","Use of a non-submerged (one-stage) protocol","Age of the patient over 65"}	1	Residual periapical infection or purulence is a contraindication for immediate placement as it can seed bacteria onto the implant surface, leading to early failure.	Advanced Implantology	\N
100c1580-d9ba-4dce-8a94-e415fe59eb54	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the specific biological rhythm recommended for distraction osteogenesis in alveolar ridge reconstruction?	{"1.0 mm once per day","0.25 mm four times per day","0.5 mm twice per week","2.0 mm once per day"}	1	Splitting the total daily distraction (1mm) into smaller increments (e.g., 0.25mm x 4) reduces tissue tension and is less traumatic to the developing microvasculature (the regenerate).	Advanced Implantology	\N
da136552-9820-4e09-8d65-7b6b18d90224	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a history of radiation therapy (50 Gy) to the mandible requires implants. What advanced management step is often indicated to improve the success rate?	{"Immediate loading of implants","Use of hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) therapy","Placing the implants under general anesthesia","Using short implants only"}	1	HBO therapy helps overcome the "3-H" (hypovascular, hypocellular, hypoxic) nature of irradiated bone, stimulating angiogenesis and improving the bone's healing capacity.	Advanced Implantology	\N
bb52e960-f4d5-4592-9660-32ed0cd9269e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 32-year-old female presents with submandibular swelling, a muffled "hot potato" voice, and an inability to swallow her saliva. What is the immediate surgical priority?	{"Extraction of the offending tooth under local anesthesia","Definitive airway assessment and management","Immediate administration of oral Clindamycin","Panoramic radiograph to identify the source"}	1	Signs like dysphagia and a "hot potato" voice indicate potential airway compromise due to deep space involvement (lateral pharyngeal space); airway management takes precedence over all other care.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
c8a22143-a79c-4fab-a1ec-c263bdae04d5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Why is a patient with poorly controlled Type 2 Diabetes (HbA1c 9.5%) more prone to the rapid spread of an odontogenic infection?	{"Increased blood viscosity","Impaired neutrophil chemotaxis and phagocytosis","Presence of sugar in the saliva","Faster bacterial replication in hyperglycemia"}	1	Hyperglycemia impairs the function of leukocytes, specifically their ability to move to the site (chemotaxis) and ingest bacteria (phagocytosis), allowing the infection to spread rapidly.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
77e4b406-a22a-43fb-a698-201585496d6e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with well-controlled epilepsy is categorized as ASA:	{I,II,III,IV}	1	Well-controlled systemic disease or significant risk factor is ASA II. [Box 1-8]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
e5a1689e-7d6d-49d6-be5a-01649daf0c2d	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During the incision and drainage (I&D) of a submandibular space infection, why must the surgeon's hemostat be advanced with caution toward the medial side of the mandible?	{"To avoid the facial artery","To avoid the sublingual gland","To protect the lingual nerve and submandibular duct","To avoid the masseter muscle"}	2	Blunt dissection must be mindful of the regional anatomy; the lingual nerve and the submandibular (Wharton's) duct are located medially and can be damaged by aggressive dissection.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
310b5db8-7ab9-47c8-97b1-4248ed33a80b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a maxillary molar infection develops unilateral periorbital edema and loss of the pupillary light reflex. Which complication is suspected?	{"Maxillary sinusitis","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Canine space infection","Ludwig's Angina"}	1	The spread of infection via the ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus can lead to cranial nerve palsies and orbital signs, representing a life-threatening emergency.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
854be386-ef15-4252-b4b7-26f16af241bf	oral_surgery	BDS-I	An infection from a mandibular second molar spreads below the mylohyoid muscle. Into which facial space will this infection primarily drain?	{"Sublingual space","Submandibular space","Buccal space","Pterygomandibular space"}	1	The roots of the second and third molars often lie below the mylohyoid muscle attachment; therefore, infections from these teeth drain into the submandibular space.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
ff1b41d8-bea2-4a6e-a6ca-f6072f65c455	medicine	BDS-I	"Prognathism" (enlarged jaw) is a hallmark of:	{"Addison’s Disease","Cushing’s Disease",Acromegaly,Hypothyroidism}	2	Prognathism and enlargement of the tongue/jaw characterize Acromegaly.	Endocrine Disease	\N
62725942-e627-467e-8448-3bffd564564f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Ludwig's Angina." Which three spaces are bilaterally involved in this condition?	{"Buccal, Submental, Sublingual","Submandibular, Sublingual, Submental","Pterygomandibular, Lateral pharyngeal, Submental","Sublingual, Canine, Submandibular"}	1	Ludwig’s Angina is defined by a firm, woody cellulitis involving the submandibular, sublingual, and submental spaces bilaterally.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
8f60dce1-cbde-4e30-b8fe-b430e17655fe	oral_surgery	BDS-I	After extracting #14, the surgeon notes a 5mm oro-antral communication (OAC). The sinus is healthy. What is the most appropriate management?	{"Leave it to heal by secondary intention","Gelfoam and a figure-of-eight suture to stabilize the clot","Immediate buccal fat pad flap","Direct closure with a full-thickness palatal flap"}	1	For communications of 2-6mm in a non-infected sinus, maintaining a stable blood clot using hemostatic agents and a suture is usually sufficient for closure.	Maxillary Sinus	\N
7c12b417-6a1b-4dbe-a2ba-23d6e58a5e3d	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a chronic oro-antral fistula (OAF) that has been present for 4 weeks. What must be done before attempting surgical closure?	{"Treat any existing sinus infection","Place the patient on 6 months of antibiotics","Perform a Caldwell-Luc on the contralateral side","No preparation is needed"}	0	Surgical closure of an OAF will fail if the sinus is actively infected; any sinusitis must be resolved with antibiotics and irrigation before the flap is turned.	Maxillary Sinus	\N
95e4385d-f9a9-4a72-aaa4-7b01b2746ab5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the surgical advantage of the "Buccal Fat Pad Flap" for closing OACs in the molar region?	{"It provides a highly vascularized, epithelialized surface","It provides a rich source of autogenous bone","It is easy to harvest and has a high success rate due to its vascularity","It prevents the need for any sutures"}	2	The buccal fat pad is a pedicled graft with an excellent blood supply that can be easily mobilized to cover defects in the posterior maxilla.	Maxillary Sinus	\N
284aaafe-8428-475a-b8d1-5ed22b96c748	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with unilateral maxillary sinus pain, foul-smelling nasal discharge, and a history of a failed RCT on #3. What is the most likely diagnosis?	{"Viral rhinosinusitis","Odontogenic maxillary sinusitis","Allergic rhinitis","Maxillary ameloblastoma"}	1	Foul-smelling discharge and a history of dental pathology (periapical or periodontal) are hallmark signs of sinusitis of odontogenic origin.	Maxillary Sinus	\N
ae0a2c3f-48b8-44c3-bff8-75dd5035125a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During a "Caldwell-Luc" procedure, where is the initial incision made to gain access to the antrum?	{"In the midline of the palate","In the buccal vestibule above the premolar/molar roots","Along the infraorbital rim","Through the gingival sulcus of the incisors"}	1	The Caldwell-Luc involves an incision in the canine fossa/vestibule to create a "window" into the maxillary sinus for root retrieval or pathology removal.	Maxillary Sinus	\N
dd8f00fc-73e3-4d75-bedc-a68785525e13	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which facial space infection is characterized by severe trismus and the inability to palpate the mandibular notch?	{"Submandibular space","Pterygomandibular space","Sublingual space","Canine space"}	1	The pterygomandibular space is bounded medially by the medial pterygoid muscle; infection here causes severe trismus due to muscle irritation (splinting).	Odontogenic Infections	\N
781ebf87-988f-40b1-9144-b08bf9309fba	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "diabetic" patient with a deep neck infection shows signs of "Necrotizing Fasciitis." What is the mandatory surgical intervention?	{"High-dose oral Penicillin","Immediate aggressive surgical debridement of necrotic tissue","Observation and IV fluids","Incisional biopsy only"}	1	Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency requiring the immediate removal of all non-viable tissue to stop the spread of the infection.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
7afba0cc-d7b6-4be5-8a81-dfc4395bbefc	oral_surgery	BDS-I	When retrieving a root tip from the maxillary sinus, what is the purpose of a "nasoantral window" (antrostomy)?	{"To allow the patient to breathe better","To provide drainage and allow the sinus to clear post-operatively","To gain a better view of the root tip","To prevent the root from moving into the nose"}	1	An antrostomy in the inferior meatus is created during complex sinus surgery to facilitate drainage and prevent the accumulation of blood/serum.	Maxillary Sinus	\N
82dddcec-3244-4ff7-bac1-3522e6e7fa9c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In the management of a severe odontogenic infection, "medical support" includes the correction of which metabolic state?	{"Metabolic alkalosis","Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance",Hypervitaminosis,Hypothyroidism}	1	Patients with severe infections often have high fevers and poor oral intake, necessitating IV fluids and electrolyte monitoring for stability.	Odontogenic Infections	\N
b2bd1d9a-ea5f-43a1-98f8-2e5b8c6f326d	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Orthopnea" indicates that the patient should be treated in which position?	{"Fully supine",Trendelenburg,"More upright sitting position",Prone}	2	Supine positioning overwhelms the heart in CHF patients. [Box 1-12]	Medical Management	\N
2f6c3a28-5c7c-4eef-b326-766253468311	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a persistent periapical lesion on a tooth with a high-quality RCT. During "exploratory" endodontic surgery, the clinician uses a surgical microscope. What is the most common "hidden" finding?	{"A large radicular cyst","A vertical root fracture or accessory canal","A missing pulp chamber","A calcified canal"}	1	Hupp notes the "exploratory" nature of endodontic surgery allows for the visualization of fractures or anatomy that 2D and even 3D X-rays may miss.	Endodontic Surgery	\N
593a3868-4e2e-41b9-9095-5ee6acc67f0e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	When performing a biopsy of a 3cm radiolucent lesion in the mandible, the surgeon notes a "hollow" cavity with no lining. What is the likely diagnosis?	{"Odontogenic Keratocyst","Traumatic (Simple) Bone Cyst",Ameloblastoma,"Central Giant Cell Granuloma"}	1	Simple bone cysts often appear as empty cavities without an epithelial lining, which is a key diagnostic feature during surgical exploration.	Pathology	\N
c442f957-acba-4527-a0e0-71f88d4de75d	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For an apicoectomy on #7, the surgeon chooses a "Luebke-Ochsenbein" (submarginal) flap. What is the primary indication for this flap?	{"To provide maximum visibility of the root","To prevent gingival recession around existing prosthetic crowns","To avoid the need for sutures","To minimize the risk of infection"}	1	This flap remains in the attached gingiva, avoiding the marginal gingiva, thus preventing recession that could expose crown margins in aesthetic areas.	Endodontic Surgery	\N
0e23687e-3534-4a7c-8673-9fb790db4d3b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 2cm lesion is noted on the lateral border of the tongue. It is firm, ulcerated, and has "rolled" borders. Which biopsy technique is mandatory?	{"Excisional biopsy","Incisional biopsy of the most representative area","Fine needle aspiration (FNA)","Cytology smear only"}	1	For large, suspicious, or potentially malignant lesions, an incisional biopsy is required to establish a diagnosis before planning a radical resection.	Pathology/Biopsy	\N
5b7830ea-3729-429a-92b8-3cdb5cd6f1df	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which antibiotic is specifically contraindicated in the breastfeeding mother due to potential risk to the infant?	{Penicillin,Cephalexin,Tetracycline,Erythromycin}	2	Tetracyclines should be avoided as they may affect bone/teeth of the infant. [Table 1-2]	Medical Management	\N
5a9d64d6-fc11-4cae-9157-7ce27554969c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Why is it recommended to resect exactly 3mm of the root apex during endodontic surgery?	{"To make the tooth shorter","To remove the majority of apical ramifications and accessory canals","To ensure the root is level with the bone","To provide enough space for a crown"}	1	Studies cited in Hupp indicate that a 3mm resection removes roughly 98% of the apical ramifications that cause endodontic failure.	Endodontic Surgery	\N
921820fe-2243-4a26-a455-97d0a93d77c2	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During the biopsy of a suspected "Central Vascular Malformation," what is the first step before the incision?	{"Administration of IV steroids","Aspiration with a large-bore needle","General anesthesia","Cauterization of the area"}	1	Aspiration is mandatory to rule out a high-flow vascular lesion, which could lead to life-threatening hemorrhage if incised without preparation.	Pathology/Biopsy	\N
b52edabd-0da3-4fc1-8870-a1710115b628	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the purpose of using a "frozen section" during the resection of an ameloblastoma?	{"To determine the age of the lesion","To ensure that the surgical margins are free of tumor cells","To stop the bleeding intraoperatively","To identify the patient's blood type"}	1	Frozen sections provide a rapid intraoperative diagnosis to confirm that the surgeon has cleared the tumor and is in healthy bone/tissue.	Pathology	\N
15b509a1-1393-4da9-8287-b48a8bfa1d6d	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In "Marsupialization" of a large cyst, what is the biological objective?	{"To remove the entire cyst lining immediately","To reduce the intracystic pressure and allow for new bone formation","To burn the lining using chemicals","To fill the cavity with bone graft"}	1	Marsupialization decompresses the cyst, stimulating the body to fill the defect with bone, thereby protecting vital structures like the IAN or roots.	Pathology	\N
bcb0c890-83da-43dd-b7c2-7a4f53e798b4	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 45-year-old male presents with a painless, 1cm, "leaf-like" mass under his denture. What is the likely diagnosis and management?	{"Epulis fissuratum; Excisional biopsy","Fibroma; Incisional biopsy","Squamous cell carcinoma; FNA","Mucocele; Observation"}	0	Epulis fissuratum is a reactive hyperplasia. Since it is small (<1-2cm), the entire lesion should be removed (excisional biopsy) for diagnosis.	Pathology/Biopsy	\N
5e03ae65-e673-41f6-a02c-2e8d1d150cd6	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For a retrograde filling (root-end filling), which material is currently preferred for its biocompatibility and sealing ability?	{Amalgam,"Mineral Trioxide Aggregate (MTA)","Composite resin","Zinc oxide eugenol"}	1	MTA is highly biocompatible, seals well in the presence of moisture, and promotes cementum formation over the root end.	Endodontic Surgery	\N
447264e9-9e07-4488-971d-5830f26e1210	oral_surgery	BDS-I	When performing an incisional biopsy of a suspected malignancy, why should the surgeon include a piece of "normal-appearing" tissue at the edge?	{"To make the sample larger","To provide the pathologist with a transition zone for comparison","To help the wound heal faster","To prevent the spread of cells"}	1	Including the margin between the lesion and normal tissue allows the pathologist to see how the tumor interacts with/invades the surrounding structures.	Pathology/Biopsy	\N
48ed574f-4321-47ce-a43e-9129c947abfb	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the primary clinical indicator that an oral ulcer requires a biopsy?	{"It is painful to touch","It has been present for more than 2 weeks with no identifiable cause","It bleeds when the patient brushes","It is smaller than 2mm"}	1	Any lesion or ulcer that does not resolve within 10-14 days after removing local irritants must be biopsied to rule out malignancy.	Pathology/Biopsy	\N
ee58d4e1-b876-46b0-8173-0dbea38061f9	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a "mucocele" on the lower lip. What additional step is required during the excisional biopsy to prevent recurrence?	{"Suturing the muscle layer","Removing the associated minor salivary gland","Using a laser for the cut","Applying alcohol to the site"}	1	Recurrence is likely unless the underlying minor salivary gland (the source of the mucous) is removed along with the lesion.	Pathology	\N
f1aa3383-8788-4c68-9d0a-58172dd38e2a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In endodontic surgery, why is the root end prepared with a "0-degree" bevel (flat) rather than a steep bevel?	{"It makes the surgery faster","It minimizes the number of exposed dentinal tubules and leakage","It is easier to suture the flap","It prevents the root from cracking"}	1	Modern surgical endodontics favors a minimal bevel to reduce the surface area of the root end, thereby reducing the chance of leakage.	Endodontic Surgery	\N
b72d322a-8752-4550-9cb8-833ae225cc36	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a large, multinodular mass in the parotid region. Which technique is most useful for a preliminary diagnosis?	{"Excisional biopsy of the parotid","Fine Needle Aspiration (FNA)","Scaling and root planing","Panoramic radiograph"}	1	FNA is a minimally invasive and highly accurate method for diagnosing salivary gland and neck masses without risking facial nerve damage.	Pathology/Biopsy	\N
09b2c1cc-1e48-47ef-84db-f848263f6758	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with a subconjunctival ecchymosis and a "flat cheek" after being hit with a baseball. Where is the most likely fracture located?	{"Mandibular symphysis","Zygomaticomaxillary complex (ZMC)","Le Fort I","Coronoid process"}	1	ZMC fractures (tripod fractures) involve the malar eminence and the orbital floor, leading to aesthetic flattening and subconjunctival hemorrhage.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
37a5bd85-c2a1-460a-9dd2-049c34500d84	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 10-year-old presents with a "greenstick" fracture of the mandible. Why is this type of fracture unique to pediatric patients?	{"Children have more mineralized bone","The bone is more organic and flexible, leading to an incomplete break","Children heal slower than adults","It only happens in the condyle"}	1	Greenstick fractures occur because young bone is less calcified and more flexible, allowing it to bend and break only on one side (tension side).	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
b5053dee-6bf8-4b32-a1a9-e7b6f1afb011	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has an avulsed #8. It has been kept dry in a tissue for 75 minutes. What is the most likely long-term outcome after replantation?	{"Successful re-attachment of the PDL","Replacement resorption (ankylosis)","Normal pulp vitality","Spontaneous exfoliation within 1 week"}	1	An extra-oral dry time of >60 minutes results in the death of PDL cells, leading the body to treat the root as bone and undergo ankylosis.	Dentoalveolar Trauma	\N
914b1acf-ca58-483b-9dca-67c8fb6c2c73	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In the management of a complex orbital floor fracture, why is "intraoperative navigation" used?	{"To replace the need for a surgeon","To provide real-time 3D feedback on the position of the orbital implants","To reduce the cost of the plates","To measure the patient's heart rate"}	1	Navigation (Preface) allows the surgeon to see the exact 3D position of the reconstruction plate relative to the preoperative plan and the contralateral "normal" orbit.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
6b7774fb-c704-43cd-8ad3-a2e42f929182	oral_path	BDS-I	Jefferson burst:	{C2,C1,C7,L1}	1	Atlas break.	Trauma	\N
89eb4568-737a-4e79-8cd2-b3ecd27ca4b9	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Battle’s Sign" (ecchymosis behind the ear). What does this clinically indicate?	{"Fracture of the mandible","Fracture of the cranial base (basilar skull fracture)","Fracture of the zygomatic arch","Soft tissue injury to the ear"}	1	Ecchymosis over the mastoid process (Battle's sign) is a classic indicator of a fracture involving the base of the skull.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
bf45e33f-5d1b-418a-a30a-35d589eabb28	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During the fixation of a mandibular angle fracture, the surgeon uses a single plate on the "superior border." This follows which principle?	{"Compression plating","Tension band principle","Load-bearing fixation","Rigid internal fixation"}	1	Placing a plate on the superior border (tension zone) of the mandible counteracts the distracting forces of the muscles of mastication.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
94f93ec6-f4da-4863-92ca-05d6f03b2c64	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a midface trauma has "CSF Rhinorrhea." What is the first-line management protocol?	{"Packing the nose with gauze","Elevation of the head and avoiding nose-blowing; Neurosurgical consult","Immediate surgical closure of the dural tear","Frequent suctioning of the nose"}	1	CSF leaks indicate a communication with the brain; conservative management (head elevation) is often successful, but meningitis must be prevented.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
9e882bf0-d13a-4381-a68a-ffd923014c2e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which tooth injury has the highest risk of pulpal necrosis?	{Subluxation,"Extrusive luxation","Intrusive luxation",Concussion}	2	Intrusion causes the most severe damage by crushing the neurovascular bundle and the PDL, resulting in nearly 100% necrosis in mature teeth.	Dentoalveolar Trauma	\N
4306ea6b-e352-4891-a34c-8adc5414b5c0	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In a Le Fort II fracture, the fracture line passes through which structure that differentiates it from a Le Fort I?	{"The lateral maxillary wall","The nasal bones and infraorbital rim","The zygomatic arch","The pterygoid plates"}	1	Le Fort II is a pyramidal fracture that includes the nasal bones and the orbital floor/rim, whereas Le Fort I is purely a horizontal maxillary fracture.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
5153ca21-370d-4b16-be4c-2972267cc228	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the hallmark clinical finding of a "mandibular condyle" fracture on opening?	{"The jaw deviates away from the side of the fracture","The jaw deviates toward the side of the fracture","The patient can open 50mm without pain","There is no deviation"}	1	The lateral pterygoid muscle on the fractured side is non-functional, so the muscle on the healthy side pushes the jaw toward the affected side.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
85d3be64-1f03-4f41-a51c-63c02e702963	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "blow-out" fracture of the orbit primarily involves which wall?	{"The lateral wall","The orbital floor (maxilla)","The orbital roof (frontal bone)","The medial wall only"}	1	The thin bone of the orbital floor (above the maxillary sinus) is the most common site for a blow-out fracture due to hydraulic pressure.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
cbf9081f-bea8-4ae4-88b9-ba1c2e93fc83	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For a subluxated tooth with no alveolar fracture, how long should the flexible splint remain in place?	{"2 weeks","6 weeks","3 months","6 months"}	0	Subluxated teeth (loosened but not displaced) usually require only 2 weeks of flexible splinting to allow the PDL to reorganize.	Dentoalveolar Trauma	\N
77062ea0-40d4-4488-9b31-9fca71292541	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which nerve is most commonly at risk during the surgical approach to a "condylar neck" fracture via a preauricular incision?	{"Lingual nerve","Facial nerve (Temporal and Zygomatic branches)","Inferior alveolar nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"}	1	The facial nerve branches pass through the parotid gland and over the zygomatic arch, placing them directly in the surgical field for TMJ access.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
2b415091-f101-449d-b766-32e8a6437df8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	When suturing a facial laceration that involves the "vermilion border," what is the most important step?	{"Using the largest suture possible","Aligning the \\"white line\\" (vermilion border) first","Closing the skin before the muscle","Using only staples"}	1	Precise alignment of the vermilion border is critical; even a 1mm step-deformity is highly noticeable and aesthetically poor.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
d0b82791-73a3-40b2-aa73-99afac47049f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a mandibular fracture is in "Intermaxillary Fixation" (IMF). What is the primary risk during the immediate postoperative period?	{"Weight gain","Aspiration if the patient vomits","Tooth decay","Hair loss"}	1	If a patient vomits while their teeth are wired together, they are at high risk for aspiration pneumonia; wire cutters must be at the bedside.	Maxillofacial Trauma	\N
50f9a354-c44c-4eb5-ac59-79691ab6ebf5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During a Le Fort I osteotomy, the maxilla is "down-fractured." Which vessel is the primary source of nutrition for the mobilized segment?	{"Greater palatine artery","Ascending pharyngeal and palatine soft tissue pedicle","Infraorbital artery","Facial artery"}	1	The soft tissue attached to the palate and pharynx provides the essential blood supply to keep the bone and teeth viable after the bone cuts.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
e2f75c84-6ad2-4854-8470-992a492361f9	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In distraction osteogenesis, what is the biological rationale for the "latency period" (usually 5-7 days)?	{"To allow the patient to recover from anesthesia","To allow for the formation of a \\"soft callus\\" between the bone ends","To wait for the swelling to go down","To ensure the patient can pay for the device"}	1	If distraction starts too soon, the callus doesn't form; if too late, the bone consolidates prematurely. The latency period allows the initial healing response.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
da81448f-c9a0-49ff-ab09-e809ba110d7f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a "skeletal Class II" deformity with a deficient mandible. Which surgery is the standard for mandibular advancement?	{"Le Fort I","Bilateral Sagittal Split Osteotomy (BSSO)",Genioplasty,"Mandibular setback"}	1	BSSO allows the mandible to be split and slid forward (advancement) while maintaining bone-to-bone contact for healing.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
894d7fcd-3e71-443b-bc98-71e43cd1d185	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Why is a distraction rhythm of 0.25mm four times a day (1mm total) preferred over 1.0mm once a day?	{"It is more convenient for the surgeon","It preserves the microvasculature and prevents \\"ischaemic\\" failure of the regenerate","It makes the bone harder","It requires fewer follow-up visits"}	1	As per Hupp, smaller, frequent increments are more "physiologic" and allow the blood vessels and nerves to stretch without tearing.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
47885ed6-34bb-4b32-9428-2baee01d1a56	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a 5cm defect in the mandible following tumor resection. Which "Advanced" material can be used to induce bone growth without an autogenous graft?	{"Titanium mesh alone","Bone Morphogenetic Protein-2 (BMP-2) on a collagen carrier","Plaster of Paris","Acrylic resin"}	1	BMP-2 is osteoinductive and is a contemporary alternative to autogenous bone grafts for reconstructing large jaw defects (Preface/Ch 29).	Reconstruction	\N
b59f428f-8ad3-45bd-93ae-fa15f15b9b2b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which movement of the maxilla during a Le Fort I is used to correct a "Gummy Smile"?	{Advancement,"Superior repositioning (Impaction)",Setback,"Inferior repositioning"}	1	Moving the maxilla upward (impaction) reduces the vertical excess that causes excessive gingival display.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
b8ec4ae1-6fe0-4558-b075-12d494b80ad8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the most common long-term neurosensory complication of a Mandibular BSSO?	{"Loss of taste","Paresthesia of the lower lip (IAN damage)","Paralysis of the tongue",Blindness}	1	Because the bone is split directly along the canal of the inferior alveolar nerve, temporary or permanent numbness is a frequent risk.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
5d9c4894-2642-4e70-a99a-836fa05eb3df	oral_surgery	BDS-I	3D virtual computerized planning is particularly superior to 2D planning in which type of deformity?	{"Simple Class II","Facial Asymmetry (involving \\"yaw\\" and \\"roll\\")","Single tooth malalignment","Small chin"}	1	Asymmetry involves complex rotations in three dimensions that are difficult to visualize or plan accurately on 2D cephalometrics.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
b409b6ae-e86c-401b-9c66-23854700744f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Genioplasty" involves a horizontal osteotomy of the symphysis. What is a key advantage of this procedure over a chin implant?	{"It is faster","It allows for the correction of vertical, horizontal, and transverse chin positions","It does not require any cuts in the bone","It is cheaper"}	1	Genioplasty is highly versatile; it can advance, push back, lengthen, or shorten the chin using the patient's own bone.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
479dc15e-b77f-4601-9bd4-9091ae3293b5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In the reconstruction of a jaw defect, what is the primary advantage of a "Vascularized Free Flap" (e.g., Fibula flap)?	{"It is easier to perform than a block graft","It brings its own blood supply, allowing for healing in a scarred or irradiated bed","It requires no hardware for fixation","It is a minor procedure"}	1	Microvascular surgery allows the bone to survive independently of the recipient site's blood supply, which is critical in compromised patients.	Reconstruction	\N
566a57c1-60f9-4664-b918-5485f6e1fc21	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Premature consolidation" in distraction osteogenesis is caused by:	{"Distracting too fast","Distracting too slowly or having too long a latency period","Using BMPs","The patient being too young"}	1	If the rate is too slow, the bone heals across the gap before the desired length is achieved.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
85484049-7850-409d-af5f-d61512eddf9c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Sarpe" (Surgically Assisted Rapid Palatal Expansion) is used for which condition?	{"Mandibular excess","Transverse maxillary deficiency in an adult","Gummy smile","Large chin"}	1	Once the palatal sutures fuse in adults, surgery is needed to release the bone before the orthodontist can expand the arch.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
88e59892-a8f6-4f93-a9f9-4afdc9662d23	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which surgery is used to move the entire maxilla, including the palate and the dental arch, as a single unit?	{BSSO,"Le Fort I",Genioplasty,IVRO}	1	Le Fort I is the "workhorse" of orthognathic surgery for midface corrections.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
4c483e22-2ed2-4315-94ba-585b587c9af2	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the "Gold Standard" site for harvesting a non-vascularized autogenous bone block graft for jaw reconstruction?	{"The skull","The iliac crest (hip)","The ribs","The tibia"}	1	The iliac crest provides a large volume of both cortical and cancellous (marrow) bone, which is ideal for grafting.	Reconstruction	\N
637a7fa8-85aa-49e4-91e8-24f3e1383022	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In orthognathic surgery, "Model Surgery" is performed to:	{"Teach dental students","Create surgical splints that guide the jaws into the planned position intraoperatively","See if the teeth are white enough","Practice giving injections"}	1	Model surgery (or 3D printing) creates the "templates" that the surgeon uses to lock the jaws into the new occlusion during the operation.	Dentofacial Deformities	\N
f4799d7e-2f30-4e86-b154-6325981337a8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 55-year-old female experiences sharp, stabbing, "electric" pain in the right V2 distribution triggered only by applying lipstick. She is pain-free between episodes. What is the diagnosis?	{"Myofascial Pain","Trigeminal Neuralgia","Atypical Facial Pain","TMJ Ankylosis"}	1	Paroxysmal, trigger-point-induced, electric-shock pain is the pathognomonic description of Trigeminal Neuralgia (Ch 30).	Facial Pain	\N
7f139927-4e50-464e-8e4b-2cdb229a31cd	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has a "clicking" sound in the TMJ during opening and closing. The click occurs at 20mm of opening. What is the likely diagnosis?	{"Anterior Disc Displacement with Reduction (ADDR)","Disc Displacement without Reduction",Osteoarthritis,"Muscle spasm"}	0	The click signifies the condyle jumping back onto the posterior band of the disc ("reducing") during translation.	TMJ Disorders	\N
4869ff9d-dee8-4180-a03d-cf9d67d2b165	oral_surgery	BDS-I	During a clinical exam, the surgeon palpates the "lateral pole" of the condyle. Where is this located anatomically?	{"Inside the ear canal","Immediately anterior to the external auditory meatus","On the cheek near the nose","Behind the angle of the mandible"}	1	The lateral pole is the most accessible part of the joint for palpation, located just in front of the ear.	TMJ Disorders	\N
ce5b366f-18f8-48f0-ab6d-790109f485a7	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with a sudden "locked" jaw and cannot open more than 20mm. The jaw deviates toward the affected side. What is this called?	{ADDR,"Acute Disc Displacement without Reduction (Closed Lock)",Subluxation,Hypermobility}	1	In "closed lock," the disc is stuck in front of the condyle, acting as a mechanical obstruction to translation.	TMJ Disorders	\N
4e2e4767-4557-4d7f-9c94-6ecded1fd3e2	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which muscle should be palpated intraorally to check for tenderness associated with clenching?	{Masseter,Temporalis,"Medial Pterygoid (at the attachment near the angle)",Buccinator}	2	The medial pterygoid is palpated intraorally behind the tuberosity or at its insertion on the medial aspect of the mandibular angle.	TMJ Disorders	\N
b932f8b3-f2bc-4053-875a-a2533f1481f3	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Crepitus" (a grating/sandpaper sound) on TMJ auscultation is most indicative of:	{"A healthy joint","Degenerative Joint Disease (Osteoarthritis)","Simple muscle strain","Early disc displacement"}	1	Crepitus indicates that the disc or cartilage has worn away, leading to bone-on-bone contact and remodeling.	TMJ Disorders	\N
ba75d4eb-8a76-4ebf-a47b-9ef1ce7c8e6b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	How is the "Posterior Attachment" (bilaminar zone) of the TMJ palpated during an exam?	{"Directly on the skin","By placing a finger in the external auditory meatus and having the patient close","By pressing on the forehead","It cannot be palpated"}	1	Applying pressure anteriorly through the ear canal tests for inflammation in the sensitive, vascularized posterior attachment of the joint.	TMJ Disorders	\N
2c57252b-82c6-4296-a39e-521c56363848	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Rhinoplasty" involves the surgical modification of the:	{Ears,Nose,Chin,Forehead}	1	Rhinoplasty is cosmetic/functional nose surgery. [Preface]	Cosmetic Surgery	\N
eb5597b3-5301-42b9-8002-797e93ce6f64	oral_surgery	BDS-I	"Arthrocentesis" is a minimally invasive TMJ procedure that primarily works by:	{"Removing the condyle","Lavaging the joint space to remove inflammatory mediators and \\"unlock\\" the disc","Replacing the disc with a plastic one","Numbing the muscles"}	1	Lavage under pressure can break down small adhesions and "wash out" cytokines that cause pain and limited motion.	TMJ Disorders	\N
98e6ec72-fe6c-421e-9638-c3cc24396147	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has pain in the TMJ region that is worse in the morning and is associated with "scalloped" borders on the tongue. What is the diagnosis?	{"Trigeminal Neuralgia","Myofascial Pain secondary to nocturnal bruxism","Bone tumor","Ear infection"}	1	Scalloped tongue and morning jaw pain are classic signs of parafunctional habits like night-time clenching or grinding.	TMJ Disorders	\N
dc07cbb4-0ddb-404e-b233-f321d4d85f29	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 60-year-old patient had a Myocardial Infarction (MI) 3 months ago. He is now asymptomatic, can climb stairs, and has been cleared by his cardiologist. According to the 6th Edition of Hupp, what is the protocol for elective dental work?	{"Wait at least 6 months regardless of health","Elective work can proceed with medical clearance and stress reduction","Only emergency work is allowed for the first 2 years","All dental work is contraindicated forever"}	1	The 6th Edition explicitly states that the "automatic 6-month wait" is unnecessary given modern thrombolytic care, provided the patient is stable and cleared.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
a5c3cba6-6b3a-4b2c-8136-c7c86187267b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has stable angina and can perform daily activities without chest pain. What is their ASA Physical Status Classification?	{"ASA I","ASA II","ASA III","ASA IV"}	1	ASA II represents a patient with mild systemic disease (like stable angina or controlled hypertension) that does not limit their functional capacity.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
81da8282-d286-42b3-a0b1-b40eee6e3f55	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient on Warfarin has an INR of 2.5. He requires the extraction of a single symptomatic tooth. What is the recommended management?	{"Stop Warfarin for 7 days before the extraction","Proceed with the extraction and use local hemostatic measures (sutures/Gelfoam)","Give a Vitamin K injection before the start","Refer to a hospital for a blood transfusion"}	1	For an INR between 2.0 and 3.0, simple oral surgery is safe to perform without stopping anticoagulation, as long as local measures are used.	Hospital Management	\N
b16e431f-2f49-48da-875f-535c92b5328f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which clinical finding is a "Red Flag" for Congestive Heart Failure (CHF) in a pre-op evaluation?	{"Frequent headaches","Orthopnea (the need to sleep on multiple pillows to breathe)","Yellowing of the eyes","Increased appetite"}	1	Orthopnea indicates that the heart cannot handle the increased venous return when the patient is flat, leading to pulmonary congestion.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
3c1d4241-923d-4614-9f10-bc7afe65eff5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient has been taking 20mg of Prednisone daily for 1 year for Rheumatoid Arthritis. What is the primary concern during a 2-hour surgical procedure?	{"The patient will be too happy","Acute adrenal crisis due to suppression of the HPA axis","Increased risk of cavities","Faster wound healing"}	1	Chronic steroids suppress the body's ability to produce its own cortisol in response to stress, which can lead to life-threatening hypotension (crisis).	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
926e05c2-317d-4f92-ae26-1c80e172f784	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the maximum "Cardiac Dose" of Epinephrine (1:100,000) for an ASA III patient?	{"0.04 mg (approx. 2 cartridges)","0.2 mg (approx. 11 cartridges)","1.0 mg (50 cartridges)","No limit"}	0	To avoid excessive stress on the heart, epinephrine should be limited to 0.04mg in cardiac-compromised patients.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
aed00554-98b1-40b7-97a3-bbd3fc5dd8e8	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient’s Blood Pressure is 165/95 mmHg. How is this classified according to the Hupp 6th Ed. guidelines?	{Normal,"Mild to Moderate Hypertension","Severe Hypertension",Hypotension}	1	Systolic 140-199 or Diastolic 90-109 falls into the mild-to-moderate range where dental work can proceed with caution.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
3b15803d-3c45-4737-ba69-06c47f09a894	oral_surgery	BDS-I	For a patient with a history of "Asthma," which class of analgesics should be used with extreme caution?	{Paracetamol,"NSAIDs (like Ibuprofen or Aspirin)",Narcotics,"Local anesthetics"}	1	A subset of asthmatics (approx. 10%) has "Aspirin-sensitive asthma" where NSAIDs can trigger a life-threatening bronchospasm.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
96963625-026b-4da8-960c-3e442043adf2	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient on Hemodialysis requires dental extractions. When is the best time to schedule the surgery?	{"On the morning of the dialysis","The day after a dialysis session","One hour before the dialysis starts","It doesn't matter"}	1	Scheduling the day after dialysis ensures that the heparin has worn off and the patient is in their best metabolic and fluid state.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
2cbbdacd-fbf7-4b71-933c-6f65f21ca967	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is the primary reason for a patient to be "NPO" (Nothing by Mouth) for 8 hours before General Anesthesia?	{"To make them lose weight","To prevent the aspiration of gastric contents into the lungs","To keep the mouth clean for the surgeon","To prevent the patient from needing a bathroom break"}	1	Aspiration of stomach acid during anesthesia is a major cause of death; a "full stomach" is a contraindication for elective GA.	Hospital Management	\N
3fee9f27-0f6d-48fb-86ab-40d7a25a3843	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which laboratory test is most critical for a patient with Liver Cirrhosis before surgery?	{"Blood sugar","Coagulation profile (PT/INR) and Platelet count","Hearing test","Cholesterol level"}	1	The liver makes clotting factors, and cirrhosis causes a large spleen that eats platelets; both lead to severe surgical bleeding.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
b5be578c-6233-468d-8933-10ccd056bfc2	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A Type 1 Diabetic patient is scheduled for surgery at 9:00 AM and is NPO. What should they do with their morning dose of insulin?	{"Take the full dose","Take a half dose of NPH (long-acting) and skip the regular (short-acting) insulin","Take a double dose to be safe","Eat a candy bar and take the full dose"}	1	To prevent hypoglycemia while the patient isn't eating, the dose must be reduced and the patient's glucose monitored.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
995a8f3b-f787-470b-b16b-2883b0211f3f	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In the "Review of Systems," a patient reports "Paroxysmal Nocturnal Dyspnea" (PND). This is a hallmark of:	{"Lung cancer","Left-sided Heart Failure","Chronic Sinusitis",Asthma}	1	PND is when a patient wakes up gasping for air shortly after falling asleep, a classic sign of fluid backup in the lungs.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
0efe0f14-df59-44ac-863d-ad9c67da67ee	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Cardiac" patient is undergoing surgery. Why is regular "verbal contact" maintained throughout the procedure?	{"To keep the surgeon from getting bored","To monitor the patient’s level of consciousness and well-being","To practice speech therapy","To make the patient pay more"}	1	Verbal contact is the best way to monitor for early signs of distress, syncope, or a changing medical status.	Hospital Management	\N
0cb45371-3255-4f59-b3c6-ba989c2c3a99	oral_surgery	BDS-I	What is "ASA IV"?	{"A healthy 20-year-old","A patient with a constant threat to life (e.g., unstable angina)","A patient with mild asthma","A patient who had a cold 2 weeks ago"}	1	ASA IV represents the highest risk category for any elective surgery, usually requiring a hospital setting.	Pre-op Evaluation	\N
60ceb69b-f0d3-4de4-b194-ea92022c5833	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A 62-year-old male with a history of Myocardial Infarction (MI) 4 months ago requires an emergency extraction for a non-restorable tooth with associated cellulitis. What is the most appropriate management?	{"Defer for exactly 2 months to meet the 6-month rule","Proceed with caution if the MI recovery was uneventful and anxiety is minimized","Defer all treatment except for high-dose systemic antibiotics","Refer immediately to a hospital-based oral surgeon for monitored care"}	1	While the 6-month rule is traditional, straightforward procedures can be done earlier if recovery was uneventful and anxiety is low. [Box 1-11]	Medical Management	\N
aa7a195d-3e2d-463f-b047-c475e593b1a6	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient currently taking 10mg of Prednisone daily for rheumatoid arthritis requires a complex surgical procedure. According to the "Rule of Two," what is the perioperative protocol?	{"No supplementation required","Instruct the patient to take 10mg extra on the day of surgery","Instruct the patient to double the usual daily dose on the day of surgery and for 2 days post-op","Discontinue steroids 48 hours before surgery to allow adrenal recovery"}	2	For patients currently taking steroids, doubling the dose for the day of surgery and the first two postoperative days is the standard protocol. [Box 1-20]	Medical Management	\N
812c5b2c-b310-4840-a658-43ea704016ca	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A Type 1 Diabetic patient is scheduled for an IV-sedated wisdom tooth extraction and must remain NPO for 6 hours. What is the correct morning insulin management?	{"Take the full dose of NPH insulin only","Take half the dose of regular insulin only","Omit morning insulin and start an IV D5W drip at 150 mL/hr","Take the full dose of both NPH and regular insulin"}	2	If a patient must remain NPO, the morning dose of insulin is omitted and an IV infusion of 5% dextrose in water is started. [Box 1-19]	Medical Management	\N
c31fbcc0-e466-4cfe-8a32-e416d976d372	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A drug is assigned to FDA Pregnancy Category C if:	{"Controlled studies in women fail to demonstrate fetal risk","Animal studies show adverse effects but no human studies are available","Positive evidence of human fetal risk exists but benefits may outweigh risk","Controlled studies in women show evidence of fetal abnormalities"}	1	Category C refers to drugs where animal studies show risk but no human studies are available, or no studies are available at all. [Box 1-27]	Medical Management	\N
d498ccf6-d7da-4c6e-aaa2-e76b7f020596	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient experiences immediate-onset urticaria and pruritus after a local anesthetic injection. What is the primary drug of choice?	{"Oral Diphenhydramine 50 mg","Epinephrine 1:1000 (0.3 mL) SC or IM","IV Hydrocortisone 100 mg","Inhaled Metaproterenol"}	1	Immediate-onset skin signs (erythema, urticaria, pruritus) following drug administration are treated with 0.3 mL of 1:1000 Epinephrine. [Table 2-3]	Medical Emergencies	\N
417b9938-e63b-4774-9693-9071bb5458a5	oral_surgery	BDS-I	To increase venous return to the heart during a syncopal episode, the dental chair should be positioned:	{"Fully upright (90 degrees)","Trendelenburg (Head-down, feet-raised)","Semi-fowler's (45 degrees)","Prone (Face down)"}	1	Positioning the legs above the level of the trunk (Trendelenburg) facilitates increased venous return. [Figure 2-1, A]	Medical Emergencies	\N
813128c9-d611-4a44-ab84-e92441b64980	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient with a history of stable angina who experiences chest pain only during vigorous exertion and is well-controlled with medication is classified as:	{"ASA I","ASA II","ASA III","ASA IV"}	2	Severe systemic disease that is not incapacitating, such as stable angina or well-controlled Type 1 Diabetes, is ASA III. [Box 1-8]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
7e9c63f9-1958-4984-832b-d53099c1ac0e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	All of the following are components of the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health (HITECH) Act of 2009 EXCEPT:	{"Addressing electronic health records","Telemedicine and the Internet","Liability for breach of patient data","Mandatory use of nitrous oxide in all surgical cases"}	3	HITECH focuses on electronic records, telemedicine, and the security of health information, not specific anesthetic protocols. [Preface]	Medicolegal	\N
53e05975-f1f4-447a-8655-8ae2243af401	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In a cardiac patient, what is the maximum recommended dose of Epinephrine 1:100,000 in a single 30-minute period?	{"0.01 mg","0.04 mg","0.1 mg","0.2 mg"}	1	For patients with ischemic heart disease, epinephrine should be limited to 0.04 mg (approx. 2 cartridges of 1:100,000). [Chapter 1, Ischemic Heart Disease]	Medical Management	\N
1edd88a1-83f2-4dc5-9eaa-e8b5c8dd3d9e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which of the following is an "ASA VI" classification?	{"A moribund patient","A patient with severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life","A declared brain-dead patient whose organs are being removed for donor purposes","A patient with well-controlled hypertension"}	2	ASA VI is specifically reserved for declared brain-dead organ donors. [Box 1-8]	Preoperative Evaluation	\N
314a867d-07eb-46f3-bb4e-83b730bd5dae	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient presents with shortness of breath when lying flat in the dental chair, requiring a more upright position to breathe. This clinical sign is known as:	{Dyspnea,Orthopnea,Hemoptysis,Cyanosis}	1	Orthopnea is dyspnea on assuming the supine position, a hallmark of congestive heart failure. [Box 1-5, Box 1-12]	Medical Management	\N
a4d3b1fa-e5c3-42c6-8106-1c11357043ca	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Bone Morphogenetic Proteins (BMPs) are primarily indicated in modern maxillofacial surgery for:	{"Enhancing local anesthetic duration","Surgical reconstruction of jaw defects","Treating TMJ crepitus","Managing anaphylactic shock"}	1	The 6th edition highlights BMPs specifically in the context of reconstructing jaw defects. [Preface, Chapter 29]	Reconstruction	\N
7d8321f4-11d4-4576-bb97-dd871cdb646e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A Type 2 Diabetic patient who can eat breakfast before a morning extraction should:	{"Eliminate the morning hypoglycemic agent","Take the usual morning dose of the hypoglycemic agent","Triple the dose of the hypoglycemic agent","Switch to NPH insulin for that day only"}	1	If a Type 2 diabetic can eat before/after surgery, they should take their normal morning dose of hypoglycemic agent. [Box 1-19]	Medical Management	\N
b7a4864d-2bc5-40cf-9fff-fc96a4347c28	oral_surgery	BDS-I	When performing the "ABC" of Basic Life Support, "Airway" can be maintained by all the following EXCEPT:	{"Head tilt-chin lift","Pushing the mandible forward by the angles","Pulling the tongue forward with a suture","Placing the patient in a prone position"}	3	Prone positioning obstructs the airway. Airway is maintained via head tilt, mandible protrusion, or tongue traction. [Box 2-3]	Medical Emergencies	\N
cc2d491a-e80b-45dd-9e30-7005ad6d5a1c	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A "Le Fort I" osteotomy is used to correct midface deformities by making a horizontal cut at which level?	{"Above the orbits","Below the mandible","Horizontal level above the teeth in the maxilla","At the level of the zygomatic arch"}	2	Le Fort I is a horizontal maxillary osteotomy above the level of the tooth roots. [Table of Contents, Preface]	Facial Trauma	\N
68362632-4615-41f0-91c5-fa2dd558d49e	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Which of the following is a "Category B" drug, generally considered safe for use in the first trimester of pregnancy?	{Tetracycline,Lidocaine,Diazepam,Aspirin}	1	Lidocaine is classified as Category B (no demonstrated fetal risk in animal studies). [Box 1-27, Table 1-2]	Medical Management	\N
08468467-00ac-4a5c-9f71-40bfcfec270a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	An interincisal opening of less than what value is considered a "Red Flag" for referral to a specialist in odontogenic infections?	{"40 mm","30 mm","20 mm","10 mm"}	2	Trismus with an opening of <20 mm is a key criterion for referring a patient to an oral and maxillofacial surgeon. [Odontogenic Infection Focus]	Odontogenic Infection	\N
588cec21-d3d7-47c4-9bbe-5eff36ae284a	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A temperature exceeding what threshold suggests a systemic involvement requiring specialist referral for an odontogenic infection?	{99°F,100°F,101°F,105°F}	2	A temperature >101°F indicates a severity level that warrants referral. [Odontogenic Infection Focus]	Odontogenic Infection	\N
7837cccd-69dd-4671-958a-e56106966636	oral_surgery	BDS-I	In 3D virtual computerized surgical planning for orthognathic surgery, "Image Prediction" is used primarily to:	{"Eliminate the need for clinical examination","Improve the accuracy of soft tissue and skeletal outcomes","Reduce the surgeon's fee","Replace the need for postoperative antibiotics"}	1	3D planning and image prediction enhance the precision of the surgical outcome. [Preface]	Orthognathic	\N
3bce0900-7b35-43ce-9b65-3c6f06b1f27b	oral_surgery	BDS-I	Why are "surgical instruments kept out of the patient's sight" in an anxiety-reduction protocol?	{"To maintain a sterile field","To minimize nonpharmacologic triggers of anxiety","To comply with HITECH regulations","Because they are considered a biohazard before use"}	1	This is a specific nonpharmacologic measure to reduce patient stress. [Box 1-9]	Medical Management	\N
d03ccf1b-3544-4d62-b2f3-8c08e215e6b0	oral_surgery	BDS-I	A patient on Warfarin (Coumadin) requires a routine dental extraction. Surgery is generally considered safe if the INR is:	{"Exactly 1.0","Between 2.0 and 3.0","Greater than 4.5","Less than 1.5"}	1	Most surgical procedures can be safely performed when the INR is between 2.0 and 3.0. [Box 1-23]	Medical Management	\N
