942e2e1c-584b-48e8-b97d-b373b1a91fdf	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has lost taste on the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue but has normal general sensation. Which nerve is injured?	{"Lingual nerve","Chorda tympani",Glossopharyngeal,Hypoglossal}	1	Chorda tympani carries taste fibers from the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue.	Tongue	\N
2496c53f-053e-4e40-804c-c0936ac05e6f	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a deviated uvula to the right side when saying "Ah". Which nerve is likely damaged?	{"Right Vagus","Left Vagus","Right Glossopharyngeal","Left Glossopharyngeal"}	1	The uvula deviates away from the side of the lesion (the normal side pulls it); thus, a right deviation indicates a left vagal lesion.	Cranial Nerves	\N
247ad83d-219e-4f82-bf73-d38728ae2c66	anatomy	BDS-I	After a difficult delivery, a newborn cannot suckle properly due to buccinator paralysis. Where was the facial nerve likely injured?	{"Internal acoustic meatus","Geniculate ganglion","Stylomastoid foramen","Parotid gland"}	2	The mastoid process is absent at birth, making the facial nerve superficial at the stylomastoid foramen and prone to injury.	Embryology	\N
0d2f4aac-5de3-4afc-b74e-e59d7b257b9b	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with herpes zoster vesicles on the auricle, facial palsy, and hearing loss. What is this syndrome?	{Horner's,Bell's,"Ramsay Hunt",Eagle's}	2	Ramsay Hunt syndrome is herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion, affecting nerves VII and VIII.	Clinical Neurology	\N
d54a84fd-a62f-48e6-aadf-f882eeb5ccfb	anatomy	BDS-I	A 70-year-old male has an ulcer on the lateral margin of his tongue. Which lymph node should the clinician palpate first?	{Submental,Submandibular,Jugulo-omohyoid,Retro-pharyngeal}	1	The lateral margins of the tongue drain primarily into the submandibular lymph nodes.	Lymphatics	\N
39543ed4-1a70-4994-8128-dca27529f97e	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a midline bulge in the posterior pharyngeal wall. The clinician suspects a "cold abscess." What is the source?	{"Infected tonsil","Caries of cervical spine","Parotid abscess","Maxillary sinusitis"}	1	A cold abscess (tubercular) from the cervical spine tracks behind the prevertebral fascia, causing a midline bulge.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
9ab06890-bb83-4206-b0df-32426ab02ad0	anatomy	BDS-I	A child presents with epistaxis from the anteroinferior part of the nasal septum. What is this area called?	{"Little’s Area","Meckel’s Cave","Piriform fossa","Sella turcica"}	0	Little’s area (Kiesselbach’s plexus) is the most common site for nosebleeds.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
b7c60f8d-e80b-4b88-9509-cb9668d82dad	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient with a severe infection of the "voice box" is struggling to breathe. Which muscle is likely paralyzed, failing to abduct the vocal cords?	{Cricothyroid,"Lateral cricoarytenoid","Posterior cricoarytenoid",Thyroarytenoid}	2	The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor of the vocal cords and is known as the "safety muscle".	Larynx	\N
d18aec8f-b387-4867-a2e1-573423c1d0f8	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "nasal twang" in their voice and liquid regurgitates through the nose. Which structure is failing to function?	{Epiglottis,"Soft palate","Lingual tonsil","Piriform fossa"}	1	Paralysis of the soft palate (vagus nerve) causes nasal regurgitation and a nasal twang.	Pharynx	\N
1ddc9be6-9dbf-4b8e-aec2-073d7306da26	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Port Wine Stains" on the skin of their forehead and upper eyelid. This corresponds to the distribution of which nerve?	{"Ophthalmic nerve","Maxillary nerve","Mandibular nerve","Facial nerve"}	0	Cutaneous naevi (port wine stains) map out the distribution of trigeminal nerve divisions; the forehead is V1.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
880284ea-d84d-4727-9984-d25688480bc5	anatomy	BDS-I	During an emergency, a clinician needs to palpate the "Anesthetist's Artery." Where is it found?	{"Angle of mandible","In front of the tragus","Root of the nose","Behind the mastoid"}	1	The superficial temporal artery is easily palpable in front of the tragus and is used by anesthetists.	Blood Supply	\N
6e1ba933-4502-42a2-b63d-8d862b014b02	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a tumor in the "Smuggler’s Fossa." Where is this located?	{Nasopharynx,Oropharynx,"Piriform fossa","Maxillary sinus"}	2	The piriform fossa is called the "smuggler's fossa" because it can be used to hide small precious objects.	Larynx	\N
b7766a38-aecf-4c02-a819-1ced3fdee730	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "Tongue Tie." Which structure is too short or anteriorly placed?	{"Plica fimbriata","Frenulum linguae","Vallate papilla","Lingual tonsil"}	1	Tongue tie (ankyloglossia) is caused by a restrictive frenulum linguae.	Tongue	\N
b5a57f9f-ce35-4c48-8888-cf63a48c0011	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient with long-standing diabetes presents with reduced vision. The clinician sees microaneurysms in the retina. Diagnosis?	{Glaucoma,Cataract,"Diabetic retinopathy","Retinal detachment"}	2	Diabetic retinopathy involves pathologic changes in retinal blood vessels including microaneurysms.	Ophthalmology	\N
49aa7e4c-bba7-4118-85e1-6dbda0c0691b	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient cannot protrude their tongue straight; it deviates to the right. Which muscle and nerve are affected?	{"Left Genioglossus - XII","Right Genioglossus - XII","Right Hyoglossus - IX","Left Styloglossus - X"}	1	The genioglossus is the protrusor; in a unilateral XII nerve lesion, the tongue deviates to the paralyzed side.	Tongue	\N
94d39e0a-c22c-4613-9e7f-c980907e6620	anatomy	BDS-I	An 8-year-old child presents with a "Strawberry tongue." This is characteristic of which condition?	{"Pernicious anemia","Scarlet fever","Vitamin B deficiency","Oral Candidiasis"}	1	Scarlet fever causes atrophy of lingual mucosa, leading to the "strawberry tongue" appearance.	Tongue	\N
f03540e6-f7d6-4c03-ad94-d3840ff2f84a	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with white plaque-like lesions on the tongue. The patient is HIV positive. What is the likely diagnosis?	{Mumps,"Hairy leukoplakia",Glossitis,"Ludwig's Angina"}	1	Epstein-Barr virus causes hairy leukoplakia (white mucosal lesions) often seen in HIV-positive patients.	Oral Mucosa	\N
0335951a-00a5-4111-8d29-e964f148dc5e	anatomy	BDS-I	A clinician is performing a tracheostomy. What is the most preferred site for the incision?	{"Above the hyoid bone","2nd or 3rd tracheal rings","Thyroid cartilage","Cricothyroid membrane"}	1	The most preferred site for tracheostomy is the 2nd or 3rd tracheal rings.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
8aa34756-9abf-45e6-a036-0b57a8069c59	anatomy	BDS-I	A 40-year-old female sway to the right while walking and cannot perform rapid pronation/supination (dysdiadochokinesia) of the right arm. MRI shows a tumor. Where?	{"Left Cerebrum","Right Cerebellum","Medulla Oblongata",Midbrain}	1	The cerebellum controls equilibrium and fine movements; its control is ipsilateral.	Neuroanatomy	\N
0f74e6d3-8e3f-489a-9a1c-52b601fba370	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "nasal twang" after a tonsillectomy. Which nerve plexus might have been irritated or damaged?	{"Brachial plexus","Pharyngeal plexus","Cervical plexus","Pterygoid plexus"}	1	The pharyngeal plexus supplies the muscles of the soft palate; its dysfunction causes nasal twang.	Pharynx	\N
a7afce9e-8077-4070-b53e-c91337258881	anatomy	BDS-I	A clinician wants to test the "sneeze reflex." Which nerve is the afferent path?	{Ophthalmic,Maxillary,Mandibular,Facial}	1	The maxillary nerve (V2) is the afferent path for the sneeze reflex.	Cranial Nerves	\N
69e94c72-1424-4e39-9d2b-e806a4f2a1b1	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has lost the "jaw jerk reflex." Which nerve is involved?	{"Maxillary nerve","Mandibular nerve","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"}	1	The mandibular nerve (V3) mediates the masseteric (jaw jerk) reflex.	Cranial Nerves	\N
5dbeeff7-93a3-4301-ab8a-7041fd601234	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "clasp-knife rigidity" and a positive Babinski sign. Where is the lesion?	{"Lower Motor Neuron","Upper Motor Neuron","Basal Ganglia",Cerebellum}	1	These are characteristic signs of an Upper Motor Neuron (UMN) lesion.	Neuroanatomy	\N
6980eea0-4b42-4cdb-8f8e-40cbf2543639	anatomy	BDS-I	A child has a "small nose" and "toneless voice" due to obligate mouth breathing. What is the diagnosis?	{Tonsillitis,Adenoids,"Cleft Palate",Glossitis}	1	Hypertrophy of the nasopharyngeal tonsils (adenoids) leads to these clinical features.	Pharynx	\N
20f09530-ff7c-434d-8fb6-abc28dbaaf4a	anatomy	BDS-I	During a surgery in the posterior triangle of the neck, a nerve is injured, resulting in "Wry Neck" (Torticollis). Which nerve?	{Phrenic,"Long thoracic","Spinal Accessory",Vagus}	2	The spinal accessory nerve (XI) supplies the sternocleidomastoid; its irritation/spasm causes torticollis.	Neck Triangles	\N
85db1561-8381-4a5c-bcfd-c670d4cdbb3a	anatomy	BDS-I	A 55-year-old female presents with tremors, weight loss, and bulging eyes (exophthalmos). Diagnosis?	{"Bell's Palsy","Toxic Goitre",Mumps,"Horner's Syndrome"}	1	These are classic symptoms of toxic goiter (hyperthyroidism).	Endocrine	\N
8751cf0b-d399-4f57-8c64-af10ca2a3e25	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Crocodile Tears" (lacrimation while eating). What is the cause?	{"Sympathetic overactivity","Aberrant regeneration of VII nerve","Trigeminal neuralgia","Parotid duct stone"}	1	Aberrant regeneration of fibers for the submandibular gland into sheaths supplying the lacrimal gland causes this syndrome.	Cranial Nerves	\N
8b38458e-0799-45df-9ab1-89ce0e152cc7	anatomy	BDS-I	A clinician is checking for jaundice. Which part of the oral cavity is a good site for observation?	{"Dorsum of tongue","Undersurface of tongue","Hard palate",Gingiva}	1	The undersurface of the tongue is a good site for observing jaundice.	Tongue	\N
161af711-9942-4645-bbd5-d24eb830d981	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "Magenta tongue." Which deficiency is suspected?	{"Vitamin A","Vitamin B complex","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"}	1	Vitamin B complex deficiency leads to characteristic changes like a magenta tongue.	Tongue	\N
4f81fd2a-6ef5-40e6-a725-29ec5424bb9a	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a "bruit" heard over the origin of the left common carotid. An angiogram shows 75% narrowing. Which vessel is most likely involved?	{"External Carotid","Internal Carotid","Vertebral Artery","Maxillary Artery"}	1	Narrowing (stenosis) of the internal carotid artery at its origin is a common cause of transient ischemic attacks.	Blood Supply	\N
e73ce4cd-967c-4521-8fe5-6713c70baf39	anatomy	BDS-I	Following parotidectomy, a patient loses taste on the anterior 2/3 of the tongue and has a dry mouth. Which nerve was damaged?	{Auriculotemporal,"Chorda tympani",Lingual,"Marginal mandibular"}	1	The chorda tympani carries taste and secretomotor fibers for the submandibular/sublingual glands; it is closely related to the parotid area.	Cranial Nerves	\N
58912909-480c-47f0-a1c6-8d89bb19faa4	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "bitemporal hemianopia." Where is the lesion?	{"Optic nerve","Central part of optic chiasma","Optic tract","Visual cortex"}	1	A pituitary tumor compressing the central part of the optic chiasma (decussating nasal fibers) causes bitemporal hemianopia.	Neuro-ophthalmology	\N
e7bc29fa-1450-4bde-bf51-10c3e92a999a	anatomy	BDS-I	A child has poorly mineralized soft bones and "bossing" of the frontal bones of the skull. Diagnosis?	{Scurvy,Rickets,Syphilis,Osteoarthritis}	1	Rickets is defective mineralization of bone matrix, leading to skull deformities like frontal bossing.	Pathology	\N
54f78ee0-6b3e-42ac-b862-52ce9e7d5dac	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Quinsy." Where is the abscess located?	{"Parotid space","Peritonsillar space","Retropharyngeal space","Submental space"}	1	Quinsy is another name for a peritonsillar abscess.	Pharynx	\N
8deedda4-52dd-4d42-b12b-dbb8db715d19	anatomy	BDS-I	During a surgical removal of a submandibular gland, the clinician must be careful of which artery?	{Maxillary,Lingual,Facial,"Superficial temporal"}	2	The facial artery is the most commonly damaged artery during submandibular gland removal.	Salivary Glands	\N
da9293eb-8277-49d0-917d-3e102fcd8b50	anatomy	BDS-I	A 75-year-old man has a painless swelling below his chin and an ulcer on the tip of his tongue. Which lymph nodes are involved?	{Submandibular,Submental,"Deep cervical",Retro-pharyngeal}	1	The tip of the tongue and floor of the mouth drain into the submental lymph nodes.	Lymphatics	\N
65ba1fd3-dbab-40f8-a3b3-86bb7c67a18f	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "diplopia" (double vision) and compensatory head-tilting. Which cranial nerve is injured?	{III,IV,VI,II}	1	The trochlear nerve (IV) supplies the superior oblique; its paralysis causes diplopia on downward gaze and compensatory head tilting.	Cranial Nerves	\N
c6f28833-cb38-4f7e-a5ea-32e87784d40e	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "Hoarse voice" following a thyroid surgery. Which nerve was likely damaged?	{"Superior laryngeal","Recurrent laryngeal",Glossopharyngeal,Hypoglossal}	1	The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies most larynx muscles; damage causes hoarseness.	Larynx	\N
f2132d57-b60d-4a50-8732-5e2f28f671cf	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Pulsating Exophthalmos" after a head injury. Which vessel is ruptured?	{"Middle meningeal artery","Internal carotid artery","Facial vein","Central artery of retina"}	1	Rupture of the internal carotid within the cavernous sinus leads to this condition.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
