5b22378b-6318-4ff4-ba14-87d18d6a806d	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a "Cephalhaematoma" that is restricted to the parietal bone. Where is the blood located?	{"Subcutaneous tissue","Subaponeurotic space","Subperiosteal space","Epidural space"}	2	Cephalhaematoma is a subperiosteal collection of blood, bound by suture lines.	Scalp	\N
8612b368-7452-43b9-9bee-10287d07d848	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "Pulp space infection" (Felon) of the index finger. Failure to decompress can lead to necrosis of:	{"The joint","The proximal phalanx","The diaphysis of terminal phalanx","The nail bed"}	2	In children, the blood supply to the diaphysis passes through the pulp space; pressure can cause necrosis.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
37a2bf1f-80b5-4c3d-9630-c9b3cb8ef2c7	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "internal strabismus" and ipsilateral facial palsy. The lesion is likely in:	{Midbrain,Pons,Medulla,Cerebellum}	1	The nuclei of VI and VII are located in the pons; a lesion here can affect both.	Neuroanatomy	\N
e03edf84-5a31-48f5-89da-0afc02caa78b	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "clasp-knife rigidity" and exaggerated reflexes on the left side. A CT shows a lesion in the:	{"Right internal capsule","Left internal capsule","Right cerebellum","Left cerebellum"}	0	Upper motor neuron signs on the left side indicate a lesion in the right cerebral hemisphere, often the internal capsule.	Neuroanatomy	\N
d888cf8c-7647-403c-9c37-a7b754265f23	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "nasal twang" and the soft palate drops. Which cranial nerve is damaged?	{VII,IX,X,XII}	2	The vagus nerve (X) supplies the muscles of the soft palate via the pharyngeal plexus.	Cranial Nerves	\N
0a637395-e2f6-41eb-966f-dfdf3e33bf0c	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Epiphora" (tears overflowing). This can be a sign of paralysis of which muscle?	{Frontalis,Buccinator,"Orbicularis oculi","Zygomaticus major"}	2	Paralysis of the lower part of the orbicularis oculi prevents tears from entering the lacrimal puncta, causing epiphora.	Cranial Nerves	\N
