78ffbca0-1738-491a-a284-824559ae7534	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Dysphagia." What does this clinical term mean?	{"Difficulty in speaking","Difficulty in breathing","Difficulty in swallowing","Loss of taste"}	2	Dysphagia is the clinical term for difficulty in swallowing.	Clinical Medicine	\N
d3cfb201-9b78-4cef-8113-bece0690b6a2	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "Pharyngeal fistula" opening on the side of the neck. This is a developmental defect of:	{"Thyroglossal duct","Pharyngeal arches","Rathke's pouch","Sella turcica"}	1	Many facial and neck defects result from a lack of proper transformation of the pharyngeal arches.	Embryology	\N
d1a200c7-da18-4240-bab9-6a0864c1ca32	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Ankylosis" of a primary tooth. What has happened?	{"The root is infected","The root has fused with the bony socket","The tooth is impacted","The tooth is missing"}	1	Ankylosis occurs when the tooth root fuses with the underlying hard tissue (bone).	Dentistry	\N
2b89ca60-39a9-4e23-b513-933220dca6e1	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Gingivitis." What is the primary cause?	{"Viral infection","Accumulation of plaque or calculus","Vitamin D deficiency",Trauma}	1	Gingivitis is usually caused by the accumulation of plaque or calculus containing bacteria.	Dentistry	\N
bd86ffc4-0db3-4852-a6ca-cb40751c2612	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Oral candidiasis." How does it present?	{"Red beefy tongue","White plaque-like lesions","Vesicles on the lip","Swollen gums"}	1	Oral candidiasis presents as white plaque-like fungal lesions.	Oral Mucosa	\N
a49375e7-d951-4907-b95c-2bc121622d3d	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Myopia." How is this corrected?	{"Convex lens","Concave lens","Bifocal lens","Surgery only"}	1	Myopia is corrected using a concave (diverging) lens.	Ophthalmology	\N
7f4ab010-76b0-4276-88c4-1630f4537066	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Ptosis" and "Lateral strabismus." Which nerve is paralyzed?	{III,IV,VI,VII}	0	The oculomotor nerve (III) paralysis causes ptosis and lateral squint.	Cranial Nerves	\N
5cd48e67-15c2-40b0-a9ae-c6807cf311ae	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Benedikt’s syndrome." Which part of the brain is affected?	{Pons,Medulla,Midbrain,Cerebellum}	2	Benedikt’s syndrome is caused by vascular ischemia of the tegmentum of the midbrain.	Neuroanatomy	\N
42882a2f-f592-4be4-9099-5889e8c59294	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 4-month-old infant with bilateral cataracts and hepatomegaly shows recurrent hypoglycemia. Which biochemical mechanism explains the hypoglycemia in this condition?	{"Direct toxicity of galactose on pancreatic beta cells","Inhibition of glycogen phosphorylase by accumulated Galactose-1-phosphate","Increased conversion of glucose to sorbitol in the liver","Rapid depletion of ATP due to galactokinase activity"}	1	In classical galactosemia, accumulated galactose-1-phosphate inhibits glycogen phosphorylase, the key enzyme for liver glycogenolysis, preventing the release of glucose during fasting.	Carbohydrate Metabolism	\N
94510676-6f2a-4eff-b24c-b4786f88068f	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	While evaluating a patient for dental procedures, you notice "mottling" and a rough ridge on the teeth. The patient is from a region with high fluoride in drinking water. Fluoride is called a "two-edged sword" because:	{"It prevents caries at <2 ppm but causes fluorosis at >5 ppm","It strengthens enamel but causes rapid root resorption","It inhibits bacterial growth but increases oral acidity","It prevents osteoporosis but causes skeletal fragility"}	0	At concentrations <2 ppm, fluoride creates a protective fluoroapatite layer on enamel to prevent caries; however, chronic ingestion of water with >5 ppm leads to dental fluorosis.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
88c3f6ad-78d8-4175-8cc1-f59b66ae856a	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient presenting with swollen, bleeding gums and perifollicular hemorrhages is diagnosed with Scurvy. The biochemical defect involves the failure of which post-translational modification?	{"Gamma-carboxylation of glutamic acid residues","Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in procollagen","Glycosylation of the triple helix structure","Cleavage of N-terminal signal sequences"}	1	Vitamin C is essential for lysyl and prolyl hydroxylase enzymes, which convert proline and lysine to hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine. This is required to stabilize the collagen structure.	Vitamins / Collagen	\N
d5e347dd-84e7-44bd-bf9d-8024f06c2cd5	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A child with von Gierke’s disease (GSD Type Ia) presents with gouty arthritis. What is the metabolic basis for the elevated serum uric acid in this patient?	{"Increased protein catabolism due to hypoglycemia","Inhibition of xanthine oxidase by lactate","Diversion of Glucose-6-phosphate to the HMP shunt, increasing ribose-5-phosphate","Direct competitive inhibition of renal urate transporters by glucose"}	2	Lack of glucose-6-phosphatase leads to accumulation of G-6-P, which enters the HMP shunt, producing excess ribose-5-phosphate, a precursor for purine synthesis and subsequent uric acid production.	GSD / Nucleotide Metabolism	\N
6456c8a3-acdf-4c4f-83b1-90bc746f02ee	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In a patient with Ornithine Transcarbamoylase (OTC) deficiency, why is there a marked increase in urinary orotic acid?	{"OTC directly converts ornithine to orotic acid","Carbamoyl phosphate accumulates in mitochondria and shunts to the cytosol for pyrimidine synthesis","Ammonia toxicity forces the liver to synthesize orotic acid as a secondary nitrogen waste","Orotic acid cannot be converted to citrulline in the absence of OTC"}	1	OTC deficiency causes mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate to accumulate and leak into the cytosol, where it enters the pyrimidine biosynthetic pathway, resulting in excessive orotic acid production.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
d129be99-06d9-49b8-ba10-3eba632575d3	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A child with "kinky" hair and growth retardation is diagnosed with Menkes syndrome. This condition is primarily a disorder of:	{"Copper absorption/transport","Iron storage","Zinc-dependent protein synthesis","Manganese-activated enzymes"}	0	Menkes syndrome is caused by a deficiency of copper due to defective intestinal absorption and transport. Copper is a vital cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which is needed for collagen cross-linking.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
50e96a4e-44a2-46e1-9433-41ad8d671732	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 25-year-old male develops hemolytic anemia after taking the antimalarial drug Primaquine. Which enzyme deficiency and resulting metabolic failure are responsible?	{"G6PD deficiency; failure to maintain reduced glutathione","Pyruvate kinase deficiency; failure of the Na-K pump","Aldolase B deficiency; accumulation of fructose-1-phosphate","Hexokinase deficiency; failure of anaerobic glycolysis"}	0	G6PD deficiency reduces the production of NADPH, which is essential for maintaining reduced glutathione. Without it, reactive oxygen species from drugs like Primaquine cause oxidative damage and hemolysis.	Carbohydrate Metabolism	\N
2a0d8bf5-a160-4ff3-afe6-21c77e8a96a9	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 17-year-old male with tremors and behavioral changes exhibits a golden-brown Kayser-Fleischer ring on slit-lamp examination. What is the primary molecular defect?	{"Mutation in the ceruloplasmin gene causing copper transport failure","Mutation in the ATP7B gene encoding a copper-transporting ATPase","Deficiency of metallothionein in the liver and kidneys","Defective biliary secretion of zinc leading to secondary copper toxicity"}	1	Wilson’s disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene, which encodes a protein vital for copper secretion into bile and its incorporation into ceruloplasmin.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
e548a530-67e8-43a3-ba48-a73f0ff37ad0	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In classic Phenylketonuria (PKU), why must Tyrosine be supplemented in the diet, making it a "conditionally essential" amino acid?	{"Tyrosine is used to competitively inhibit phenylalanine hydroxylase","Phenylalanine is converted to Tyrosine by an alternative pathway that is saturated in PKU","The primary pathway for Tyrosine synthesis from Phenylalanine is blocked","Tyrosine is rapidly excreted in the urine as phenylpyruvate"}	2	PKU is due to a deficiency in phenylalanine hydroxylase. Since this enzyme is required to synthesize Tyrosine from Phenylalanine, Tyrosine becomes essential for these patients.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
b3c895c9-4c83-41f5-978f-1517d9045a6a	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with sickle cell anemia experiences a "crisis" triggered by high altitudes. The "sickling" of red blood cells under these conditions is due to:	{"Increased oxygen affinity of HbS causing cell swelling","Polymerization of HbS in the deoxygenated state","Cleavage of the beta-globin chain by low pH","Binding of 2,3-BPG to oxy-hemoglobin"}	1	In HbS, valine replaces glutamate at the 6th position of the beta-chain. In the deoxygenated state, these mutant hemoglobin molecules polymerize into fibers that distort the RBC into a sickle shape.	Hemoglobinopathy	\N
4f12e497-7af6-4a01-8496-0d21a46c79ca	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 42-year-old male reports that his urine turns black upon standing for a few hours. This phenomenon is due to the auto-oxidation of:	{Phenylpyruvate,Homogentisate,Melanin,Bilirubin}	1	In Alkaptonuria, the deficiency of homogentisic acid oxidase leads to the accumulation of homogentisic acid, which undergoes auto-oxidation to form a blackish pigment called "alkapton".	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
44c24793-1194-453a-9eaf-aaf56cb03d45	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A neonate presents with severe vomiting and jaundice after starting breastfeeding. Laboratory tests show high plasma galactose and absent uridyltransferase activity. What is the metabolic reason for the cataract development in this child?	{"Accumulation of Galactose-1-phosphate in the retina","Reduction of galactose to galactitol by aldose reductase in the lens","Inhibition of glucose uptake by high levels of galactose","Direct oxidative damage by galactose to lens proteins"}	1	In galactosemia, unmetabolized galactose enters the polyol pathway. Aldose reductase converts it to galactitol, which is osmotically active and causes lens swelling and opacity.	Carbohydrate Metabolism	\N
6b7db76c-9b09-4eac-bb8c-d583ae130164	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with von Gierke’s disease exhibits painful joints. The biochemical link between G6Pase deficiency and hyperuricemia involves which pathway?	{"Increased breakdown of muscle protein","Sequestration of phosphate in Glucose-6-phosphate","Diversion of G6P to the HMP shunt increasing ribose-5-phosphate","Activation of the urea cycle due to hyperammonemia"}	2	Accumulated G6P is shunted into the HMP pathway, leading to excess ribose-5-phosphate. This increases the synthesis of phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate (PRPP), stimulating purine production and subsequent uric acid catabolism.	GSD / Nucleotide Metabolism	\N
8453b3a5-b588-43f3-8fd5-f7320f58c75b	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A chronic alcoholic is found with mental confusion, ataxia, and involuntary eye movements. A biochemical assay of an HMP shunt enzyme in RBCs reveals a 10-fold reduction in its affinity for its cofactor. Which enzyme and cofactor are involved?	{"Transketolase; Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; NADP+","Glutathione reductase; FAD","Pyruvate dehydrogenase; Lipoic acid"}	0	This describes Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The genetic lesion is an alteration in transketolase, reducing its affinity for thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), which is already low in alcoholics due to malnutrition.	Vitamins / HMP Shunt	\N
0f6a0ca3-af2b-4fba-a0f8-52d26c43e800	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	While performing a dental procedure on an infant with Hurler syndrome, you note spaced dentition and macroglossia. What specific product is accumulating in the lysosomes of this patient?	{"Keratan sulfate","Heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate","GM2 ganglioside",Sphingomyelin}	1	Hurler syndrome (MPS I) is caused by a deficiency of alpha-L-iduronidase, leading to the accumulation of heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate in various tissues.	Mucopolysaccharidosis	\N
6b78b4df-eb69-4c15-a2cc-1f5b1211c863	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 25-year-old male develops hemolytic anemia after taking Primaquine. The drug triggers this reaction by inducing oxidative stress. Which molecule is deficient in his RBCs, making them susceptible?	{"Oxidized Glutathione (GSSG)","Reduced Glutathione (GSH)",NADH,"Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide (FAD)"}	1	G6PD deficiency prevents the production of NADPH, which is essential for maintaining reduced glutathione (GSH). GSH is required to neutralize reactive oxygen species generated by drugs like Primaquine.	Carbohydrate Metabolism	\N
255ecb5b-01c3-4bde-ab7b-f81e2e3fb804	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient presents with "M-spike" in the gamma region on serum protein electrophoresis and Bence-Jones proteins in urine. These urinary proteins are:	{"Heavy chains of IgG","Albumin-globulin complexes","Light chains (kappa or lambda) of immunoglobulins","Intact pentameric IgM"}	2	In Multiple Myeloma, malignant plasma cells produce excess monoclonal light chains (kappa or lambda), known as Bence-Jones proteins, which are small enough to be filtered and excreted in urine.	Plasma Proteins	\N
58e036eb-654c-415a-8252-cb5da518c4f4	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In a case of acute myocardial infarction, which enzyme marker appears earliest in the plasma, despite its lack of absolute cardiac specificity?	{"Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH1)",Myoglobin,"Aspartate Transaminase (AST)","Creatine Phosphokinase (CK-MB)"}	1	Myoglobin is the earliest marker (4–6 hours), but it is not cardiac-specific. CK-MB is the earliest cardiac-specific marker (6–18 hours).	Clinical Enzymology	\N
1a1083ca-f9a6-4753-9ecf-8c43c6bf2623	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 42-year-old male reports his urine turns black upon standing. Radiographs show narrowing of intervertebral discs. The accumulated metabolite is:	{Phenylpyruvate,"Homogentisic acid",Melanin,"Orotic acid"}	1	Alkaptonuria involves a deficiency of homogentisic acid oxidase. Accumulated homogentisic acid auto-oxidizes to form the black pigment "alkapton".	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
d5998522-1e3e-446c-b662-c12d259c2711	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why is dietary Tyrosine considered "conditionally essential" in patients with Phenylketonuria (PKU)?	{"It is needed to inhibit Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Tyrosine becomes the primary source of Phenylalanine","The conversion of Phenylalanine to Tyrosine is blocked","Tyrosine is used to synthesize essential ketone bodies"}	2	In PKU, the deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase prevents the conversion of Phenylalanine to Tyrosine, making Tyrosine a necessary dietary supplement.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
848cd126-8e04-43a3-ac99-eb642afa61da	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A newborn with "maple syrup" odor in the urine shows neurological deterioration. The biochemical defect involves which process?	{"Deamination of neutral amino acids","Oxidative decarboxylation of branched-chain alpha-keto acids","Transamination of aromatic amino acids","Decarboxylation of sulfur-containing amino acids"}	1	MSUD is caused by a deficiency in the branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex, which decarboxylates the keto acids of Leucine, Isoleucine, and Valine.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
cc43f20c-39ba-49cb-948c-b5d179b2aca7	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A medical student presents with yellowish palms and a "Broad beta band" on lipoprotein electrophoresis. This condition, Type III Hyperlipoproteinemia, is due to:	{"Deficiency of LDL receptors","Overproduction of Apo B-100","Abnormality in Apo-E leading to remnant accumulation","Deficiency of Lipoprotein Lipase"}	2	Type III Hyperlipoproteinemia (Broad Beta Disease) is characterized by the accumulation of IDL and remnant particles due to an abnormality in Apo-E, which prevents their clearance.	Lipid Metabolism	\N
abcd1881-0aab-4975-98f6-34dd16ba8213	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Fluoride is described as a "two-edged sword" in dental health. At what concentration does it typically cause "mottling" of teeth (fluorosis)?	{"< 1 ppm","1–2 ppm","> 5 ppm","0.5 ppm"}	2	Trace amounts of fluoride (<2 ppm) prevent caries, but chronic intake of water containing >5 ppm causes dental fluorosis, characterized by mottling.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
9cd93de3-bfb5-4d0d-bcaa-c4acb849f471	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Scurvy results in bleeding gums because Vitamin C is a necessary cofactor for the:	{"Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors","Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen","Synthesis of thromboxane A2","Glycosylation of fibrinogen"}	1	Vitamin C is essential for lysyl and prolyl hydroxylases, which stabilize the triple helix of collagen. Deficiency leads to fragile blood vessel walls.	Vitamins / Collagen	\N
617b579d-4875-481b-af61-373d51078b34	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In the liver, one gene produces ApoB-100 (100 kDa), while in the intestine, the same gene produces ApoB-48 (48 kDa). This is a result of:	{"Alternative DNA splicing","RNA Editing","Post-translational cleavage","Competitive inhibition of translation"}	1	This is a classic example of RNA editing, where a cytidine deaminase converts a CAA codon to a termination codon UAA in the intestinal mRNA, resulting in the truncated ApoB-48 protein.	Molecular Biology	\N
e96588e0-d0e9-435d-88b4-662a7d942253	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 30-year-old male with epigastric pain radiating to the back has a serum amylase of 1450 units/L. Which other enzyme is late to rise but more specific for this condition?	{SGPT,"Alkaline Phosphatase",Lipase,"Acid Phosphatase"}	2	In acute pancreatitis, serum amylase rises quickly but is less specific. Lipase is more specific and remains elevated longer.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
299ac602-411e-44db-ae34-2ae775ad4491	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with hyperammonemia and high urinary orotic acid likely has a deficiency in which urea cycle enzyme?	{"Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I","Ornithine transcarbamoylase (OTC)","Argininosuccinate lyase",Arginase}	1	OTC deficiency causes mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate to build up and shunt into the cytosol for pyrimidine synthesis, resulting in high orotic acid levels.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
5808943d-6cac-4fb7-822b-de1778ca6be8	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which form of Vitamin A is specifically involved in the visual cycle as a part of Rhodopsin?	{"Retinoic acid",Retinol,11-cis-retinal,All-trans-retinol}	2	11-cis-retinal binds with the protein opsin to form Rhodopsin. Upon exposure to light, it isomerizes to all-trans-retinal.	Vitamins	\N
cd87a715-340f-49db-b1e4-e413f6e5f256	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Wilson’s disease is characterized by copper toxicity in the liver and brain. The molecular defect is in:	{"Ceruloplasmin synthesis","ATP7B gene (copper-transporting ATPase)","Metallothionein sequestration","Biliary zinc secretion"}	1	Wilson’s disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene, which is responsible for excreting copper into bile and incorporating it into ceruloplasmin.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
5a3c094e-f667-4c14-985a-10b5a4555e04	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A strict vegan presents with megaloblastic anemia and numbness in the extremities. Which biochemical reaction is impaired due to his vitamin deficiency?	{"Conversion of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA","Hydroxylation of Phenylalanine to Tyrosine","Synthesis of Heme from ALA","Conversion of Glycine to Serine"}	0	Vitamin B12 is required for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Deficiency leads to methylmalonic acidemia and neurological symptoms due to impaired myelin synthesis.	Vitamins	\N
9ebfb419-0763-40d5-85ad-7c8faa6eac49	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Question	{A,B,C,D}	0	Explanation	Topic	\N
f881bf83-9f93-448f-a45e-3e9ede68d51a	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	To differentiate between von Gierke’s (GSD I) and Cori’s disease (GSD III), an intravenous glucagon challenge test is performed. In which state and condition will GSD III show a rise in blood sugar?	{"Fasting state; because debranching enzyme is functional","Postprandial state; because phosphorylase can act on outer branches","Fasting state; because gluconeogenesis is intact","Postprandial state; because glucose-6-phosphatase is intact"}	1	In GSD III, the debranching enzyme is missing but phosphorylase is normal. In the postprandial state, glucagon stimulates phosphorylase to release glucose from the outer branches of glycogen. In the fasting state, only "limit dextrin" remains, which phosphorylase cannot degrade further.	Glycogen Storage Disorders	\N
6eadc22a-c9b4-45a6-909e-9854512ac156	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with high-fat intake shows a "creamy layer" on top of their plasma after overnight refrigeration. This is due to an accumulation of:	{LDL,VLDL,Chylomicrons,IDL}	2	Chylomicrons are the least dense lipoproteins and are rich in dietary triacylglycerols. Because they are lighter than water, they float to the top of plasma when stored at 4°C, forming a characteristic creamy layer.	Lipid Metabolism	\N
7a4db94b-5a54-441b-b8be-9add1c68568f	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In the treatment of Methanol poisoning, Ethanol is administered intravenously. What is the biochemical rationale for this "twisted" therapy?	{"Ethanol neutralizes the acidic metabolites of methanol","Ethanol acts as a competitive inhibitor of Alcohol Dehydrogenase","Ethanol increases the renal clearance of formaldehyde","Ethanol stabilizes the retinal membranes against oxidative damage"}	1	Ethanol has a much higher affinity for Alcohol Dehydrogenase than methanol. By acting as a competitive inhibitor, it prevents the conversion of methanol into toxic formaldehyde and formic acid, allowing methanol to be excreted safely.	Clinical Toxicology / Enzymes	\N
fbc811be-0de2-4b2f-bd68-d548bc76ed46	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with a history of Tuberculosis treated with Isoniazid (INH) presents with "Pellagra-like" symptoms. The biochemical basis is the inhibition of:	{"Niacin absorption in the gut","Pyridoxine phosphokinase","Tryptophan pyrrolase",Kynureninase}	1	INH is a structural analog of Vitamin B6. It inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase, preventing the activation of B6 to PLP. Since PLP is a cofactor for the pathway converting Tryptophan to Niacin, a secondary niacin deficiency occurs.	Vitamins / Metabolism	\N
dc96fa67-017f-44c4-a3fc-99c3108734a6	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why does "Malignant PKU" fail to respond to a simple low-phenylalanine diet?	{"It involves a total deletion of the Phenylalanine Hydroxylase gene","The defect lies in the synthesis or recycling of Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4)","Tyrosine becomes toxic to the central nervous system","Phenylalanine is absorbed through an alternative \\"leaky\\" transporter"}	1	Malignant PKU is due to a deficiency in BH4 (tetrahydrobiopterin), which is a required cofactor not only for phenylalanine hydroxylase but also for enzymes synthesizing serotonin and catecholamines. Diet alone cannot fix the neurotransmitter deficit.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
dafc96a1-4f28-49a3-ad85-d0e8ebab437f	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A 45-year-old male with severe epigastric pain has a serum amylase of 600 U/L and lipase of 900 U/L. Why is lipase considered more specific than amylase for his diagnosis?	{"Amylase is only found in the pancreas","Lipase remains elevated for a shorter duration, indicating acute status","Amylase can be elevated in mumps and intestinal obstruction","Lipase is filtered more rapidly by the kidneys"}	2	While Amylase is sensitive, it is found in salivary glands and other tissues; it can be elevated in mumps (parotitis) or renal failure. Lipase is more specific to pancreatic tissue and remains elevated longer (3–7 days).	Clinical Enzymology	\N
796b75bd-62c2-486f-8df4-597d0b79934c	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	On a lipoprotein electrophoresis, a "Broad Beta Band" is a diagnostic hallmark. This indicates an abnormality in which apoprotein?	{"Apo B-100","Apo C-II","Apo E","Apo A-I"}	2	A Broad Beta Band occurs in Type III Hyperlipoproteinemia (Remnant Removal Disease). It is caused by an abnormality in Apo E, which prevents the liver from recognizing and clearing chylomicron remnants and IDL.	Lipid Metabolism	\N
2946862d-04b2-48dc-a315-b1f670dfba4e	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with chronic alcoholism presents with "Burning Feet Syndrome." Which vitamin deficiency is classically associated with this manifestation?	{"Thiamine (B1)","Pantothenic acid (B5)","Pyridoxine (B6)","Cobalamin (B12)"}	1	Pantothenic acid (Vitamin B5) deficiency is linked to "Burning Feet Syndrome," characterized by numbness, tingling, and fatigue. This was famously documented by Dr. Gopalan in malnourished populations.	Vitamins	\N
bf0a0219-ce1c-4089-98f4-f66277d1b06b	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In the visual cycle, what occurs immediately after light hits the Rhodopsin molecule in the retina?	{"Synthesis of new 11-cis-retinal","Photo-isomerization of 11-cis-retinal to all-trans-retinal","Binding of Retinoic acid to nuclear receptors","Dissociation of Opsin into its amino acid components"}	1	Rhodopsin contains 11-cis-retinal. When light strikes it, the molecule undergoes photo-isomerization to all-trans-retinal, triggering the nerve impulse.	Vitamins	\N
374b1ca8-cc53-4999-887b-d337ddbe76f3	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A neonate with "Cabbage-like" odor in the urine and liver failure is suspected of Tyrosinosis (Type I). The defective enzyme is:	{"Tyrosine transaminase","p-Hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase","Homogentisate oxidase","Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase"}	3	Tyrosinemia Type I (Tyrosinosis) is caused by a deficiency of Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase. The accumulation of metabolites leads to the characteristic "cabbage-like" odor and severe liver/renal damage.	Amino Acid Metabolism	\N
a4ed3549-0638-48dc-a435-f339bda965e1	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why is the "Anion Gap" elevated in a patient with Diabetic Ketoacidosis?	{"Increase in serum chloride to compensate for glucose loss","Accumulation of unmeasured organic acids like acetoacetate","Rapid excretion of potassium by the kidneys","Increase in bicarbonate levels due to respiratory compensation"}	1	The Anion Gap represents unmeasured anions (Proteins, Sulfates, Phosphates, and Organic acids). In DKA, the accumulation of ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate) increases this gap.	Acid-Base Balance	\N
37718b58-87cb-458b-ae3e-468c712979c5	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A child with kinky, brittle hair and growth retardation has a defect in the ATP7A gene. This affects the activity of which enzyme?	{Tyrosinase,"Lysyl oxidase","Cytochrome c oxidase","All of the above"}	3	Menkes Syndrome (Copper deficiency) affects all copper-dependent enzymes. Lysyl oxidase (collagen cross-linking), Tyrosinase (melanin), and Cytochrome c oxidase (ETC) are all impaired, leading to kinky hair, pale skin, and hypothermia.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
c79d8f33-3ad4-460d-9616-3e23e76b7c74	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which laboratory test is the "Gold Standard" for confirming a diagnosis of Congenital Sucrase-Isomaltase Deficiency (CSID)?	{"Breath Hydrogen Test","Sucrose Tolerance Test","Small intestine biopsy for disaccharidase activity","Stool acidity (pH) measurement"}	2	While tolerance tests are useful, the Gold Standard for CSID is a small intestine biopsy to directly assay the activity of sucrase and isomaltase enzymes.	Carbohydrate Metabolism	\N
f8beed05-3492-4192-b9d3-84d1e50e2349	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In Multiple Myeloma, the Bence-Jones proteins found in urine exhibit a unique "Heat Test" property. They:	{"Precipitate at 100°C and stay solid","Precipitate at 40-60°C and redissolve at 80-100°C","Only precipitate in the presence of strong mineral acids","Are only visible under polarized light when cooled"}	1	Bence-Jones proteins (monoclonal light chains) characteristically precipitate when heated to 40-60°C but uniquely redissolve at boiling temperatures (80-100°C).	Plasma Proteins	\N
18239c8f-cbdd-40f5-bd2b-f08f64517a04	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A "Broad Beta Disease" patient is at high risk for peripheral vascular disease. The primary accumulated particle is:	{"Nascent VLDL",LDL,"IDL (VLDL Remnants)",HDL3}	2	In Type III Hyperlipoproteinemia, the failure of Apo-E mediated clearance leads to the accumulation of IDL and chylomicron remnants, which are highly atherogenic.	Lipid Metabolism	\N
9940dbb0-2a88-4a66-96f6-057418f34175	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A child with suspected GSD Type Ia (Von Gierke) presents with joint pain. Which biochemical link explains this presentation?	{"Accumulation of lactic acid inhibits renal urate excretion","G-6-P accumulation shunts to HMP pathway increasing PRPP","Hypoglycemia triggers excessive protein catabolism","Direct inhibition of xanthine oxidase by glucose-6-phosphate"}	1	G-6-P builds up due to Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency and enters the HMP shunt, producing excess ribose-5-phosphate, a precursor for purine synthesis and uric acid.	GSD / Nucleotides	\N
65057e98-0e3c-4f62-a505-90c5082eb989	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In differentiating GSD Type Ia from GSD Type III (Cori’s), a glucagon challenge test is done. What result is seen in GSD Type III?	{"No rise in blood glucose in the fasting state","Rise in blood glucose in the fasting state","No rise in blood glucose in the post-prandial state","Blood glucose remains normal in both states"}	0	In GSD III, the debranching enzyme is missing. During fasting, phosphorylase can only act on outer branches, leaving "limit dextrin," so glucagon cannot raise blood glucose.	Carbohydrate Metabolism	\N
3190982d-cb6e-44fa-a5c5-4ac393cddbae	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with chronic alcoholism presents with "Burning Feet Syndrome." Which vitamin deficiency is the likely culprit?	{"Thiamine (B1)","Pantothenic acid (B5)","Pyridoxine (B6)","Cobalamin (B12)"}	1	Pantothenic acid (B5) deficiency is classically associated with "Burning Feet Syndrome" (Gopalan’s syndrome).	Vitamins	\N
