d87bc07a-482a-4c95-a3e1-89ddad8d8681	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which laboratory finding is expected in a patient with "Nephrotic Syndrome"?	{Hypolipidemia,"Proteinuria > 3.5 g/day",Hyperalbuminemia,"Negative Fouchet's Test"}	1	Nephrotic Syndrome is defined by heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/day), hypoalbuminemia, and hyperlipidemia.	Clinical Biochemistry	\N
88450ea3-5a56-4235-ab38-c1169b13f2ee	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A firefighter collapses with dizziness and rapid breathing. He is treated with Amyl Nitrite and Sodium Thiosulfate for:	{"Carbon Monoxide poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","Methanol poisoning","Lead toxicity"}	1	Cyanide blocks the electron transport chain; it is treated with Amyl Nitrite (to create methemoglobin) and Sodium Thiosulfate.	Clinical Toxicology	\N
78c57101-5f7d-46f5-9f8e-d5f469feb2fc	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient arrives 5 days after a suspected MI. Which marker is most useful for confirming the event at this late stage?	{Myoglobin,CK-MB,"Troponin I",LDH5}	2	Troponin I is a cardiac-specific marker that stays elevated for 5–9 days, making it ideal for late diagnosis. CK-MB returns to normal in 48 hours.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
e97a6e26-6c5c-478f-b9b0-17e95f13a54f	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	To differentiate if an elevated ALP is due to bone disease or liver disease, which enzyme should be tested next?	{SGOT,Amylase,GGT,"Acid Phosphatase"}	2	Gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT) is highly sensitive to biliary obstruction but is not found in bone; thus, its elevation confirms a hepatic origin for ALP.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
b9484904-c3f6-48c7-b222-9e2c9a1bb14f	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis, why is urinary amylase often preferred over serum amylase for a late presentation?	{"Urinary amylase is more specific","Serum amylase falls rapidly due to renal clearance","Amylase is only produced by the kidney in disease","Urinary amylase is easier to measure"}	1	Serum amylase rises quickly but falls rapidly because the kidneys clear it. Urinary amylase remains elevated longer and is more reliable for slightly delayed diagnosis.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
3b74357e-70b5-4fcc-aba8-2752f179187d	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A "flipped pattern" on LDH electrophoresis (LDH1 > LDH2) is classically diagnostic of:	{"Acute Hepatitis","Myocardial Infarction","Muscular Dystrophy","Renal Failure"}	1	In normal serum, LDH2 > LDH1. In myocardial infarction, the heart releases excess LDH1, causing the levels to "flip" within 12–24 hours.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
c6871ba2-f764-41dd-a1ce-d7dac5faf42c	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why is the AST/ALT ratio > 2.0 often seen in alcoholic hepatitis compared to other forms of liver damage?	{"ALT is destroyed by alcohol","Alcohol induces mitochondrial AST synthesis","AST is a smaller molecule and leaks faster","Alcohol prevents the renal clearance of AST"}	1	In alcoholic hepatitis, the AST/ALT ratio increases beyond 2.0, partly because AST is found in both cytosol and mitochondria, and alcohol causes specific mitochondrial damage.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
49ab034a-dd56-4004-a041-bafc11cd4823	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which enzyme serves as a "suicide inhibitor" when Allopurinol is used to treat Gout?	{"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase","Xanthine Oxidase","PRPP Synthetase","Adenosine Deaminase"}	1	Xanthine oxidase converts allopurinol to alloxanthine, which then acts as a suicide inhibitor by binding irreversibly to the enzyme's active site.	Nucleotide Metabolism	\N
612d4a7b-87ff-434c-b694-07bc37537a17	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient has metastatic prostate cancer. Which laboratory finding is the most specific for monitoring treatment response?	{"Total Acid Phosphatase","Alkaline Phosphatase","Tartrate-labile Acid Phosphatase",SGPT}	2	While total ACP is raised, tartrate-labile ACP is specifically secreted by the prostate and serves as a better tumor marker for prostatic carcinoma.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
14d9c3d8-26c1-4eda-a984-0d0a497392af	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	During the "Second Wind Phenomenon" in McArdle's disease, what biochemical change allows the patient to continue exercising?	{"Accumulation of lactic acid","Mobilization of blood-borne fuels like glucose and fatty acids","Activation of muscle phosphorylase by calcium","Spontaneous renaturation of glycogenin"}	1	In McArdle’s disease, the initial failure of glycogenolysis causes cramps. After a few minutes, the body mobilizes glucose and fatty acids from the blood, providing an alternative fuel source.	Glycogen Storage Disorders	\N
19cffe4d-54a9-4fca-b451-262e2e39b3b4	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Ethanol is used to treat Methanol poisoning because it acts as:	{"A non-competitive inhibitor of Alcohol Dehydrogenase","A suicide inhibitor of Formaldehyde","A competitive inhibitor of Alcohol Dehydrogenase","An allosteric activator of the Urea Cycle"}	2	Ethanol has a higher affinity for Alcohol Dehydrogenase than methanol. It outcompetes methanol, preventing the formation of toxic formaldehyde.	Clinical Toxicology	\N
a93d6984-564c-4d9a-a526-870b328a4fda	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which protein is the earliest marker for MI, though its lack of specificity requires other tests for confirmation?	{"Troponin T",Myoglobin,CK-MB,LDH1}	1	Myoglobin is the earliest marker (4–6 hours) but is not cardiac-specific as it is also found in skeletal muscle.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
e8fb2a09-02fe-43ca-82d5-3073821038fc	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In "I-cell disease," why are lysosomal enzymes found at high levels in the plasma instead of inside the lysosomes?	{"Failure to synthesize the enzyme proteins","Deficiency of the Mannose-6-phosphate tagging enzyme","Defective transport of enzymes across the mitochondrial membrane","Rapid degradation of the lysosomal membrane"}	1	In I-cell disease, a defect in GlcNAc-phosphotransferase prevents the addition of the mannose-6-phosphate tag, so enzymes are secreted into the plasma instead of being targeted to lysosomes.	Molecular Biology	\N
e8680ed0-a7d3-4f28-ae8a-bc8ebf1958bd	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	The proofreading activity of DNA Polymerase III is primarily due to its:	{"5' to 3' polymerase activity","3' to 5' exonuclease activity","5' to 3' exonuclease activity","Topoisomerase activity"}	1	DNA Polymerase III ensures high fidelity through its 3' to 5' exonuclease activity, which allows it to remove mispaired nucleotides immediately after incorporation.	Molecular Biology	\N
b6850b7e-2219-4e75-bf10-94ac9ed2c588	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	The "Wobble Hypothesis" explains why:	{"Multiple codons can code for the same amino acid","One codon can code for multiple amino acids","DNA replication is semi-conservative","Transcription is always 5' to 3'"}	0	The Wobble Hypothesis explains degeneracy; the third base of a codon can pair flexibly with the anticodon, allowing 61 codons to be served by fewer tRNAs.	Molecular Biology	\N
8f50d3eb-6589-45ff-938c-0856893255aa	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why does Isoniazid (INH) therapy lead to the excretion of Xanthurenic acid in urine?	{"INH directly converts tryptophan to xanthurenate","INH inhibits the Vitamin B6-dependent enzyme kynureninase","INH increases the renal threshold for tryptophan","INH is a structural analog of xanthurenic acid"}	1	INH inhibits the activation of Vitamin B6. Without PLP, the enzyme kynureninase fails, shunting tryptophan metabolism toward the production of xanthurenic acid.	Vitamins / Amino Acids	\N
