1a2a97f5-ced0-44fe-95eb-815725a48970	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why do patients with Vitamin B6 deficiency excrete "Xanthurenic acid" in their urine?	{"B6 directly breaks down xanthurenate","Lack of PLP inhibits Kynureninase","B6 is needed for the synthesis of xanthurenate","Xanthurenate is a vitamer of B6"}	1	In the tryptophan-niacin pathway, the enzyme kynureninase requires PLP (B6). Deficiency shunts kynurenine into an alternative pathway, producing xanthurenic acid.	Vitamins / Amino Acids	\N
eaec19e8-e63e-4264-9bb2-0f44841ebcdc	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A "flipped" LDH pattern (LDH1 > LDH2) in a patient with chest pain suggests:	{"Acute Hepatitis","Skeletal muscle injury","Myocardial Infarction","Pulmonary Embolism"}	2	Normally, LDH2 is higher than LDH1. In Myocardial Infarction, heart tissue releases LDH1, causing it to exceed LDH2 ("flipped pattern") within 12–24 hours.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
