4f07b8f6-fa63-4249-8957-d3bac6801fee	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which DNA sequencing method relies on the use of chain-terminating dideoxynucleotides (ddNTPs)?	{Maxam-Gilbert,"Sanger Method",PCR,"Northern Blotting"}	1	The Sanger Method (dideoxy method) uses ddNTPs to terminate DNA synthesis at specific bases for sequencing.	Molecular Biology	\N
274052bf-8167-414f-a52a-a5de627c8823	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A child with Von Gierke's disease (GSD Ia) has severe hyperlipidemia. What is the metabolic driver for this?	{"Inhibition of Hormone Sensitive Lipase by glucose","Blockade of gluconeogenesis forcing fat mobilization","Increased activity of Lipoprotein Lipase","Failure of the liver to synthesize VLDL"}	1	In GSD Ia, the inability to release glucose from the liver blocks gluconeogenesis. The body compensates for hypoglycemia by mobilizing fat stores, leading to high plasma free fatty acids and ketone bodies.	GSD / Lipids	\N
838e4c73-f2c1-40fa-a19f-40ad4d2759f9	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	In a patient with Alkaptonuria, why does the urine give a "false positive" Benedict's test?	{"Homogentisic acid is a reducing sugar","Homogentisic acid has strong reducing activity","The disease is always associated with diabetes","Alkaptonuria causes high renal clearance of glucose"}	1	Homogentisic acid is a strong reducing agent. It reacts with the copper reagent in Benedict's test, leading to a false positive result for reducing substances, even in the absence of glucose.	Amino Acids	\N
34939beb-12be-43c9-bc5b-4f975114c35f	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient on high-dose Isoniazid (INH) therapy develops peripheral neuropathy. Supplementation of which vitamin would have prevented this?	{"Thiamine (B1)","Riboflavin (B2)","Pyridoxine (B6)","Cobalamin (B12)"}	2	INH inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase, the enzyme required to activate Vitamin B6 to PLP. This deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy, which is prevented by B6 supplementation.	Vitamins	\N
7d697b3f-e7d6-4476-8d0f-6897c36d6431	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which lipoprotein particle is considered the most "lethally dangerous" or atherogenic due to its small size and high cholesterol content?	{"Large VLDL","Nascent Chylomicrons","Small dense LDL (sdLDL)",HDL3}	2	While all LDL is considered "bad," small dense LDL (sdLDL) is the most dangerous fraction because it easily infiltrates the arterial wall, leading to atherosclerosis and coronary heart disease.	Lipid Metabolism	\N
eecf3e8b-c127-43f1-b520-cf3d5df30c38	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A neonate with severe hypoglycemia and high levels of medium-chain acylcarnitines (C8) is suspected of MCAD deficiency. Why is this condition "non-ketotic"?	{"Ketone bodies are used up too fast by the brain","Defective beta-oxidation prevents the generation of Acetyl-CoA","The liver is unable to synthesize HMG-CoA","MCAD is the rate-limiting enzyme for ketogenesis"}	1	MCAD deficiency blocks the beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids. Since beta-oxidation is the source of Acetyl-CoA for ketone body synthesis, the patient develops hypoketotic hypoglycemia.	Lipid Metabolism	\N
f27d70ff-775a-4d6c-ba7f-6512d663461c	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	On a serum protein electrophoresis, a "sharp alpha-2-globulin" band in the presence of very low albumin is a hallmark of:	{"Multiple Myeloma","Acute Hepatitis","Nephrotic Syndrome","Chronic Inflammation"}	2	In Nephrotic Syndrome, large proteins like alpha-2-macroglobulin are retained while albumin is lost in urine. This creates a characteristic pattern of low albumin and a sharp alpha-2 peak.	Plasma Proteins	\N
ea654a36-019d-4e4f-b72b-19d764d76875	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which specific "RNA Editing" event occurs in the human intestine to produce Apolipoprotein B-48?	{"A-to-I editing of a start codon","C-to-U conversion of a CAA codon to UAA","Methylation of the 5' cap","Deletion of an entire exon from the transcript"}	1	In the intestine, cytidine deaminase converts a CAA codon to a UAA (Stop) codon in the ApoB mRNA. This produces the truncated ApoB-48 protein instead of the full-length ApoB-100 made in the liver.	Molecular Biology	\N
5d16b869-6910-46ba-8124-731e328380bf	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A patient with chest pain has an LDH1 level higher than LDH2. This "flipped pattern" is diagnostic of Myocardial Infarction if it occurs within:	{"1-2 hours","4-6 hours","12-24 hours","5-7 days"}	2	Normally, LDH2 is higher than LDH1. In MI, heart tissue releases excess LDH1, causing the levels to "flip" (LDH1 > LDH2) within 12 to 24 hours after the event.	Clinical Enzymology	\N
69f8f481-adff-4d21-9458-e877e420a7d0	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Why is the "Anion Gap" calculated in patients with Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)?	{"To measure the loss of bicarbonate in urine","To detect unmeasured organic acids like acetoacetate","To calculate the exact dose of insulin required","To differentiate between DKA and Lactic Acidosis"}	1	The Anion Gap measures "unmeasured" anions. In DKA, the accumulation of ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate) increases this gap, helping to confirm metabolic acidosis.	Acid-Base Balance	\N
8b109540-3147-4e11-a5c2-b7e465e5dc08	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	Which copper-dependent enzyme's failure is responsible for the "hypopigmented hair" seen in Menkes syndrome?	{"Lysyl oxidase","Cytochrome c oxidase",Tyrosinase,"Superoxide dismutase"}	2	Tyrosinase is a copper-dependent enzyme essential for melanin synthesis. Its failure in Menkes syndrome (copper deficiency) results in hypopigmented hair and light skin color.	Mineral Metabolism	\N
