e11504e0-83b1-4ee8-81bd-1d608c0d25ed	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Which gene is associated with dentin mineralization?	{MAP1B,PHEX,Fgf-8,Gsc}	1	PHEX is implicated in dentin mineralization.	Genetics	\N
9f65c58d-e513-48e8-a4ad-4026f079aab6	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	The reduced thickness of the stellate reticulum at the crown tip indicates:	{"Tooth death","Imminent eruption","Beginning of formation","Root resorption"}	2	Indicates the beginning of hard tissue formation.	Tooth Development	\N
8ec8eb6a-0121-4b46-b949-c42a8bddcc3e	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	The specific gravity of dental enamel is:	{2.1,2.5,2.8,3.2}	2	The specific gravity is 2.8.	Enamel Physics	\N
80f28178-3044-4ca3-879a-0b4e5a3ea805	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Peritubular dentin is what % more calcified than intertubular?	{0.09,0.25,0.4,0.6}	2	Approximately 40% more calcified.	Dentin Structure	\N
acfc9acc-2338-498f-8591-edde47983db7	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Dentin sialoprotein (DSP) is a marker for:	{Bone,Enamel,"Dentin synthesis",PDL}	2	Specific marker for dentin synthesis.	Dentin Chemistry	\N
64023dd7-0f85-4d86-8fba-a00b6319ad5b	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	A structureless layer of enamel found towards the cervical area is:	{Gnarled,Prismless,"Neonatal line",Imbrication}	1	Prismless enamel is found most commonly toward the cervical area.	Enamel Structure	\N
ad1be3f8-1bc9-4f7e-9d92-a6c556abdb5e	anatomy	BDS-I	A neurosurgeon performing a craniotomy at the pterion must be cautious of a specific vessel lying deep to the bone. Which vessel is most likely at risk of rupture leading to an extradural hematoma?	{"Posterior division of middle meningeal artery","Anterior division of middle meningeal artery","Internal carotid artery","Maxillary artery"}	1	The pterion is a critical landmark where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. Deep to it lies the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery. Injury here commonly results in an extradural (epidural) hemorrhage.	Osteology & Clinical Anatomy	\N
316ac45d-eec9-40aa-ba15-0d1741297462	anatomy	BDS-I	During tooth development, which of the following tissues is uniquely derived from the ectoderm, while the others are derived from neural crest cells?	{Dentin,"Dental Pulp",Enamel,Cementum}	2	Enamel is derived from the ectoderm (oral epithelium). All other hard and soft tissues of the tooth, including dentin, pulp, cementum, and the periodontal ligament, are derived from neural crest cells.	Oral Embryology	\N
dfa08a67-13fc-4252-86ed-627e62f46e6a	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a deviation of the tongue to the right side upon protrusion. However, the ability to elevate the posterior part of the tongue is intact. Which muscle and nerve are likely spared?	{"Genioglossus - Hypoglossal nerve","Hyoglossus - Glossopharyngeal nerve","Palatoglossus - Vago-accessory complex","Styloglossus - Hypoglossal nerve"}	2	All muscles of the tongue are supplied by the Hypoglossal nerve (XII) except the palatoglossus. The palatoglossus is a muscle of the soft palate and is supplied by the pharyngeal plexus (vago-accessory complex).	Muscle & Nerve Supply	\N
d1f47f08-3948-4db6-899e-f8cafb0c8c74	anatomy	BDS-I	Which structure separates the joint cavity of the TMJ into two distinct compartments?	{"Fibrous capsule","Articular disc","Sphenomandibular ligament","Lateral ligament"}	1	The articular disc is an oval fibrous plate that separates the TMJ cavity into upper and lower compartments.	TMJ Anatomy	\N
2de7b7a9-492e-4c0f-abc6-22006725b19b	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient diagnosed with Ludwig's angina presents with severe swelling. This condition is essentially cellulitis involving which specific anatomical location?	{"Submandibular space","Floor of the mouth","Retropharyngeal space","Parotid space"}	1	Ludwig’s angina is a dangerous cellulitis of the floor of the mouth, often caused by an infection from a carious molar tooth. It can force the tongue upwards and obstruct the airway.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
5a446092-a6fe-4b0b-995f-681ca9cc4c81	anatomy	BDS-I	Which muscle of mastication is responsible for the initial "depressing" action required to open the mouth?	{"Medial pterygoid",Temporalis,"Lateral pterygoid",Masseter}	2	The lateral pterygoid is the only muscle of mastication that assists in depressing the mandible (opening the mouth). The masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid are primarily elevators (closing the mouth).	Muscles of Mastication	\N
8760b81e-1828-4ab5-9dcf-88f0221af1d6	anatomy	BDS-I	A clinician wants to provide secretomotor block to the parotid gland. The postganglionic parasympathetic fibers for this gland are carried by which nerve?	{"Facial nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve","Chorda tympani","Greater petrosal nerve"}	1	While the preganglionic fibers originate from the glossopharyngeal nerve and relay in the otic ganglion, the postganglionic secretomotor fibers reach the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve.	Salivary Glands	\N
207df5d5-13d2-4cec-ac87-1b599d12154b	anatomy	BDS-I	The mandible is the second bone in the body to begin ossification. Most of the body of the mandible ossifies via which process?	{"Endochondral ossification","Intramembranous ossification","Ectopic ossification","Cartilaginous replacement"}	1	The mandible's greater part ossifies in membrane (intramembranous). Certain parts like the condyle and coronoid processes show secondary cartilaginous growth.	Osteology	\N
6d11bc4a-5124-4af7-ab8d-14b537c31594	anatomy	BDS-I	All muscles of the soft palate are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus EXCEPT for one muscle which is supplied by the mandibular nerve (V3). Identify it.	{"Levator veli palatini","Musculus uvulae",Palatopharyngeus,"Tensor veli palatini"}	3	Most soft palate muscles are supplied by the vago-accessory complex. The Tensor veli palatini is the exception, being supplied by the nerve to the medial pterygoid, a branch of the mandibular nerve (V3).	Muscle & Nerve Supply	\N
8507920b-f453-4327-9d1b-1427aa94d85f	anatomy	BDS-I	Foramen spinosum is a vital opening in the middle cranial fossa. Which structure passes through it alongside the middle meningeal artery?	{"Mandibular nerve","Nervus spinosus","Maxillary nerve","Middle meningeal vein"}	1	The foramen spinosum transmits the middle meningeal artery, the middle meningeal vein, and the meningeal branch of the mandibular nerve, also known as the nervus spinosus.	Osteology (Foramina)	\N
d33eaa60-7721-47fa-81d0-d91bcd39a956	anatomy	BDS-I	In the chronology of dental eruption, which is generally the first permanent tooth to erupt in the oral cavity?	{"Maxillary central incisor","Mandibular central incisor","First mandibular molar","First maxillary molar"}	2	The first permanent teeth to erupt are typically the first mandibular molars, appearing at approximately 6 years of age. They are often called the "six-year molars".	Dental Chronology	\N
1283e527-04e2-480e-b74b-41c20103d275	anatomy	BDS-I	A dentist performing an extraction of a lower third molar notices temporary numbness of the cheek. Which nerve was likely involved by the local anesthetic?	{"Inferior alveolar",Buccal,Lingual,Mental}	1	During extraction of the 3rd molar, the buccal nerve may get involved by local anesthesia, causing temporary numbness of the cheek.	Clinical Local Anesthesia	\N
b4bc02c5-db6a-49a6-93e5-513686377365	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "uncinate fits" characterized by imaginary disagreeable odors. Which part of the brain is likely affected by a lesion?	{Hippocampus,"Amygdaloid body",Thalamus,"Mammillary body"}	1	A lesion affecting the uncus and amygdaloid body may cause ‘uncinate fits’ characterized by imaginary disagreeable odors and a ‘dreamy state’.	Neuroanatomy	\N
3b2054e6-3610-45f4-a605-53d8b76679a6	anatomy	BDS-I	During a clinical exam, a patient is asked to puff out their mouth and blow air forcibly. This specifically tests the integrity of which muscle?	{"Orbicularis oris",Buccinator,Risorius,Mentalis}	1	Testing the buccinator involves asking the patient to puff the mouth and then blow out air forcibly.	Muscle Testing	\N
e3508de9-cf63-4c53-bd3d-0d955c5ffa26	anatomy	BDS-I	Which specific tissue is described as comprising the greater part of the tooth crown and root, while remaining a vital tissue throughout life?	{Enamel,Dentin,Cementum,"Alveolar Bone"}	1	Dentin comprises the greater part of the crown and root and is considered a vital tissue because it responds to stimuli.	Oral Histology	\N
f3d93866-84c0-4527-b6d0-c5b180997df2	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient suffering from syphilis can read a book but shows no pupillary constriction when a light is shone in their eyes. Where is the lesion?	{"Optic Chiasma","Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Pretectal nucleus","Ciliary ganglion"}	2	This is the Argyll Robertson pupil; the light reflex is lost while the accommodation reflex is retained due to a lesion in the pretectal nucleus.	Neuro-ophthalmology	\N
9c7c6ec7-7b98-46f6-b3d9-570d9a324e02	anatomy	BDS-I	Which fiber group of the Periodontal Ligament (PDL) is the most numerous and is primarily responsible for resisting vertical masticatory pressures?	{"Alveolar crest",Horizontal,Oblique,Apical}	2	Oblique fibers are the most numerous in the PDL and resist vertical/apical forces during mastication.	Periodontology	\N
0cb80958-3229-4ca8-9bac-acd6b70f7820	anatomy	BDS-I	An infection in the "dangerous area of the face" is most likely to spread to the cavernous sinus through which route?	{"Facial artery","Emissary veins",Lymphatics,"Pterygoid plexus"}	1	Infections from the upper lip travel via the facial vein and emissary veins to the cavernous sinus.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
cd7acb65-ca24-4212-9507-bf894cadc64c	anatomy	BDS-I	Which cell type in the dental pulp is responsible for producing the myelin sheath of pulpal nerves?	{Fibroblasts,"Endothelial cells","Schwann cells",Odontoblasts}	2	Schwann cells in the pulp form the myelin sheath of nerves and are associated with all pulp nerves.	Oral Histology	\N
e8e271c8-0d69-44cb-a20e-9add605f056f	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "nasal twang" in their voice and liquid regurgitates through the nose during swallowing. Which nerve's bilateral lesion is suspected?	{Hypoglossal,Vagus,Glossopharyngeal,Facial}	1	Bilateral lesions of the Vagus nerve cause the soft palate to fail to rise, leading to nasal regurgitation and a nasal twang.	Cranial Nerves	\N
5b9519df-19a8-417a-b34a-85511c46a832	anatomy	BDS-I	What is the total daily volume of saliva secreted by a healthy human, and which gland contributes the most?	{"500ml (Parotid)","1500ml (Submandibular)","1000ml (Submandibular)","750ml (Parotid)"}	2	Humans secrete 750–1000 mL daily; the submandibular gland accounts for approximately 60% of this volume.	Salivary Glands	\N
1d72391d-9a95-4c31-a9a2-2617badb793c	anatomy	BDS-I	A 40-year-old male presents with saliva dribbling from the right angle of his mouth and an inability to close his right eye. What is the diagnosis?	{"Bell’s Palsy","Trigeminal Neuralgia","Frey's Syndrome","Horner’s Syndrome"}	0	Bell's Palsy is a paralysis of the facial nerve (VII) at the stylomastoid foramen, often caused by viral infection.	Clinical Neurology	\N
5fcec315-d7ea-46bd-a2dd-81af52536003	anatomy	BDS-I	Which histological feature of enamel appears as alternating dark and light bands when viewed by reflected light?	{"Lines of Retzius","Hunter-Schreger bands","Enamel tufts","Enamel spindles"}	1	Hunter-Schreger bands are an optical phenomenon produced by changes in the direction of enamel rods.	Enamel Histology	\N
294262b6-d14c-459c-9998-2e9a09541564	anatomy	BDS-I	In the "Papez circuit," which structure directly follows the hippocampal formation?	{"Mammillary body","Cingulate gyrus",Fornix,"Anterior thalamic nucleus"}	2	The Papez circuit involves: Hippocampal formation → Fornix → Mammillary body → Anterior thalamic nucleus → Cingulate gyrus.	Limbic System	\N
6cc1eae9-a6b1-45a8-a2ce-0184f09ed385	anatomy	BDS-I	Which nerve provides motor innervation to the Tensor Veli Palatini muscle?	{"Vago-accessory complex",Glossopharyngeal,"Mandibular (V3)",Facial}	2	Most soft palate muscles are supplied by the vago-accessory complex, but the Tensor Veli Palatini is supplied by V3.	Muscle & Nerve	\N
426fa593-59f6-4990-9f39-63b3c876e15f	anatomy	BDS-I	The "dangerous layer" of the scalp is so named because it contains which of the following?	{"Large arteries","Emissary veins","Dense connective tissue","Sensory nerves"}	1	The 4th layer (loose areolar tissue) is dangerous because emissary veins can carry infection from here to intracranial sinuses.	Scalp Anatomy	\N
14f51aaf-2bd5-405b-a2e5-4a55a10de875	anatomy	BDS-I	Which salivary gland duct opens into the vestibule of the mouth opposite the second upper molar tooth?	{Submandibular,Sublingual,Parotid,Lingual}	2	The parotid (Stenson’s) duct opens in the vestibule opposite the second maxillary molar.	Salivary Glands	\N
b0a150ae-0007-4df2-b0a0-7282bdc411bf	anatomy	BDS-I	Which specific type of bone is found at the sites of attachment for periodontal ligament fibers (Sharpey's fibers)?	{"Cancellous bone","Bundle bone","Cortical plate","Haversian bone"}	1	Bundle bone is the part of the alveolar bone proper where the Sharpey’s fibers of the PDL are embedded.	Osteology	\N
adb502b6-3ca3-4ff1-b95b-8fda52c3d58b	anatomy	BDS-I	What is the inorganic component percentage of mature enamel?	{0.7,0.45,0.96,0.9}	2	Enamel is the most highly mineralized tissue, consisting of 96% inorganic material (hydroxyapatite).	Enamel Histology	\N
35297736-cbbd-4b82-b093-76238a007ff3	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient is unable to shrug their shoulders. Which nerve is most likely injured in the posterior triangle?	{"Long thoracic","Spinal accessory",Phrenic,Suprascapular}	1	The spinal accessory nerve (XI) supplies the trapezius muscle, which is responsible for shrugging the shoulders.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
954b34b5-efb6-43d2-a227-74ffb8bd2996	anatomy	BDS-I	Which area of the tongue is devoid of taste buds?	{"Circumvallate papillae","Fungiform papillae","Filiform papillae","Foliate papillae"}	2	Filiform papillae are the most numerous but do not contain taste buds.	Tongue Histology	\N
c1d24826-9470-4e0c-9ff0-21030f28f65f	anatomy	BDS-I	The "Anesthetist's Artery" used for taking a pulse on the face is the:	{"Facial artery","Maxillary artery","Superficial temporal artery","Mental artery"}	2	The superficial temporal artery is known as the anesthetist's artery as it is easily palpable in front of the tragus.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
abf48213-8198-4916-904f-2ddf17f75feb	anatomy	BDS-I	Which structure represents the anterior limit of the forebrain?	{"Stria terminalis","Lamina terminalis","Corpus callosum","Optic chiasma"}	1	The lamina terminalis represents the anterior limit of the forebrain.	Neuroanatomy	\N
6162d4c4-1a02-4cbd-a88f-b2184787226c	anatomy	BDS-I	"Dead tracts" in dentin are formed due to:	{"Excessive mineralization","Death of odontoblasts","Pulp stones","Secondary dentin"}	1	Dead tracts are empty dentinal tubules resulting from the death of odontoblasts, appearing dark in transmitted light.	Dentin Histology	\N
e84f5eb2-0440-4e4e-9355-09b2bfbf4ddb	anatomy	BDS-I	Which muscle is the only abductor of the vocal cords (the "safety muscle" of the larynx)?	{Cricothyroid,"Lateral cricoarytenoid","Posterior cricoarytenoid",Thyroarytenoid}	2	The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor of the vocal cords and is vital for maintaining the airway.	Larynx Anatomy	\N
b05bb9f4-c181-46d2-8e5c-08bfdaad552e	anatomy	BDS-I	During root development, the "Hertwig’s epithelial root sheath" is formed by the union of which two layers?	{"IEE and OEE","IEE and Stellate reticulum","OEE and Stratum intermedium","Dental papilla and Dental follicle"}	0	The inner enamel epithelium (IEE) and outer enamel epithelium (OEE) join to form the root sheath.	Tooth Development	\N
ed6cd5dc-035e-4b60-95c6-f20f74c72e2a	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a deviated uvula to the left side when saying "Ah". Which nerve is likely damaged?	{"Left Vagus","Right Vagus","Left Glossopharyngeal","Right Glossopharyngeal"}	1	The uvula deviates away from the side of the lesion; therefore, a deviation to the left indicates right-sided Vagus damage.	Cranial Nerves	\N
93f310c6-cc89-44dc-824e-40938fd86d45	anatomy	BDS-I	What is the total volume of Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) in an average adult?	{"50 ml","100 ml","150 ml","250 ml"}	2	The total volume of CSF in the subarachnoid space and ventricles is about 150 ml.	Neuroanatomy	\N
20fe8553-ab35-490b-8a7c-2ebd5f06683e	anatomy	BDS-I	Which cell type is the first to differentiate during the start of tooth formation?	{Odontoblasts,Ameloblasts,Cementoblasts,Fibroblasts}	1	In the enamel organ, the ameloblasts differentiate first, followed by the odontoblasts.	Tooth Development	\N
6d7ec5c5-9173-4a5d-9ae2-e0871c44ce32	anatomy	BDS-I	A "cold abscess" in the posterior triangle of the neck usually originates from which condition?	{"Infected tonsils","Caries of the cervical spine","Salivary gland stone","Infected scalp"}	1	A cold abscess is a tubercular abscess (often from caries of the spine) that tracks down into the neck.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
fae3447c-b062-4a00-bcac-7ef6f95e9728	anatomy	BDS-I	Which structure is the primary source of nutrition for the layers of the retina?	{"Central artery of retina","Choroidal capillaries","Vitreous humour","Aqueous humour"}	1	The outer five layers of the retina (rods/cones) are nourished by diffusion from the choroidal capillaries.	Ophthalmology	\N
1576d7c3-fde0-44e9-87e1-fdfde3c0fea0	anatomy	BDS-I	The "Gomphosis" type of joint is specifically found between:	{"Frontal and Parietal bones","Tooth and Jaw bone","Atlas and Axis","Sphenoid and Ethmoid"}	1	Gomphosis is the specialized fibrous joint between the root of the tooth and its bony socket.	Osteology	\N
c54db378-bb1a-4826-b06a-27f5751d0b44	anatomy	BDS-I	Which nerve provides secretomotor fibers to the lacrimal gland via the pterygopalatine ganglion?	{Optic,Facial,Trigeminal,Oculomotor}	1	Secretomotor fibers for the lacrimal gland travel from the facial nerve through the pterygopalatine ganglion.	Cranial Nerves	\N
ee314289-cc62-410f-afe3-02f318566686	anatomy	BDS-I	What is the most common variety of cleft palate?	{"Bilateral involving soft palate","Unilateral involving hard palate","Isolated bifid uvula","Complete bilateral"}	1	The most common variety is a unilateral cleft palate involving the hard palate.	Embryology	\N
0814331f-8525-4543-a294-6a1029a98fbd	anatomy	BDS-I	Which zone of the dental pulp is described as containing a high density of cells and being more prominent in young teeth?	{"Odontogenic zone","Cell-free zone","Cell-rich zone","Pulp core"}	2	The cell-rich zone contains a high density of fibroblasts and mesenchymal cells.	Pulp Histology	\N
c0c342c5-aea5-4cca-b60d-eda8714e6b32	anatomy	BDS-I	A neurosurgeon ligating vessels at the pterion to treat an extradural hematoma is targeting which artery?	{Maxillary,"Internal Carotid","Middle Meningeal","Superficial Temporal"}	2	The middle meningeal vessels lie deep to the pterion and are the common source of extradural hematoma.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
5020dbbb-ecfb-48c4-ac71-3da88b924446	anatomy	BDS-I	Which pharyngeal arch gives rise to the muscles of mastication?	{First,Second,Third,Fourth}	0	The first pharyngeal (mandibular) arch gives rise to the muscles of mastication.	Embryology	\N
