5cec70fb-65dd-43f5-923d-fdf55e0b47ff	anatomy	BDS-I	"Little’s Area" on the nasal septum is the most common site for:	{Anosmia,Epistaxis,Rhinitis,Sinusitis}	1	This highly vascular area is the common site for nosebleeds.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
2273b77e-9ab6-4fcf-950d-0372fcd5889d	anatomy	BDS-I	Which muscle is the only one in the soft palate not supplied by the pharyngeal plexus?	{"Levator veli palatini","Musculus uvulae","Tensor veli palatini",Palatopharyngeus}	2	The tensor veli palatini is supplied by the mandibular nerve (V3).	Muscle & Nerve Supply	\N
e97ce5e0-928c-470a-ac01-3b21f5460473	anatomy	BDS-I	Which tooth is usually the first to erupt in the permanent dentition?	{"Central incisor","First molar",Canine,"First premolar"}	1	The "six-year molars" (first permanent molars) usually erupt first.	Dental Chronology	\N
35871885-ec91-44c9-b073-0739ba86492c	anatomy	BDS-I	"Hertwig’s epithelial root sheath" is formed by the union of:	{"IEE and OEE","IEE and Stellate reticulum","OEE and Dental papilla","Dental papilla and Dental follicle"}	0	The inner and outer enamel epithelia join at the cervical loop to form HERS.	Tooth Development	\N
43d56653-f330-4e3d-9a18-5c84424ab30c	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient cannot protrude their tongue. Which nerve is likely injured?	{VII,IX,X,XII}	3	The hypoglossal nerve (XII) supplies the muscles for tongue movement.	Cranial Nerves	\N
5587dd95-a4c0-44a0-9b73-cd8fe654c09e	anatomy	BDS-I	"Sclerotic dentin" is most commonly seen in:	{"Young teeth","Deciduous teeth","Older teeth or under caries","Unerupted teeth"}	2	It is a protective or aging change where tubules mineralize.	Dentin Histology	\N
b23ae613-15c1-4c18-9c8a-e4ec96b97151	anatomy	BDS-I	Which pharyngeal arch gives rise to the muscles of mastication?	{First,Second,Third,Fourth}	0	The first (mandibular) arch gives rise to these muscles, supplied by V3.	Embryology	\N
432812fa-db27-468a-8b02-97ec181ec5e4	anatomy	BDS-I	"Frey’s Syndrome" involves which gland?	{Thyroid,Parotid,Submandibular,Lacrimal}	1	It follows surgery or trauma to the parotid gland.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
e8476610-54d1-44cb-9e45-4e8110f54eb4	anatomy	BDS-I	What is the inorganic percentage of mature enamel?	{0.7,0.9,0.96,0.45}	2	Enamel is 96% inorganic material.	Enamel Histology	\N
f49bdc75-cf3e-4b6e-9100-9c29a27bc64d	anatomy	BDS-I	Which nerve supplies the Trapezius muscle?	{"Long thoracic","Spinal accessory",Phrenic,Axillary}	1	The spinal accessory nerve (XI) supplies the trapezius.	Anatomy	\N
b5fd8db5-4af2-477e-8076-4eaff5a77b87	anatomy	BDS-I	"Ludwig’s Angina" involves cellulitis of the:	{"Parotid space","Submandibular space","Floor of the mouth","Retropharyngeal space"}	2	It is a cellulitis of the floor of the mouth.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
52fa30b4-f49f-4a21-8ded-400980cf1d0e	anatomy	BDS-I	Which structure is the "safety muscle" of the tongue?	{Hyoglossus,Genioglossus,Styloglossus,Palatoglossus}	1	Genioglossus prevents the tongue from falling back.	Anatomy	\N
177557c4-2b23-4bc5-8baa-cba440501ee7	anatomy	BDS-I	"Interglobular dentin" is found most commonly in which part of the tooth?	{"Cusp tips","Near the DEJ in the crown","Root apex","Pulp core"}	1	It is typically found in the crown, just below the mantle dentin.	Dentin Histology	\N
9e013ffc-b26f-444d-ae96-50e395ea1720	anatomy	BDS-I	Which specific muscle has the "largest name" in anatomy?	{Sternocleidomastoid,"Levator labii superioris alaeque nasi",Temporoparietalis,"Depressor septi nasi"}	1	This muscle holds the trivia record for the longest name.	Anatomy	\N
cfad2c6a-5e1b-4d42-a592-42010ae8dcd7	anatomy	BDS-I	A 25-year-old boxer receives a blow to the side of the head and becomes unconscious. A CT scan reveals a lens-shaped hematoma between the skull and dura. Which surgical landmark is used to drain this?	{Inion,Pterion,Asterion,Nasion}	1	The pterion overlies the anterior division of the middle meningeal artery. Rupture leads to an extradural hematoma.	Osteology	\N
26d09883-7727-4ee4-b35b-6d335b77e2f2	anatomy	BDS-I	A 45-year-old patient presents with a "mask-like" facial appearance on the right side, inability to whistle, and food accumulating in the right vestibule. Forehead wrinkles are absent on the right. Diagnosis?	{"Supranuclear lesion of VII","Bell's Palsy","Trigeminal Neuralgia","Horner's Syndrome"}	1	Bell's Palsy is an infranuclear (LMN) lesion of the facial nerve at the stylomastoid foramen, affecting both upper and lower facial quadrants.	Cranial Nerves	\N
4790713b-a585-4839-a6d6-41b254881191	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient with chronic tonsillitis complains of pain in the ear. Which nerve is responsible for this referred pain?	{"Facial nerve","Vagus nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve"}	2	The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) supplies both the palatine tonsil and the middle ear (via the tympanic branch), causing referred pain.	Pharynx	\N
afc2a201-d15b-4b0c-89b8-8d0bdfff2792	anatomy	BDS-I	Following a deep infection of a lower molar, a patient presents with brawny swelling of the floor of the mouth, pushing the tongue upwards. What is this condition called?	{Quinsy,"Ludwig’s Angina",Mumps,"Frey's Syndrome"}	1	Ludwig’s Angina is cellulitis of the floor of the mouth, often originating from a carious molar tooth.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
bc06800f-b61c-4265-a3ba-bc8d8d0b29df	anatomy	BDS-I	A 19-year-old female notices sweating and flushing on her cheek every time she eats. She had a history of parotid surgery 6 months ago. What is the anatomical basis?	{"Chorda tympani lesion","Damage to auriculotemporal nerve","Damage to buccal nerve","Trigeminal neuralgia"}	1	Frey’s syndrome occurs due to aberrant regeneration where secretomotor fibers in the auriculotemporal nerve join fibers of the great auricular nerve.	Salivary Glands	\N
470d994e-2031-4b97-a6b6-d2c41d94b9e8	anatomy	BDS-I	A teenager with infected acne on the upper lip presents with high fever and ophthalmoplegia. The clinician suspects cavernous sinus thrombosis. What is the primary route?	{"Facial artery","Superior ophthalmic vein","Deep facial vein","Maxillary artery"}	1	Infection from the "dangerous area of the face" reaches the cavernous sinus via the facial vein and superior ophthalmic vein.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
112cb118-25eb-4b6a-988d-281061b1dda5	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient's jaw gets stuck in a wide-open position after a massive yawn. In which direction is the mandible most likely dislocated?	{Posteriorly,Superiorly,Anteriorly,Medially}	2	The most common TMJ dislocation is anterior, often caused by a blow to the chin when the mouth is wide open (e.g., yawning).	TMJ	\N
8ec40470-f270-4b30-8755-2c651ed8aa71	anatomy	BDS-I	A neurosurgeon notices clear fluid leaking from a patient's ear after a skull base fracture. Fracture of which structure is most likely?	{"Cribriform plate","Tegmen tympani","Foramen ovale","Styloid process"}	1	Fracture of the middle cranial fossa often fractures the tegmen tympani, leading to CSF otorrhea.	Osteology	\N
c9c9e702-fc9d-408b-9f09-2bbd2359a24a	anatomy	BDS-I	During a thyroidectomy, a patient's parathyroid glands are accidentally removed. What is the most likely clinical outcome?	{Hypercalcemia,Hypocalcemia,Hyperthyroidism,Epistaxis}	1	The parathyroid glands regulate calcium; their removal causes hypoparathyroidism and subsequent hypocalcemia.	Endocrine	\N
715ccb05-9a1b-4522-99ca-59a182d6c2d1	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with herpes zoster vesicles on the auricle, facial palsy, and hearing loss. What is this syndrome?	{Horner's,Bell's,"Ramsay Hunt",Eagle's}	2	Ramsay Hunt syndrome is herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion, affecting nerves VII and VIII.	Clinical Neurology	\N
c78cbfb4-96df-493d-a883-7541c3adad16	anatomy	BDS-I	A 70-year-old stroke patient cannot move the lower left side of his face but can wrinkle his forehead symmetrically. Where is the lesion?	{"Right facial nerve at stylomastoid foramen","Left facial nerve at stylomastoid foramen","Right corticonuclear fibers","Left corticonuclear fibers"}	2	In supranuclear (UMN) lesions, the upper face is spared because it receives bilateral cortical innervation.	Cranial Nerves	\N
cfed2baf-7f73-4861-9ebb-9f334d9385fe	anatomy	BDS-I	A child with large adenoids is a constant "mouth breather." Which tonsil is hypertrophied?	{"Palatine tonsil","Lingual tonsil","Nasopharyngeal tonsil","Tubal tonsil"}	2	Hypertrophy of the nasopharyngeal tonsil is known as adenoids, which can block the posterior nasal apertures.	Pharynx	\N
1b5ff4ab-d8c2-49df-8529-8c6be802aa8a	anatomy	BDS-I	A 35-year-old woman feels pain and swelling beneath her right jaw every time she eats, which subsides after the meal. Radiographs reveal a stone. Where is it most likely located?	{"Parotid duct","Submandibular duct","Sublingual duct","Thyroglossal duct"}	1	Salivary stones (calculi) are most common in the submandibular duct (Wharton's duct).	Salivary Glands	\N
ec9749ec-3238-4ac0-a75d-d3475ef78e67	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presenting with "Black Eye" several hours after a head injury. In which layer of the scalp did the blood collect?	{"Connective tissue","Aponeurotic layer","Loose areolar tissue",Pericranium}	2	Blood in the loose areolar tissue (safety/dangerous layer) can track forward into the eyelids, causing a black eye.	Scalp	\N
6eb1d1d3-231f-47a7-9e08-0cd1486ba698	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis. The clinician tests the "ciliospinal reflex," which is absent. What is the diagnosis?	{"Bell’s Palsy","Horner's Syndrome","Ramsay Hunt Syndrome","Eagle’s Syndrome"}	1	Horner’s syndrome is caused by a lesion of the sympathetic pathway, characterized by this classic triad.	Clinical Neurology	\N
552d95be-86dd-4ee3-83ca-7e5ddedd6618	anatomy	BDS-I	A 12-year-old boy presents with earache and sore throat. The clinician notes enlarged "tonsillar lymph nodes." Which nodes are these?	{Submental,Submandibular,Jugulodigastric,Jugulo-omohyoid}	2	The jugulodigastric node is the principal node for the palatine tonsil and is enlarged in tonsillitis.	Lymphatics	\N
000c0980-f4d2-4a43-99ba-4d71f1e574a4	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has total blindness in the right eye. The lesion is likely in which specific vessel?	{"Middle cerebral artery","Central artery of retina","Ophthalmic vein","Internal carotid artery"}	1	The central artery of the retina is a functional end artery; its occlusion results in sudden total blindness.	Ophthalmology	\N
0be2eaba-c577-4649-b1d0-7e2306483e56	anatomy	BDS-I	Following a car accident, a patient's eyeball is protruding and pulsating in sync with his heartbeat. What is the likely cause?	{"Pituitary tumor","Arteriovenous fistula in cavernous sinus","Frontal sinus infection","Orbital cellulitis"}	1	A rupture of the internal carotid artery within the cavernous sinus creates an arteriovenous fistula, leading to pulsating exophthalmos.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
861218c5-824a-44cd-bd0c-475d9f611bfb	anatomy	BDS-I	A child is seen with a midline swelling in the neck that moves upwards when he protrudes his tongue. What is the diagnosis?	{"Brachial cyst","Thyroglossal cyst","Submandibular stone","Enlarged lymph node"}	1	A thyroglossal cyst is a midline swelling that moves with the hyoid bone and tongue.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
cf79b754-e588-4f8b-bb80-0542c4a8bfaf	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient with syphilis shows no pupillary constriction to light but normal constriction during accommodation. Where is the lesion?	{"Ciliary ganglion","Pretectal nucleus","Optic chiasm","Edinger-Westphal nucleus"}	1	This is the Argyll Robertson pupil, caused by a lesion in the vicinity of the pretectal nucleus.	Neuro-ophthalmology	\N
8d46aa8f-8655-44b2-b62a-9a190c0ae5da	anatomy	BDS-I	An 8-year-old presents with high fever, sore throat, and neck rigidity. passive flexion of the neck is painful. What is the suspected condition?	{Mumps,Laryngitis,Meningitis,Torticollis}	2	High grade fever and neck rigidity (extensor spasm) are signs of meningitis.	Clinical Neurology	\N
b3c3775c-fe7f-4ae6-b1c3-28d292b6f4c2	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient cannot shrug their shoulders. Which cranial nerve is likely injured?	{Vagus,Accessory,Hypoglossal,Glossopharyngeal}	1	The spinal accessory nerve (XI) supplies the trapezius muscle, responsible for shrugging.	Cranial Nerves	\N
e80d6674-1fc8-4695-996c-ce9c8ffa45e6	anatomy	BDS-I	A dentist accidentally inserts a needle too far posteriorly during an IAN block and the patient develops transient facial palsy. Which structure was entered?	{"Pterygoid plexus","Parotid gland","Submandibular gland","Infratemporal fossa"}	1	The facial nerve branches course through the parotid gland; an anesthetic injection here causes temporary paralysis.	Salivary Glands	\N
6f6688f7-013f-46db-b447-6827d3180442	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient complains of recurring throat pain that radiates to the ear, aggravated by swallowing. Radiographs show an elongated styloid process. Diagnosis?	{"Eagle’s Syndrome","Frey's Syndrome","Horner's Syndrome",Quinsy}	0	Eagle’s syndrome (styalgia) is caused by an elongated styloid process or calcified stylohyoid ligament.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
4f0d87e6-900f-4f05-a8e4-4aae622a3ac9	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has lost the "gag reflex." Which nerve provides the afferent limb of this reflex?	{Vagus,Glossopharyngeal,Facial,Trigeminal}	1	The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) provides sensory supply to the pharynx, forming the afferent limb of the gag reflex.	Cranial Nerves	\N
47fb0fc2-d782-4988-a0b3-c0877f30af33	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "internal strabismus" (medial squint) in the right eye. Which nerve is paralyzed?	{"Right Oculomotor","Right Trochlear","Right Abducent","Right Optic"}	2	The abducent nerve (VI) supplies the lateral rectus; its paralysis leads to an unopposed medial pull (medial squint).	Ophthalmology	\N
255f0848-f05a-47d2-9788-8c33fb170832	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has lost taste on the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue but has normal general sensation. Which nerve is injured?	{"Lingual nerve","Chorda tympani",Glossopharyngeal,Hypoglossal}	1	Chorda tympani carries taste fibers from the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue.	Tongue	\N
899528e5-9348-4e47-8cda-64c069ea9558	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a deviated uvula to the right side when saying "Ah". Which nerve is likely damaged?	{"Right Vagus","Left Vagus","Right Glossopharyngeal","Left Glossopharyngeal"}	1	The uvula deviates away from the side of the lesion (the normal side pulls it); thus, a right deviation indicates a left vagal lesion.	Cranial Nerves	\N
1ff7c870-cdb1-49e4-9b65-6fbe63575796	anatomy	BDS-I	After a difficult delivery, a newborn cannot suckle properly due to buccinator paralysis. Where was the facial nerve likely injured?	{"Internal acoustic meatus","Geniculate ganglion","Stylomastoid foramen","Parotid gland"}	2	The mastoid process is absent at birth, making the facial nerve superficial at the stylomastoid foramen and prone to injury.	Embryology	\N
422e4acf-dd06-479e-a44e-1ab5cfe95c53	anatomy	BDS-I	A 70-year-old male has an ulcer on the lateral margin of his tongue. Which lymph node should the clinician palpate first?	{Submental,Submandibular,Jugulo-omohyoid,Retro-pharyngeal}	1	The lateral margins of the tongue drain primarily into the submandibular lymph nodes.	Lymphatics	\N
6ad2687a-f4ff-45e9-9e71-21f344cdf112	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with a midline bulge in the posterior pharyngeal wall. The clinician suspects a "cold abscess." What is the source?	{"Infected tonsil","Caries of cervical spine","Parotid abscess","Maxillary sinusitis"}	1	A cold abscess (tubercular) from the cervical spine tracks behind the prevertebral fascia, causing a midline bulge.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
5f225454-3eaf-4964-8951-704afda1cb5b	anatomy	BDS-I	A child presents with epistaxis from the anteroinferior part of the nasal septum. What is this area called?	{"Little’s Area","Meckel’s Cave","Piriform fossa","Sella turcica"}	0	Little’s area (Kiesselbach’s plexus) is the most common site for nosebleeds.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
5fbf196a-198f-4e7b-9670-46e286345c20	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient with a severe infection of the "voice box" is struggling to breathe. Which muscle is likely paralyzed, failing to abduct the vocal cords?	{Cricothyroid,"Lateral cricoarytenoid","Posterior cricoarytenoid",Thyroarytenoid}	2	The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor of the vocal cords and is known as the "safety muscle".	Larynx	\N
c55d0b45-f562-4407-b8f6-821bf850d191	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "nasal twang" in their voice and liquid regurgitates through the nose. Which structure is failing to function?	{Epiglottis,"Soft palate","Lingual tonsil","Piriform fossa"}	1	Paralysis of the soft palate (vagus nerve) causes nasal regurgitation and a nasal twang.	Pharynx	\N
d1e4a418-6f5d-4da2-bc16-3b64829a702c	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "Port Wine Stains" on the skin of their forehead and upper eyelid. This corresponds to the distribution of which nerve?	{"Ophthalmic nerve","Maxillary nerve","Mandibular nerve","Facial nerve"}	0	Cutaneous naevi (port wine stains) map out the distribution of trigeminal nerve divisions; the forehead is V1.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
643ca325-1893-49a7-9ef4-7b00f613ec98	anatomy	BDS-I	During an emergency, a clinician needs to palpate the "Anesthetist's Artery." Where is it found?	{"Angle of mandible","In front of the tragus","Root of the nose","Behind the mastoid"}	1	The superficial temporal artery is easily palpable in front of the tragus and is used by anesthetists.	Blood Supply	\N
14a84550-521a-4012-a939-233b5b72a7e4	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a tumor in the "Smuggler’s Fossa." Where is this located?	{Nasopharynx,Oropharynx,"Piriform fossa","Maxillary sinus"}	2	The piriform fossa is called the "smuggler's fossa" because it can be used to hide small precious objects.	Larynx	\N
6a59ab17-b272-49ed-b1a5-fe33bd3c88c2	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "Tongue Tie." Which structure is too short or anteriorly placed?	{"Plica fimbriata","Frenulum linguae","Vallate papilla","Lingual tonsil"}	1	Tongue tie (ankyloglossia) is caused by a restrictive frenulum linguae.	Tongue	\N
a03ffd54-70a8-4d94-ba7b-52e787fd1817	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient with long-standing diabetes presents with reduced vision. The clinician sees microaneurysms in the retina. Diagnosis?	{Glaucoma,Cataract,"Diabetic retinopathy","Retinal detachment"}	2	Diabetic retinopathy involves pathologic changes in retinal blood vessels including microaneurysms.	Ophthalmology	\N
25765291-2bae-4a2b-acae-0fd01ad011b2	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient cannot protrude their tongue straight; it deviates to the right. Which muscle and nerve are affected?	{"Left Genioglossus - XII","Right Genioglossus - XII","Right Hyoglossus - IX","Left Styloglossus - X"}	1	The genioglossus is the protrusor; in a unilateral XII nerve lesion, the tongue deviates to the paralyzed side.	Tongue	\N
cf9ab015-7a6d-4905-92c3-34555aad087f	anatomy	BDS-I	An 8-year-old child presents with a "Strawberry tongue." This is characteristic of which condition?	{"Pernicious anemia","Scarlet fever","Vitamin B deficiency","Oral Candidiasis"}	1	Scarlet fever causes atrophy of lingual mucosa, leading to the "strawberry tongue" appearance.	Tongue	\N
9f6cd30b-bfc2-404c-9137-de7f5f5e580b	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with white plaque-like lesions on the tongue. The patient is HIV positive. What is the likely diagnosis?	{Mumps,"Hairy leukoplakia",Glossitis,"Ludwig's Angina"}	1	Epstein-Barr virus causes hairy leukoplakia (white mucosal lesions) often seen in HIV-positive patients.	Oral Mucosa	\N
da7076dc-f1df-45e4-bef9-7ce853caed93	anatomy	BDS-I	A clinician is performing a tracheostomy. What is the most preferred site for the incision?	{"Above the hyoid bone","2nd or 3rd tracheal rings","Thyroid cartilage","Cricothyroid membrane"}	1	The most preferred site for tracheostomy is the 2nd or 3rd tracheal rings.	Clinical Anatomy	\N
043ebab8-f60a-4331-a150-72365c3e4eec	anatomy	BDS-I	A 40-year-old female sway to the right while walking and cannot perform rapid pronation/supination (dysdiadochokinesia) of the right arm. MRI shows a tumor. Where?	{"Left Cerebrum","Right Cerebellum","Medulla Oblongata",Midbrain}	1	The cerebellum controls equilibrium and fine movements; its control is ipsilateral.	Neuroanatomy	\N
0598c62e-47a9-46a0-a351-d521a704df42	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has a "nasal twang" after a tonsillectomy. Which nerve plexus might have been irritated or damaged?	{"Brachial plexus","Pharyngeal plexus","Cervical plexus","Pterygoid plexus"}	1	The pharyngeal plexus supplies the muscles of the soft palate; its dysfunction causes nasal twang.	Pharynx	\N
59907685-50e8-4b41-866d-1662da8b981e	anatomy	BDS-I	A clinician wants to test the "sneeze reflex." Which nerve is the afferent path?	{Ophthalmic,Maxillary,Mandibular,Facial}	1	The maxillary nerve (V2) is the afferent path for the sneeze reflex.	Cranial Nerves	\N
92bb2920-0b6d-41e0-851e-7199b63b5b36	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has lost the "jaw jerk reflex." Which nerve is involved?	{"Maxillary nerve","Mandibular nerve","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"}	1	The mandibular nerve (V3) mediates the masseteric (jaw jerk) reflex.	Cranial Nerves	\N
559f1cbf-3211-438f-a95b-09c2ee90cd97	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient has "clasp-knife rigidity" and a positive Babinski sign. Where is the lesion?	{"Lower Motor Neuron","Upper Motor Neuron","Basal Ganglia",Cerebellum}	1	These are characteristic signs of an Upper Motor Neuron (UMN) lesion.	Neuroanatomy	\N
96be5f1b-ad46-4276-b023-489c76376c80	anatomy	BDS-I	A child has a "small nose" and "toneless voice" due to obligate mouth breathing. What is the diagnosis?	{Tonsillitis,Adenoids,"Cleft Palate",Glossitis}	1	Hypertrophy of the nasopharyngeal tonsils (adenoids) leads to these clinical features.	Pharynx	\N
c9eb40a1-ae4b-4760-b965-9517e4b242e5	anatomy	BDS-I	During a surgery in the posterior triangle of the neck, a nerve is injured, resulting in "Wry Neck" (Torticollis). Which nerve?	{Phrenic,"Long thoracic","Spinal Accessory",Vagus}	2	The spinal accessory nerve (XI) supplies the sternocleidomastoid; its irritation/spasm causes torticollis.	Neck Triangles	\N
4c036be1-67cc-4653-947d-5cbf0270fb01	anatomy	BDS-I	A 55-year-old female presents with tremors, weight loss, and bulging eyes (exophthalmos). Diagnosis?	{"Bell's Palsy","Toxic Goitre",Mumps,"Horner's Syndrome"}	1	These are classic symptoms of toxic goiter (hyperthyroidism).	Endocrine	\N
36e90cee-6c03-4b2b-8cb9-e151be9c0bee	anatomy	BDS-I	A patient presents with "Crocodile Tears" (lacrimation while eating). What is the cause?	{"Sympathetic overactivity","Aberrant regeneration of VII nerve","Trigeminal neuralgia","Parotid duct stone"}	1	Aberrant regeneration of fibers for the submandibular gland into sheaths supplying the lacrimal gland causes this syndrome.	Cranial Nerves	\N
