b8910f52-963b-4fda-9225-1e1bf8702bad	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	An image of a test tube containing a solution that has turned brick-red with a precipitate after being heated with a blue reagent\n Identify the most likely test performed	{"Biuret test","Molisch test","Benedict’s test","Ninhydrin test."}	2	Benedict’s test is used to detect reducing sugars (like glucose or lactose). Reducing sugars reduce the cupric ions in the reagent to cuprous oxide, which forms a characteristic brick-red precipitate upon heating, as described in standard biochemistry practical manuals		/api/uploads/ab56e75925917065bd98b23454aa26e9.png
b19b9812-9c8d-4413-9b42-60d96c416d51	physiology_biochem	BDS-I	A metabolic pathway diagram showing the conversion of Glucose to Glucose-6-Phosphate in the liver, noting a high Km for the enzyme involved \nIdentify the specific enzyme. 	{Hexokinase,Glucokinase,Phosphofructokinase,"Glucose-6-Phosphatase. "}	1	Glucokinase is an isoenzyme of hexokinase found primarily in the liver and pancreatic beta cells. It has a high Km (low affinity) for glucose, allowing it to function only when blood glucose levels are elevated, which is a key concept in Satyanarayana’s text regarding carbohydrate metabolism.		/api/uploads/1ed62f5a3de543c6d5bbe4589e1bfce0.png
