647a58b6-39a6-4133-8303-17072001fa16	anatomy	BDS-I	A superior view of the cranial base (internal surface) with a pointer indicating a large oval opening in the greater wing of the sphenoid bone\nWhich structure passes through the indicated foramen?	{"Maxillary nerve","Mandibular nerve","Middle meningeal artery","Internal carotid artery. "}	1	The indicated structure is the Foramen Ovale, which transmits the Mandibular nerve (V3), Accessory meningeal artery, Lesser petrosal nerve, and Emissary veins (MALE) 		/api/uploads/212994359fb5a2b8ed72565be2a597ef.png
5081d713-61f7-4bb7-afe0-7ef03fa7d430	anatomy	BDS-I	Lateral view of the face highlighting a thick, quadrilateral muscle originating from the zygomatic arch and inserting into the ramus of the mandible\nIdentify the muscle shown. 	{"Temporalis ","Lateral pterygoid ",Masseter,"Medial pterygoid."}	2	The masseter is a powerful muscle of mastication that elevates the mandible to close the jaw; its superficial fibers originate from the zygomatic process of the maxilla and the anterior two-thirds of the zygomatic arch 		/api/uploads/fccb1ff97ec38e6281057347d43bd06c.png
050b8980-6c58-49b3-a037-3c013726712d	anatomy	BDS-I	A view of the first cervical vertebra (C1) showing a ring-like structure without a body or spinous process\nIdentify this vertebra.	{Axis,Atlas,"Seventh cervical vertebra","Typical thoracic vertebra."}	1	The Atlas (C1) is unique because it lacks a body and a spinous process, consisting of anterior and posterior arches and two lateral masses that articulate with the occipital condyles 		/api/uploads/bdc48fd5ba9cf3dd512a1996f7af436c.png
