e6bf7073-97f9-4ac0-92d2-b98475bb53b6	oral_med_rad	BDS-IV	A radiograph shows teeth that appear abnormally long and stretched. This is known as elongation. How is this corrected?	{"Increase the vertical angulation","Decrease the vertical angulation","Shift the horizontal angulation mesially","Use a higher KVp."}	0	Elongation occurs when the vertical angulation is too flat (insufficient). To correct this and project the image properly onto the film, the operator must increase the vertical angulation of the X-ray tube head.		/api/uploads/26350c580039261d751dd90c9548d47c.png
ae542d6a-7715-442b-8ecc-82d2d58b547e	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	What is the most likely diagnosis?	{"LeFort I","LeFort II","LeFort III","Zygomatic Complex Fracture."}	1	LeFort II, or pyramidal fracture, typically involves the infraorbital rim and the bridge of the nose, resulting in the characteristic mid-face retrusion or 'dish-face' appearance.\n		/api/uploads/2012a8434dacfca6e8bab847217013cb.png
