43c24c9f-b54a-42d0-bdc2-6224c430a477	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	This radiographic feature is characteristic of:	{"Le Fort II fracture","Orbital blow-out fracture","Zygomaticomaxillary complex fracture","Maxillary sinusitis."}	1	The 'hanging drop' sign on a radiograph or CT scan represents the herniation of orbital fat and the inferior rectus muscle through a fracture in the orbital floor into the maxillary sinus antrum, common in blow-out fractures.		/api/uploads/717f6bcb648e2d5279ecc9514c0cbe33.png
965d2dbb-b9bc-40b4-8759-19b643218950	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	This fracture is best described as:	{"Horizontally Favorable","Horizontally Unfavorable","Vertically Favorable",Comminuted.}	1	A fracture of the mandibular angle is 'horizontally unfavorable' when the pull of the masseter and medial pterygoid muscles tends to distract (displace) the proximal fragment superiorly and medially away from the distal fragment.\n		/api/uploads/0996ef39ead3539ccd70661d038e866f.png
6d024ec7-069a-4906-8bfb-649470b1189c	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	What is the primary treatment for this condition?	{" Surgical curettage to induce bleeding","Systemic antibiotics","Irrigation with saline and placement of an obtundent dressing","Flap surgery and primary closure."}	2	This depicts Alveolar Osteitis (Dry Socket). The management focuses on palliative care; curettage is contraindicated as it increases pain. Treatment involves gentle irrigation to remove debris and placing a sedative dressing like Zinc Oxide Eugenol (ZOE) or Alveogyl.\n		/api/uploads/f52677881c71fb092ef79e3d99ab630e.png
caca0a4b-2e5f-4eea-8015-e09fe8b27ee0	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	What is the most likely diagnosis?	{Ameloblastoma,"Radicular Cyst","Dentigerous Cyst",Odontoma.}	0	Ameloblastoma is a benign but locally aggressive odontogenic tumor. it is described as having a characteristic soap-bubble appearance due to its multilocular nature and tendency to expand the jaw.\n		/api/uploads/e42cc809b9afbf9fecb9e21c22b4320d.png
02d28c52-642e-481b-ba74-925f73e540da	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	Identify the instrument.	{"Coupland Elevator","Cryer’s Elevator","Warwick James Elevator","Winter’s Crossbar."}	1	Cryer's elevators are paired instruments with triangular blades used to remove roots of mandibular molars by utilizing the adjacent empty socket, following the wheel-and-axle principle.		/api/uploads/39e9bb8b04443477400a6d9da6cc880d.png
9184a2a9-088f-41d3-81e2-9cd136031693	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	Which injury does this suggest?	{"Nasal Bone Fracture","Mandibular Angle Fracture","Le Fort II or III Fracture","Isolated Dentoalveolar Fracture."}	2	Periorbital ecchymosis (Panda eyes) is a classic sign of midface fractures (Le Fort II/III) or base of skull fractures, resulting from blood tracking into the periorbital tissues.		/api/uploads/de58a1fa1aaba02bc6bf8c352496c276.png
22a7ccb1-2515-4f44-b101-b440f38ef351	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	Identify the surgical procedure shown in an image	{Alveoloplasty,"Caldwell-Luc Operation","Gillies Temporal Approach"," Frenectomy."}	1	 The Caldwell-Luc procedure involves creating a window in the canine fossa of the maxilla to gain access to the maxillary sinus for the removal of pathology or foreign bodies.		/api/uploads/a3502d5a0d3d8f4eaf764b7b5b8965d3.png
0d08a682-e20f-40f4-8dc4-15fd8469eed5	oral_surgery	BDS-IV	Identify the type of mandibular third molar impaction	{Mesioangular,Distoangular,Horizontal,Vertical.}	0	Based on Winter's Classification, mesioangular impaction occurs when the long axis of the third molar is angled towards the second molar. This is generally considered the easiest type of mandibular impaction to extract.\n		/api/uploads/f13ec1d3923e4ef51d1740153919245b.png
14059246-b8d4-4f13-bb79-3989e8e44d56	pedodontics	BDS-IV	A clinical image shows a tooth that appears clinically 'double,' with a single root canal seen on the radiograph. What is this condition?\n 	{Fusion,Concrescence,"Gemination  ","Dilaceration.  "}	2	Gemination is the attempt by a single tooth bud to divide, resulting in a bifid crown with a single root and a common root canal. In fusion, there are usually two separate root canals, and the tooth count is reduced unless fused with a supernumerary tooth.		/api/uploads/e7b75c1bd67ba06e38dcd1d923a423c0.png
3c4ae709-4efc-483c-ae08-3135c78020d3	pedodontics	BDS-IV	An image shows a child's teeth with 'mottled enamel,' displaying brownish stains and pitted surfaces across multiple quadrants. What is the most likely cause?\n 	{"Amelogenesis Imperfecta","Dentinogenesis Imperfecta","Dental Fluorosis  ","Enamel Hypoplasia due to trauma"}	2	Dental fluorosis occurs due to excessive fluoride ingestion during the period of enamel formation (calcification). Based on Dean’s Fluorosis Index described in Marwah, it manifests as bilateral, symmetrical opaque white spots or brown pitting.\n 		/api/uploads/2246627a26fc2ca9a1b2c961cdf2acf6.png
1dca28b1-ca6e-4399-8e03-e66ff2613a44	pedodontics	BDS-IV	A clinical photograph shows a small, whitish, keratin-filled cyst on the mid-palatal raphe of a newborn infant. What is the most likely diagnosis?	{"Bohn's Nodules","Epstein Pearls","Dental Lamina Cyst","Natal Teeth."}	1	Epstein pearls are small, white, cystic vesicles found along the median palatal raphe, representing trapped epithelial remnants. Bohn’s nodules, conversely, are found on the buccal or lingual aspects of the alveolar ridges, as per Marwah and McDonald.		/api/uploads/35403e1d16e823f3dc7eb0a6afea7cf9.png
eea5a423-8664-485c-8e04-6673ff151328	pedodontics	BDS-IV	An IOPA radiograph shows a conical-shaped tooth located between the two permanent maxillary central incisors. Name the anomaly.	{Paramolar,Distomolar,Mesiodens,Gemination.}	2	A mesiodens is a supernumerary tooth located in the midline between the maxillary central incisors. It is the most common supernumerary tooth and often causes interference with the eruption of permanent teeth, requiring early diagnosis and intervention.		/api/uploads/6a42c5087a30291ace9e3bccd65ebfa0.png
1e1a85fb-8c1e-4d29-a75f-ffdb397a5ff1	pedodontics	BDS-IV	An image shows a radiograph with a stainless steel band on the primary second molar and a wire loop extending to touch the distal surface of the primary canine after the loss of the primary first molar. Identify the appliance.	{"Distal Shoe","Band and Loop","Nance Palatal Arch  ","Lingual Arch."}	1	The Band and Loop is a fixed, passive, unilateral space maintainer used primarily for the premature loss of a single primary first molar or primary second molar after the eruption of the permanent first molar.		/api/uploads/f90e0ae4b96520ab6046c077fee1fde2.png
478e64c6-c174-4d70-8643-62a55d35f9bf	pedodontics	BDS-IV	A clinical image shows a newborn with two teeth present in the mandibular anterior region at the time of birth. These are classified as	{"Neonatal teeth","Natal teeth","Bohn's nodule","Epstein pearls."}	1	Natal teeth are those present at the time of birth. If the teeth erupt within the first 30 days of life, they are termed Neonatal teeth. They are most commonly found in the mandibular primary incisor region.\n 		/api/uploads/2f5d9c09f6e3e55c40d23415cc4e0eb9.png
e993a345-0258-4ea2-bfba-fd8eceef25ac	pedodontics	BDS-IV	 \nAn image of a 3-year-old child reveals extensive carious lesions on the maxillary anterior teeth, while the mandibular incisors remain completely healthy. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n 	{"Rampant caries","Early Childhood Caries (ECC)  ","Radiation caries","Arrested caries."}	1	 This specific pattern, often called 'Nursing Bottle Caries', involves the maxillary incisors while sparing mandibular incisors because the tongue covers the lower teeth during sucking and saliva from the submandibular gland provides a buffering effect.		/api/uploads/1f878c46a7140df3d40ef6d9018d3686.png
d374fd4c-fa81-4c9f-bf3a-01213d00cbf5	pedodontics	BDS-IV	A radiographic image shows a primary molar with a 'bull-shaped' appearance, an elongated pulp chamber, and an apically displaced furcation. This condition is:\n 	{Taurodontis," Dilaceration",Concrescence,Fusion.}	0	Taurodontism is characterized by an enlarged pulp chamber and a shortened root, giving the tooth a 'bull-like' appearance. It occurs due to the failure of the Hertwig’s Epithelial Root Sheath to invaginate at the proper horizontal level.		/api/uploads/77e9862df33a1d4dba2f1f8f721aef92.png
1d9486b2-1738-4cfa-aeeb-d30588e300bc	pedodontics	BDS-IV	An image shows a child with a 'V-shaped' maxillary arch, high palatal vault, and an anterior open bite. Which habit is most likely associated with this clinical presentation?\n 	{"Tongue thrusting","Mouth breathing","Thumb sucking",Bruxing.}	2	Chronic thumb sucking exerts pressure on the palate and teeth, leading to a constricted maxillary arch, proclination of upper incisors, and an anterior open bite.		/api/uploads/e4d84dddba91b79d9372f537842cc2b0.png
e4ebf6df-333d-4a31-9edc-21348eb9b0a5	pedodontics	BDS-IV	Radiograph showing 'Shell Teeth' appearance with very thin dentin and extremely large pulp chambers. This is a characteristic feature of:\n 	{"Dentinogenesis Imperfecta Type I","Dentinogenesis Imperfecta Type II","Dentinogenesis Imperfecta Type III","Dentin Dysplasia."}	2	Dentinogenesis Imperfecta Type III (Brandywine isolate) is characterized by 'shell teeth' where the dentin is very thin and the pulp chambers are remarkably large due to restricted dentin formation.\n 		/api/uploads/dea7b68438ad9be6f5c40c7ab9a2c48a.png
be331bea-32e8-4322-b867-7cd9c5fdcdb5	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	In a ground section of a tooth, brown-colored bands are seen running obliquely from the dentino-enamel junction (DEJ) toward the enamel surface. \nIdentify these structures.	{"Hunter-Schreger bands","Incremental lines of Retzius","Enamel lamellae","Enamel tufts."}	1	These lines represent the rhythmic mineralization of enamel matrix and appear as concentric circles in transverse sections and oblique lines in longitudinal sections.		/api/uploads/9e7eb35c7aa7129b35b9e13dbfe412b1.png
fa4705b7-afd6-4f5c-847c-f20e5e7c7104	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	An occlusal view of a maxillary first molar shows a fifth cusp located on the mesiolingual cusp.\n Identify this feature. 	{"Cusp of Bolk","Cusp of Carabelli","Distolingual tubercle","Parastyle. "}	1	The Cusp of Carabelli as a non-functional accessory cusp typically found on the lingual surface of the mesiolingual cusp of the permanent maxillary first molar.		/api/uploads/0f0b67922f1059f9f3887a0a8552952d.png
bf64a7ac-cbff-47b7-beb1-486b36b2974c	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	 A histological section of root dentin shows a dark, granular-appearing zone located just beneath the cementum. What is this layer? 	{" Predentin","Interglobular dentin","Tomes' granular layer","Hyaline layer of Hopewell-Smith. "}	2	Ten Cate's Oral Histology identifies Tomes' granular layer as a characteristic feature of root dentin, believed to be formed by the looping and coalescing of the terminal ends of dentinal tubules.		/api/uploads/21d4d39a14d148a47fb5280a64bc01bc.png
2a33433a-af9e-4002-a255-624b5515c885	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	A lingual view of a mandibular first premolar shows a distinct groove crossing the mesial marginal ridge. What is the name of this groove? 	{"Mesiolingual developmental groove","Central groove","Distolingual groove","Buccal groove."}	0	As noted in Wheeler's Dental Anatomy, the mesiolingual developmental groove is a unique identifying feature of the mandibular first premolar, separating the mesial marginal ridge from the lingual cusp.		/api/uploads/fb97f82fec56eeaaaa61e334fe7b83e1.png
ccaa0340-5ba7-4397-aecf-de6acd1a11e2	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	A microscopic image of the apical third of a root shows cells trapped within lacunae inside the mineralized tissue\nIdentify the tissue. 	{"Acellular cementum","Cellular cementum","Primary dentin","Alveolar bone."}	1	Cellular cementum (Secondary cementum) contains cementocytes housed in lacunae and is primarily found in the apical third and furcation areas of the root to compensate for occlusal wear, as detailed in Orban's and Ten Cate's.		/api/uploads/360f19c43634cbb61c0e6173ad0d8a58.png
120c483f-cf38-48bf-87ff-423f193e39b0	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Identify the tooth that exhibits an 'H-shaped' occlusal groove pattern and a prominent mesiobuccal cervical ridge (Moll's tubercle). 	{"Primary Mandibular First Molar","Primary Maxillary First Molar","Permanent Maxillary First Molar","Primary Maxillary Second Molar. "}	1	The primary maxillary first molar is characterized by a unique 'H' groove pattern on its occlusal surface and a distinct cervical bulge on the mesiobuccal aspect 		/api/uploads/61b24a47d6cb7f1335f9b767423c612d.png
6ec313e6-709c-4c7a-be1a-20725dd2f748	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	In a histological image of the pulp-dentin complex, identify the tall columnar cells with nuclei located basally, away from the dentin. 	{" Ameloblasts"," Odontoblasts",Cementoblasts,"Fibroblasts. "}	1	Odontoblasts are the peripheral cells of the dental pulp responsible for dentinogenesis; they are tall columnar in the crown and become cuboidal or flat in the root 		/api/uploads/e5228630e80efcadeab6fa75efabebeb.png
254041b7-630d-4ef6-ae34-202a985cbd4e	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Identify the histological feature characterized by hypocalcinated, leaf-like structures extending from the enamel surface toward the DEJ. 	{"Enamel Spindles","Enamel Tufts","Enamel Lamellae","Enamel Rods. "}	2	Enamel lamellae are thin, leaf-like defects filled with organic material that extend from the enamel surface and may reach the dentino-enamel junction 		/api/uploads/59e902aa70010d8bf9d30932c00c1b14.png
1161ad67-0926-4b22-a0ea-5b8cdedcbfa9	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Lingual view of a Maxillary Lateral Incisor showing a deep pit. Name this anatomical feature.	{Cingulum,"Lingual fossa","Foramen cecum (Lingual pit)","Mamelon. "}	2	The lingual pit, or foramen cecum, is frequently found on maxillary lateral incisors at the junction of the cingulum and the lingual fossa, making it highly susceptible to caries 		/api/uploads/99146b3ab616a83bdb7ec73cb52e0f22.png
3f3a429a-5910-441c-9c82-850db3e52190	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Histological section of dentin showing a clear, cell-free zone between the pulp and the odontoblastic layer\nThis is known as:	{"Zone of Weil","Cell-rich zone","Pulp proper",Predentin.}	0	The Zone of Weil (cell-free zone) is located immediately subadjacent to the odontoblastic layer in the dental pulp and contains a rich network of capillaries and nerve fibers 		/api/uploads/390c53726fb9ca20b3ca04a4da397bf8.png
a259a90d-3798-443b-9d24-b3af816822b0	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Proximal view of a maxillary central incisor\nWhat is the shape of the cervical line (CEJ) on the mesial surface	{Straight,"Curved towards the incisal","Curved towards the root","Irregular. "}	1	In Dental Anatomy, the mesial curvature of the cervical line in a maxillary central incisor is the greatest of any tooth in the mouth, curving incisally.		/api/uploads/578185179db8e453c1c80512475ad671.png
d9342dbb-4485-49ad-8bfc-ebca642f1937	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	High magnification of enamel showing alternating light and dark bands \nIdentify these optical phenomena	{"Enamel lamellae","Enamel tufts","Hunter-Schreger bands","Enamel spindles."}	2	Hunter-Schreger bands are an optical phenomenon produced by changes in the direction of enamel rods, appearing as alternating light (parazones) and dark (diazones) bands.		/api/uploads/4515dd3703253560fa6f9164999c313a.png
335c99d1-78da-46a4-99e2-e4741021dc58	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	In a histological slide of developing tooth germ, the structure appearing as a 'bell' shows four distinct layers. Identify the layer located between the internal enamel epithelium and the stellate reticulum. 	{"Stratum intermedium","Dental follicle","Dental papilla","Cervical loop."}	0	The stratum intermedium consists of two to three layers of squamous cells essential for the mineralization of enamel by providing alkaline phosphatase.		/api/uploads/356d496eb6310f5c47c0beaf437a1bee.png
05278fa8-f2fa-406b-a8f8-f0fc7eaf2b07	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Incisal view of a Mandibular Lateral Incisor \nHow is the incisal edge typically oriented relative to the root axis?	{"Twisted mesially","Twisted distolingually","Perfectly straight","Tilted labially."}	1	The mandibular lateral incisor is characterized by an incisal edge that is twisted distolingually on the root base to follow the mandibular arch curvature.		/api/uploads/976ba57170a7735bc482b619ea50db63.png
d931f4fe-d224-4dc0-800a-306f41845c1c	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Labial view of a newly erupted Mandibular Central Incisor showing three rounded protuberances on the incisal edge\nName these features:	{Cingula,Mamelons," Tubercles","Cusplets. "}	1	Mamelons are the three rounded protuberances found on the incisal ridges of newly erupted incisors, representing the three labial developmental lobes as described in Wheeler's.		/api/uploads/a758ded17ba218ea3329afa4f5b2b685.png
ff40611d-aec4-4bf6-9c90-dfb362f1095e	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	In which region of the tooth is 'Gnarled Enamel' most frequently found?	{"Cervical region","Mid-coronal enamel","Cuspal and incisal regions","Enamel-Dentin Junction. "}	2	gnarled enamel as the complex, irregular twisting of enamel rods which increases the tissue's resistance to fracture, predominantly located in the cusps and incisal edges where masticatory stress is highest.		/api/uploads/307bc40943bf6a4dd32a945faef15555.png
544d91e7-9846-4bb3-b44a-57b64333d93a	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Proximal view of a Mandibular First Premolar showing a very small lingual cusp and a large buccal cusp\nWhat is the significant feature of the occlusal plane in this tooth?	{"It is tilted buccally","It is tilted lingually","It is perfectly horizontal","It is tilted mesially."}	1	The occlusal table of the mandibular first premolar is unique because it slopes sharply in a lingual direction due to the small size of the lingual cusp and the prominence of the buccal cusp, which aligns with the functional requirements of the mandibular arch.		/api/uploads/8b94d573e00ca619bd68db557f11a183.png
907c3a26-eda0-44f4-9b15-a7900bd563e9	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	In an H&E stained section of the periodontal ligament (PDL), specialized elastic-like fibers are seen running parallel to the root surface and often terminating in the walls of blood vessels. Identify these fibers. 	{"Collagen fibers ","Oxytalan fibers","Elaunin fibers ","Reticular fibers."}	1	Oxytalan fibers are a type of immature elastic fiber found in the PDL. They run vertically from the cementum and are thought to regulate vascular flow or provide support during masticatory stress, according to Ten Cate's.		/api/uploads/ba2a8a050a188f30573810a9f4a039bc.png
a261f7b9-9468-47b5-9ef7-477260a9a574	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	A histological section of the submandibular salivary gland shows a mixed acinus where serous cells form a crescent-shaped cap over the mucous cells. What is this histological feature called? 	{"Serous demilunes","Intercalated ducts ","Myoepithelial caps ","Striated ducts. "}	0	Serous demilunes (or Demilunes of Giannuzzi) are characteristic of mixed salivary glands. Although modern fixation suggests they may be an artifact of traditional histological preparation, they remain a key diagnostic feature for mixed acini in standard H&E sections.		/api/uploads/7673b5a5cda20c658590d72df3744bde.png
4cc8a6c9-3201-45c6-8ae0-cc663c552a32	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	In an H&E stained section of a developing tooth, a layer of tall columnar cells is seen with nuclei positioned away from the basement membrane (reverse polarity). What is the function of these cells? 	{"Dentin formation","Enamel formation","Cementum formation","Pulp regeneration. "}	1	These cells are pre-ameloblasts/ameloblasts. As per dental histology textbooks, they exhibit reverse polarity of nuclei before they begin secreting enamel matrix.		/api/uploads/b37f5a542094c1c4244d7ad7c8ff692b.png
e393850d-68ad-4238-bcfc-0e98212236dd	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Which incremental lines in dentin represent the rhythmic, daily deposition of dentin matrix? 	{"Contour lines of Owen","Incremental lines of von Ebner","Neonatal line","Perikymata. "}	1	Lines of von Ebner are analogous to cross-striations in enamel and represent the daily apposition of dentin at a rate of 4-6 micrometers in the crown 		/api/uploads/00b9ff87fa9f25c63166326f042a46bd.png
3403e1f9-4a95-4166-875d-0174b969a4fb	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	What are the dark, brush-like structures extending from the dentino-enamel junction (DEJ) into the enamel? 	{"Enamel spindles","Enamel tufts","Enamel lamellae","Dentinal tubules"}	1	Enamel tufts are hypomineralized ribbon-like structures that project from the DEJ into the enamel for about one-third of its thickness, resembling tufts of grass 		/api/uploads/ffca3e3c1d67c7c5d3e576309b81dbbb.png
a2cd5ea1-1bdb-4947-afc8-48f075ddc567	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	High magnification of dentin showing a specialized layer of dentin immediately adjacent to the pulp\n What is this layer? 	{"Mantle dentin","Circumpulpal dentin"," Predentin","Secondary dentin."}	2	Predentin is the initial, unmineralized organic matrix of dentin located between the odontoblasts and the mineralized dentin, 		/api/uploads/fe0dee937c69f0b6bf63a2924b8a4343.png
96c4491b-0ba6-4293-9599-3fb8c6af3433	anatomy	BDS-I	A histology slide showing a tissue with cells in lacunae and a glass-like, homogenous matrix\nIdentify the tissue.	{" Fibrocartilage","Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Compact bone."}	2	Hyaline cartilage is characterized by a pearly bluish-white, homogenous matrix with chondrocytes arranged in isogenous groups within lacunae 		/api/uploads/bf59a8f9e489ce9d024dbd87851d072c.png
397eb391-4596-407f-8e3d-f47452841ec1	anatomy	BDS-I	A diagram of the brachial plexus with an arrow pointing to the nerve arising from the posterior cord that supplies the deltoid muscle\nIdentify the nerve. 	{"Radial nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve. "}	1	The axillary nerve originates from the posterior cord (C5, C6) and supplies the deltoid and teres minor muscles 		/api/uploads/0b522d3422fbab6cd106af41d0e78c43.png
583cd43d-7ead-4c80-b3c6-b7a03f87eb31	anatomy	BDS-I	Which nerve provides general somatic sensation (touch, pain, temperature) to this highlighted region? 	{"Glossopharyngeal nerve","Lingual nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Chorda tympani."}	1	The lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve (V3), provides general sensation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, while the chorda tympani carries taste fibers to the same area.		/api/uploads/0c01b0a9b62fbb45adfd01e973555b08.png
72e0d888-b41a-40d5-acb0-ca2fff5a6fba	anatomy	BDS-I	Histological slide of compact bone showing concentric lamellae surrounding a central canal  \nIdentify the structural unit of compact bone shown in the image.	{Osteon,Trabeculae,"Medullary cavity",Periosteum.}	0	The Osteon (or Haversian system) is the fundamental functional unit of compact bone, consisting of concentric lamellae of bone matrix surrounding a central Haversian canal.		/api/uploads/e0e3e0571cd632ae9e9d713aa21c8186.png
2bc6177d-020b-4bdb-a287-3f0191a526c4	anatomy	BDS-I	Diagram of a typical long bone with an arrow pointing to the region between the epiphysis and the diaphysis\nWhat is the name of this transitional zone where active longitudinal growth occurs?	{Metaphysis,Apophysis,Endosteum,"Cortex. "}	0	The metaphysis is the most actively growing part of a long bone, situated between the epiphysis and the diaphysis, containing the epiphyseal plate.		/api/uploads/7fbf78921921b529eae3dab36d7284f1.png
561c6e82-da92-480f-8f1d-e880f0384094	anatomy	BDS-I	Cross-section of the spinal cord showing H-shaped grey matter with an arrow pointing to the posterior projection\nThe structure indicated (dorsal horn) primarily contains which type of neurons?	{"Somatic Motor","Autonomic Motor",Sensory,Sympathetic.}	2	the posterior (dorsal) horns of the spinal cord grey matter are primarily involved in receiving and processing sensory information.		/api/uploads/da5c71d32b15c6a68ff43e63290f6e27.png
ce1cd23a-cda7-4946-a190-559c3bf1c367	anatomy	BDS-I	A histological image shows a neuron with a single process that divides into a peripheral and a central branch.\nWhere is this cell type typically located?	{"Cerebral cortex","Ventral horn of the spinal cord ","Dorsal root ganglion","Retina. "}	2	Pseudounipolar neurons are characteristically found in the sensory ganglia of spinal and cranial nerves, such as the dorsal root ganglion 		/api/uploads/47659e0e67ebad481c89897398a38260.png
