d463612e-a75d-4e2c-bba6-61025ccf636e	surgery	BDS-III	An image shows a firm, nodular lesion at the umbilicus in a 60-year-old patient with weight loss and epigastric pain. What is this clinical sign? 	{"Cullen's sign ","Sister Mary Joseph's nodule ","Grey Turner's sign","Troisier’s sign."}	1	Sister Mary Joseph's nodule is a metastatic umbilical deposit indicating advanced intra-abdominal malignancy, most commonly an adenocarcinoma of the stomach, colon, or ovary. 		/api/uploads/cbfb8a6cf85155850765480638b757d6.png
3e949636-f29c-40a4-a169-d99cb318aef9	surgery	BDS-III	A clinical sign showing 'Risus Sardonicus'—a characteristic sardonic grin due to facial muscle spasms.	{"Tetanus ","Gas gangrene ",Rabies,"Bell's palsy"}	0	Risus Sardonicus is caused by the spasm of the facial muscles (masticatory and circumoral muscles) due to the tetanospasmin toxin produced by Clostridium tetani		/api/uploads/817d289c55b5726f11653d8d1db97cd1.png
aa399593-f5c0-4de2-8b33-900127d65923	dental_anatomy	BDS-I	Identify the terminal portions of the principal fibers of the periodontal ligament that are embedded into the cementum and alveolar bone	{"Oxytalan fibers","Sharpey's fibers","Indifferent fiber plexus","Eluanin fibers. "}	1	Sharpey's fibers are the mineralized ends of the PDL principal fibers (Type I collagen) that provide the primary attachment of the tooth to the surrounding bone.		/api/uploads/d4f255f17273340643eed8eaee86fe59.png
7cd6f5b2-cfd7-402e-911e-63b14b17be1e	oral_path	BDS-III	Histopathology slide showing multiple small, round, or oval spaces within islands of tumor cells, resembling 'Swiss cheese'.Which condition is most likely? \n	{"Pleomorphic Adenoma","Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma ","Mucoepidermoid Carcinoma ","Warthin's Tumor. "}	1	the 'Swiss cheese' or cribriform pattern is the most common and characteristic histopathological feature of Adenoid Cystic Carcinoma, representing pseudocystic spaces filled with basophilic mucoid material.\n		/api/uploads/ac9248ed22af5dbe2dac8eab035a3691.png
7f569784-6fb4-4cce-98bd-e09f1262c470	oral_path	BDS-III	Radiograph of the posterior mandible showing a multilocular radiolucency with a 'Soap bubble' appearance. Identify the lesion. \n	{"Odontogenic Keratocyst ","Central Giant Cell Granuloma ","Ameloblastoma ","Cherubism. "}	2	 Ameloblastoma is a benign but locally invasive odontogenic tumor that characteristically presents as a multilocular radiolucency, often described as 'soap bubble' or 'honeycombed' on a radiograph.\n		/api/uploads/189f2c9adb068b6b09ba1f12fe4aad73.png
a4653d58-a36c-40bf-b998-d3dd89a6d359	oral_path	BDS-III	Clinical photograph of the buccal mucosa showing interlacing white lines known as Wickham's striae.What is the most probable diagnosis? \n	{"Leukoplakia ","Reticular Lichen Planus ","Candidiasis ","White Sponge Nevus."}	1	Wickham's striae are fine, lace-like white lines characteristic of reticular Lichen Planus, caused by focal thickening of the granular layer and hyperkeratosis.\n		/api/uploads/476298639b258a0d2eb2350420a71c65.png
63442a4b-6500-481e-a2fe-18995a282b42	oral_path	BDS-III	Radiograph showing a 'Sun-ray' or 'Sun-burst' periosteal reaction in the mandible.] Which malignancy is associated with this appearance?\n	{"Ewing's Sarcoma",Osteosarcoma,"Multiple Myeloma","Squamous Cell Carcinoma."}	1	 Osteosarcoma of the jaws often displays a 'sun-ray' appearance due to the formation of spicules of new bone (osteoid) radiating outward from the cortex under the periosteum.\n		/api/uploads/9ae9a46e0524d3384a0c53ff85178a27.png
5ea9ca55-abc6-4da4-80b0-98c5ef0f3b0c	oral_path	BDS-III	Histopathology showing 'Ghost Cells' within an odontogenic epithelial lining.] Which lesion is characterized by these features? \n	{"Calcifying Odontogenic Cyst (Gorlin Cyst) ","Dentigerous Cyst ","Radicular Cyst ","Ameloblastic Fibroma."}	0	COC is uniquely identified by the presence of 'ghost cells,' which are enlarged, eosinophilic epithelial cells that have lost their nuclei but preserved their cell outlines.\n\n		/api/uploads/1b66a8ceb25df66405c7bf654ab2626a.png
70d9f257-d180-4980-b59c-9b78c4345c0b	oral_path	BDS-III	A clinical image shows a 'mulberry'-like appearance of the first permanent molar with numerous small, poorly developed globules of enamel on the occlusal surface. This is indicative of:	{"Hutchinson's Incisors","Mulberry Molars","Turner's Hypoplasia","Regional Odontodysplasia."}	1	As per Shafer's and Orbans, Mulberry molars are a manifestation of congenital syphilis where the occlusal surface of the first permanent molar is narrower than the cervical portion and is covered with multiple small globular enamel growths.\n		/api/uploads/ab48957585e5302cb56966d2e4c8d09a.png
8e9e8fa2-513e-46f2-ab70-a807d3612f5b	oral_path	BDS-III	A radiograph of a 40-year-old patient reveals a 'soap-bubble' multilocular radiolucency in the molar-ramus area of the mandible. Which odontogenic tumor is most likely? \n	{Ameloblastoma,"Odontogenic Keratocyst","Adenomatoid Odontogenic Tumor","Ameloblastic Fibroma."}	0	 Ameloblastoma is a locally invasive epithelial odontogenic tumor. Radiographically, it often presents as a multilocular radiolucency described as 'soap-bubble' (large loculations) or 'honeycomb' (small loculations) due to the presence of bony septa.\n\n		/api/uploads/ade6db6e07c9787c0f87f427faaa96fe.png
d91f1370-199c-403e-9f00-39dd96e1a0c4	oral_path	BDS-III	A periapical radiograph shows a well-defined, unilocular radiolucency attached to the cemento-enamel junction (CEJ) of an unerupted mandibular third molar. What is the most probable diagnosis? \n	{"Radicular Cyst","Dentigerous Cyst","Eruption Cyst","Stafne's Bone Cyst."}	1	defines the dentigerous (follicular) cyst as one that encloses the crown of an unerupted tooth and is attached to the tooth at the CEJ. This is a key diagnostic feature to distinguish it from other odontogenic cysts\n\n		/api/uploads/61c381d8faf1fae55279ef97138dbd37.png
45d5e289-6bf6-4851-a955-53bab1c461b4	oral_path	BDS-III	A section of a lesion from the tongue shows large cells with abundant granular cytoplasm and a small, vesicular nucleus. The overlying epithelium shows Pseudoepitheliomatous Hyperplasia (PEH). Diagnosis? 	{"Squamous Cell Carcinoma","Granular Cell Tumor",Hemangioma,"Pyogenic Granuloma."}	1	Granular Cell Tumor is often mistaken for SCC because of the prominent PEH, but the characteristic large granular cells and S-100 protein positivity (neural origin) confirm the diagnosis.\n\n		/api/uploads/428354d34c6b4560cd60b1aadcd48899.png
3d789941-182a-4b29-a69f-76f6bf04e732	oral_path	BDS-III	Histopathology reveals 'Star-shaped' or stellate reticulum-like cells in the center of follicles, surrounded by a layer of tall columnar cells with reverse polarity. \n	{"Squamous Cell Carcinoma","Calcifying Epithelial Odontogenic Tumor",Ameloblastoma,"Adenomatoid Odontogenic Tumor"}	2	The follicular pattern of Ameloblastoma is characterized by islands of odontogenic epithelium where peripheral cells show 'Vickers-Gorlin' features (reverse polarity) and central cells resemble stellate reticulum.\n\n		/api/uploads/f04e482d6634a1c1f03f3b1e6afca24c.png
b618776c-aa81-4593-8fa5-850289e5c2c8	oral_path	BDS-III	A clinical image shows a 'white, lace-like' pattern (Wickham's striae) on the buccal mucosa.\n	{" Leukoplakia","Oral Lichen Planus",Candidiasis," Psoriasis"}	1	 the characteristic reticular pattern of Lichen Planus as Wickham's striae, which is a result of hyperkeratosis and acanthosis in a specific lace-like arrangement.\n\n		/api/uploads/4ea2dd22fd4c317e2ccf499ae8d4c57e.png
b61b34ed-7bbe-42ec-83e3-3d464697b9c9	oral_path	BDS-III	An H&E section of a lesion shows 'Tzanck cells' within an intraepithelial suprabasal split\n	{"Bullous Pemphigoid","Pemphigus Vulgaris","Lichen Planus","Herpes Simplex. "}	1	Pemphigus Vulgaris by the presence of acantholysis, where loss of desmosomal attachments leads to suprabasal clefting (Vesicle formation) and the presence of rounded, floating Tzanck cells.\n		/api/uploads/f16318b243e9fd6de7c2fb1a8f5608da.png
\.
